return json_decode(<<<'GRAMMAR_V5_TUTOR'
[{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d01-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Grammar, meaning and units of structure","text":"Grammar, meaning and units of structure\n\nGrammar describes the patterns by which words and larger units combine and change form. A grammatical analysis asks how an expression is built; interpretation asks what it means in a particular context. Neither question can be answered reliably by word order alone.\n\nBegin with a familiar message: The nurse opened the window. Its words work together to identify someone, an event and something affected. Reversing nurse and window changes who does what, even though most words remain unchanged. Grammar therefore carries meaning. Vocabulary also matters: The window congratulated the nurse follows a recognisable structural pattern but normally needs an imaginative context. Structural well-formedness and ordinary real-world plausibility are different judgements. When evaluating an unfamiliar sentence, identify which judgement you are making before calling it wrong.\n\nA phrase is a unit organised around a head, its central word; it can contain one word or several. In the unusually quiet room, room heads a noun phrase, quiet heads an adjective phrase and unusually is an adverb. A clause organises a situation around a verb, often with a subject and complements. The nurse opened the window is a finite clause. To open the window is a non-finite clause: its verb has no independent tense. This course introduces these terms now and analyses their internal structure in Days 13–15.\n\nA full written sentence normally contains a main clause and appropriate boundary punctuation. A subordinate clause can be part of that sentence: The nurse opened the window because the room was warm. Because introduces a reason but does not make the first event happen in the grammar itself; the writer asserts that causal relationship. Short answers, labels and conversational fragments can be meaningful without a full main clause. No entry on a sign and After lunch in reply to When? are useful expressions; formal paragraph writing normally requires fuller structures.\n\nGrammatical correctness, contextual acceptability and editorial style must be distinguished. A sentence may be grammatical but too informal for a report, or perfectly clear in conversation but unclear without its shared setting. Standard British and American forms can differ without one being a mistake. A descriptive account records established patterns; a prescriptive convention recommends choices for a stated purpose. In this course, British English supplies the editorial default, while alternatives are identified explicitly. An asterisk in a grammar book often marks an unacceptable intended reading; it does not prove every imaginable use impossible.\n\nmain clause + because + subordinate clause: The second clause supplies the writer’s reason for the first situation.\n\nThe courier arrived.\nA main clause forms a complete neutral written statement.\n\nThe courier arrived after the office closed.\nA subordinate time clause locates the arrival relative to the closure.\n\nTo reach the archive before noon, Sara took an earlier bus.\nThe opening non-finite clause states a purpose; Sara is understood as the person reaching the archive.\n\nClose the window is a grammatical imperative with an ordinarily unexpressed subject. Do not add you merely to satisfy a simplified statement that every sentence must display a subject.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=1&section=gv5-d01-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Subjects - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subjects"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d01-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Inside words: morphemes, roots, stems and word formation","text":"Inside words: morphemes, roots, stems and word formation\n\nA morpheme is a smallest unit carrying lexical meaning or grammatical information. A root supplies the central lexical material; a stem is the form to which an inflection is attached. Inflection supplies grammatical forms, while derivation creates a new lexical item.\n\nCompare help, helpful and helpfulness. Help carries the central meaning; -ful helps form an adjective, and -ness helps form a noun. These added parts cannot normally stand alone as words, so they are bound morphemes. Help can stand alone, making it a free morpheme. Meaningful parts are not the same as syllables: helpfulness has three readily identifiable morphemes, but a long word need not contain a meaningful part for each syllable. Avoid splitting letters into pieces unless each piece has a defensible role in contemporary English.\n\nInflection relates forms of one lexical item: file/files, walk/walked/walking and small/smaller. Derivation changes the vocabulary item: teach/teacher or happy/unhappy/happiness. Derivation can change word class, as happy to happiness does, but need not: happy and unhappy are both adjectives. In teachers, teach is the root, teacher is the stem receiving the plural ending and -s marks plural number. A base is the material to which an affix attaches at a particular step; it can already contain other affixes. These labels describe different analytical roles rather than competing spellings.\n\nA prefix precedes its base, as un- in unfair; a suffix follows it, as -ment in agreement. Compounding combines lexical bases: raincoat or classroom. Conversion uses an established form in a new class without an overt affix: a text and to text. Spelling does not supply a perfect guide to structure: compounds may be closed, hyphenated or written separately. Learn a new word together with its meaning, word class and usual pattern. A productive pattern permits new formations readily, but a familiar suffix cannot be added freely to every possible base.\n\nRoots and stems can be irregular or show changes of spelling and sound. The past went is an inflectional form of go even though it is not built by visibly adding -ed. Morphological analysis is therefore more than chopping off endings. Contemporary structure also differs from etymology, the historical origin of a word: learners need not reconstruct ancient roots before understanding a current English form. This introductory model intentionally avoids deciding every disputed boundary, such as the status of English possessive -’s; that construction receives a phrase-level account on Day 2.\n\nroot + derivational suffix + inflectional suffix: teach + -er + -s forms a plural noun meaning people who teach.\n\nThe gardeners repaired the gate.\ngarden + -er forms a person noun; plural -s gives gardeners.\n\nThe unfair decision was withdrawn.\nun- derives unfair from fair without changing adjective class.\n\nPlease email the revised schedule.\nEmail is used as a verb here; its form does not require a new derivational ending.\n\nSome grammatical contrasts have no visible added suffix: sheep may be singular or plural. Interpret the word with its determiner, verb and context.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=1&section=gv5-d01-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"MORPHEME | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/morpheme"},{"publisher":"INFLECTION | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/inflection"},{"publisher":"STEM | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/stem"},{"publisher":"Word formation - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-formation"},{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d01-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Word classes and the difference between category and function","text":"Word classes and the difference between category and function\n\nA word class is a grammatical category identified through a combination of form, distribution and possible combinations. A function is a role within a larger structure. Noun is a category; subject and modifier are functions. A word’s meaning alone does not establish its class.\n\nNouns commonly head expressions naming people, things or ideas: teacher, bridge, patience. Verbs organise actions, events or states: repair, happen, know. Adjectives commonly describe or classify nouns: a narrow bridge. Adverbs modify activities, qualities, other adverbs or wider statements: works carefully, unusually narrow, fortunately. These are major open classes: new members enter them readily. Many words have more than one class, so classify their use in a particular sentence. The spelling clean occurs as an adjective in a clean room and as a verb in clean the room.\n\nOther classes include determiners, pronouns, prepositions, conjunctions and interjections. Determiners help specify a noun’s reference or quantity: the report, this report, some reports. Pronouns can head expressions such as she, someone or mine. Prepositions head relational expressions: under the table, after lunch. Conjunctions link units: paper and ink; because it rained. Interjections express reactions: Oh! These are relatively closed classes, although change is possible. Closed does not mean semantically empty: compare before lunch with after lunch, where one small word changes the time relationship.\n\nUse several clues together. Plural marking often identifies nouns; comparison often identifies gradable adjectives; auxiliary patterns identify verbs. Each clue has exceptions. News is singular, wooden normally lacks comparative wooden-er, and fast is an adverb in move fast despite lacking -ly. A noun can modify another noun: in a stone wall, stone remains a noun functioning as a premodifier. An adjective can follow a linking verb: the wall is old. Category and position therefore interact, but neither simply equals the other. Detailed patterns are developed in the dedicated noun, verb and adjective lessons.\n\nGrammars differ in some category boundaries. Traditional accounts often call my a possessive adjective; this course usually calls it a possessive determiner because it occupies a determiner position. Some modern accounts treat pronouns as a subclass of nouns or distinguish determinative as a word class from determiner as a function. Recognise the labels without assuming the analysis has changed the sentence’s meaning. The immediate goal is a defensible explanation supported by behaviour: name the category, then state its function in the expression. Do not turn every describing word into an adjective or every -ly word into an adverb.\n\ndeterminer + adjective + noun: The adjective modifies the noun; the determiner specifies reference.\n\nnoun + noun: The first noun can function as a modifier without changing class.\n\nThose careful researchers checked the figures.\nThose is a determiner, careful an adjective and researchers the noun head.\n\nThe college will finance the project.\nFinance is a lexical verb after modal will; the project is its object.\n\nThe finance office remained open unusually late.\nFinance is a noun modifier, open is an adjective complement, and unusually modifies adverb late.\n\nOne spelling can belong to different classes: They water the plants uses a verb, while The water is cold uses a noun. Explain the local pattern rather than assign one permanent label to the spelling.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=1&section=gv5-d01-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Determiners ( the, my, some, this ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/determiners-the-my-some-this"},{"publisher":"Nouns: compound nouns - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-compound-nouns"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d01-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Subjects, predicates and the first sentence patterns","text":"Subjects, predicates and the first sentence patterns\n\nThe subject is a grammatical element identified by structural behaviour, not simply the person doing an action. The predicate is what is predicated of that subject. Objects, predicative complements and adjuncts perform different roles within clause structure.\n\nStart with The librarian opened the parcel. The librarian is the subject; opened the parcel is the predicate. Opened is the verb and the parcel is the direct object. In a neutral declarative clause, English usually places these in subject–verb–object order, abbreviated SVO. Identify the whole subject phrase before naming its head: in The new librarian opened the parcel, the new librarian is the subject and librarian is its head. Keeping those two answers separate prevents a common analysis mistake. The predicate can be short or extended with additional information.\n\nNot every clause has an object. The visitors waited follows subject–verb, or SV. The room became quiet contains a linking verb and a subject complement: quiet describes the room rather than a separate affected object. A complement can be a noun phrase: Leena is a doctor. In The coach gave the players advice, the players is an indirect object and advice is the direct object. In They considered the advice useful, useful is an object complement, describing the advice. Learn these contrasts as meaningful patterns rather than force all sentences into SVO.\n\nAdjuncts add circumstances or comments: The visitors waited outside yesterday. Outside gives place and yesterday gives time. A complement is selected by a head’s pattern; an adjunct supplies a less tightly selected addition. With put, a location normally completes the pattern: Put the parcel on the desk. Deletion is a helpful clue, not an infallible test, since some selected complements can be left understood. A passive subject can be affected rather than an agent: The parcel was opened by the librarian. Subject is therefore a syntactic function, while agent and patient describe semantic roles.\n\nThe course uses verb narrowly for the verbal element and predicate broadly for everything predicated of the subject. In broader syntactic terminology, objects are also complements because the verb selects them; when distinguishing clause patterns, subject complement and object complement mean predicative descriptions. State which sense of complement is intended. Dummy it in It is raining and dummy there in There are two seats occupy subject positions without naming an ordinary participant. Questions and some emphatic clauses change ordinary word order, so the first noun before a verb is not a universal subject test. These constructions receive detailed treatment later.\n\nS + V + O: A transitive clause: The mechanic repaired the bicycle.\n\nS + linking V + subject complement: A predicative clause: The bicycle looks new.\n\nThe musicians rehearsed.\nSV: the musicians is subject; rehearsed is the verb and complete predicate.\n\nThe volunteers painted the classroom yesterday.\nSVO plus time adjunct: yesterday is outside the object noun phrase.\n\nThe board considered the proposed timetable impractical.\nThe proposed timetable is direct object; impractical is its predicative complement.\n\nImperatives normally omit an expressed subject: Read the notice. Questions can put an auxiliary before the subject: Did the visitors wait? Analyse the whole pattern.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=1&section=gv5-d01-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Subjects - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subjects"},{"publisher":"Objects - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/objects"},{"publisher":"Complements - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/complements"},{"publisher":"Adjuncts - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adjuncts"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d02-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Noun meanings and the formation of plurals","text":"Noun meanings and the formation of plurals\n\nA noun is a word class whose members typically head noun phrases. Meaning labels such as concrete, abstract, collective and material describe overlapping kinds of nouns; number is the grammatical contrast between singular and plural where that contrast is available.\n\nA common noun identifies a kind: river, student or proposal. A proper noun is conventionally a name: Aisha, Lahore or the Indus. Concrete nouns refer to things ordinarily perceived through the senses, such as a bell; abstract nouns refer to qualities, states or ideas, such as fairness. A collective noun names a group, such as committee. A material noun names a substance, such as copper. These labels overlap: committee is common and collective, while copper is common, concrete and material in its substance meaning. They are not seven exclusive boxes into which every noun must fit.\n\nRegular count plurals normally add -s or -es: roads, boxes, wishes. Consonant plus y usually changes to -ies, as city/cities; vowel plus y normally keeps y, as key/keys. Plural forms must be learned with the noun when spelling is irregular: child/children, woman/women, mouse/mice, tooth/teeth. Some nouns keep one form for both numbers: one sheep, six sheep. Check the whole expression before choosing a verb, since the spelling alone may not reveal number. Apostrophes do not form ordinary plurals: three shops, not three shop’s.\n\nSome borrowed nouns retain foreign-origin patterns: analysis/analyses, criterion/criteria and phenomenon/phenomena. Others have established alternatives: indexes and indices or appendixes and appendices. Meaning and field can affect which plural is conventional; consult a current dictionary or disciplinary style guide rather than assume the older-looking form is always superior. Compound plurals usually mark the noun that functions as head: two car parks and three sisters-in-law. In lexicalised compounds the exact form can be conventional, so memorising the complete singular/plural pair is safer than applying a spelling slogan to every multiword expression.\n\nProper names can acquire count uses without losing their connection to naming. Two Aishas may mean two people called Aisha; a Picasso may mean a work by Picasso. This is a contextual shift, not evidence that proper nouns never take articles or plurals. Semantic categories also shift with sense: glass is a substance in broken glass and a count object in three glasses. Number, countability and reference interact but are distinct. A grammatically plural word is not necessarily a group noun, and a collective singular does not necessarily denote just one person. Analyse the particular lexical sense and noun phrase.\n\nsingular count noun → plural form: Usually add -s/-es, but retain the noun’s established irregular form.\n\nhead noun inside a compound → plural head: sister-in-law → sisters-in-law; car park → car parks.\n\nThe children planted three trees.\nChildren is an irregular plural; trees is a regular plural.\n\nTwo analyses reached different conclusions.\nAnalyses is the plural of analysis; it is distinct from the verb analyses.\n\nSeveral sisters-in-law attended the reunion.\nThe plural marks sisters, the head of this compound.\n\nA noun’s spelling may remain unchanged: this sheep is restless but these sheep are restless. The determiner and verb make number explicit.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=2&section=gv5-d02-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Nouns: singular and plural - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-singular-and-plural"},{"publisher":"Nouns: compound nouns - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-compound-nouns"},{"publisher":"Nouns: countable and uncountable - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-countable-and-uncountable"},{"publisher":"Nouns: form - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-form"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d02-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Countability, special number patterns and group agreement","text":"Countability, special number patterns and group agreement\n\nCountability describes how a noun sense combines with numbers and determiners. A count use presents separate units; a noncount use presents a mass, substance, activity or abstraction without ordinary unit counting. The distinction is grammatical and depends on lexical meaning and context.\n\nCount nouns permit expressions such as a chair and several chairs. Noncount nouns in ordinary senses combine with some or much rather than a/an and do not take ordinary plural -s: information, advice, furniture, equipment, evidence and research. Use a piece of information, two items of equipment, three chairs or several studies when units matter. Evidence is not counted as evidences in ordinary general prose; a piece of evidence or several kinds of evidence states the intended unit. Learn each noun in a complete phrase, especially where another language uses a different countability pattern.\n\nA noun may have count and noncount senses. Experience means accumulated knowledge in She has experience, but an experience names a particular event. Two coffees can mean two servings; different wines can mean types. These changes are conventional, not permission to count every noncount noun freely. Advice and information normally retain their noncount pattern in everyday and academic prose. Much research is natural formal English. Where specialised dictionaries document a restricted plural, such as researches for particular investigations, identify that sense and register; use studies or research projects as the transparent general default.\n\nSome nouns have special number patterns. News and mathematics take singular agreement in their ordinary topic meanings despite final -s. Scissors, trousers, goods and belongings normally have plural form and agreement. One pair of scissors is singular because pair heads the expression; two pairs of scissors are plural. People is normally plural when meaning persons, while peoples can refer to distinct populations. Police normally takes a plural verb when referring to officers collectively. A noun’s final letters do not therefore predict either its countability or the verb automatically. Check its meaning and its head phrase.\n\nBritish group nouns can take singular agreement for a unit or plural agreement for members: The team is ready; The team are changing into their different uniforms. American English generally prefers singular agreement with a formally singular collective; the members of the team makes a plural construal explicit in either variety. Avoid claiming that a country has one exceptionless rule for all collectives. Keep the intended perspective clear when choosing pronouns. A group’s membership can be many people even when its grammar is singular; grammatical number and real-world number are different kinds of information. Day 10 develops these agreement choices further.\n\npiece/item/measure + of + noncount noun: A countable unit expression allows explicit counting without inventing a plural of the material noun.\n\nThe technician replaced two items of equipment.\nItems supplies countable units; equipment retains its ordinary noncount form.\n\nThe news is encouraging, but the police are cautious.\nNews is grammatically singular; police normally requires plural agreement.\n\nThe committee are exchanging their separate notes.\nA British member-focused plural is appropriate; the members of the committee is a clear alternative.\n\nA countability shift needs an established interpretation: two teas readily means servings, but two advices is not the ordinary way to request two recommendations.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=2&section=gv5-d02-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Nouns: countable and uncountable - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-countable-and-uncountable"},{"publisher":"Nouns: singular and plural - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-singular-and-plural"},{"publisher":"Subject–verb agreement - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subject-verb-agreement"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"},{"publisher":"RESEARCH definition and meaning | Collins English Dictionary","url":"https://www.collinsdictionary.com/dictionary/english/research"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d02-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Genitives, possession and noun relationships","text":"Genitives, possession and noun relationships\n\nThe genitive is a construction commonly marked by -’s or an apostrophe in writing. It expresses several relationships, including possession, association, authorship and time. An of-construction is another way to relate noun phrases, but the two patterns are not always interchangeable.\n\nWith an ordinary singular noun, write the student’s notebook. With a regular plural ending in s, write the students’ notebooks. With an irregular plural, write the children’s notebooks. First determine how many owners or associates are meant, then place the apostrophe. The apostrophe is not a general signal that a word ends in s. Compare two teachers, one teacher’s desk and two teachers’ desks: the three expressions communicate different grammatical information. Possessive pronouns such as hers, ours and theirs do not take an additional apostrophe.\n\nThe genitive often acts like a determiner: Amina’s bicycle identifies a bicycle through its relation to Amina. The relationship is not always literal ownership. Amina’s article may mean the article she wrote; tomorrow’s meeting relates the event to a time; the committee’s decision identifies its institutional source. The report’s conclusion and the conclusion of the report are both normal. People and organisations often favour the -’s pattern, while long phrases or some part–whole relationships may favour of. These are usage tendencies, not a grammatical rule that inanimate nouns can never take -’s.\n\nPlace the marker at the end of a relevant compound or phrase: my sister-in-law’s address. In a shared genitive, Sara and Imran’s proposal can identify one joint proposal; Sara’s and Imran’s separate proposals makes separate association explicit. The exact punctuation and wording can become awkward with pronouns or long coordination, so recast rather than rely on punctuation to carry an unclear meaning. Names ending in s allow editorial alternatives such as James’s and James’; follow a consistent style and recognise both. Do not mark one conventional style as universally ungrammatical.\n\nA double-genitive construction combines of with a genitive: a friend of Amina’s or a colleague of mine. It can select one member of an associated set and differs from of + object pronoun. A portrait of Amina depicts Amina; a portrait of Amina’s associates the portrait with her, often as owner or creator, depending on context. The marker’s ability to occur at a phrase edge motivates analysing it as a clitic in many modern grammars; traditional teaching often calls it a possessive ending. Knowing both labels is useful, but the practical task remains deciding the intended relationship and the exact phrase the marker belongs to.\n\nsingular noun + ’s; regular plural in -s + ’: the visitor’s coat; the visitors’ coats\n\na + noun + of + genitive: a neighbour of mine; a friend of Leena’s\n\nThe doctor’s bag was beside the doctors’ coats.\nThe first genitive is singular; the second is regular plural.\n\nThe company’s response arrived after a week’s delay.\nThe genitives express an institutional source and a duration.\n\nA colleague of my brother’s has rented the flat.\nThe outer expression selects a colleague through the genitive phrase my brother’s.\n\nNames ending in s have style-dependent written genitives. James’s answer and James’ answer should not be used as competing right/wrong choices without a specified editorial convention.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=2&section=gv5-d02-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Possession ( John’s car, a friend of mine ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/possession-john-s-car-a-friend-of-mine"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d02-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Noun phrases, modifiers, apposition and nominalisation","text":"Noun phrases, modifiers, apposition and nominalisation\n\nA noun phrase is organised around a noun or pronoun head and may contain determiners, modifiers and complements. Nominalisation forms or uses a noun to express an event, process or quality. Apposition places related noun phrases together to identify or describe a referent.\n\nExpand a simple noun step by step: report; the report; the detailed report; the detailed report on housing. Report remains the head. The specifies reference, detailed modifies it before the head and on housing adds information after the head. These dependents belong inside the same noun phrase. When the phrase acts as subject, recognise the whole unit rather than report alone. Expansion helps a writer be precise, but putting every available detail before a head makes a phrase difficult to read. Place information where its relationship is clearest.\n\nNouns can function as premodifiers: a research centre, a school library and an application form. The first noun does not become an adjective merely because it modifies another noun. A noun–noun expression can name many relationships: a school bus serves a school, a stone wall is made of stone and a history teacher teaches history. The relation must be learned from meaning and convention. Noun modifiers are often singular, but established plural modifiers occur in a sports centre and a savings account. Do not mechanically delete every -s before a second noun.\n\nApposition offers another way to identify: my colleague Farah integrates the name into the phrase, while Farah, our new coordinator, adds a supplementary description. Punctuation depends on whether information identifies or supplements, so do not add commas merely because a second noun phrase appears. Noun phrases can also contain complements such as the decision to postpone the inspection, where the infinitival clause specifies the decision’s content. Longer phrases may embed other phrases: applications from graduates of local colleges. Their hierarchical structure matters more than a flat list of nearby words.\n\nNominalisation allows an action or quality to become a noun: the council approved the plan becomes the council’s approval of the plan; the road is wide relates to the width of the road. This can make an event a topic that later sentences discuss. It can also hide who acted: the approval alone leaves the decision-maker unstated. Preserve agency, time and modality when rewriting. The proposal for a new inspection system is different from the proposal’s inspection system: the former identifies proposed content, while the latter may assign a system to a proposal awkwardly. Choose a structure for the relationship you intend, not merely because it sounds formal.\n\ndeterminer + premodifiers + noun head + postmodifiers/complements: The two recent reports on flooding are one expanded noun phrase.\n\nverb clause → related nominalisation: The council approved the plan → the council’s approval of the plan.\n\nThe research centre needs a new application form.\nResearch and application are noun modifiers, not necessarily adjectives.\n\nOur neighbour, a retired engineer, repaired the pump.\nThe supplementary appositive describes the already identified neighbour.\n\nThe committee’s rejection of the revised timetable surprised the applicants.\nRejection nominalises reject; the genitive preserves the rejecting body and of preserves the rejected content.\n\nPlural modifiers may be conventional: a sports centre is standard. Dictionary-backed established compounds override a beginner’s singular-modifier tendency.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=2&section=gv5-d02-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Nouns: compound nouns - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-compound-nouns"},{"publisher":"Possession ( John’s car, a friend of mine ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/possession-john-s-car-a-friend-of-mine"},{"publisher":"Complements - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/complements"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d03-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Articles, identification and general reference","text":"Articles, identification and general reference\n\nArticles are a, an and the. A/an introduces one member of a countable class; the normally signals that the intended referent is identifiable in the situation or discourse. Zero article means that no article occurs, not that the noun phrase has no grammatical structure.\n\nStart with a singular count use: a report, an interview. A/an depends on the next sound rather than the next letter: a university, an hour, an unusual report and a useful report. The next sound may belong to an adjective, not to the noun. An introduces one unspecified member for the listener, but the speaker may already know which one: I spoke to a specialist yesterday can describe a specific person. Do not confuse indefinite form with a speaker having no particular individual in mind.\n\nUse the when the intended referent is identifiable. A visitor rang the bell. The visitor asked for directions introduces the person and then refers back. First mention is not the only source of identification: Please close the door can work when the relevant door is obvious. A bridging link also helps: We entered a house. The kitchen was bright. The kitchen is identifiable through its relationship to the house even though kitchen has not appeared before. If several doors or kitchens are equally relevant, add enough description to establish which is meant.\n\nGeneral reference has several structures. Tigers are mammals uses a bare plural for the class generally. A tiger is a mammal describes a representative member. The tiger is endangered presents the species as a category; this singular generic pattern is conventional but cannot replace every bare-plural generalisation mechanically. Noncount nouns often have zero article for general reference: Water is essential. The water in this bottle is warm identifies a particular quantity. A noun being abstract does not itself forbid the: Justice matters, but the justice of the decision was disputed.\n\nDefiniteness, specificity and uniqueness are related ideas, not synonyms. The author of this report can identify a unique role even if the reader does not know the author’s name. I need a book about wetlands can be nonspecific, whereas I bought a book about wetlands may refer to a particular purchased book. Context decides whether a description sufficiently identifies its referent; grammar does not supply a universal first-mention checklist. A/an normally accompanies a singular count use, but determiners and constructions permit other patterns, developed below. The article belongs to the noun phrase and does not tell you the noun’s function by itself.\n\na/an + singular count noun: One member of a class, with the article chosen by the following sound.\n\nthe + identifying noun phrase: The writer presents the referent as recoverable from context, discourse or descriptive information.\n\nA pilot phoned. The pilot left a message.\nFirst indefinite mention is followed by an identifiable reference to the same person.\n\nThe roof of the library needs repairs.\nThe of-phrase helps identify the intended roof without an earlier mention of roof.\n\nCopper conducts electricity; the copper in this cable is damaged.\nThe first clause is generic; the second identifies a particular material quantity.\n\nLetters alone do not decide a/an: an MP uses the initial sound /em/, while a USB cable begins with /juː/. Accent and how an abbreviation is spoken can change the appropriate choice.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=3&section=gv5-d03-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"A/an and the - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/a-an-and-the"},{"publisher":"Nouns: countable and uncountable - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-countable-and-uncountable"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d03-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Articles with institutions, names and abstract nouns","text":"Articles with institutions, names and abstract nouns\n\nArticle patterns depend on noun meaning, conventional names and the activity being described. A geographical or institutional expression must be learned as a whole pattern, with regional alternatives identified where they affect interpretation.\n\nCompare school as an institution and a school as a building or organisation. The children are at school normally describes participation as pupils. I waited outside the school identifies a particular location. British English commonly uses in hospital for a patient, while in the hospital can identify a building; American English commonly uses in the hospital for the patient meaning too. Bed has a conventional zero-article sleep use: go to bed. These are established constructions, not evidence that every building used for its purpose loses its article. A person may go to the bank for ordinary banking.\n\nNames have conventional article patterns. Many personal names, cities, countries and individual mountains have zero article: Sana, Karachi, Pakistan and Mount Kenya. Some country names conventionally include the, including the United Kingdom and the United States. Rivers, seas, oceans and mountain ranges commonly take the: the Indus, the Arabian Sea and the Himalayas. A plural name or a descriptive political name can therefore differ from a simple country name. Learn the complete name, rather than infer the article from national importance or size. Local institutional names also have their own conventions.\n\nAbstract and material nouns can be general, specific or conventionally countable in different senses. Education is important is general; the education she received was expensive identifies particular education. She had an excellent education uses a recognised count interpretation of an educational experience. The same contrast explains life and a life, knowledge and a detailed knowledge of the region. Do not add a/an to ordinary information or advice merely because an adjective intervenes. Useful advice remains noncount. Meals, languages, sports and subjects often have zero article in general activity uses, but a restrictive description can identify an instance: the lunch we ordered arrived late.\n\nArticle omission also occurs in names, headlines, notices, coordinated expressions and role descriptions. They elected her chair is a recognised complement construction; it does not license They bought chair for an ordinary object. Proper names may acquire modified or count uses, as a young Mozart or the London of her childhood, and such expressions need contextual interpretation. British and American conventions should be described at the construction level. At university is a useful British educational pattern; at college is common in American contexts. A writer choosing a particular national convention should preserve it while recognising legitimate standard alternatives.\n\nat school / in hospital in relevant British institutional uses: The noun evokes the institutional activity or participant status.\n\nthe + specified abstract/material noun: The phrase identifies a particular instance, kind or quantity.\n\nHer son is at school, but she is at the school for a meeting.\nThe first describes pupil status; the second identifies a location.\n\nThe expedition crossed the Indus before travelling towards Islamabad.\nThe river name takes the; the city name normally does not.\n\nA detailed knowledge of local law would help; knowledge alone is not enough.\nThe first has an established qualified count expression; the second is a general noncount use.\n\nInstitutional zero article is lexical and regional: go to prison does not imply that every prison-related noun phrase must omit the. The prison beside the river needs the when it identifies a building.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=3&section=gv5-d03-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"A/an and the - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/a-an-and-the"},{"publisher":"Nouns: countable and uncountable - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-countable-and-uncountable"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d03-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Determiners, distribution and quantity","text":"Determiners, distribution and quantity\n\nA determiner helps specify reference or quantity within a noun phrase. Articles, demonstratives, possessive determiners, numbers and many quantifiers take part in this system. Their distribution depends on number, countability and the meaning of the construction.\n\nDemonstratives distinguish near or currently prominent reference from more distant reference: this form, that form, these forms, those forms. Possessive determiners include my, your, her, our and their; they normally replace an article in the central determiner position: my report, not the my report. Whose and which can introduce noun phrases in questions: Which route is open? What kind of evidence is available? In this course, these uses are called determiner uses. Some school descriptions use labels such as demonstrative adjective; identifying the noun-phrase role matters more than confusing terminology with correctness.\n\nCardinal numbers state quantities, such as three rooms; ordinal numbers order members, such as the third room. Quantifiers specify amount or number more loosely: many rooms, much space, several applications and enough evidence. Many combines with plural count nouns; much combines with noncount nouns. Much is especially ordinary in negatives and questions, and also in formal positive phrases such as much research. A lot of can combine with either pattern. Enough precedes a noun but follows an adjective: enough time and clear enough. Its meaning is sufficiency for a stated or understood purpose, not simply a large quantity.\n\nEach and every distribute attention across individual members: each proposal is different; every proposal needs a signature. Each can stand alone and can occur with of: each of the proposals. Every normally needs a singular count head; use every one of the proposals rather than every of them. Both refers to two together; either selects or permits one of two, and neither excludes the two alternatives. All is not limited to two: all the proposals. These words can interact with negation: not all proposals passed means some did not, whereas no proposals passed excludes every one. Their meanings cannot be reduced to interchangeable substitutes for many.\n\nThe of-pattern distinguishes a whole general class from a specified set: many students but many of the students. Compare all students, all the students and all of the students; all allows some patterns that each or several do not. Both my colleagues and both of my colleagues are possible. Distributive quantity and grammatical agreement interact, but agreement receives full treatment on Day 10. Either can also mean both in expressions such as on either side of the road, so interpretation must follow the construction. A determiner is not defined as a word that must always be directly adjacent to its noun; adjectives and other modifiers may intervene.\n\nquantifier + plural count/noncount noun: Choose many applicants or much evidence according to the noun’s sense.\n\nquantifier + of + specified noun phrase: Many of the applicants selects from an identified set.\n\nEach of the three files needs a label.\nEach distributes the requirement to individual files and takes an of-phrase.\n\nBoth our buses have enough space.\nBoth identifies the two buses; enough gives a sufficiency judgement about noncount space.\n\nNot all of the recommendations were adopted.\nNegation excludes complete adoption without saying that none were adopted.\n\nEither side of a road can mean each of its two sides. This established construction differs from choosing either of two proposed routes.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=3&section=gv5-d03-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Determiners ( the, my, some, this ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/determiners-the-my-some-this"},{"publisher":"Much, many, a lot of, lots of : quantifiers - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/much-many-a-lot-of-lots-of-quantifiers"},{"publisher":"A/an and the - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/a-an-and-the"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d03-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Some, any, few and little: meaning under quantity and negation","text":"Some, any, few and little: meaning under quantity and negation\n\nQuantity expressions differ in the expectations they create as well as the nouns they permit. Some and any interact with questions, negation and unrestricted choice; few and little often imply insufficiency, whereas a few and a little present a positive quantity.\n\nUse a few with plural count nouns and a little with noncount nouns: a few pages, a little time. Removing a changes the usual implication: few pages suggests not many, often fewer than hoped; little time suggests not much. A few and a little assert that some quantity is available, often enough for a modest purpose. The real situation matters: a few mistakes can be reassuring in a long report but serious in a safety procedure. Grammatical form expresses a perspective; it does not calculate whether a quantity is objectively good or bad.\n\nSome often introduces an indefinite quantity in an affirmative statement: We bought some paper. Any is common in negatives and open questions: We did not buy any paper; Do you have any paper? These are tendencies, not a rule banning some from questions. Would you like some tea? is an ordinary offer, and Have you got some spare paper? can suggest an expected positive answer. Any also has a free-choice use: Choose any seat means there is no restriction on which seat you select. That use is compatible with a singular count noun.\n\nIn careful edited comparison, fewer commonly accompanies plural count nouns and less accompanies noncount nouns: fewer delays, less disruption. Established exceptions include measurements, sums and time spans regarded as amounts: less than ten minutes and less than twenty pounds. Less is also widespread with plural count nouns, especially in informal usage; a formal editor may prefer fewer ordinary individual items. Do not mark less than five miles wrong by applying a count-noun slogan to a measurement. Meaning and editorial convention are both relevant. More and most do not have separate count and noncount forms.\n\nScope helps explain quantity expressions that seem similar. I did not read some of the reports can identify a particular subset that went unread, while I did not read any of the reports normally says none were read. Some can be stressed to create a contrast, so intonation and context contribute to interpretation. Few and little are negative in orientation and can license expressions associated with negation: Few applicants have ever complained. A few applicants have complained instead presents an existing positive set. Avoid classifying either sentence by a memorised positive-versus-negative word list without interpreting what is asserted about the set.\n\nfew/a few + plural count noun; little/a little + noncount noun: The article changes the usual negative or positive orientation.\n\nnot + any + relevant set: Ordinarily expresses absence of even one member or amount in that set.\n\nWe have a little fuel, so we can reach the next village.\nA little presents a positive amount, linked here to sufficiency.\n\nFew applicants had read all the instructions.\nFew presents a small proportion and is negative in orientation.\n\nThe repair took less than thirty minutes but required fewer tools.\nMinutes is a measured span; tools are separate counted objects.\n\nSome is normal in offers and questions with a positive expectation: Would you like some water? Do not replace it automatically with any.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=3&section=gv5-d03-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Some and any - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/some-and-any"},{"publisher":"Little, a little, few, a few - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/little-a-little-few-a-few"},{"publisher":"Less or fewer ? - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/less-or-fewer"},{"publisher":"Much, many, a lot of, lots of : quantifiers - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/much-many-a-lot-of-lots-of-quantifiers"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d04-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Personal forms, case and reflexive meaning","text":"Personal forms, case and reflexive meaning\n\nPersonal pronouns mark speaker, addressee or other referents. Their forms can distinguish grammatical case, such as I/me and she/her. Reflexive forms such as myself and themselves can refer back to a local subject or emphasise a participant; those uses must be distinguished from ordinary object forms.\n\nCompare She thanked him with He thanked her. She and he are subject forms; him and her are object forms. The forms show grammatical relationships even when the people remain the same. I, we and they contrast with me, us and them; you and it do not show this case contrast in spelling. Possessive determiners accompany a noun, as my folder and their house, while independent possessives can stand without that noun, as mine and theirs. Hers, ours, yours and theirs have no additional apostrophe. Learn each form together with a short sentence rather than memorising a list without its function.\n\nCoordination does not automatically change the case expected in careful edited prose: Sara and I attended; the tutor spoke to Sara and me. Temporarily removing Sara and can help reveal the ordinary subject or object pattern, although coordination has additional informal usage conventions. Do not insert I simply because it sounds polite. After be, object forms are normal standard usage: It is me and That is her. It is I survives as a highly formal alternative; calling every object form after be wrong confuses a restrictive traditional preference with present-day grammar.\n\nWhen subject and object refer to the same participant in a simple clause, a reflexive often expresses that relation: Leena blamed herself. Leena blamed her normally directs blame towards a different female referent. Reflexives can also emphasise: Leena herself checked the figures; Leena checked the figures herself. The emphatic form adds contrast or responsibility and is not the subject on its own. By herself can mean alone or without help. Do not assume that every first-language reflexive construction needs an English reflexive: She washed and dressed is ordinary when no special emphasis on doing it unaided is intended.\n\nCase choices interact with register and construction. Than me is standard in comparisons, while than I am uses an expanded clause; compare their actual meanings rather than enforcing a bare subject form. Informal coordinated subjects with me occur in established speech, but Sara and I is the conservative edited choice. Some independent or polite reflexives occur, such as people like yourself; they are not all governed by the simple same-clause rule for direct-object reflexives. This course therefore teaches the local subject–object contrast as a reliable basic pattern while identifying emphatic, polite and discourse-sensitive uses separately.\n\nsubject + verb + reflexive object: Leena blamed herself presents subject and object as the same person.\n\ncoordinated object after a preposition: The invitation is for Bilal and me in careful edited prose.\n\nThe curator gave us her notes; the final copy is hers.\nUs is an object form, her accompanies a noun, and hers is an independent possessive.\n\nThe scientist herself recalculated the result.\nHerself is emphatic; the scientist remains the subject phrase.\n\nFarah is taller than me, but her brother is taller than I am.\nBoth comparison patterns are standard; the latter includes an explicit clause.\n\nA polite expression such as someone like yourself can be standard without a local reflexive antecedent. This does not make Myself attended the seminar an ordinary neutral replacement for I attended the seminar.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=4&section=gv5-d04-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Pronouns: personal ( I, me, you, him, it, they, etc.) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/pronouns-personal-i-me-you-him-it-they-etc"},{"publisher":"Pronouns: reflexive ( myself, themselves, etc.) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/pronouns-reflexive-myself-themselves-etc"},{"publisher":"Possession ( John’s car, a friend of mine ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/possession-john-s-car-a-friend-of-mine"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d04-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Pronoun families and relative choices","text":"Pronoun families and relative choices\n\nPronoun families are grouped by their grammatical patterns and meanings. Interrogative pronouns ask for an identity, relative expressions connect a clause with an antecedent, demonstratives point, indefinites leave identity open, and reciprocal expressions present a mutual relationship.\n\nWho, what and which can ask for an identity: Who arrived? What happened? Which do you prefer? This, that, these and those can point without repeating a noun: These are ready. When a noun follows, the same forms have determiner uses: these reports. Indefinite pronouns such as someone, anything and nobody refer without naming an individual or thing. Everybody refers to people distributively but normally takes a singular present verb: Everybody knows. Each, either and neither can stand independently when the relevant set is understood. Their determiner uses were introduced on Day 3.\n\nRelative clauses give information connected with an antecedent: the driver who helped us, the form that I submitted and the office which opened yesterday. Who is used for people; which is used for things and can introduce a relative comment on a larger situation. Whose expresses a relationship and can refer to people or things: a house whose roof needs repair. That is possible in many defining relatives, but it does not replace who or which freely in every supplementary construction. Day 15 develops defining, supplementary and reduced relatives in detail; today concentrates on reference and pronoun form.\n\nWho commonly functions as either subject or object in contemporary English: Who arrived? Who did you invite? Whom is an object form associated especially with formal prose and directly preceding prepositions: To whom was the letter addressed? Avoid choosing whom solely because a verb or preposition occurs nearby. In Who do you think will lead the group?, who is the subject of will lead, not the object of think. Identify the pronoun’s role inside its own clause. A formal object-relative construction can use whom, while standard object who should not be rejected without a specified register requirement.\n\nEach other and one another ordinarily express mutual relations among two or more participants. The inherited convention reserving each other for two and one another for more than two is not a dependable modern grammar rule; the two are normally interchangeable. They helped themselves can describe self-directed action, while they helped each other describes mutual help. Mutual reference does not always mean every member acted towards every other member; context determines the pattern. Each other is written as two words, and its genitive is each other’s. Different grammatical frameworks analyse some traditional pronoun labels differently, so this course makes the actual construction explicit.\n\nantecedent + relative clause: The learner who asked the question links who to learner.\n\nplural participants + reciprocal expression: The colleagues supported each other presents mutual support.\n\nThose are the files that we requested.\nThose has an independent demonstrative use; that connects a relative clause to files.\n\nThe house whose windows were broken is being repaired.\nWhose relates windows to the inanimate antecedent house.\n\nWho do you believe can solve the problem?\nWho is subject of can solve despite the intervening do you believe.\n\nObject who is established standard English, especially outside highly formal preposition-first patterns. A formal exercise must state that it requires whom if both forms otherwise fit.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=4&section=gv5-d04-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Who, whom - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/who-whom"},{"publisher":"Each other, one another - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/each-other-one-another"},{"publisher":"Determiners ( the, my, some, this ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/determiners-the-my-some-this"},{"publisher":"Pronouns: indefinite (- body, - one, - thing, - where ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/pronouns-indefinite-body-one-thing-where"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d04-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Reference chains, agreement and inclusive pronouns","text":"Reference chains, agreement and inclusive pronouns\n\nReference is the connection between an expression and what it identifies in context. Anaphora looks back to an earlier expression, cataphora anticipates a later one, and deixis depends on the speaker’s situation. Clear pronoun choices maintain those connections across sentences.\n\nRead Mina found a wallet. She handed it to the receptionist. She refers back to Mina and it to the wallet; both are anaphoric links. A pronoun does not always replace an earlier noun: I and you identify participants in the current exchange, while this can point to an object in view. When she entered the hall, Mina noticed the broken window can use she cataphorically, with Mina supplying the later identification. The order of mention alone therefore does not decide whether a pronoun is grammatical. The reader must have enough information to establish the intended relationship.\n\nKeep reference clear when several potential antecedents appear. Aisha told Hina that she had won the prize leaves the winner uncertain without more context. Repeating a name can improve clarity even though the original sentence is grammatically possible. A pronoun can refer to a larger event or proposition: The bridge was closed. This delayed deliveries. Here this refers to the closure situation, not simply to bridge. For formal exposition, a fuller phrase such as this closure often makes the link clearer. Do not declare every such broad reference wrong, but ask whether readers can recover it reliably.\n\nSingular they is an established way to refer to an indefinite individual: Someone has left their umbrella; if anyone calls, ask them to wait. It also serves people who use they as their personal pronoun. A singular referent does not change its usual verb forms: they are, they have. You should not automatically replace singular they with he or insist that unknown people have a binary gender. Respect a person’s stated pronouns and retain clear reference. Where a sentence becomes cumbersome, pluralising the noun phrase or repeating a name may help, provided the meaning remains equivalent.\n\nAgreement is not a mechanical matching of neighbouring letters. Someone normally takes singular has, while subsequent they has its established are/have forms even when the referent is one person. British collective reference can follow a member-focused plural: The committee have finished their separate reports. A writer must make the intended construal coherent, rather than impose one universal rule about all group nouns. Themselves is widely used with singular they. Themself is also documented, but authorities differ in its status: Cambridge labels it not standard, while Merriam-Webster records it and discusses its increasing use. Institutional editorial preferences can therefore differ. This introductory lesson uses themselves as the broad default without calling every documented alternative nonexistent. Day 10 treats collective and notional agreement in detail.\n\nindefinite singular antecedent + singular they: Someone has left their folder is a standard gender-neutral reference chain.\n\nambiguous pronoun → explicit reference: Repeat a name or descriptive noun when more than one antecedent is plausible.\n\nEvery applicant should bring their identification.\nTheir can refer inclusively to each individual applicant.\n\nBefore he spoke, Omar checked the microphone.\nHe can anticipate the later name Omar; this is cataphoric reference.\n\nThe reservoir was contaminated. This finding prompted a new inspection.\nThis finding makes the connection to the preceding proposition explicit.\n\nPronouns can identify a situation rather than one noun: She resigned. That surprised us. This is grammatical when the event reference is clear; a fuller noun phrase can improve precision.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=4&section=gv5-d04-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Pronouns: personal ( I, me, you, him, it, they, etc.) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/pronouns-personal-i-me-you-him-it-they-etc"},{"publisher":"Sexist language - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/sexist-language"},{"publisher":"THEY | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/they"},{"publisher":"DEIXIS | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/deixis"},{"publisher":"Subject–verb agreement - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subject-verb-agreement"},{"publisher":"THEMSELF Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster","url":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/themself"},{"publisher":"THEMSELF | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/themself"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d04-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Dummy subjects, generic reference and local interpretation","text":"Dummy subjects, generic reference and local interpretation\n\nA dummy subject fills a grammatical subject position without identifying a normal participant, as it in It is raining. Generic pronouns refer to people generally. Practical binding concerns how pronouns and reflexives are linked to other expressions within grammatical structure.\n\nIn English, a neutral finite statement normally has a subject even when no ordinary actor is involved: It is raining; It is nearly noon. It is not a person causing rain or a previously mentioned object. Existential there introduces something’s existence or presence: There is a spare chair in the corridor. This there differs from locative there in Put the chair there, where it indicates a place. Grammatical descriptions differ in how they analyse the following noun phrase, but treating there as a dummy subject is useful for understanding why the sentence does not need a named actor before is.\n\nDummy it also permits delayed content: It is useful to check the references; It is clear that the figures differ. The infinitival or that-clause supplies the important content, while it fills the initial subject slot. This arrangement is called extraposition in the relevant constructions and receives advanced treatment on Day 23. Do not assume every it is empty: I found the report. It is useful has an ordinary referring it. The form alone does not settle the interpretation; examine what it is doing in that particular sentence.\n\nGeneric you, we, one and they allow different perspectives. You can learn a great deal by listening addresses people generally in many instructional contexts. One must check one’s sources is more formal and can sound detached. We can include writer and readers in a shared generalisation; unspecified they can mean a relevant institution or authority, though naming the authority may be clearer in formal reporting. Once a perspective is selected, avoid unexplained switches such as One should check your sources. Generic reference is not an excuse to invent claims about what every reader personally experiences.\n\nFor ordinary reflexive objects, identify the relevant local subject: Nida said that Sana blamed herself normally links herself with Sana. Replacing herself with himself does not ordinarily allow it to refer across that finite clause to an earlier male name. These practical restrictions are often discussed under binding in syntax. They are not a complete theory of every reflexive use: emphatic forms, exempt or perspective-sensitive reflexives and picture-noun constructions can be more complex. A pronoun can also be grammatically possible yet misleading if its antecedent is unclear. Separate the structural restriction from the editorial task of making a reference chain easy to follow.\n\nit + weather/time expression: It is raining fills the subject position without a referring participant.\n\nthere + be + noun phrase: There is a spare chair introduces the existence or presence of the chair.\n\nIt is snowing outside.\nIt is a dummy weather subject.\n\nThere are three unclaimed parcels at reception.\nExistential there introduces parcels; careful edited agreement is plural are.\n\nAmir said that Leena blamed herself for the delay.\nThe local subject Leena supplies the ordinary antecedent of herself.\n\nReflexives used for politeness or emphasis do not all follow the ordinary local-object pattern. Do not extend a beginner’s same-clause test into a claim that all other reflexive constructions are impossible.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=4&section=gv5-d04-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Dummy subjects - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/dummy-subjects"},{"publisher":"Pronouns: personal ( I, me, you, him, it, they, etc.) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/pronouns-personal-i-me-you-him-it-they-etc"},{"publisher":"Pronouns: reflexive ( myself, themselves, etc.) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/pronouns-reflexive-myself-themselves-etc"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d05-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Lexical verbs, auxiliaries and the principal forms","text":"Lexical verbs, auxiliaries and the principal forms\n\nVerbs are a word class whose members organise clauses and select characteristic patterns. Lexical verbs contribute central meanings such as wait, repair and know. Auxiliaries help express grammatical meanings and constructions. Finite and non-finite describe a verb form’s grammatical status within a clause, not whether it denotes an action.\n\nLearn a lexical verb through its principal forms. Write has the plain form write, third-person singular present writes, past wrote, past participle written and -ing form writing. A regular verb normally uses the same -ed form for its past and past participle: repair, repaired, repaired. An irregular verb has an established alternative pattern, as write/wrote/written or cut/cut/cut. Identical spelling can therefore serve different grammatical roles. The clause She cut the paper contains a past form, while has cut contains a past participle selected by has. A learner’s dictionary supplies the relevant forms and pronunciation.\n\nBe, have and do are primary verbs that can function as auxiliaries or lexical verbs. Compare She has a bicycle with She has repaired a bicycle; only the second has is a perfect auxiliary. Be forms include be, am, is, are, was, were, been and being. Have has the forms have, has, had and having; do has do, does, did, done and doing. Had serves as both past and past-participle form. Core modal auxiliaries include can, could, may, might, shall, should, will, would and must. They normally select a plain verb form: can swim, not can swims. Modality receives fuller semantic treatment on Day 17.\n\nIn a finite verb phrase, the first verb is finite and the following forms are selected by preceding auxiliaries: has written, is writing, may have written. Non-finite forms include to-infinitives, plain infinitives, -ing forms and past participles. The form after to in to write does not carry its own person agreement. A past participle is not a past-tense verb merely because its name contains past; The work will be finished refers forward in time. Finite does not mean that a form always visibly changes for every subject: modal can has the same spelling with I and she.\n\nInflectional form, clause status and time reference are different dimensions. A plain form occurs in several environments, including after a modal and in an imperative such as Close the door. Modern descriptions differ in how they classify certain untensed or subjunctive constructions, so a visible -s or -ed ending cannot serve as the sole test for finiteness. Regular spelling also has conditions: stopped doubles a consonant, while opened does not; consult stress and spelling conventions rather than double every final consonant. British learnt/learned and American learned illustrate legitimate form variation, not an automatic difference in grammatical correctness.\n\nlexical verb: plain / -s / past / past participle / -ing: write / writes / wrote / written / writing are the five principal form categories.\n\nmodal auxiliary + plain verb: Can write uses a plain form after the modal.\n\nThe researcher writes carefully.\nWrites is the third-person singular present form of the lexical verb.\n\nThe researcher has written three reports.\nHas is finite; written is the past participle selected by perfect have.\n\nThe researcher may have been working late.\nMay is finite; have is plain after the modal, been follows perfect have, and working follows progressive be.\n\nA form can be irregular without changing its spelling across categories: cut serves as plain, past and past participle in different structures. Context and auxiliary selection distinguish the functions.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=5&section=gv5-d05-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"},{"publisher":"Do - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/do"},{"publisher":"Contractions - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/contractions"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d05-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Verb-phrase order, do-support, questions and negation","text":"Verb-phrase order, do-support, questions and negation\n\nA verb phrase can contain a lexical verb with one or more auxiliaries. English auxiliaries have an ordered relationship and select the form of the next verb. Do-support supplies auxiliary do where a neutral question or negative needs an auxiliary and no suitable one is already present.\n\nBegin with an existing auxiliary. She is working becomes Is she working? and She is not working. The finite auxiliary moves before the subject in this yes–no question, and not follows that auxiliary in the negative. With a modal, the same basic pattern gives Can she swim? and She cannot swim. The lexical verb does not move to the front by itself in an ordinary present-day neutral question. Be can also invert directly when it links a subject with a description: Is she ready? Do not automatically add do simply because there is only one written verb.\n\nFor an ordinary lexical verb without another auxiliary, use do, does or did: Do they work here? Does she work here? Did she work here? The lexical verb returns to its plain form because the auxiliary carries the relevant finite contrast. Negatives follow the same arrangement: They do not work here; she did not work here. Do can occur both as auxiliary and lexical verb: Do they do laboratory work? A neutral affirmative subject question ordinarily needs no added do: Who wrote the report? Object questions do use it when needed: Who did they invite? Emphatic contexts can produce different patterns.\n\nThe common auxiliary order is modal, perfect have, progressive be, passive be and lexical verb. Each item selects what immediately follows: have selects a past participle; progressive be selects an -ing form; passive be selects a past participle. Thus may have been waiting is interpretable through nested selections rather than a memorised label alone. Longer combinations can be grammatical but cumbersome, so choose a simpler wording where it preserves meaning. Questions invert the first finite auxiliary only: Has she been waiting? Negation normally follows that same first auxiliary.\n\nContractions join forms in writing and speech: is not/isn’t, do not/don’t, I am/I’m and they have/they’ve. Their suitability depends on register, audience and emphasis; formal writing does not have one universal grammatical ban on them. The spelling ’s can mean is or has, and ’d can mean had or would; the surrounding pattern resolves ambiguity where possible. Auxiliary and lexical have show regional and constructional variation, including Have you got a pen? and Do you have a pen? Neutral be normally avoids do-support in finite questions, but imperatives such as Do be careful and Don’t be late show that do and be are not universally incompatible.\n\nfinite auxiliary + subject + remaining verb phrase: Has she been waiting? inverts only the first finite auxiliary.\n\ndo/does/did + subject + plain lexical verb: Did the learners finish? uses did, leaving finish plain.\n\nDoes your brother repair bicycles?\nDoes carries the present third-person contrast; repair remains plain.\n\nThe visitors have not been waiting long.\nNot follows finite have; been and waiting retain the selected forms.\n\nDo be patient while the file loads.\nEmphatic imperative do can occur with be; this is a special construction, not an ordinary neutral yes–no question.\n\nDo is not added mechanically to every question. A neutral subject question such as Who arrived? and a be-question such as Are they ready? already have their appropriate structures.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=5&section=gv5-d05-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Do - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/do"},{"publisher":"Contractions - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/contractions"},{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"},{"publisher":"Inversion - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/inversion"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d05-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Valency, objects, predicative complements and sentence patterns","text":"Valency, objects, predicative complements and sentence patterns\n\nVerb valency concerns the participants and complement patterns associated with a verb use. Transitive uses have an object; intransitive uses do not. Linking and complex-transitive patterns contain predicative complements that describe a subject or object rather than introduce another independent participant.\n\nCompare The child laughed with The child opened the box. Laughed is intransitive here; opened is transitive and has the box as direct object. This is a grammatical pattern distinction, not simply a distinction between physical actions and states. She owns a bicycle contains a direct object even though owning is not a momentary action. Conversely, The glass broke has a subject undergoing a change without a direct object. Identify grammatical function from the structure and the particular verb use. Some verbs allow more than one pattern: She is reading and She is reading a novel are both possible.\n\nA ditransitive pattern contains two objects: The tutor gave the learner a booklet. The learner is the indirect object and a booklet the direct object. In The tutor gave a booklet to the learner, this course calls to the learner a prepositional complement rather than a second noun-phrase object. Some traditional accounts extend indirect object more broadly, so compare the stated analysis before treating a terminology difference as a grammatical fault. A linking pattern relates a subject to a description or identity: The learner seemed tired; Leena became a doctor. Tired and a doctor are predicative complements.\n\nComplex-transitive patterns add a complement describing the object: The panel considered the plan practical; They appointed Leena coordinator. The second complement is not an indirect object receiving a separate thing. Other verbs require a location complement: Put the folder on the shelf. On the shelf completes the placement pattern; merely saying Put the folder normally leaves the instruction incomplete. Complements can therefore be noun phrases, adjective phrases, prepositional phrases or clauses, depending on the verb. Optional adjuncts add circumstances: She read the report yesterday. Distinguishing optionality from the verb’s meaning requirements needs more than a single deletion test.\n\nThe verb’s lexical meaning and construction jointly determine which pattern is available. Explain normally takes a content object with a to-phrase for the listener: explain the rule to me, not explain me the rule in the broad edited standard used here. Tell allows tell me the rule, illustrating a genuine lexical difference rather than a universal conversion rule between two-object and prepositional patterns. Valency counts may include the subject as an argument in linguistic descriptions; a classroom list of objects often counts something different. Day 13 develops hierarchical parsing and diagnostic tests. Avoid defining every object as a person physically receiving an action or every complement as an adjective after be.\n\nS + V; S + V + O; S + V + subject complement: The guests laughed; the guests opened the gate; the guests seemed tired.\n\nS + V + indirect object + direct object; S + V + object + object complement: She gave him a key; they painted the gate blue.\n\nThe lecturer sent the students a reading list.\nThe students and a reading list are the two noun-phrase objects in this use.\n\nThe committee found the explanation unconvincing.\nExplanation is object; unconvincing is a complement describing it, not an adverb modifying found.\n\nPlease put the parcel beside the desk.\nThe placement verb selects a locative complement; beside the desk states the required destination.\n\nThe same lexical verb can have transitive and intransitive uses. She is eating does not become ungrammatical merely because eat can also take an object in She is eating an apple.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=5&section=gv5-d05-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"VALENCY | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/valency"},{"publisher":"Objects - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/objects"},{"publisher":"Complements - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/complements"},{"publisher":"Subjects - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subjects"},{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d05-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Alternations, dynamic meanings and states","text":"Alternations, dynamic meanings and states\n\nDynamic meanings present events, activities or changes; stative meanings present situations such as knowledge or possession without an internal activity sequence. An ergative alternation relates a transitive use with an intransitive use in which the affected object becomes the subject, as The wind opened the door and The door opened. Its intransitive partner is active, rather than a passive construction.\n\nA dynamic use can involve a deliberate action, such as writing a note, or an uncontrolled event, such as a branch falling. A stative use describes a situation such as knowing the answer, owning a bicycle or resembling a parent. This distinction concerns how a meaning is presented, not whether the real world changes over a lifetime. A person can own a bicycle only temporarily, but ownership can still be described as a state. Begin by interpreting the particular sentence rather than assigning an unchanging dynamic or stative label to the dictionary spelling.\n\nSeveral verbs have different senses with different patterns. I think the plan is useful states an opinion; I am thinking about the plan presents mental activity. She has a bicycle expresses possession; she is having lunch describes an activity. The soup tastes salty describes its perceived quality; the cook is tasting the soup describes an action. Progressive forms are more typical of dynamic uses, but the relationship is not an absolute prohibition. A speaker can present temporary behaviour with be: He is being unusually quiet. Future lessons develop the semantic choices among simple, progressive and perfect constructions.\n\nIn an ergative alternation, the affected participant appears as object in one clause and subject in another: The heat melted the wax; the wax melted. The second clause is active intransitive, not passive: it has no passive be plus participle construction. The subject need not be a conscious doer, so defining every subject as the actor produces a false analysis. Not all transitive verbs permit this alternation: The courier delivered the parcel does not yield The parcel delivered with the ordinary completed-delivery meaning. Whether an alternation is available is a lexical and constructional fact to learn.\n\nProgressive stative uses can be marked by register, temporary construal or evolving convention. I’m loving the new course is common informal evaluative English; that does not license an ordinary factual I’m knowing the answer in every context. Conversely, a temporary context can make a progressive natural with a verb otherwise often labelled stative, as The repairs are costing more than expected. Ergative is also used in wider linguistic typology for case and alignment systems. The English teaching label here refers only to the illustrated causative/inchoative alternation; it does not claim that English has a general ergative case system. Day 19 revisits alternation alongside voice.\n\ncausing subject + transitive verb + affected object ↔ affected subject + intransitive verb: The wind opened the gate ↔ The gate opened.\n\nsame spelling + different lexical sense → different aspect possibilities: Think as opinion contrasts with think as ongoing consideration.\n\nThe falling branch broke the window. The window broke.\nThe affected window is object in the transitive use and subject in the intransitive use.\n\nShe has two bicycles, but she is having lunch now.\nPossession and eating lunch are different senses of have.\n\nThe repairs are costing the club more than expected.\nThe progressive presents an ongoing developing financial consequence; a blanket stative ban would be misleading.\n\nA lexical spelling does not fix one aspect class across all senses. The cook is tasting the sauce is an ordinary dynamic use even though The sauce tastes sweet describes a state.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=5&section=gv5-d05-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"ERGATIVE | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/ergative"},{"publisher":"Stative verbs | LearnEnglish","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/b1-b2/stative-verbs"},{"publisher":"VALENCY | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/valency"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d06-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Present simple: tense, time and a speaker’s general view","text":"Present simple: tense, time and a speaker’s general view\n\nThe present simple is a finite present-tense construction without progressive or perfect marking. It commonly presents states, habits and generalisations; present tense does not restrict its reference to this instant.\n\nBegin with a distinction between a form and the time you mean. In Sara checks the filters every Friday, checks is present tense, but the sentence describes a routine across many Fridays. In Sara knows the building, knows describes a state that holds now without claiming it is permanent. Use the base form with I, you, we and they: they check. With a third-person singular subject, add the appropriate ending: she checks, he watches, the pump carries water. Be has its own forms am, is and are. Have becomes has; do becomes does. Read the complete subject before choosing the form.\n\nFor ordinary lexical verbs, make a negative with do not or does not followed by the base form: The sensor does not work. Make a question by placing do or does before the subject: Does the sensor work? The ending belongs to does, so does not works duplicates the marking. With be, use The sensor is not ready and Is the sensor ready?, without added do. Frequency expressions such as often and every week help communicate a habit, but they are clues to meaning, not switches that automatically force one construction. A routine can be temporary and still be presented as a routine.\n\nPresent simple also supports instructions, demonstrations, commentaries and conventional summaries of texts: First you loosen the screw; the article argues for repair. These uses need not describe timeless truths. Context can even give a present form future reference, as in The inspection begins tomorrow, or past narrative reference, as in So I open the door and there is the missing parcel. Learn these as established uses of a grammatical form rather than extra inflected tenses. In formal prose, maintain a clear viewpoint, while allowing a justified change when moving from a past event to a continuing consequence.\n\nAspect concerns how a situation is viewed internally; tense locates a grammatical viewpoint relative to a reference time. Many grammatical accounts recognise present and past as English’s two inflectional tenses, while classroom accounts often organise twelve tense-aspect patterns. These descriptions count different things and need not conflict if the terminology is explicit. Simple does not mean easy or short. A simple-present verb can occur in a highly embedded sentence. Present-tense form does not certify factual truth: The moon consists of cheese is grammatically formed but factually false. Grammatical analysis and evaluating a claim are separate tasks.\n\nsubject + base/-s form: A present simple affirmative with ordinary lexical verbs.\n\ndoes/do + subject + base?; subject + does/do not + base: Question and negative forms; only the auxiliary carries present agreement.\n\nThe clinic opens at eight on weekdays.\nOpens presents a regular schedule; clinic is third-person singular.\n\nDoes this code identify the customer or the order?\nDoes precedes this code; identify remains the base form.\n\nThe report describes the leak that damaged the store last winter.\nDescribes gives a current textual summary; damaged locates a different, past event. Both tenses are justified.\n\nPresent forms can refer beyond present time: The workshop starts on Monday. Do not infer the event time from the verb ending alone.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=6&section=gv5-d06-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Present simple ( I work ) - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-simple-i-work"},{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d06-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Present progressive: activity, temporary patterns and changing states","text":"Present progressive: activity, temporary patterns and changing states\n\nThe present progressive, also called present continuous, consists of present be plus an -ing form and normally presents a situation as ongoing, temporary, developing or arranged.\n\nBuild this construction with am, is or are and the -ing form: I am checking; the technician is checking; the technicians are checking. Keep both parts. The technician checking the meter is not a complete ordinary declarative clause on its own. Add not after be for a negative, and move be before the subject for a question: Is the technician checking the meter? The main activity does not need to be happening at precisely the second of speech. I am reading a long biography can remain true while you stop to eat. The relevant interval is understood from context.\n\nCompare a general description with a current arrangement: We use the upstairs room on Fridays describes a routine; We are using the upstairs room this month presents a temporary pattern. Neither form guarantees permanence or a fixed duration. Progressive constructions also describe change: The river is rising. With always, they can convey a speaker’s reaction to repeated events: She is always finding useful shortcuts can express admiration, while He is always losing receipts can express irritation. The emotional evaluation comes from the context and wording, not from a compulsory negative meaning in always.\n\nVerb meaning matters. Know and belong normally resist a progressive interpretation in their ordinary state senses: I know the password; the keys belong to Maya. Other verbs change interpretation: I think the estimate is low expresses an opinion, while I am thinking about the estimate presents mental activity. We have a small office describes possession; We are having a meeting describes an event. Avoid learning an absolute blacklist of spellings. Ask which sense is being used and whether the construction invites an activity, temporary behaviour or developing experience.\n\nA progressive viewpoint does not itself assert that an event reaches its endpoint. The team is building a bridge does not entail that a bridge will be completed. The same construction can combine with repeated events, as in The phone is ringing every few minutes: a sequence of bounded rings forms an ongoing temporary pattern. Some innovative or expressive progressive statives are established in informal use, while other combinations remain unusual. Describe them by sense, register and communicative purpose rather than assuming that every unfamiliar -ing form is either universally wrong or universally interchangeable with the simple.\n\nam/is/are + -ing: Progressive affirmative.\n\nam/is/are + not + -ing; Am/Is/Are + subject + -ing?: Negation and subject–auxiliary inversion.\n\nI am sorting the photographs at the moment.\nAm + sorting presents current activity.\n\nThe library is staying open later during revision week.\nA repeated temporary arrangement can take progressive marking.\n\nThe director is being unusually patient while we test the equipment.\nBeing presents temporary behaviour, not a permanent identification.\n\nAn -ing ending is not enough to identify a progressive: Swimming helps me relax contains an -ing clause as subject, without progressive be.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=6&section=gv5-d06-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Present continuous ( I am working ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-continuous-i-am-working"},{"publisher":"Stative verbs | LearnEnglish","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/b1-b2/stative-verbs"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d06-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Present perfect: experience, results and time extending to now","text":"Present perfect: experience, results and time extending to now\n\nThe present perfect combines present have with a past participle, connecting a prior situation with a present reference point. Its uses include experience, present results and situations continuing to now.\n\nUse have or has followed by the past participle: I have finished; she has written; the files have arrived. Regular participles often end in -ed, but irregular ones must be learnt: written, seen, gone, taken. Make negatives with have not or has not and questions by moving have or has before the subject: Has the courier arrived? Do not add does. The construction looks back from now. We have received the parcel can announce a result relevant to a present conversation, while We received it at ten yesterday locates an event at a finished past time.\n\nFor experience, present perfect places an event somewhere in a period leading up to now: I have visited that museum twice. It need not identify the exact dates. For continuing states, it can link a past beginning with now: I have known Amal for six years. For a current tally, I have checked twelve forms today counts completed checks within a relevant period treated as open. Today can also accompany simple past when the speaker treats the relevant opportunity or activity as finished. The temporal interpretation must be read from the situation, rather than assigning one automatic construction to a time word.\n\nA definite finished-past adverbial locating the same event normally conflicts with present perfect in standard usage: I have sent it yesterday should be I sent it yesterday. However, the mere presence of a date does not create an error: I have lived here since 2019 is standard, because 2019 marks the beginning of a continuing interval. A past date inside another clause can also coexist with present perfect. British English often uses present perfect with just, already and yet where American English also readily uses simple past. These are established patterns of variation, not evidence of inferior grammar.\n\nPresent relevance is a useful teaching description but cannot mean that every past event whose consequences matter must take present perfect. A writer can say The factory closed in 2020, so employment is still low. Likewise, perfect marking does not always assert a completed activity: I have lived here for years can describe continued residence. Separate the event, its reference interval and the speaker’s current purpose. In a formal account, explicitly dated historical events normally take past forms, while a present assessment may use perfect to summarise the evidence accumulated so far. Changes in form should track these distinctions.\n\nhave/has + past participle: Present perfect; have is finite and the participle is non-finite.\n\nHave/Has + subject + past participle?: A perfect question requires inversion of have, without do-support.\n\nThe committee has published its decision.\nA present announcement looks back to completed publication.\n\nHave you ever repaired a radio?\nEver asks about experience up to now, without a specified occasion.\n\nI have kept every letter she sent during her first year abroad.\nHave kept has a present viewpoint; sent locates the earlier letter-writing in its own past interval.\n\nSimple past with just/already/yet is common standard American usage. Specify the editorial convention when a task contrasts regional defaults.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=6&section=gv5-d06-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Present perfect simple ( I have worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-i-have-worked"},{"publisher":"Past simple or present perfect? - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-or-past-simple"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d06-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Present perfect progressive and cumulative application","text":"Present perfect progressive and cumulative application\n\nThe present perfect progressive consists of have/has been plus an -ing form. It presents activity over a period connected to now, including recent activity whose traces are still evident.\n\nBuild the form in order: have or has, then been, then the activity in its -ing form. We have been repairing the fence. For a negative, place not after have: We have not been repairing it. For a question, invert the first auxiliary: Have you been repairing it? Been is the participle of be selected by perfect have, while repairing is selected by progressive be. This order follows the grammar of the verb phrase you studied on Day 5. A long verb phrase still needs only its first auxiliary to carry present agreement.\n\nUse the construction to foreground duration or process: I have been preparing the accounts all morning. The work may continue, or it may have just stopped. If a colleague arrives to find ledgers everywhere, the same sentence can explain the present evidence without claiming that you are still working. Compare I have prepared three statements, which foregrounds a completed tally, with I have been preparing statements, which foregrounds the activity. The simple and progressive perfect can overlap: She has worked here for years and She has been working here for years can both describe current employment.\n\nCount the events and check the verb sense before choosing. A single arrival is normally expressed as The parcel has arrived, rather than has been arriving; a repeated pattern can make The parcels have been arriving all morning natural. A continuing state such as knowing a person normally uses perfect simple: I have known her since school. For duration, for introduces a length of time and since a starting point. Neither word automatically requires progressive aspect. I have been ill for a week is perfect simple with a state, not a progressive form.\n\nApply the whole first phase while reading a present-time paragraph. Identify noun-phrase heads before selecting has or have; distinguish a determiner from a pronoun; track what it or they refers to; then analyse the auxiliary chain and intended time. This is cumulative grammatical reasoning, not a separate tense trick. In Today the volunteers are sorting books; they have already labelled two shelves, volunteers controls are, they refers back clearly, sorting has progressive marking and have labelled supplies a completed result. A learner’s ability to recognise these forms should be checked separately from the ability to produce and explain an original paragraph.\n\nhave/has + been + -ing: Perfect progressive links a process to the present.\n\nHow long + have/has + subject + been + -ing?: Question foregrounding duration when the context establishes an ongoing/recent activity.\n\nShe has been practising the introduction for an hour.\nThe focus is duration of practice connected with now.\n\nYour sleeves are dusty. Have you been cleaning the shelves?\nRecent activity is inferred from present traces; it need not still be occurring.\n\nThe volunteers have been checking labels, and each box now has a clear destination.\nHave agrees with volunteers; has agrees with each box. Activity leads to a present result.\n\nA repeated series of short events can be progressive: Customers have been arriving since nine. The construction does not require one unbroken action.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=6&section=gv5-d06-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Present perfect simple or present perfect continuous? - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-or-present-perfect-continuous"},{"publisher":"For or since ? - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/for-or-since"},{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d07-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Past simple: completed events, states and narrative steps","text":"Past simple: completed events, states and narrative steps\n\nThe past simple uses a finite past form and commonly locates events or states within a past frame. It can describe single events, sequences, repeated activities and past states.\n\nTo report a completed event, use the past form: The courier knocked. Regular forms normally add -ed; irregular forms include took, wrote, brought and left. A past form need not mention its date in the same sentence if the conversation already establishes when: Who moved the box? Mina moved it. Past simple can also describe a state: The office belonged to a local charity. Do not equate past tense with physical action. In a narrative, a sequence such as She unlocked the door, entered and switched on the light normally advances the story from one event to the next.\n\nOrdinary lexical verbs use did not + base in negatives and Did + subject + base in questions: They did not return; Did they return? Did carries the tense, so did returned duplicates past marking. Be instead uses was or were and inverts directly: Was the office open? It was not open. A subject question normally lacks added did: Who opened the gate? Compare Who did you meet?, where who is the object and you is the subject. Negation, emphasis or another structure can change the auxiliary pattern, so first identify what the question is asking.\n\nPast simple can cover a long period or a repeated routine: She worked there for twenty years; We walked to school every day. The length of an event does not by itself require progressive aspect. A finished past interval normally permits simple past even if its effects continue now: The bridge closed last month, and traffic is still diverted. A present consequence does not force present perfect. Conversely, a past form may express hypothetical distance rather than past time in a later lesson’s conditional construction. Meaning comes from the whole clause and context.\n\nIn historical and biographical writing, establish dates and reference frames clearly. Past forms report events; present forms can evaluate a source that is available now: The expedition left in 1912; the diary describes its first week. Such a shift is justified, not a lapse in consistency. The historic present is another established narrative choice, but a beginner should maintain one narrative viewpoint until a reason for changing it is clear. Tense consistency means coherent relationships between viewpoints, rather than banning every different tense from one paragraph or assuming that each past verb moves the narrative clock forward.\n\nsubject + past form: Past simple affirmative.\n\ndid not + base; Did + subject + base?: Ordinary lexical negatives and non-subject questions.\n\nThe assistant locked the cabinet at six.\nLocked locates a completed event in a specified past interval.\n\nWho brought the spare battery?\nWho is the subject, so this affirmative subject question uses brought without did.\n\nThe laboratory operated for thirty years before it closed.\nA long completed period can use simple past; duration does not compel progressive.\n\nPast be has was/were rather than did be: Were the windows open? It also appears in irrealis constructions, which Day 18 develops.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=7&section=gv5-d07-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Past simple ( I worked ) - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/past-simple-i-worked"},{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d07-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Past progressive: background, overlap and an unfinished viewpoint","text":"Past progressive: background, overlap and an unfinished viewpoint\n\nThe past progressive combines was/were with an -ing form, presenting a situation as in progress around a past reference time. It does not by itself establish completion or interruption.\n\nUse was or were + -ing: I was listening; they were listening. Add not after was/were for a negative and invert that auxiliary for a question: Were they listening? This construction lets a narrator place a reader inside an activity. At ten, the workers were loading the van says loading was in progress around ten. It does not itself tell us when the task started or finished. A past time expression is therefore compatible with progressive aspect. The slogan yesterday means simple past is too broad: Yesterday at ten, we were loading the van is grammatical.\n\nWhen one event occurs during another, progressive often supplies background while simple past introduces the event: I was checking the address when the taxi arrived. Calling this an interruption is convenient, but the arrival need not stop the checking. Two progressives can present overlap: While I was checking, Salma was packing. Two simple pasts can also describe concurrent completed activities: While I checked the address, Salma packed. These are different viewpoints, not competing versions where one must be an error. A question must specify the intended scene before it can demand a particular aspect.\n\nProgressive also supports temporary repeated past activity: During the renovation, we were meeting in the kitchen every morning. Compare We met in the kitchen every morning for an ordinary retrospective habit. The progressive can suggest an unfolding pattern with boundaries in the context. With always, a speaker may express an attitude to repetition: He was always volunteering for difficult jobs. Context may make this admiring rather than critical. State verbs retain sense-dependent restrictions. I knew the answer is the ordinary form for knowledge; I was thinking about the answer presents an active process.\n\nThe so-called imperfective viewpoint shows an internal phase without asserting the whole event’s completion. She was crossing the road when I saw her does not guarantee that she reached the far side, though ordinary knowledge may suggest it. Do not confuse what the grammar entails with what a reader reasonably infers. Similarly, an achievement can receive a progressive reading when an event has an approach or repeated phases: The guests were arriving as the musicians tuned up. This does not make arrive an unrestricted substitute for every stative verb. Interpret the lexical meaning and the viewpoint together.\n\nwas/were + -ing: Past progressive affirmative.\n\nWhile + past progressive, past simple/progressive: A common way to frame an overlapping situation, not a compulsory tense recipe.\n\nAt sunrise, the team was unloading the truck.\nThe narrator views unloading as in progress at sunrise.\n\nThe radio was playing when the power failed.\nWas playing is background; failed marks a new event. The relation is supplied by context.\n\nGuests were arriving throughout the afternoon, while volunteers were directing them to the hall.\nTwo overlapping repeated patterns are presented as ongoing.\n\nA short event can occur in a progressive when viewed in development or repetition: A light was flashing. Duration is not a mechanical verb-type label.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=7&section=gv5-d07-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Past continuous ( I was working ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/past-continuous-i-was-working"},{"publisher":"Stative verbs | LearnEnglish","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/b1-b2/stative-verbs"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d07-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Past perfect and past perfect progressive: looking back from then","text":"Past perfect and past perfect progressive: looking back from then\n\nThe past perfect uses had plus a past participle to relate a prior situation to a past viewpoint. The past perfect progressive adds been + -ing to foreground a process or duration leading towards that viewpoint.\n\nImagine arriving at a workshop yesterday and finding it empty. The instructor had left tells the reader that the departure preceded your arrival. Had left is past perfect: had + the participle left. It looks back from a past viewpoint rather than directly from now. Make negatives with had not and questions with Had + subject: Had the instructor left? No separate did is needed. The reference point can appear in the same sentence, elsewhere in the story or in the shared conversation. It need not be a second event explicitly named after every perfect verb.\n\nPast perfect does more than put the earliest of two events in a special form. It can establish background information, report an earlier statement, or show that a plan changed: I had intended to stay, but the last train was leaving. Where before or after already establishes sequence, simple past may be sufficient: After she locked the room, she handed over the keys. Past perfect can still foreground completion: After she had locked the room, she handed over the keys. Do not mark every simple past before another past as wrong, or assume that past perfect cannot appear with dates.\n\nFor activity leading towards a past viewpoint, use had been + -ing: We had been waiting for an hour when the caretaker arrived. The waiting may then stop or continue. It can also explain traces at a past moment: The pavement was wet because it had been raining. Rain might already have stopped. Compare had written three letters, a completed tally, with had been writing letters, an activity. With states such as knowing someone, ordinary past perfect simple is used: We had known each other for years. Since and for express starting point and duration; they do not compel progressive aspect.\n\nAnalyse reference relationships before rewriting. In When the assistant arrived, the gate had closed, closing is earlier than arrival. In When the assistant arrived, the gate closed, the natural reading links closing with the arrival, though wider context can modify that interpretation. A transformation from one to the other may change the facts. Perfect marking also occurs in counterfactual constructions discussed later: If we had left earlier is an imagined past, not a historical claim that we actually left earlier. Keep time sequence, modality and the truth of a proposition distinct when interpreting a perfect form.\n\nhad + past participle: Prior situation viewed from a past reference point.\n\nhad been + -ing: Activity or duration leading up to a past viewpoint.\n\nBy noon, the team had checked all the meters.\nThe checks are completed by the past reference point noon.\n\nHad you been working there long when the building closed?\nHad been working asks about duration preceding the closure.\n\nThe guide had promised a longer visit, but we left early because the weather worsened.\nThe earlier promise is distinguished from the later departure; the promise does not guarantee fulfilment.\n\nPast perfect accepts definite past reference points: By 2018, the shop had closed. The restriction on present perfect with finished event times does not apply identically.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=7&section=gv5-d07-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Past perfect simple ( I had worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/past-perfect-simple-i-had-worked"},{"publisher":"Past perfect continuous ( I had been working ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/past-perfect-continuous-i-had-been-working"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d07-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Past habits, used to, habitual would and coherent reporting","text":"Past habits, used to, habitual would and coherent reporting\n\nUsed to + base describes a past habit or state contrasted with the present. Habitual would + base recalls repeated past events within an established past frame; it is distinct from other modal meanings of would.\n\nTo describe an earlier routine, you can use simple past: We walked along the canal after school. Used to can explicitly contrast that routine with the present: We used to walk there, but the path is closed now. It also describes a past state: The yard used to belong to my grandparents. The form does not change with the person of the subject. In carefully edited questions and negatives, use Did you use to live here? and We did not use to live here. Spellings with used after did occur, but the uninflected use is this course’s formal editorial default.\n\nOnce a past frame is established, would can recall typical events: During our summer visits, Grandfather would open the shop early and we would sweep the pavement. It need not imply a conditional promise. Ordinary continuing states usually take used to or simple past rather than habitual would: We used to own a small shop. Some repeated situations containing be can occur with habitual would when they mean something that repeatedly happened, such as On market days, the street would be crowded. The key is an established repeated-event reading, not a prohibition on the spelling be.\n\nDistinguish used to from be used to and get used to. I used to work nights describes an earlier habit. I am used to working nights means night work is familiar; to here is a preposition, so working follows it. I am getting used to working nights describes becoming accustomed. The similarity in sound conceals different structures. Avoid turning a whole story into would without first orienting the reader: outside a past frame, would can suggest a hypothetical outcome, a polite request or a future viewed from the past. Clear time framing prevents this ambiguity.\n\nWhen reporting history, preserve whether an event was asserted, remembered as a habit, merely planned or imagined. The phrase was going to open expresses a past intention, without proving that opening occurred. Used to normally implies a contrast with current circumstances, but it should not be used to invent a change that the evidence does not support. If a source simply records regular deliveries in 1990, simple past may be the most neutral choice. A reliable historical paragraph can combine simple past narrative, progressive background, perfect anteriority and habitual constructions when each serves a clear temporal or discourse function.\n\nused to + base; did not use to + base: Past habit/state and the formal negative default.\n\npast frame + would + base: A typical repeated past event, when context establishes the habitual reading.\n\nbe/get used to + noun or -ing: Being/becoming accustomed; not the same construction as used to + base.\n\nThe station used to have a wooden roof.\nUsed to describes a former state.\n\nWhen I was an apprentice, we would check the shutters every evening.\nThe opening frame establishes habitual past events.\n\nI used to work alone, but I am now used to sharing the workshop.\nThe first construction describes a former habit; the second describes current familiarity.\n\nHabitual would can describe repeated temporary states in context: On delivery days, the corridor would be full of boxes. It does not normally replace used to for an unchanging possession state.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=7&section=gv5-d07-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Used to - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/used-to"},{"publisher":"Would - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/would"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d08-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Combining perfect and progressive viewpoints","text":"Combining perfect and progressive viewpoints\n\nPerfect constructions relate a situation to a later reference point; progressive constructions present a situation from within its development. They can combine, and neither construction alone tells the whole story about completion or present consequences.\n\nCompare The technician has repaired the pump with The technician has been repairing the pump. In the first, the repair is presented as an achieved event relevant now. In the second, attention falls on the activity over a recent interval. The second sentence does not by itself say whether the pump is now repaired. Form a perfect with have plus a past participle, and a progressive with be plus an -ing form. Their combination is have been repairing: been is the participle of be, and repairing carries the progressive meaning. With a past reference point, change the finite auxiliary to had.\n\nInterpret the whole expression, including the verb and its object. She has written three reports presents three completed products. She has been writing reports presents a process, perhaps repeatedly, without specifying how many were completed. A perfect simple can also describe a continuing state: We have owned this shop for years. A perfect progressive can describe an activity that has just stopped: You look exhausted; have you been carrying boxes? The visible exhaustion is evidence for recent activity; it does not prove the person is still carrying anything while the question is asked.\n\nThe same contrast operates with a past viewpoint: By noon, they had repaired the roof presents the repair as achieved before noon, whereas They had been repairing the roof before the storm arrived describes earlier activity. A simple perfect does not necessarily promise a permanent result. The roof has been repaired does not rule out a new leak tomorrow. Conversely, ongoing activity can produce results without reaching an intended endpoint. Consider a wet floor after someone has been washing it. Ask separately whether an event occurred, whether its endpoint was reached, and which consequences the context makes relevant.\n\nAdvanced analysis distinguishes grammatical aspect from the situation type expressed by the whole predicate. Write reports allows an open activity; write three reports supplies a countable endpoint. This interaction is sometimes called lexical aspect or situation aspect. Perfect progressive does not automatically mean a longer duration than perfect simple, nor is simple a guarantee of a short event. Both I have worked here for ten years and I have been working here for ten years can be appropriate; the latter puts greater attention on the activity or its continuing course. Translation and editing should preserve the intended viewpoint rather than mechanically substituting forms.\n\nhave/has/had + past participle: A perfect construction interpreted relative to now or another reference point.\n\nhave/has/had + been + -ing: Perfect and progressive meanings combine; completion must be inferred from the full context.\n\nThe archivist has catalogued eighty letters.\nThe stated total concerns completed cataloguing events.\n\nThe archivist has been cataloguing letters all morning.\nThe activity occupies a recent interval; the completed total is unspecified.\n\nThe floor was slippery because someone had been washing it.\nEarlier washing explains a later past condition; washing need not still have been happening.\n\nAn achievement verb can occur in a progressive when context supports repetition or an unfolding stage: The old engine has been stopping unexpectedly describes repeated stops, not one indefinitely extended instant.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=8&section=gv5-d08-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Present perfect simple or present perfect continuous? - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-or-present-perfect-continuous"},{"publisher":"Past perfect simple or past perfect continuous? - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/past-perfect-simple-or-past-perfect-continuous"},{"publisher":"Present perfect simple ( I have worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-i-have-worked"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d08-m02","category":"grammar","title":"States, actions, temporary interpretations and repetition","text":"States, actions, temporary interpretations and repetition\n\nStative and dynamic describe interpretations of predicates. A stative reading presents a condition or relationship; a dynamic reading presents activity or change. Many verbs support more than one reading, and progressive compatibility depends on that reading and its context.\n\nUse the simple form for ordinary possession, knowledge or belief: I have two sisters; We know the address; I believe the explanation. These describe conditions rather than actions unfolding before us. Merely adding at the moment does not make every state naturally progressive: I know the answer at the moment is ordinary English. The term stative is a useful tendency, not a permanent prohibition attached to a spelling. Compare We have a small kitchen, describing possession, with We are having lunch, describing an event. The verb is the same word, but its meaning and pattern change.\n\nSeveral familiar contrasts illustrate the importance of interpretation. I think the figures are wrong expresses an opinion; I am thinking about the figures describes a process of consideration. The soup tastes salty reports a perception; The cook is tasting the soup describes deliberate sampling. He is polite presents a characteristic; He is being unusually polite describes behaviour in the current situation. Progressive be is normally about conduct or a temporary presentation, not ordinary identity: She is being a dentist is not the normal way to state her profession. Read the complements and the situation before selecting a form.\n\nSome predicates permit more flexibility than a short classroom list suggests. I feel tired and I am feeling tired can both describe a current condition, with the progressive making its present or temporary character more prominent. Informal I am loving this concert presents enjoyment as an immediate experience; it is marked by context and register rather than automatically ungrammatical. Repeated brief events can also receive progressive interpretation: Someone has been tapping on the window. A progressive with always can express an evaluative view of repeated behaviour, as in He is always interrupting, without making annoyance an obligatory part of the grammar.\n\nTemporary does not define progressive, and permanent does not define simple. We are currently living with relatives can be progressive without a promise that the arrangement will soon end; I live with relatives is a neutral characterisation, even when the arrangement is temporary. A speaker chooses how to package the situation. Duration is another independent property: I have known her for thirty years is lengthy but normally simple. Where a creative advertisement or a special discourse uses a normally stative verb progressively, identify the intended interpretation and register. Do not extend that exceptional use to every context, and do not reject it solely because a memorised list says never.\n\nsimple have + noun phrase: Possession or another stative relationship, according to the noun and context.\n\nbe + having/thinking/tasting + complement: A dynamic interpretation such as experiencing, considering or deliberately sampling.\n\nThe parcel weighs two kilograms.\nWeighs expresses a measured property; simple is ordinary.\n\nThe assistant is weighing the parcel.\nWeighing describes an action performed on the parcel.\n\nI have been thinking about your suggestion, although I still think it needs revision.\nThinking is a process; think expresses the opinion reached or held.\n\nSome expressions allow simple and progressive state-like readings: I feel better and I am feeling better are both established. A learner should explain the viewpoint rather than force a universal ban.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=8&section=gv5-d08-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Stative verbs | LearnEnglish","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/b1-b2/stative-verbs"},{"publisher":"Present perfect simple or present perfect continuous? - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-or-present-perfect-continuous"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d08-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Duration, starting points and temporal endpoints","text":"Duration, starting points and temporal endpoints\n\nFor normally introduces a duration, since a starting point in temporal uses, by a latest time or deadline, and until the endpoint of a continuing situation. These meanings guide interpretation but do not select one tense mechanically.\n\nA duration answers How long? Use for with a measured interval: for twenty minutes, for a decade, for several weeks. A temporal starting point answers From when? Use since with a point or a clause locating that point: since Tuesday; since the office moved. Compare We have waited for an hour and We have waited since nine. Both can describe waiting that began earlier and continues now. Do not replace for with since merely because the sentence contains a perfect construction. The complement after the preposition determines which expression accurately represents the intended interval.\n\nFor works with past, present and future reference: We waited for an hour yesterday; We have waited for an hour; We will stay for an hour. Since often combines with perfect constructions when measuring from an earlier point to a later viewpoint, but it is not exclusively a present-perfect signal. By 2022, she had worked here for five years is a past-perfect account, and She had been unwell since Monday measures to a past reference point. Since can also mean because: Since the gate was locked, we used another entrance gives a reason, not the beginning of a duration.\n\nBy sets a latest acceptable or relevant time: Send the signed form by Friday means no later than Friday. Until presents continuation to an endpoint: Keep the original until Friday means retain it through the relevant interval. Negation changes the relationship: She did not sign until Friday locates the eventual signing no earlier than Friday in the ordinary reading. Compare She had signed by Friday, which presents signing as achieved at or before that point. These are not stylistic substitutes. Preserve the intended deadline, continuation or delay when correcting a sentence, and do not change a promise into a mere prediction.\n\nThe temporal expression and the verb viewpoint contribute separate information. By six, I will have finished explicitly looks back from a future boundary; I will finish by six can communicate the same deadline without a perfect form. A finished date is compatible with past perfect when another past viewpoint is established: At the review in May, we discovered that the permit had expired in March. The finished-time restriction on ordinary present perfect does not ban past perfect with dates. Until and by can also have non-temporal uses, and temporal clauses require their own analysis. Describe the particular construction rather than converting a useful contrast into an unrestricted word-substitution rule.\n\nfor + duration; since + starting point: Measure an interval or locate its beginning.\n\nby + deadline; continuing situation + until + endpoint: Distinguish completion no later than a time from continuation up to a time.\n\nThe exhibition remained open until six.\nRemaining open continues to the stated endpoint.\n\nThe driver must return the vehicle by six.\nThe return is due no later than the deadline.\n\nBy last winter, the family had lived there for a decade.\nA past viewpoint licenses a past perfect with a duration; no present continuation is asserted.\n\nSince is not always temporal: Since you have the key, please open the cabinet means because. Its presence does not itself require a perfect construction in every clause.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=8&section=gv5-d08-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"For or since ? - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/for-or-since"},{"publisher":"By - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/by"},{"publisher":"Until - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/until"},{"publisher":"Past perfect simple ( I had worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/past-perfect-simple-i-had-worked"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d08-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Already, yet, just, lately and recently in context","text":"Already, yet, just, lately and recently in context\n\nTime adverbs help locate events and convey expectations. Their meaning, position and compatibility with a construction must be interpreted together; they are not automatic tense selectors.\n\nAlready commonly presents a situation as existing or achieved by a relevant point, often earlier than expected: The bus has already left. Yet in a question asks whether an expected event has happened by now: Has the bus left yet? In a negative, The bus has not left yet indicates that departure has not happened up to the reference point and commonly remains expected. Just can mean a very short time ago: The bus has just left. These adverbs add interpretation to the clause; the verb form still needs to fit the broader temporal setting.\n\nPosition changes emphasis and can affect naturalness. Already and recent-event just commonly follow the first auxiliary: We have already checked the locks; We had just finished. Already can also appear at the end: Have you finished already? often expresses surprise. Yet commonly appears at the end of a question or negative statement. It also has other established uses: a yet more difficult problem means a still more difficult one, while The result is promising yet uncertain uses yet as a coordinator. Identify the use before applying a rule learned for temporal questions.\n\nLately normally refers to a recent interval, especially with states, repeated events or continuing activity: I have been sleeping badly lately. Recently can do that too, but also readily locates a single completed past event: We inspected the lift recently. A precise finished time such as last Tuesday normally selects a past construction for the event: We inspected it last Tuesday. British English frequently uses present perfect for recent events with just, already and yet; American English also commonly uses simple past in such contexts: Did you send it yet? This is an established regional alternative, not evidence of carelessness.\n\nAdverbs carry scope and pragmatic expectations as well as clock time. Just may mean only, exactly, or emphatic merely, so I just need a signature need not describe a very recent need. Already does not require perfect aspect: We already know the answer is simple present. Special contexts permit negated already, for example a question challenging a presupposition, so not usually is more accurate than never. An assessment must give sufficient context when two tense-aspect forms or adverb positions remain defensible. Where the task asks for the most natural expression in a stated register, explain that criterion rather than marking every less frequent alternative as structurally impossible.\n\nhave + already/just + past participle: Common position for already and recent-event just with a perfect auxiliary.\n\nquestion/negative clause + yet: Common temporal use concerning an expected event up to the relevant point.\n\nThe catering team has already prepared the room.\nAlready indicates completion by now, perhaps earlier than expected.\n\nHas the repair team tested the alarm yet?\nYet asks whether the expected test has occurred up to now.\n\nThe manager recently visited the site, but maintenance has been irregular lately.\nRecently locates one visit; lately frames a recent interval of maintenance.\n\nThese words have other senses and constructions: yet can mean nevertheless or still in combinations, and just can mean only. Analyse the actual use instead of applying a tense rule to the spelling alone.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=8&section=gv5-d08-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Already - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/already"},{"publisher":"Yet - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/yet"},{"publisher":"Just - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/just"},{"publisher":"Late or lately ? - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/late-or-lately"},{"publisher":"RECENTLY | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/recently"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d09-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Will, shall and be going to: prediction, intention and commitment","text":"Will, shall and be going to: prediction, intention and commitment\n\nEnglish uses several constructions to refer to future time. Will and shall are modal auxiliaries, while be going to combines a form of be with going to and a plain-form verb. Their meanings overlap but their use depends on the speaker’s purpose and viewpoint.\n\nUse will with the base form: The driver will return. Add not for a negative, The driver will not return, and put will before the subject for a question, Will the driver return? The contraction will not becomes won’t. A promise, prediction, offer and decision can all use will: I will return your call; The river will rise; I will carry that bag; I will take the early train. The word future identifies the intended time, not a guarantee that a prediction is factually certain. Identify what the speaker is doing as well as when the event is expected.\n\nBe going to commonly highlights an intention already held or evidence visible now: We are going to replace the roof; Those loose tiles are going to fall. Its finite be agrees with the subject: I am, she is, they are. Make negatives and questions with be: They are not going to move; Are they going to move? Going to need not describe physical travel. Compare They are going to the clinic, where to the clinic is a destination phrase, with They are going to inspect the clinic, where inspect belongs to the intended action. Keep these different structures separate.\n\nWill and going to often both fit the same future event, with a different conversational emphasis. I will call her can make a decision or commitment; I am going to call her can state a prior intention. A context may favour one without making the other structurally impossible. Shall with I and we remains possible for formal future reference, especially in British English, though will is more widespread. Shall I open the window? offers action or asks what the speaker should do; Shall we begin? proposes joint action. These questions have a modal purpose, not merely an announcement of future time.\n\nFuture expressions interact with modality: they present expectations, intentions and commitments from the speaker’s current standpoint. A spontaneous decision is a useful context for teaching will, not a test proving that will can never describe an earlier plan. Likewise, visible evidence favours going to without banning will from evidence-based predictions. Informal gonna represents a reduced pronunciation of going to in the intended-action construction; it is unsuitable as a default spelling in formal reports. A careful transformation preserves the communicative force: changing I promise I will help into I may help weakens commitment, even though both clauses are grammatically formed.\n\nwill/shall + base form: Modal future reference; interpretation may include prediction, willingness, decision or commitment.\n\nam/is/are + going to + base form: Common intention or evidence-based prediction construction.\n\nI will send the receipt tonight.\nWill can express the speaker’s commitment to a future action.\n\nThe panels are going to fall if the supports move again.\nThe prediction is presented in relation to an existing dangerous condition.\n\nShall we discuss the draft before lunch?\nThis is a proposal about joint action, an established British use of shall.\n\nThe form going to can describe destination rather than intention: We are going to Lahore contains a destination phrase. Do not analyse every occurrence as a future auxiliary construction.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=9&section=gv5-d09-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Future: will and shall - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-will-and-shall"},{"publisher":"Future: be going to ( I am going to work ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-be-going-to-i-am-going-to-work"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d09-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Present forms for arrangements, timetables and future facts","text":"Present forms for arrangements, timetables and future facts\n\nPresent-tense forms can have future reference. Present progressive commonly presents an arrangement, while present simple commonly presents a timetable, fixed schedule or future fact. These uses rely on context and do not create new verb endings.\n\nConsider The interview starts at nine tomorrow. Starts is present tense, but tomorrow places the event in the future. A timetable, programme or scheduled event commonly uses present simple: The coach departs at six; The term ends in July. Negative and question formation stays the same as for present simple: The coach does not stop here; When does the coach leave? Be also keeps its ordinary present forms: The ceremony is on Friday. A future time expression helps the reader recognise the intended reference; without one, the surrounding conversation may supply it.\n\nPresent progressive commonly presents an arrangement: I am meeting the surveyor at ten; We are moving next month. An arrangement can involve other people, a booking, or the speaker’s own settled plan; another person need not appear in every sentence. Build it with finite be and an -ing form, and form questions and negatives with be. Compare Are you meeting the surveyor? and You are not meeting the surveyor today. The construction presents the action as planned or arranged from the present viewpoint. It does not assert that the future is immune to cancellation or disruption.\n\nA single event can be described as schedule or arrangement: The delegation leaves on Thursday and The delegation is leaving on Thursday may both be natural. The first can report its programme, while the second draws attention to an arrangement in progress. An objective item should not ask for the only correct form when both readings are available. Present progressive is not the ordinary neutral choice for a bare speculative prediction such as a distant weather guess; will or going to normally serves that purpose. Context may support marked conversational uses, so explain the default rather than invent an absolute prohibition.\n\nForm and temporal interpretation are distinct levels of analysis. A present-tense schedule can concern one future occasion, not an eternal habit: The eclipse begins at 14:10 tomorrow. Present simple also occurs in subordinate time clauses such as when the train arrives, a structure studied in the final module of this day. Do not confuse that syntactic pattern with a main-clause timetable merely because both verbs look present. When editing, preserve the source of certainty: The timetable says the train leaves at six explicitly attributes the schedule; I expect the train will leave at six expresses an expectation. Both are grammatical but make different claims.\n\npresent simple + future context: Commonly presents a schedule, fixed programme or future fact.\n\nam/is/are + -ing + future context: Commonly presents a current arrangement for a later event.\n\nThe examination begins at nine on Monday.\nPresent begins reports an official schedule with future reference.\n\nWe are interviewing two applicants tomorrow.\nThe progressive presents an arrangement already established.\n\nThe timetable says the last bus departs at 22:15, but we are leaving earlier.\nA stated schedule and a personal arrangement coexist without requiring identical constructions.\n\nPresent simple can refer to a unique future fact, not only a repeated timetable: Her birthday falls on a Sunday next year. Do not require repetition where the context supplies a fixed future occurrence.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=9&section=gv5-d09-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Future: present continuous to talk about the future ( I’m working tomorrow ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-present-continuous-to-talk-about-the-future-i-m-working-tomorrow"},{"publisher":"Future: present simple to talk about the future ( I work tomorrow ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-present-simple-to-talk-about-the-future-i-work-tomorrow"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d09-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Future progressive, perfect and perfect progressive","text":"Future progressive, perfect and perfect progressive\n\nFuture-oriented auxiliary combinations can present an activity in progress at a later point, a situation viewed from that later point, or the duration of earlier activity up to it. The construction and the temporal context must both be considered.\n\nFuture progressive uses will be plus an -ing form: At ten tomorrow, I will be interviewing applicants. It places the reader within an activity expected to be in progress at that point. Negate the finite modal, I will not be interviewing, and invert it in a question, Will you be interviewing? Do not change be to is after will: a modal selects the base form. This construction can also present an expected course of events, as in I will be passing the pharmacy, so I can collect the prescription. The intended time is supplied by the context.\n\nFuture perfect uses will have plus a past participle: By ten, I will have interviewed three applicants. It looks back from a future boundary and presents what will then be completed or true. A state can also extend to that boundary: By June, she will have known him for ten years. Future perfect progressive uses will have been plus -ing: By ten, I will have been interviewing for two hours. It draws attention to activity and its duration up to the future viewpoint. It does not necessarily state that the activity stops at that boundary.\n\nCompare completed quantity with continuing course: By Friday, we will have inspected six bridges reports a completed total; By Friday, we will have been inspecting bridges for a week highlights the period of activity. Neither construction alone guarantees the whole project is finished unless the project’s limits are stated. With some predicates, both future perfect simple and progressive are defensible: In May, I will have worked here for five years and I will have been working here for five years can describe the same employment period. A question must specify the desired construction or intended focus if it is to have a unique answer.\n\nThe so-called future perfect is a combination of modal will and perfect have; its conventional label describes a common temporal interpretation. A deadline does not automatically make it obligatory: We will finish by Friday is ordinary English beside We will have finished by Friday. The latter explicitly adopts a later retrospective viewpoint. Progressive restrictions also remain sensitive to meaning: will have known is the ordinary form for a continuing knowledge state, while will have been considering describes a process. Avoid mechanically generating all possible auxiliaries for every verb. A formally elaborate sequence can be semantically awkward even when the component endings appear in the correct order.\n\nwill + be + -ing: An activity viewed as in progress at a future point.\n\nwill + have + past participle: A retrospective view from a future point.\n\nwill + have + been + -ing: Activity and its duration leading up to a future viewpoint.\n\nAt this time tomorrow, the committee will be hearing evidence.\nThe future point lies within the projected hearing activity.\n\nBy the end of tomorrow, the committee will have heard six witnesses.\nThe future perfect presents a completed count by the boundary.\n\nBy noon, the volunteers will have been working for four hours.\nThe perfect progressive foregrounds duration without necessarily ending the work at noon.\n\nSome duration predicates allow either simple or progressive perfect forms with overlapping reference: will have lived and will have been living can both measure a period up to a future point. A duration phrase does not alone select one uniquely.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=9&section=gv5-d09-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Future continuous ( I will be working ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-continuous-i-will-be-working"},{"publisher":"Future perfect simple ( I will have worked eight hours ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-perfect-simple-i-will-have-worked-eight-hours"},{"publisher":"Future perfect continuous ( I will have been working here ten years ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-perfect-continuous-i-will-have-been-working-here-ten-years"},{"publisher":"Present perfect simple or present perfect continuous? - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-or-present-perfect-continuous"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d09-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Future in the past, time clauses and consistent viewpoints","text":"Future in the past, time clauses and consistent viewpoints\n\nFuture reference is relative to a viewpoint. A past narrator can describe an event that was still future then. Time and condition clauses follow their own patterns, and reporting may shift a viewpoint without requiring every verb to become the same tense.\n\nFuture in the past describes a later situation viewed from an earlier past point: On Monday, she said she would return on Tuesday. Would is the usual backshifted counterpart of will in this reporting pattern. Past forms of other future expressions are also available: She was going to return; She was leaving the next day. The construction does not by itself establish that the planned return happened. Add the outcome if it matters: She was going to return, but the storm cancelled the ferry. Identify the earlier viewpoint before deciding what a later expression means.\n\nIn ordinary future time clauses, use a present form rather than future will: I will call when the meeting ends; We will leave after she has signed the register. Present perfect in the time clause can emphasise prior completion. The main clause can still contain will. Similar teaching patterns occur with open conditions: If the weather improves, we will leave. This guidance concerns a particular subordinate use; it is not a ban on will after the spelling when or if. Distinguish a clause locating an event from an embedded question asking what time an event will occur.\n\nCompare I will phone when the train arrives with I do not know when the train will arrive. The first when-clause supplies the time of phoning; the second is an embedded interrogative complement expressing an unknown arrival time. Will is appropriate in that embedded question. In an if-clause, will may express willingness or insistence rather than simple future location: If you will hold the ladder, I can carry the tools. This means if you are willing to hold it. A later lesson develops the full range of conditional meanings. Do not remove a modal mechanically when it carries such an independent meaning.\n\nBackshift is common after past reporting verbs, but reporting also considers whether a statement remains true or a plan remains future at report time. She said the office opens at nine can retain a currently valid schedule; She said the office opened at nine adopts a past reporting viewpoint without necessarily asserting that the schedule changed. Choose and explain the viewpoint. Temporal consistency is coherence, not uniform tense: Yesterday I learned that the exhibition opens next month contains a past discovery and a future schedule. Time and place words also depend on the reporting context. Use explicit dates when a transformation would otherwise make tomorrow, next week or the following day ambiguous.\n\npast reporting verb + would + base form: A common future-in-the-past reporting pattern.\n\nmain future clause + when/after/until + present form: Common future time-clause pattern; not the pattern of every clause introduced by the same word.\n\nThe witness said she would send the photographs the next day.\nWould presents sending as future from the earlier statement.\n\nWe will publish the report after the auditors have checked the figures.\nThe present perfect time clause presents checking as prior to publication.\n\nI cannot tell you when the report will appear.\nThe when-clause is an embedded question, so future will is appropriate.\n\nWill can occur in a conditional clause with willingness: If you will sign here, we can proceed. Explain its modal meaning instead of teaching never use will after if.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=9&section=gv5-d09-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Future in the past - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-in-the-past"},{"publisher":"Conditionals: if - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conditionals-if"},{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"By - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/by"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d10-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Person, number and the structure of the subject","text":"Person, number and the structure of the subject\n\nSubject–verb agreement is the correspondence between a subject’s grammatical person and number and the finite verb forms that show those distinctions. Agreement depends on structure and interpretation, not simply the nearest visible noun.\n\nCompare The guard checks the door and The guards check the door. In present simple, an ordinary lexical verb normally adds -s with a third-person singular subject. I and you use the base form even when referring to one person: I check; you check. Be has am, is and are in the present and was and were in the past. Have has has with a third-person singular subject. Most other past-tense verbs do not change for number: the guard checked and the guards checked. Agreement therefore does not mean adding an ending to every verb in every sentence.\n\nFind the complete subject and its head, the central noun in an ordinary noun phrase. In The condition of the external walls is worrying, condition is the head; walls occurs inside a modifying of-phrase. A relative clause or parenthetical phrase also does not normally replace that head as agreement controller: The folder that contains the photographs is missing. When auxiliaries occur, the finite auxiliary carries the relevant agreement: The folders have been moved. Do not alter moved to make it plural. Subject identification is a structural analysis; merely crossing out every prepositional phrase can remove information needed to interpret the sentence.\n\nCoordination with and normally creates a plural subject: The clerk and the porter are waiting. An accompanying phrase such as together with, as well as or along with is not ordinary subject coordination: The clerk, together with two porters, is waiting. If two descriptions identify a single individual, the subject may be interpreted as singular: The founder and treasurer is one person in this organisation. If the founder and the treasurer are different people, use plural agreement. Articles and context help identify the intended reference; do not decide identity from a memorised surface formula alone.\n\nThe familiar head-noun rule is a reliable starting point for ordinary noun phrases, not an exhaustive theory of English agreement. Quantificational constructions, collectives and notional readings require further analysis in later modules. Distinguish genuine coordination from a phrase merely mentioning another person. Also distinguish the subject from an object or predicative complement: The main difficulty is the missing records does not make records the subject of is in this ordinary clause. Where the number or identity of coordinated referents is unclear, rewriting to make that identity explicit is often better than defending an ambiguous choice by rule recital.\n\nthird-person singular subject + present verb in -s: A common present-simple agreement pattern; be and some verbs have special forms.\n\nhead noun + modifying phrase + finite verb: In an ordinary noun phrase, the modifier does not replace the head as agreement controller.\n\nThe colour of the curtains has faded.\nSingular colour controls has; curtains is inside the of-phrase.\n\nThe inspectors have been reviewing the photographs.\nPlural inspectors controls finite have; the other forms do not acquire plural endings.\n\nThe director, as well as two advisers, is attending.\nAs well as introduces an accompanying phrase rather than a second coordinated subject.\n\nMost lexical past-tense forms do not distinguish singular and plural: The driver arrived and The drivers arrived. Do not manufacture a plural past ending.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=10&section=gv5-d10-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Subject–verb agreement - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subject-verb-agreement"},{"publisher":"Subject-Verb Agreement","url":"https://webapps.towson.edu/ows/sub-verb.htm"},{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d10-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Collectives, indefinite pronouns and competing agreement cues","text":"Collectives, indefinite pronouns and competing agreement cues\n\nEnglish agreement can reflect grammatical form, the meaning of a group, or proximity in particular coordinated patterns. Collective nouns and quantifying pronouns require qualified guidance about reference, register and established regional variation.\n\nA collective noun names a group: team, government, committee, family. In British English, singular agreement commonly presents the group as a unit, while plural agreement can present its members: The committee has issued its report; The committee have changed their minds. American English more often uses singular agreement with these ordinary group nouns, especially when acting as one unit. This is a broad convention rather than a claim that every collective has identical behaviour in all varieties. Police ordinarily takes plural agreement: The police are investigating. Do not transfer a rule for committee blindly to police.\n\nIndefinite pronouns and determiners form different patterns. Everyone, somebody, nobody and each ordinarily take singular finite agreement: Everyone is ready; Each of the rooms has a window. Both, few, many and several are plural in their ordinary countable uses. Singular they can refer back to a grammatically singular person expression without changing its verb: Everyone has their own ticket. All, some and most depend on what is quantified: Some of the water is contaminated; Some of the wells are contaminated. None can also support singular or plural interpretation, particularly when referring to a countable set.\n\nNeither of and either of commonly take singular agreement in careful formal prose: Neither of the plans is suitable. Plural agreement occurs in established conversational usage, especially when a plural noun phrase is close to the verb; a register-sensitive exercise should identify its target convention. With subjects joined by or or nor, a common editing convention makes the verb agree with the nearer coordinated part: Neither the director nor the assistants are available; Neither the assistants nor the director is available. Recast awkward combinations, particularly those mixing person, rather than assuming proximity alone makes every possible sequence elegant or uncontroversial.\n\nNotional agreement follows an interpretation of number rather than surface form alone. Proximity agreement follows a nearby eligible expression in constructions where that convention is established. These should not be confused with accidental agreement attraction, as in The cost of the repairs are high in ordinary formal prose. Collective pronouns should make the intended unit-versus-members reference clear across the passage, though a writer may deliberately shift perspective when context supports it. None of is especially unsuitable for an absolute always-singular rule: authoritative usage accounts describe both singular and plural verbs. State the desired register and interpretation when a task depends on the choice.\n\ncollective noun + singular/plural verb: Possible unit/member contrast, particularly in British English; conventions vary by noun and variety.\n\neach/everyone + singular verb; both/several + plural verb: Ordinary agreement of these specific quantifying expressions.\n\nEveryone has their own locker.\nEveryone takes singular has; singular their provides inclusive personal reference.\n\nThe committee have not agreed among themselves.\nBritish plural agreement foregrounds the members’ differing views.\n\nNeither the secretary nor the assistants are free.\nPlural are follows the common nearer-subject convention, with assistants nearest.\n\nNone of the candidates is available and None of the candidates are available both occur in standard usage; guidance differs in its preferred register. A trustworthy answer key must not reject one without an explicitly justified criterion.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=10&section=gv5-d10-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Group nouns | LearnEnglish","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/english-grammar-reference/group-nouns"},{"publisher":"Nouns: singular and plural - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-singular-and-plural"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"},{"publisher":"Neither, neither … nor and not … either - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/neither-neither-nor-and-not-either"},{"publisher":"No, none and none of - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/none"},{"publisher":"none pronoun - Definition, pictures, pronunciation and usage notes | Oxford Advanced Learner's Dictionary at OxfordLearnersDictionaries.com","url":"https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/definition/english/none_1"},{"publisher":"Subject-Verb Agreement","url":"https://webapps.towson.edu/ows/sub-verb.htm"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d10-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Relative clauses, inversion and existential constructions","text":"Relative clauses, inversion and existential constructions\n\nEach clause has its own agreement relationships. Relative-clause verbs follow the intended antecedent, and inversion can place a subject after an auxiliary or verb. Existential there introduces an additional construction whose formal agreement commonly follows its postverbal noun phrase.\n\nIn The drivers who work nights receive an allowance, the relative pronoun who refers to plural drivers, so its clause uses work. The main clause also has plural drivers as its subject. In The driver who works nights receives an allowance, both relevant subjects are singular. Analyse the clauses separately; the verb of one clause does not mechanically control the other. A longer subject can contain a plural antecedent inside a singular outer phrase: One of the drivers who work nights receives an allowance. Outer one controls receives, while drivers supplies the usual antecedent for who work nights.\n\nExpressions such as one of those people who usually select a relative clause describing the plural group: She is one of those people who notice everything. A relative attached specifically to the individual one has a different interpretation and may take a singular verb. The only one of the drivers who has a permit normally singles out the one permitted individual. Do not decide all relative attachments from the presence of the word only alone. Where the intended scope is important, make it explicit: She is the only driver who has a permit; among the permitted drivers, she is the only one available.\n\nIn questions, the subject often follows a finite auxiliary: Are the documents ready? Does the document contain a signature? In locative inversion, the subject can follow the lexical verb: On the shelf were three sealed boxes. Find boxes before selecting were. Existential There is a problem and There are several problems conventionally match the form of be to the postverbal noun phrase in careful formal writing. Contracted There’s several problems is widespread in conversation; revise it to There are several problems for the stated formal convention without presenting all conversational occurrences as evidence of ignorance.\n\nGrammatical frameworks differ in analysing existential there: many modern accounts treat it as a dummy subject, while traditional teaching calls the following noun phrase the real subject. Both descriptions can support the same practical formal-agreement guidance if terminology is made explicit. Not every word after a verb is a subject: compare On the desk lies the report with The clerk filed the report. Negative inversion also leaves agreement with the actual subject: Never have the records been so clear. Parsing requires clause structure and reference, not an instruction to choose whichever noun is closest to the finite verb.\n\nantecedent + who/which/that + agreeing finite verb: Relative-clause agreement reflects the intended antecedent.\n\nThere is + singular noun phrase; There are + plural noun phrase: Common careful formal pattern in existential be-constructions.\n\nOne of the books that explain the method is missing.\nBooks controls explain in the relative; one controls main-clause is.\n\nNear the entrance stand two noticeboards.\nPlural noticeboards follows stand and is its subject.\n\nThere are three reasons why the proposal needs revision.\nThe plural postverbal noun phrase selects are in this formal existential construction.\n\nInformal contracted there’s commonly introduces plural associates. For formal prose, use the agreed full form; distinguish register-sensitive editing from a universal ban on attested conversational grammar.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=10&section=gv5-d10-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"There is, there’s and there are - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/there-is-there-s-and-there-are"},{"publisher":"Subject-Verb Agreement","url":"https://webapps.towson.edu/ows/sub-verb.htm"},{"publisher":"Inversion - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/inversion"},{"publisher":"Relative clauses - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/relative-clauses"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d10-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Quantities, measures, titles and plural-looking forms","text":"Quantities, measures, titles and plural-looking forms\n\nAgreement with a quantity depends on what is measured or counted and how it is construed. The spelling of a noun, a percentage or a numerical expression does not by itself establish the number required in every use.\n\nSome nouns look plural but take singular agreement in their ordinary meanings: The news is encouraging; Mathematics is compulsory. Other nouns normally take plural agreement: The trousers are wet; The scissors are blunt. A pair of scissors is different because pair is singular: This pair of scissors is blunt. Series has the same spelling in singular and plural: This series is useful; These two series are useful. Use determiners, meaning and a learner’s dictionary where the form alone is misleading. Do not add an invented singular police or a plural news merely to make the ending match a simplified pattern.\n\nFractions and percentages commonly follow the number interpretation of the of-complement: Half of the water is gone; Half of the bottles are empty; Twenty per cent of the applicants were absent. A percentage without an explicit complement still requires context: Twenty per cent is a substantial share treats the proportion as one amount. If the complement is a collective, its own possible unit/member readings can matter. A number of applications have arrived is a conventional plural quantifying construction; The number of applications has risen treats number as the quantity being discussed. These are different structures, not contradictory examples of the same rule.\n\nA measure phrase can denote one amount: Ten kilometres is too far to walk today; Three years is a long wait. Plural agreement remains natural when separate units or their passage are in view: Three years have passed. A title naming one work commonly receives singular agreement despite plural words inside it: Great Expectations is a novel. This does not make every plural proper name singular in every variety; team and organisation names may follow collective conventions. Mathematical statements also have conventional forms, such as Two plus two is four, but do not infer a universal rule for every coordinated numerical expression.\n\nMeaning can change the number behaviour of the same spelling. Statistics is difficult can name a discipline; The statistics are misleading refers to numerical figures. Politics can name an academic topic or a person’s views, with differing agreement: Her politics are well known. Such contrasts illustrate why grammatical number, semantic quantity and lexical convention must be distinguished. A rigid instruction to make the verb agree with the nearest noun fails with The number of files is increasing; an unrestricted head-noun slogan also needs qualification for quantifying A number of files are missing. Analyse the construction, then state which meaning and editorial convention support the chosen verb.\n\nfraction/percentage + of + mass/plural count noun: Agreement commonly follows the relevant mass or count interpretation.\n\na number of + plural noun + plural verb; the number of + plural noun + singular verb: Conventional contrast between quantifying several items and discussing their number.\n\nHalf of the evidence is missing.\nEvidence is a mass noun here, so the fraction has singular agreement.\n\nHalf of the witnesses are waiting outside.\nThe fraction ranges over plural people, giving plural agreement.\n\nThe number of reports has doubled, although a number of reports are still incomplete.\nThe first subject concerns a figure; the second quantifies several reports.\n\nA period can be construed as one measure or as separate years whose passage is asserted: Three years is a long time and Three years have passed are both ordinary. Do not impose singular agreement on every expression containing a measurement.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=10&section=gv5-d10-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Subject-Verb Agreement","url":"https://webapps.towson.edu/ows/sub-verb.htm"},{"publisher":"Nouns: singular and plural - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-singular-and-plural"},{"publisher":"series noun - Definition, pictures, pronunciation and usage notes | Oxford Advanced Learner's Dictionary at OxfordLearnersDictionaries.com","url":"https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/definition/english/series"},{"publisher":"Half - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/half"},{"publisher":"PERCENT | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/percent"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d11-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Adjective classes, positions and ordered description","text":"Adjective classes, positions and ordered description\n\nAn adjective is a word class with characteristic modifying and predicative uses; no single meaning or suffix identifies every member.\n\nStart with a small contrast: a quiet room and the room is quiet. In the first, quiet is an attributive modifier inside a noun phrase. In the second, it is a predicative complement after a linking verb. Both describe the room, but they occupy different structures. An adjective phrase may contain only its head adjective or additional words: very quiet, unusually quiet for this building. A noun used before another noun remains a noun in many modern analyses: stone in a stone wall names material but need not become an adjective merely because it modifies wall.\n\nAdjectives express many kinds of meaning: evaluative excellent, descriptive narrow, relational medical and participial exhausted or convincing. Participial adjectives resemble verb participles; the surrounding construction helps identify their use. The exhausted nurse needed rest describes a state, while the long shift had exhausted the nurse describes an event with a verbal object. Attributive adjective order reflects meaning: an attractive small wooden desk is neutral, with opinion before size before material. This is a preference shaped by meaning and grouping, not a mechanical requirement to fill nine slots before every noun.\n\nSome adjectives favour particular positions. Asleep normally follows a linking verb: the child is asleep, rather than an asleep child. Mere normally precedes its noun: a mere suggestion. Available can occur before or after the noun with small differences of information structure: available rooms and the rooms available tonight. Complements also affect placement: a room suitable for interviews is more natural than a suitable for interviews room. Adjectives ending in -ed often describe the affected experiencer, and -ing forms often describe a cause, but bored/boring is a lexical pattern to learn rather than a universal translation rule.\n\nPostpositive means positioned after the head noun within its phrase, not necessarily a separate clause complement. Something useful has postpositive useful; the proposal is useful has a predicative complement. Coordination and adjective grouping affect punctuation: a clear, practical explanation permits two independent evaluations, whereas a dark green folder contains a colour unit. Order can change deliberately when a writer contrasts wooden rather than metal or old rather than new. Relational adjectives may acquire evaluative readings: a very political decision concerns degree of political motivation, although the same adjective can classify a subject without degree. Analyse the actual sense and structure together.\n\ndeterminer + adjective phrase + noun: A common attributive pattern: an unusually quiet room.\n\nsubject + linking verb + adjective phrase: A predicative description: the room became quiet.\n\nThe soup is hot.\nHot is an adjective functioning as subject complement, not an object.\n\nThey reserved a small circular table.\nSize precedes shape in a neutral description.\n\nThe documents available after the hearing included a revised timetable.\nAvailable after the hearing postmodifies documents within the subject noun phrase.\n\nPosition is lexical: asleep is normally predicative, while mere is normally attributive.\n\nAn adjective's figurative or evaluative sense may be gradable even when its classifying sense is not.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=11&section=gv5-d11-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Adjectives: order","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adjectives-order"},{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Adjectives","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adjectives_2"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d11-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Degree, comparison and sense-dependent gradability","text":"Degree, comparison and sense-dependent gradability\n\nDegree constructions locate a quality on a scale or compare it with a contextual standard; the scale depends on the adjective's sense.\n\nA gradable adjective can describe different amounts of a quality: fairly warm, very warm, warmer. A comparative relates two values or groups; a superlative identifies an extreme within a comparison set. Form is partly lexical. Short adjectives commonly take -er and -est, with spelling adjustments such as large/larger and happy/happier. Many longer adjectives use more and most. Good/better/best and bad/worse/worst are irregular. Do not add more to an already comparative form for an ordinary comparison: more colder duplicates the degree marking. Check a dictionary when a two-syllable adjective allows competing forms.\n\nComparisons require a recoverable standard: this route is shorter than the coastal route. As ... as expresses equality against a standard; not as ... as denies that equality. A comparative clause may be reduced because repeated material is understood: the office is quieter than it was yesterday. When comparing quantities associated with different places, make the intended objects clear: the district's budget is smaller than the city's, rather than a budget being smaller than a city. A clause can itself supply an amount: our revenue is less than what the branch collected. Matching surface word classes is not a universal requirement.\n\nIntensifiers increase a degree, while downtoners reduce it: extremely helpful, rather difficult, slightly cheaper. Their compatibility differs: much cheaper, not normally very cheaper; absolutely impossible, rather than an ordinary scale reading of very impossible. Some adjectives have an endpoint meaning, such as complete meaning having all parts, but a related sense may allow comparison: a more complete account can mean one covering more information. Unique meaning the only example of its kind resists degree logically; unique meaning unusual can be graded in established usage. In precise formal work, choose a wording that makes the intended sense explicit.\n\nComparison involves grammar and interpretation. One of the better applications selects from a relatively strong group; one of the best applications makes a stronger ranking claim. Neither pattern requires the other. The better of two is the traditional formal default, while the best of two is common in informal standard usage. Comparative complements may be phrases or clauses, and than me is an ordinary alternative to the explicit than I am when meaning permits it. A sentence such as Kim likes Noor more than Ali is ambiguous between people liked and people doing the liking; repeating a verb or object resolves the comparison rather than merely changing a pronoun form.\n\nmore/less + adjective + than + standard: Compare a quality using a recoverable standard.\n\nas + adjective + as + standard: Express equality, with not as ... as expressing a lower degree.\n\nThis path is narrower than the track beside the canal.\nNarrower marks one comparison; than introduces its standard.\n\nHer explanation was much clearer than mine.\nMuch modifies a comparative; mine stands for my explanation.\n\nThe revised estimate is higher than we expected it to be.\nThe standard is supplied by a finite comparative clause with understood degree.\n\nFormal prose often prefers the better of two; informal standard usage also has the best of two.\n\nGradability belongs to a sense in context. A more complete account need not claim that a fully complete object becomes more than complete.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=11&section=gv5-d11-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Comparison: adjectives (bigger, biggest, more interesting)","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/comparison-adjectives-bigger-biggest-more-interesting"},{"publisher":"Comparison: clauses (bigger than we had imagined)","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/comparison-clauses-bigger-than-we-had-imagined"},{"publisher":"Unique: Usage Guide","url":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/unique"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d11-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Adverb forms, phrases, placement and local scope","text":"Adverb forms, phrases, placement and local scope\n\nAn adverb phrase is headed by an adverb; it may modify an event, an adjective, another adverb, or a larger unit, depending on its use.\n\nMany manner adverbs have an adjective plus -ly form: careful becomes carefully. The ending is a useful clue, not a definition. Fast, hard and late can be adverbs without -ly; friendly and costly are commonly adjectives. Ask what construction the word participates in. She spoke calmly describes the speaking, whereas she seemed calm characterises the subject through a linking verb. A linking verb can also take a noun phrase or other complement, so this contrast should not become a claim that every word after be must be an adjective. The whole clause determines the relation.\n\nAdverbs can express manner, place, time, frequency and degree, among other meanings. An adverb phrase may contain its own modifier: very slowly, quite unexpectedly. Adverbials are functions, not a single word class: at noon is a prepositional phrase functioning as a time adjunct, while yesterday can do similar work as an adverb. Neutral frequency adverbs often precede a lexical verb, follow main-verb be, or follow the first auxiliary: she usually walks; she is usually early; she has usually arrived by nine. These are common placements, not the only grammatical possibilities in every context.\n\nSome related forms have separate meanings. Hard means with effort or force, but hardly means almost not. Late concerns a time later than expected; lately means recently. Near concerns closeness; nearly means almost. Well commonly describes successful performance, while good is an adjective; well can also be a predicative adjective meaning healthy. Placement may change interpretation or information focus. They deliberately did not reply describes a deliberate decision not to reply; they did not deliberately reply can deny intention while leaving open whether a reply occurred. Do not diagnose scope from the nearest word alone.\n\nDistinguish an adverb's lexical category from its clause-level function and semantic scope. An adverb can modify a verb phrase while taking a broader proposition as its interpretive domain. Fronting, parenthetical position and stress create marked readings: repeatedly she had asked for a meeting foregrounds recurrence, while she had repeatedly asked is more neutral. A long object may make an intervening manner adverb acceptable even when a short-object version sounds awkward. Analyse acceptability in context rather than replacing every unconventional position automatically. When revising, state the intended frequency, manner or scope first, then choose an order that expresses it clearly.\n\nsubject + frequency adverb + lexical verb: A common neutral position: she often travels.\n\nsubject + first auxiliary + frequency adverb + remaining verb phrase: A common neutral position: she has often travelled.\n\nThe train arrived late.\nLate is an adverb here; no -ly ending is needed.\n\nThe train has rarely arrived before sunrise.\nRarely follows the first auxiliary and expresses frequency.\n\nThey did not deliberately omit the appendix.\nNegation can deny deliberate intention; it does not by itself prove whether the omission occurred.\n\nWell can be an adjective meaning healthy: I feel well today.\n\nFrequency and manner adverbs have neutral positions, but emphasis and constituent length permit alternatives.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=11&section=gv5-d11-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Comparison: adjectives (bigger, biggest, more interesting)","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/comparison-adjectives-bigger-biggest-more-interesting"},{"publisher":"Also, as well or too?","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/also-as-well-or-too"},{"publisher":"Adverbs","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adverbs_2"},{"publisher":"Adverbs and adverb phrases: position","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adverbs-and-adverb-phrases-position"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d11-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Focus, sentence adverbials and responsible interpretation","text":"Focus, sentence adverbials and responsible interpretation\n\nA focusing adverb highlights alternatives; a sentence adverbial comments on a whole proposition, its likelihood, or the speaker's relation to it.\n\nOnly, even and also guide a listener toward relevant alternatives. Only Leena signed says that no other relevant person signed. Leena signed only the form contrasts that object with other things she might have signed. Even Leena signed presents her participation as surprising against an expectation. These meanings depend on a context: surprising to whom, and compared with which alternatives? When a question offers two plausible positions without supplying that context, more than one answer may be grammatical. Your task as a writer is to make the intended comparison easy to recover, not to treat every other reading as impossible.\n\nOnly often occupies normal mid-position even when its focus is later: Leena only signed two forms can mean that two was the limit. Moving it nearer the focus can improve precision in an ambiguous written context, but adjacency is not a universal syntax rule. Also can add a new action by the same person, another participant in the same action, or another related fact. A preceding sentence usually determines which reading is relevant. In deliberate editing, write the intended alternative in a margin note, then check whether a reader has enough context to find that interpretation.\n\nSentence adverbials express stance or organise discourse: probably, fortunately, frankly and therefore do different jobs. Probably qualifies certainty; fortunately evaluates an event; frankly comments on the manner of speaking; therefore presents a conclusion. Compare Fortunately, the files survived with The files were stored fortunately, where end placement makes a manner interpretation awkward. Parenthetical commas often help separate a comment from the proposition, but punctuation follows structure and intended reading. Conjunctive adverbs do not independently license joining two full clauses by a comma: the server failed; therefore, the session ended is a standard written pattern.\n\nScope can interact with negation, quantification and presupposed alternatives. Not all applicants replied denies universal response; all applicants did not reply may be interpreted in more than one way, so an explicit none or some did not is clearer. Even presupposes a scale of expectation in many uses but does not tell us a measurable probability. An evaluative adverb can comment on an entire event without describing how an agent acted: surprisingly, the silent room was useful does not mean anyone acted surprisingly. Advanced analysis should identify these distinctions without pretending that every conversational inference is encoded as a rigid grammatical rule.\n\nonly + focused subject + predicate: Exclude relevant alternative subjects.\n\nsentence adverbial, + clause: A frequent pattern for an explicit comment on the proposition.\n\nOnly Farah checked the totals.\nThe exclusion concerns other relevant checkers.\n\nFarah also checked the totals after verifying the dates.\nThe context makes also add a second checking activity.\n\nThe totals were probably wrong; nevertheless, the estimate remained useful for planning.\nProbably qualifies confidence in the first claim; nevertheless marks concession between claims.\n\nMid-position only can focus later material. Context and stress help identify its scope.\n\nA sentence adverbial may be mobile, but mobility does not make every punctuation pattern equivalent.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=11&section=gv5-d11-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Only","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/only"},{"publisher":"Also, as well or too?","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/also-as-well-or-too"},{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d12-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Time, space and abstract relationships","text":"Time, space and abstract relationships\n\nA preposition commonly heads a phrase expressing a relationship; spatial meanings often extend to time, cause, means and other abstract relations.\n\nBegin with visible relations: a folder on the desk, a letter in the folder, a visitor at the gate. In often presents containment, on often presents contact with a surface, and at can locate an activity or point. These are useful meaning patterns, not a size-based rule that decides every case. A person may be at the office as a workplace or in the office as an interior. Direction contrasts with location: the child is in the garden, but runs into the garden. From identifies a source or starting point, while towards gives a direction without guaranteeing arrival.\n\nTime expressions have conventional patterns: at noon, on Tuesday, in October and in the evening. On Tuesday morning includes a named day and therefore differs from in the morning. At night is a conventional expression; during the night selects an interval. Since gives a starting point, for a duration, by a deadline and until an endpoint for continuation. I will send it by Friday promises completion no later than Friday; I will stay until Friday describes a continuing stay. These choices interact with tense and aspect, so a time word cannot substitute for understanding the event.\n\nAbstract uses include cause because of the delay, purpose for the examination, means by train, instrument with a key and relationship between two proposals. The same preposition can express different meanings, and alternative constructions may make related claims. Between is not restricted to exactly two: negotiations between the five named departments presents separate participants, whereas disagreement among the staff presents a group setting. By and with differ when an agent and an instrument are expressed: the cabinet was opened by the caretaker with a spare key. Learning the entire relation is more reliable than translating one preposition in isolation.\n\nSpatial perspectives permit legitimate alternatives: a bird in a tree and an apple on a tree select different relationships, not different sized locations. Abstract prepositions can be productive rather than arbitrary: under pressure and beyond the budget extend spatial schemas. Nevertheless, idiomatic selection limits prediction, and region or institutional convention may affect at the weekend versus on the weekend. Give the intended time span, route or relation before declaring an alternative wrong. In cumulative review, separate an aspect error from a preposition error: has lived here since March requires an appropriate verb phrase as well as since, and a past residence ending before now needs a different temporal frame.\n\nby + deadline: Completion occurs no later than the stated time.\n\nuntil + endpoint: A state or activity continues up to a boundary.\n\nThe parcel travelled from Quetta to Sukkur.\nFrom identifies source and to identifies destination.\n\nThe office will remain open until six.\nUntil marks the endpoint of a continuing state.\n\nThe agreement between the four laboratories covers shared equipment.\nBetween presents distinguishable participants; four does not make it ungrammatical.\n\nAt the weekend is a common British pattern; on the weekend is established American usage.\n\nBetween can relate more than two individually identified participants.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=12&section=gv5-d12-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Prepositions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositions_2"},{"publisher":"Present perfect simple ( I have worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-i-have-worked"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d12-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Prepositional phrases, complements and stranding","text":"Prepositional phrases, complements and stranding\n\nA prepositional phrase contains a preposition as head and, commonly, a complement; the complete phrase can perform several grammatical functions.\n\nIn beside the window, beside is the preposition and the window is its noun-phrase complement. The whole phrase can describe a noun, as in the desk beside the window, or locate an event, as in she waited beside the window. Pronouns normally use their object form after a preposition: with her, between you and me. The complement need not be one isolated word. A complex noun phrase such as the narrow window at the end of the hall remains a single complement of beside even though it contains another prepositional phrase.\n\nCommon complements include noun phrases, pronouns and -ing clauses: about the delay, with us, without checking the figures. Prepositions can also take other kinds of complement in particular patterns: from there has a place expression; until after the interview contains another prepositional phrase; surprised at what she wrote has a wh-clause. The familiar instruction use -ing after a preposition is useful when choosing an ordinary non-finite verbal complement, but it should not be expanded into the false claim that every complement is a gerund or that clauses can never follow a preposition.\n\nIn a question or relative construction, a preposition may remain at the end while its complement is understood earlier: Which bench did you sit on? This is preposition stranding and is normal standard English. On which bench did you sit? moves the preposition together with the question phrase, a pattern often called pied-piping. It is more formal and can sound natural in legal or academic contexts. Selection matters: we cannot freely separate parts of every idiom, and some fronted patterns are very unnatural. Where both versions are established, choose according to register and clarity rather than a blanket ban on sentence-final prepositions.\n\nFunction and category remain distinct. In the proposal on transport, the prepositional phrase postmodifies a noun; in interested in transport, it complements an adjective; in rely on transport, it belongs to the verb's lexical pattern. Modern grammatical frameworks differ in their classification of before in before lunch and before she left: some keep a broader preposition category, while many teaching grammars call the finite-clause use a conjunction. This course names the framework where it matters and always identifies the complement and function. A change of label must not turn an otherwise acceptable construction into an alleged error.\n\npreposition + noun phrase / -ing clause / licensed wh-clause: The complement type depends on the preposition and construction.\n\nwh-phrase ... preposition / preposition + wh-phrase ...: Stranding and pied-piping are alternative question patterns where licensed.\n\nThe folder beside the printer is empty.\nBeside the printer postmodifies folder; beside is its head.\n\nWe left without checking the figures.\nWithout takes an -ing clause as complement.\n\nThe question on which the panel disagreed concerned funding.\nOn which is a fronted prepositional phrase inside the relative clause.\n\nPrepositions license several complement types; at what she wrote is a genuine wh-clause complement.\n\nFrameworks may call before she left a subordinating conjunction pattern or a preposition with a clause complement; the label must be stated consistently.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=12&section=gv5-d12-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Prepositional phrases","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositional-phrases"},{"publisher":"Prepositions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositions_2"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d12-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Lexical selection, fixed combinations and meaning","text":"Lexical selection, fixed combinations and meaning\n\nLexical selection is the requirement or preference of a particular word for a complement pattern, including a specific preposition where its meaning requires one.\n\nSome verb patterns are straightforward only after the whole expression is learned: depend on a colleague, listen to a recording, discuss a problem. Discuss takes a direct object in the ordinary meaning illustrated here; listen introduces the thing heard with to. Do not infer that one pattern applies to every synonym. Explain a problem to someone differs from tell someone about a problem. A useful dictionary entry records the word's sense, the types of complement it permits, and examples of the actual construction. Keep a pattern notebook using complete short sentences, not an undifferentiated list of supposedly interchangeable prepositions.\n\nAdjectives and nouns select patterns too: interested in a subject, responsible for a decision, an increase in costs and a solution to a problem. Some words allow alternatives with overlapping meanings or different emphases; a dictionary, not a simplistic single-partner slogan, should resolve a doubtful case. Insist on a receipt and insist that the receipt be issued are both grammatical. The first contains a prepositional complement; the second contains a content clause. Insist does not always need on. Likewise, enter the room and enter into an agreement use different senses and should not be collapsed into one prohibition.\n\nThe spelling to belongs to different constructions. In hope to leave, it marks an infinitive. In look forward to leaving, it is a preposition within a lexical pattern and takes an -ing complement. Be used to describes accustomedness and differs from used to for a past habit: she is used to cycling versus she used to cycle. No visual shortcut based solely on the word to can decide the structure. Look at the governing expression and the intended meaning. A correct form may still make the wrong claim: used to work does not by itself say that work continues now.\n\nLexical grammar overlaps vocabulary, syntax and register. Different from is an appropriate British editorial default; different than is established in American English, while different to occurs especially in British speech. Formal preference does not justify scoring a standard regional alternative as universally wrong. In review, explain the selected sense and its complement, then compare a close alternative. This is particularly useful when a first language distributes relations differently, including for many Urdu-speaking learners, but no learner should be presumed to make the same error because of background. Record personal evidence from actual writing and practice, and revisit patterns that genuinely cause difficulty.\n\ninsist on + noun phrase / -ing clause: Demand or maintain something in an on-pattern.\n\ninsist that + content clause: State insistence through a proposition or mandative clause.\n\nWe discussed the revised timetable.\nDiscuss takes the timetable directly in the intended sense.\n\nRida insisted that the minutes were accurate.\nThe content clause expresses the proposition she maintained; no on is needed.\n\nThe team is used to working remotely but used to meet in person.\nAccustomedness takes -ing; the past-habit construction takes a base infinitive.\n\nInsist also takes content clauses, so insist always takes on is false.\n\nA verb's other sense can permit another pattern: enter a hall versus enter into negotiations.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=12&section=gv5-d12-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Prepositions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositions_2"},{"publisher":"Prepositional phrases","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositional-phrases"},{"publisher":"Verb patterns: verb + infinitive or verb + -ing?","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verb-patterns-verb-infinitive-or-verb-ing"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d12-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Particles, multi-word verbs and cumulative review","text":"Particles, multi-word verbs and cumulative review\n\nMulti-word verbs combine a verb with a particle, preposition or both; their object-placement patterns reveal important differences.\n\nIn turn off the lamp, off is a particle in a phrasal verb. With this separable pattern, an ordinary noun object can precede or follow the particle: turn the lamp off and turn off the lamp. A personal-pronoun object normally comes between: turn it off. This placement is not available with look after a child: after introduces its complement, so look the child after is not the ordinary care-for construction. Learn whether a particular expression is transitive or intransitive as well as whether it separates; the bus broke down needs no object, whereas someone may break down a calculation into parts.\n\nTraditional teaching often calls all multi-word expressions phrasal verbs, but a narrower classification distinguishes three patterns. A phrasal verb has a particle, such as give up; a prepositional verb has a selected preposition, such as rely on; a phrasal-prepositional verb has both, such as put up with. In put up with the noise, the noun complement follows with. The whole expression means tolerate, but its meaning is not obtained by translating each word separately. Dictionaries vary in their labels, so check the actual object placement shown by their examples rather than treating a broad label as a complete rule.\n\nFormality belongs to individual expressions and contexts. Carry out an investigation and account for an increase are entirely at home in formal writing, although put off may be less formal than postpone. A single-word substitute must preserve meaning: give up a habit, give up a seat and give up hope do not all invite the same replacement. Longer noun objects often favour position after a particle because it is easier to process the verb sequence before a heavy phrase. Personal-pronoun placement remains the useful elementary diagnostic for many separable particle verbs, but idiomatic restrictions still require lexical checking.\n\nThe cumulative tasks revisit past narrative sequence, perfect/progressive meanings, future reference, agreement and degree from Days 7–11. Separate first-encounter recognition from an immediate retry: seeing the same item again can measure recall of that item without demonstrating transfer to a new sentence. For each error, name the underlying distinction before choosing a review lesson. A perfect-aspect error in has been waiting since noon should link to aspect and temporal reference, not merely be counted as a generic preposition mistake. Open-response explanations and short corrections supply production evidence through self-review; a displayed model is an aid to analysis, not an independent automated grade.\n\nseparable verb + noun + particle / verb + particle + noun: Both positions occur with an ordinary noun object where the lexical pattern permits.\n\nseparable verb + personal pronoun + particle: Ordinary placement: turn it off, not turn off it.\n\nPlease turn the reading lamp off.\nThe noun object precedes the separable particle.\n\nPlease turn it off before leaving.\nThe personal pronoun appears between verb and particle.\n\nWe could not put up with the noise from the generator.\nPut up with is phrasal-prepositional; the complement follows with.\n\nSome phrasal verbs are intransitive: the generator broke down.\n\nA formally acceptable multi-word verb need not be replaced by a single word merely to look academic.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=12&section=gv5-d12-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Phrasal verbs and multi-word verbs","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/phrasal-verbs-and-multi-word-verbs"},{"publisher":"Prepositions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositions_2"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d13-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Heads and the central phrase classes","text":"Heads and the central phrase classes\n\nA phrase is a grammatical unit organised around a head, the element that determines its category and much of its distribution. A phrase may consist of one word or contain several nested units.\n\nIn a familiar noun phrase such as the narrow bridge, bridge is the head. The and narrow depend on that noun, while the complete noun phrase can be used as subject in The narrow bridge needs repairs. The head does not mean the first word or the longest word. It names the organising element. The five phrase classes developed here are noun phrase, verb phrase, adjective phrase, adverb phrase and prepositional phrase. Find the head first, then ask what other material belongs with it. A one-word expression can still occupy a phrase position: in They laughed, they is a noun phrase headed by a pronoun in this course's analysis.\n\nA noun phrase is headed by a noun or pronoun; an adjective phrase by an adjective; an adverb phrase by an adverb; and a prepositional phrase by a preposition. Extremely proud of her class is an adjective phrase headed by proud. Very carefully is an adverb phrase headed by carefully. Beside the noticeboard is a prepositional phrase headed by beside, containing the noun phrase the noticeboard as complement. Those labels describe form. A prepositional phrase can function as a modifier inside a noun phrase or as an adjunct in a clause, without changing its phrase category.\n\nFor verb phrases, state the convention being used. The narrow pedagogical convention groups a lexical verb with its auxiliaries: may have been waiting. It excludes an object or location phrase from that particular verb group. A broader syntactic convention includes the lexical verb and its complements inside a VP: read the proposal. Both conventions occur in reputable grammars. This course uses narrow verb group when describing auxiliary order and labels a broader VP explicitly when it includes complements. Do not decide that an analysis is wrong merely because its author has chosen the other stated convention.\n\nA phrase has an external function and internal relations. The entire adjective phrase very proud of her work can be a subject complement, while of her work is an internal complement of proud and very is a degree modifier. A category label does not encode all those functions. Likewise, noun phrases are not restricted to subjects or objects: the following day can function as a time adjunct. A prepositional phrase can also supply a predicative complement, as in the keys are under the mat. These distributions show why adverbial is a function term in this course rather than a universal synonym for adverb phrase.\n\nnoun phrase: determiner + modifiers + noun head + post-head material: Optional dependents combine around a noun head.\n\nnarrow verb group: modal + perfect have + progressive be + passive be + lexical verb: Only the selected auxiliary meanings occur; each determines the next verb form.\n\nThose three unusually steep steps need a handrail.\nSteps heads the subject noun phrase; unusually steep is an embedded adjective phrase.\n\nThe technician may have been waiting outside.\nMay have been waiting is the narrow verb group; outside gives additional location information.\n\nThe child seemed surprisingly confident about the interview.\nSurprisingly confident about the interview is an adjective phrase functioning as subject complement.\n\nNarrow and broad VP conventions differ; a labelled analysis must make its convention clear.\n\nA noun phrase may contain only a pronoun, as in She arrived, and an adjective phrase may contain only its adjective head.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=13&section=gv5-d13-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Heads","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/heads"},{"publisher":"Verb phrases","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verb-phrases"},{"publisher":"Adjective phrases: functions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adjective-phrases-functions"},{"publisher":"Adverb phrases","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adverb-phrases"},{"publisher":"Prepositional phrases","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositional-phrases"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d13-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Determiners, modifiers and selected complements","text":"Determiners, modifiers and selected complements\n\nA dependent is material associated with a phrase's head. Determiners help establish reference or quantity; modifiers add description; complements are licensed by the head's particular grammatical pattern.\n\nLook inside a noun phrase before treating every word before its verb as one undifferentiated subject. In these two detailed reports on drainage, these is a determiner, two gives a number, detailed modifies reports, and reports is the noun head. On drainage follows the head and gives its topic. A premodifier occurs before a head; a postmodifier occurs after it. These position labels do not name word classes. A noun can premodify another noun, as in drainage report, while an adjective phrase can appear after a noun, as in the reports available today.\n\nA complement is related to a head's lexical pattern, not simply anything standing after it. Proud commonly licenses of her achievement, depend licenses on the grant, and a decision can have an infinitival complement such as to postpone the launch. A modifier adds a description such as the report on the top shelf. Context and grammatical framework can affect the boundary between noun complements and modifiers. For teaching, ask whether the following expression specifies something selected by the head's meaning, such as the content of a claim, or adds an independent property, such as the place of a printed copy.\n\nComplements are not invariably obligatory. She has already eaten omits the food, although eat also licenses an object in she ate a sandwich. Conversely, the ordinary placing sense of put requires a location or destination expression: she put the tray on the table, rather than she put the tray with no recoverable continuation. Adjunct omission is often a useful first check, but optionality alone is not a complete definition of complement. The same phrase can receive different analyses after different heads: in the library is an optional place adjunct after read but supplies the required location after put the book.\n\nStructure constrains placement. An adjective with a substantial complement commonly follows its noun: students eager to volunteer. Packing the whole sequence before the noun as eager to volunteer students is not the neutral attributive pattern. Modification can also be layered: the very small office near the stairs contains a degree modifier inside the adjective phrase and a location modifier after the noun. A genitive such as the editor's draft can fill a determining role; the apostrophe does not make editor's an adjective by itself. Keep the analyses of grammatical form, internal function and position distinct, especially when comparing school terminology with a more detailed grammar.\n\npremodifier + head + postmodifier: Position is described relative to the head being modified.\n\nhead + licensed complement: The head’s lexical pattern determines which kinds of complement are possible.\n\nA detailed report on coastal erosion arrived yesterday.\nReport heads the noun phrase; detailed precedes it and on coastal erosion specifies its topic.\n\nThe students eager to volunteer met after class.\nThe complete adjective phrase eager to volunteer follows students.\n\nShe placed the signed form inside the envelope.\nThe location completes the placing pattern; its importance comes from the verb’s valency, not from being last.\n\nA licensed complement can be omitted in a suitable lexical pattern: she has eaten is complete.\n\nComplement and modifier distinctions may be gradient or framework-dependent, especially inside noun phrases; explain the evidence rather than pretending position settles the issue.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=13&section=gv5-d13-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Heads","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/heads"},{"publisher":"Complements - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/complements"},{"publisher":"Noun phrases: order","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/noun-phrases-order"},{"publisher":"Adjective phrases: functions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adjective-phrases-functions"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d13-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Embedding, coordination and structural hierarchy","text":"Embedding, coordination and structural hierarchy\n\nA hierarchical analysis groups words into units within larger units. Embedding places one unit inside another; coordination links units at a comparable structural level.\n\nIn the cover of the book, the whole expression is a noun phrase headed by cover. Inside it, of the book is a prepositional phrase, and the book is a smaller noun phrase within that phrase. This is embedding. It does not mean that all three units perform the same job. The larger noun phrase may be the subject of a clause, while the smaller one is complement of of. Brackets help you preserve these levels: [NP the cover [PP of [NP the book]]]. Read the structure from the outside inward, then check that every bracket has a matching closing bracket.\n\nCoordination links expressions using and, or and related coordinators. The carefully revised draft and the original report coordinates two noun phrases. A phrase can contain coordination internally: the red and blue folders coordinates two adjective modifiers, while the red folders and the blue folders coordinates full noun phrases. These are different structures even if a suitable context lets them refer to the same collection. Shared material can be understood rather than repeated. The editor reviewed and corrected the draft coordinates predicates sharing a subject and object; merely counting occurrences of and does not tell you how many main clauses there are.\n\nHierarchy explains attachment ambiguity. The researcher photographed the student with the telescope may describe an instrument used for photographing or identify a student carrying a telescope. One analysis attaches with the telescope to the event; another places it inside the object noun phrase. Both readings follow from available structural relations, not from a defective word ending. If the intended meaning matters, revise explicitly: using a telescope, the researcher photographed the student; or the researcher photographed the student who was carrying a telescope. An interpretation should be supported by context rather than imposed because it was the first one you imagined.\n\nCoordination often links expressions of the same category, but matching category labels is not an absolute condition in every construction. She is a doctor and very experienced can coordinate predicates with different internal categories because both characterise the same subject. Such examples require care with shared grammatical functions. Coordination tests are therefore evidence for constituency, not a proof that any two strings joined by and are interchangeable phrases. In a complete parse, state the relation between each unit and its parent. That approach prevents the common error of treating a noun embedded in an of-phrase as the head governing agreement for the whole subject.\n\n[NP determiner + head [PP preposition [NP complement]]]: A larger noun phrase can contain a prepositional phrase with its own noun phrase.\n\n[NP ...] and [NP ...]: Coordination can join full noun phrases at the same structural level.\n\nThe manager of the two clinics has resigned.\nManager heads the complete subject; clinics is embedded as complement of of.\n\nThe red and green indicators were flashing.\nRed and green coordinate within a larger noun phrase headed by indicators.\n\nThe lawyer interviewed the witness with an interpreter.\nThe final phrase can describe assistance during the interview; context is needed to exclude other attachments.\n\nDifferent categories can sometimes coordinate when their functions are compatible; identical word-class labels are not an unrestricted universal requirement.\n\nA repeated subject or object may be understood through sharing or ellipsis; analyse the chosen construction before counting clauses.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=13&section=gv5-d13-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Heads","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/heads"},{"publisher":"Complements - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/complements"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d13-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Constituency diagnostics and practical parsing","text":"Constituency diagnostics and practical parsing\n\nA constituent is a unit within a grammatical structure. Diagnostics such as substitution, movement and answering a focused question can provide evidence about units, but no one diagnostic is decisive in every context.\n\nStart a parse by finding the finite verb group and the major clause elements, then analyse the phrases inside those elements. In the new assistant filed three invoices, the new assistant is the subject noun phrase and three invoices is the object noun phrase. Replacing the subject with she gives she filed three invoices. This substitution supports the grouping of those subject words. It does not mean that the original phrase was a pronoun or that the replacement has preserved every detail. A diagnostic changes the sentence in order to test one structural possibility.\n\nUse more than one check. Ask who filed three invoices; the new assistant forms a natural answer. In the right context, fronting a unit may also be possible: three invoices, the assistant filed before lunch. This marked order supplies evidence for the object grouping, but it is less neutral than the original and may need a contrastive context. Coordination can give further evidence, as in the new assistant and the office manager filed the invoices. A failure under one test can result from meaning, information structure or register rather than proving that the tested string is not a constituent.\n\nBracket only what the analysis supports. In a very experienced teacher from Multan, very modifies experienced within an adjective phrase, while from Multan postmodifies teacher in one natural reading. Bracketing [very experienced teacher] as a single adjective phrase misidentifies teacher, the noun head. Likewise, a group of adjacent words may cross a boundary: in she sent the student a note, student a is not a natural constituent merely because the words are neighbours. Avoid constructing a parse solely from translation or a question such as what, which can elicit several kinds of answer.\n\nSubstitution tests have restrictions. One can replace some noun-phrase material but does not reproduce every nominal pattern, and it may refer to a different kind of entity from a longer expression. A movement test can change scope or require a special discourse setting. Coordination also has exceptional patterns and shared dependents. Record a tentative analysis when evidence conflicts, then compare a reputable grammar using the same framework. A practical final analysis should name each relevant category, its head and its function in the next larger unit. That is more informative than assigning a label to every individual word while leaving the sentence's larger organisation unexplained.\n\nidentify unit → test substitution → check context → compare another diagnostic: A sequence of evidence is stronger than a single mechanical trick.\n\n[clause [subject NP] [verb group] [object NP] [optional adjunct]]: A readable first-pass parse can show major relations before every internal detail is added.\n\nThe revised handbook arrived. → It arrived.\nPronoun substitution supports treating the revised handbook as a subject noun phrase.\n\nShe spoke very quietly. → How did she speak? Very quietly.\nThe manner answer supports the adverb-phrase grouping; quietly is its head.\n\nWe stored the material in the room beside the lift.\nAnalyse the room beside the lift as an embedded noun phrase within the in-phrase, rather than treating all location words as one unstructured list.\n\nA meaningful answer fragment can identify a phrase in conversation without being a complete formal sentence.\n\nFailure under movement, substitution or coordination alone does not settle every constituency question; contextual and lexical restrictions matter.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=13&section=gv5-d13-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Heads","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/heads"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Complements - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/complements"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d14-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Main, subordinate, finite and non-finite clauses","text":"Main, subordinate, finite and non-finite clauses\n\nA clause is a grammatical unit organised around a verb. Main clauses supply a principal message; subordinate clauses function within a larger structure. Finite and non-finite distinguish verb forms and clause construction, not whether an event is real.\n\nThe train stopped is a main declarative clause. In the train stopped because a signal failed, because a signal failed is a subordinate reason clause associated with the main event. The first clause can form an independent neutral statement; the because-clause normally relies on a larger construction in formal connected prose. A clause is not simply any group of words that happens to contain a noun and a verb spelling. Identify the grammatical organisation of the group and its relation to the larger sentence. A phrase can contain a clause, as in the promise that she would return.\n\nA finite clause contains a finite verb or auxiliary, as in she returns, she returned and she may return. The modal may is finite even though it does not display a person contrast such as returns versus return. A non-finite clause is headed by an infinitival or participial form without independent tense inflection: to return soon, returning home, or signed yesterday in the noun phrase the documents signed yesterday. A non-finite clause can still express time relative to context. In she hoped to return next week, next week gives time reference, while to return remains non-finite.\n\nNon-finite clauses often leave their subject understood: she hopes to return normally makes her the intended returner. Some have an expressed subject: for the visitors to arrive early would be convenient. The clause for the visitors to arrive early serves as the subject of would be convenient, despite lacking independent tense. Finite clauses may also function as subjects, objects or other complements. Whether the grant will continue remains unclear has a finite interrogative clause as its subject. The whole sentence is declarative; the embedded subject clause does not use direct-question inversion.\n\nThe main/subordinate distinction is not identical to the finite/non-finite distinction. Many subordinate clauses are finite, while other subordinate clauses are non-finite. Imperatives deserve separate attention because their ordinary main verb is a plain form and their subject is usually unexpressed: take your seat. Different grammatical frameworks discuss their finiteness differently; this course identifies the imperative construction rather than inferring that take your seat is an ungrammatical fragment. Supplementary non-finite clauses such as weather permitting have their own internal structure. The practical question is how the clause is licensed and interpreted in the complete construction, not whether it mechanically contains a written subject plus an overt tense ending.\n\nmain clause + because + finite subordinate clause: The subordinate clause supplies a reason for the principal message.\n\nfor + expressed subject + to-infinitive clause: A non-finite clause can contain its own expressed subject.\n\nThe meeting ended because the chair was ill.\nEnded and was are finite; the because-clause is subordinate.\n\nTo postpone the decision would be unwise.\nThe to-infinitival clause functions as subject of the main predicate.\n\nWhether the figures have changed remains uncertain.\nA finite embedded question functions as the subject; the complete sentence is declarative.\n\nA grammatical imperative normally has an unexpressed subject: please wait is not a fragment simply because you is absent.\n\nA non-finite clause may have an explicit subject, as in for the guests to leave, and may contain temporal expressions without acquiring independent tense.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=14&section=gv5-d14-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d14-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Clause forms and communicative purposes","text":"Clause forms and communicative purposes\n\nClause form describes grammatical construction, such as declarative or interrogative. Communicative function describes what a speaker uses it to do, such as state, ask, request or exclaim; the two do not correspond one to one.\n\nA declarative clause commonly places a subject before its verb: the workshop begins at ten. An interrogative clause commonly asks a question: does the workshop begin at ten? An imperative commonly directs action: begin the workshop at ten. An exclamative commonly expresses a reaction to a striking degree or quality: how quickly the workshop ended! These are useful central patterns, not interchangeable labels for punctuation marks. A full stop does not turn a question-shaped clause into a declarative, and an exclamation mark alone does not make a structure an exclamative clause.\n\nA question tag is a short question attached to another clause: The drawer is locked, isn’t it? For an ordinary confirmation tag, identify the statement’s auxiliary or modal and the subject it refers to. Use a subject pronoun in the tag and normally reverse polarity: a positive statement takes a negative tag; a negative statement takes a positive tag. With no auxiliary in a positive simple present or past statement, use do, does or did: Sara checks the receipts, doesn’t she? This is a useful starting pattern, not a rule for every conversational tag.\n\nInterrogatives include yes-no and wh-patterns. Does Amina teach here? is a yes-no interrogative. Where does Amina teach? asks for a place. Who teaches here? asks about the subject and does not need do-support or an extra subject after the verb. An embedded question normally has different order: I wonder where Amina teaches. The complete clause I wonder ... is declarative, although one part is an interrogative complement. Pay attention to the boundary at which each form label applies rather than treating a sentence containing any wh-word as one direct question.\n\nIn The gate has not opened, has it?, the tag repeats auxiliary has and refers to the gate as it. Negative words also matter: Nobody has signed yet, have they? treats nobody as negative and uses a positive tag. Nobody takes singular has in the statement, while the referring singular they takes have in the tag. Never similarly supports a positive confirmation tag: You never park here, do you? Two conventional special cases are I’m next, aren’t I? and Let’s check again, shall we? The uncontracted am I not? is possible in a formal style. After an imperative, will you? is common, but can you?, could you? and won’t you? can express different interpersonal attitudes. Do not apply statement polarity mechanically to commands.\n\nForm and purpose can diverge. Could you close the window? is interrogative in form but often functions as a polite request. You will submit the form today can function as an instruction, prediction or reminder depending on situation and intonation. Imperatives can offer advice, invitations or permissions as well as commands: have another biscuit. A declarative with rising intonation may seek confirmation in conversation, but the absence of inversion remains part of its grammatical form. Describe the form and the likely use separately instead of replacing grammatical analysis with a guess about the speaker's attitude.\n\nOrdinary opposite-polarity tags often invite confirmation. Falling intonation commonly signals greater confidence; rising intonation commonly leaves more room for a genuine check. Neither tone mechanically determines a speaker’s intention. A positive statement can also have a positive tag: You locked it, did you? may register newly received information, surprise or a challenge. Context and delivery decide among these readings. Thus didn’t you? and did you? can both be grammatical after You locked it, but they are not automatically interchangeable. Informal right? and yeah? can remain unchanged across different subjects; they belong to conversational usage and do not follow the auxiliary agreement pattern.\n\nThe central what- and how-exclamative patterns have their own order: what an unusual design this is; how carefully she explained it. A question can also express surprise: wasn't that remarkable? It remains interrogative in construction even when it performs an exclamatory function. In formal writing, punctuation should reflect the complete utterance and its intended force. An indirect question embedded in a statement usually ends with a full stop, while can you tell me where the office is? ends with a question mark because the complete construction is a direct interrogative. These distinctions become essential in reporting speech and in distinguishing an embedded question from a faulty inversion.\n\nAn echo question can repeat a heard statement or leave the questioned expression in its original position: You put the parcel where? This can request repetition or express surprise; it is not a failed attempt at neutral Where did you put the parcel? Negative yes-no questions also carry expectations: Isn’t the library open? often checks a belief that it is open; Won’t you sit down? can invite. The full not normally follows the subject in Is the library not open?, which sounds more formal than Isn’t the library open? When answering a negative question, add an explicit clause to avoid misunderstanding: Yes, it is open; No, it isn’t open. Form, interaction, register and interpretation must all be considered before correcting conversational word order.\n\nauxiliary + subject + lexical verb: A central yes-no interrogative pattern: does she agree?\n\nwhat + noun phrase + subject + verb / how + adjective or adverb + subject + verb: Central exclamative patterns; their word order differs from a neutral direct question.\n\nstatement + auxiliary/modal + subject pronoun?: An ordinary confirmation tag normally reverses statement polarity and preserves subject reference.\n\ndeclarative order + wh-expression in the questioned position: One conversational echo-question pattern: You sent it to whom? It is not the default neutral information question.\n\nThe door is open.\nA declarative commonly used as a statement, but it could also hint that someone should close the door.\n\nWho authorised the payment?\nA subject wh-question uses authorised directly without adding did.\n\nCould you tell me where the archive is?\nThe complete sentence is interrogative, while the embedded question where the archive is has no inversion.\n\nThe spare key is in the drawer, isn’t it?\nPositive is takes negative isn’t; it refers to the key.\n\nNobody has signed yet, have they?\nNegative nobody supports a positive tag; singular they takes have, although nobody takes has.\n\nLet’s compare the two estimates, shall we?\nThe conventional shall we tag seeks agreement to an inclusive suggestion.\n\nYou stored the files where?\nA conversational echo question keeps where in place to check or react to the location just mentioned.\n\nA subject wh-question such as who left? does not use the same auxiliary-subject inversion as who did she meet?\n\nCommunicative force depends on context: a polite interrogative can request action without being an imperative in grammatical form.\n\nOpposite polarity is the ordinary confirmation pattern, not a ban on positive-positive tags. Imperative tags have their own conventional choices.\n\nNobody can take singular has in the main clause and referring singular they with have in a tag; agreement is local to each clause.\n\nEcho questions and invariant conversational tags should not be judged solely by the pattern for neutral inverted yes-no questions.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=14&section=gv5-d14-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clause types","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clause-types"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"Question tags — Cambridge English Grammar Today","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/question-tags"},{"publisher":"Question tags — British Council LearnEnglish","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/b1-b2/question-tags"},{"publisher":"Questions: echo and checking questions — Cambridge English Grammar Today","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/questions-echo-and-checking-questions"},{"publisher":"Questions: yes-no questions — Cambridge English Grammar Today","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/questions-yes-no-questions-are-you-feeling-cold"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d14-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Simple, coordinated and subordinate architecture","text":"Simple, coordinated and subordinate architecture\n\nSentence architecture describes how clauses combine. In the finite-clause classroom classification used here, a simple sentence has one main finite clause, a compound sentence coordinates main clauses, and a complex sentence includes finite subordination.\n\nA simple sentence can still be long: the patient researcher in the upstairs office checked all the entries carefully. Its length does not create another main clause. A compound sentence joins main clauses, as in the researcher checked the entries, and her colleague verified the totals. A complex sentence includes a subordinate finite clause: the researcher checked the entries because several totals looked unusual. A compound-complex sentence has at least two coordinated main finite clauses and at least one subordinate finite clause. These labels offer a useful first map, but they do not replace a detailed analysis of every embedded unit.\n\nState the counting convention when non-finite clauses are present. For the classroom labels in this lesson, the researcher hopes to finish today has one main finite clause and an infinitival complement. We describe that full structure rather than pretending that to finish today contains no clause. Some grammatical accounts use complex more broadly to include non-finite subordination. A different count based on an explicit convention need not be an error. What matters for accurate editing is that you recognise the complement relationship and the understood subject, even if a school exercise uses a narrower label.\n\nCoordination can occur below clause level. Amina opened and checked the package coordinates predicates sharing a subject and object; it does not automatically display two separately expressed main clauses. In Amina opened the package and Bilal checked the contents, the two subjects help reveal full clause coordination. Subordinate clauses can fill selected complement positions: she believes that the claim is mistaken. Others serve as adjuncts expressing reason, condition or time: she left when the discussion ended. A clause modifying a noun, such as the claim that the witness repeated, has a different internal relation from the content clause in the claim that the witness was mistaken.\n\nSubordination establishes grammatical dependency without determining every logical inference. Although the road was wet, the driver continued presents a concession, but grammar alone does not tell us whether continuing was wise. Coordination with and can suggest sequence, addition or other relations from context. Avoid assuming that any two grammatical clauses can be joined without changing the message. When transforming a complex sentence into two independent sentences, preserve the reason, condition or qualification through explicit wording if it is important. Similarly, an embedded complement can contain further subordination: she said that she would leave if the meeting ran late. A layered parse shows the conditional clause inside the reported content rather than attaching it automatically to said.\n\nmain finite clause + coordinator + main finite clause: Full main-clause coordination under the classroom convention.\n\nmain clause containing [content clause containing [adjunct clause]]: Subordination can be nested within another subordinate clause.\n\nThe technician opened and cleaned the scanner.\nThe sentence coordinates predicates with shared arguments; and does not by itself prove two full main clauses.\n\nThe technician opened the scanner, and the assistant photographed its interior.\nTwo separately expressed main clauses form compound architecture.\n\nThe technician said that the scanner would work if the sensor was replaced.\nThe reported content includes a conditional clause; brackets can make that embedding explicit.\n\nSome descriptions count non-finite subordination when calling a sentence complex; use the specified convention and still identify the non-finite clause.\n\nA sentence with several coordinated verbs is not automatically a compound sentence with several fully independent clauses.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=14&section=gv5-d14-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Clause types","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clause-types"},{"publisher":"Complements - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/complements"},{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d14-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Clause boundaries and accessible visual parsing","text":"Clause boundaries and accessible visual parsing\n\nA sentence-boundary error occurs when written clauses are left without the relationship or punctuation their intended construction needs. A bracketed parse can reveal missing main clauses, fused clauses and comma splices.\n\nFor ordinary formal paragraphs, a subordinate reason clause alone usually leaves the sentence unfinished: because the road was blocked. Adding a main clause completes the intended relationship: because the road was blocked, the delivery arrived late. A fragment may instead lack a main predicate, as in the report beside the printer. That noun phrase is perfectly useful as a label or answer fragment, but it is not a full neutral declarative sentence about what the report did or was. Context decides whether fragmentary wording is a defect or a deliberate feature of a heading, conversation or literary style.\n\nTwo independent clauses placed together without a suitable boundary form a fused sentence: the lights failed the audience waited. Joining them only with a comma creates a comma splice: the lights failed, the audience waited. Some teaching uses run-on as a broad label for both; this course names the specific error whenever possible. Repairs include a full stop, a semicolon, a coordinator with appropriate punctuation, or subordination that preserves the intended relation. A colon can also join a complete first clause to a second clause that explains or specifies it. Do not use a connector merely because it produces grammatical punctuation; its meaning must fit.\n\nA linking adverb does not behave like a coordinating conjunction. The lights failed; however, the audience waited is a grammatical contrast between independent clauses. However does not itself license joining those clauses with a comma alone. Commas around supplementary clauses and introductory material follow additional conventions developed fully in Day 24. A comma before and between short closely connected main clauses can be a style choice; the essential point here is not to separate a subject from its predicate or create a boundary that contradicts the intended structure.\n\nUse textual brackets as a visual parsing method that is also readable by assistive technology. In [MAIN the courier waited [SUBORDINATE because the office was closed]], the inner clause supplies a reason within the main construction. More detailed brackets can identify [SUBJECT NP the courier] and [VERB GROUP waited]. A parse is a representation of an analysis, not a substitute for explaining it. If a sentence seems awkward, locate the finite clauses, identify any non-finite complements, label the connectors and then inspect the boundaries. This sequence avoids a common editing error: fixing a perceived comma problem while leaving the actual missing main clause or mistaken logical relationship untouched.\n\n[MAIN ... [SUBORDINATE because ...]]: A readable nesting shows that the reason clause is dependent in the whole construction.\n\nindependent clause ; however, independent clause: A semicolon or full stop supplies the boundary; however signals a logical contrast.\n\nThe path was flooded. We took the bridge.\nTwo independent sentences have an explicit full-stop boundary.\n\nThe path was flooded, so we took the bridge.\nSo coordinates two clauses while expressing the resulting action.\n\n[MAIN We took the bridge [SUBORDINATE because the path was flooded]].\nThe bracketed parse shows the reason clause as subordinate to the main message.\n\nFragments are normal in headings and many conversational answers; the requirement for full sentences concerns the specified writing context.\n\nRun-on has broad and narrow teaching uses. Naming fused sentence or comma splice avoids a terminology-based false disagreement.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=14&section=gv5-d14-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d15-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Defining and supplementary relative information","text":"Defining and supplementary relative information\n\nA relative clause connects to an antecedent through a grammatical relation within the clause. Integrated or defining relatives help identify or delimit a referent; supplementary or non-defining relatives add a separate associated comment.\n\nIn the students who arrived early found seats, who arrived early helps identify the relevant students. The relative clause is integrated into the noun phrase, so ordinary written presentation does not enclose it in commas. In the students, who arrived early, found seats, the relative is a supplementary comment about the already identified students. The punctuation signals a different organisation of information, not simply a longer pause. Whether the speaker has one brother or several, or one class or several, cannot be inferred reliably from a noun phrase in isolation without its discourse context.\n\nWho commonly relates to people; which commonly relates to things and can also introduce a comment about a whole preceding proposition. Whose marks a genitive relation and can refer to people or other entities: the school whose roof collapsed. Whom is an object form favoured in formal styles, especially after a fronted preposition. Object who is ordinary standard usage in many contexts, as in the colleague who we met. That introduces integrated relatives about people or things in established usage; it does not introduce the ordinary comma-marked supplementary pattern in edited standard English.\n\nRestrictive which is grammatical in British English: the route which passes the lake. Many American editorial guides prefer that for such integrated relatives, but that preference is not a universal grammatical ban on which. A relative preposition can remain at the end of a clause or move before an appropriate relative pronoun: the proposal which we agreed on; the proposal on which we agreed. That does not normally follow a fronted preposition in this construction. A whole-clause supplementary relative uses which: the train was cancelled, which delayed the meeting. Here which refers to the cancellation situation, not simply to the noun train.\n\nRelative pronouns have a function inside their clause. In the student who wrote the letter, who is subject of wrote. In the student whom the tutor praised, whom is object of praised while the tutor is the expressed subject. Avoid choosing case by the pronoun's distance from the main verb. Traditional accounts call relative that a pronoun; many modern analyses treat it as a subordinator with an associated gap. This course accepts either terminology when it is applied consistently and the missing subject or object relation is identified. Also distinguish supplement from a claim that information is unimportant: a non-defining relative can contain information central to the writer's purpose.\n\nnoun + integrated relative clause: The relative contributes to identifying or delimiting the noun’s referent.\n\nidentified noun phrase, supplementary relative, main predicate: The comma pair marks a separate associated comment.\n\nThe visitors who had booked online entered first.\nThe integrated relative restricts the relevant set to online bookers.\n\nDr Malik, whose research concerns wetlands, joined the panel.\nWhose links Dr Malik to the research in a supplementary relative.\n\nThe server stopped during registration, which prevented several applicants from finishing.\nWhich refers to the preceding event or situation as a whole.\n\nRestrictive which is established British usage; an American house-style preference for that should be labelled as style.\n\nWhose can have a non-human antecedent: a building whose entrance faces the square.\n\nObject who is common standard usage; whom remains useful in formal constructions and after fronted prepositions.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=15&section=gv5-d15-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Relative clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/relative-clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Who, whom - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/who-whom"},{"publisher":"Relative clauses referring to a whole sentence","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/relative-clauses-referring-to-a-whole-sentence"},{"publisher":"Prepositions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositions_2"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d15-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Contact clauses and non-finite relatives","text":"Contact clauses and non-finite relatives\n\nA contact relative omits an overt relative marker in an integrated construction where omission is licensed. Non-finite relatives use infinitival or participial structure rather than a full finite relative clause.\n\nIn the article that I recommended, that corresponds to the object of recommended. Because the relative is integrated and already contains its subject I, the marker can be omitted: the article I recommended. This is often called a contact or zero relative. It is not simply any relative clause with a word deleted. In the article that changed my view, that corresponds to the subject of changed. Omitting it would remove the subject relation needed by the intended ordinary finite relative, producing the article changed my view as a main-clause pattern rather than the same modifying structure.\n\nA contact relative can leave a preposition stranded: the issue we spoke about. It does not license deleting the pronoun from an ordinary supplementary relative: the editor, whom I met yesterday, has replied. Relative adverbs also allow several patterns. The place where we met, the place at which we met and the place we met can each be grammatical with the appropriate sense. The day we met is a familiar integrated temporal relative. Learn the whole construction instead of assuming that every zero relative contains the same kind of missing object.\n\nParticipial relatives include the people waiting outside and the papers received yesterday. Waiting outside gives a non-finite active description; received yesterday normally gives a passive interpretation. Expanding the first as who are waiting and the second as which were received can clarify their relationships, but a reduced construction is not always produced by mechanically deleting who and be. A finite relative may locate a completed event or express modality that a participial version does not preserve. The author who wrote the letter need not mean the same as the author writing the letter. Preserve the intended time and relation when choosing a shorter structure.\n\nInfinitival relatives also have distinct patterns. The next person to speak supplies a subject interpretation for the understood speaker, while a book to read has an understood reader and an object relation to book. For Noor to read can identify that reader explicitly in a book for Noor to read. Expressions traditionally called reduced relatives overlap with participial adjective and postmodifier analyses. A retired teacher contains an attributive participial adjective in a common analysis; it need not be explained by deleting a visible relative-clause sequence. Use the non-finite form because its grammar and meaning fit the context, not because every finite relative is assumed to have one equivalent shorter form.\n\nantecedent + subject + finite verb + object gap: An integrated object relative may omit its overt marker: the book I borrowed.\n\nnoun + -ing / past-participial / infinitival clause: Non-finite postmodification has different voice, subject and temporal interpretations.\n\nThe memo we circulated yesterday contains a correction.\nThe integrated object relative has an expressed subject we and no overt marker.\n\nThe passengers waiting by the exit boarded first.\nThe -ing clause identifies passengers engaged in waiting at the relevant time.\n\nThe first applicant to finish the task will receive feedback today.\nThe infinitival relative associates the applicant with the subject role of finish.\n\nAn ordinary integrated finite subject relative normally retains its marker: the student who arrived, rather than omitting who within the same construction.\n\nContact relatives are not the ordinary pattern for comma-marked supplementary information.\n\nParticipial and infinitival relatives do not preserve every tense, aspect or modal distinction of a possible finite version.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=15&section=gv5-d15-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Relative clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/relative-clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"},{"publisher":"Who, whom - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/who-whom"},{"publisher":"Prepositions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositions_2"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d15-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Content, nominal and embedded interrogative structures","text":"Content, nominal and embedded interrogative structures\n\nA content clause expresses a proposition, question or other content associated with a selecting word. Traditional nominal-clause labels describe some external functions; they do not mean that the internal clause has become a noun word.\n\nIn she believes that the timetable is wrong, the that-clause supplies what she believes. It is a content complement of believes. Similar clauses can follow nouns and adjectives: the claim that the timetable is wrong; certain that the timetable is wrong. That introduces the clause but does not serve as its subject or object. Compare the claim that she repeated: here the relative clause has an object relation missing after repeated, linked to claim. In the claim that the timetable is wrong, the subordinate clause is internally complete and states the content of the claim.\n\nEmbedded questions express a question within another construction: I wonder whether the hall is open; she asked why the hall had closed. They normally use subject-before-verb order rather than direct-question inversion. Subject questions remain recognisable: I know who sent the message has who as subject of sent. Whether and if can introduce many embedded yes-no questions, but they are not interchangeable in every construction. Before a to-infinitive, use whether: she cannot decide whether to apply. The question clause whether the hall is open can also function as a subject; for formal clarity, whether is the normal choice in that position.\n\nWh-expressions have more than one analysis. I wonder what she wrote presents an embedded question about the identity of her writing. I kept what she wrote can mean I kept the thing that she wrote; this is a fused-relative expression, with what combining an antecedent-like meaning and a relative relation. Many modern accounts analyse the whole fused relative as a noun phrase containing a relative clause; traditional accounts often group it with nominal clauses. State the intended interpretation and grammatical function instead of assuming that every what-clause is an indirect question. Context can also leave a genuine ambiguity between question-like content and reference to a thing.\n\nInfinitival complements express selected relationships too. She hopes to finish normally makes she the understood finisher; she wants the assistant to finish expresses the subordinate subject. An extraposed pattern moves a heavy subject clause into a later position: that the hall is open is useful becomes it is useful that the hall is open. The it in that construction does not refer to a previously named hall; it fills the ordinary subject position. Not all verbs license the same complement types or allow the same alternations. Compare suggest that she apply or suggest applying with tell her to apply. Advanced transformation therefore begins with the selecting word's pattern, the subordinate subject and the original degree of certainty or obligation.\n\nverb/adjective/noun + that + finite content clause: The subordinate clause gives associated content; introductory that is not its subject or object.\n\nwhether + to-infinitive: An embedded alternative can be expressed non-finitely: whether to leave.\n\nThe report confirms that the water is safe.\nThe internally complete that-clause states what the report confirms.\n\nI cannot remember where I stored the invoice.\nThe embedded question keeps subject I before stored.\n\nWhat the witnesses described was difficult to verify.\nA natural reading treats what the witnesses described as the things or account described, functioning as the sentence subject.\n\nThat is often omitted after some reporting and mental-process verbs, but omission depends on the construction and clarity; it is not a compulsory deletion rule.\n\nWhether is used before a to-infinitive; if to apply does not form the corresponding standard embedded-question pattern.\n\nA what-expression may be an embedded question or a fused relative, depending on interpretation and the selecting construction.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=15&section=gv5-d15-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Complements - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/complements"},{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"Verb patterns: verb + infinitive or verb + -ing?","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verb-patterns-verb-infinitive-or-verb-ing"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d15-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Adverbial clauses and semantic relationships","text":"Adverbial clauses and semantic relationships\n\nAn adverbial or adjunct clause adds a circumstance or logical relationship to another situation, including time, reason, purpose, result, condition, concession, manner or comparison.\n\nChoose an adjunct clause by the relation you intend. We left when the rain stopped locates an event in time. We left because the room was closing gives a reason. We left early so that we could catch the train expresses purpose. The room was so crowded that we could not enter expresses a result associated with the degree of crowding. These constructions do different jobs even when all describe connected events. A cause reported by the writer is not automatically a proven scientific cause; grammatical linkage expresses the relationship being asserted in that particular account.\n\nTime clauses can use when, while, before, after, until and once. Their tense choices depend on reference and meaning rather than a universal requirement to repeat the main clause's tense. In the office will open when the repairs are complete, present form refers to future time in the subordinate clause. Reason can be expressed with because, since or as, but since and as also have other senses. Make a relation explicit if ambiguity matters. Because of the cancellation is a prepositional phrase with a noun-phrase complement in the traditional account; because the service was cancelled is a finite reason clause.\n\nConcession presents a circumstance against which the main event is unexpected: although the route was longer, we chose it. Condition sets a situation relevant to the main proposition: if the permit arrives, we can begin. Unless often expresses a negative condition, but replacing every if-not construction mechanically can change scope or emphasis. Manner and comparison can use as, as if, as though and than in their appropriate patterns: arrange the folders as the instructions show; she spoke as if she knew the answer; the task took longer than we expected. The hypothetical interpretation of as if is developed further in Day 18.\n\nA clause's function should not be inferred from its opening word alone. As the bell rang can express timing, while as the hall was already full may be understood as a reason in context. So that can express an intended purpose or an actual consequence, depending on context, modality and the rest of the wording. Non-finite purpose clauses such as to avoid a delay normally share an understood subject with the main clause in the relevant construction; for a different participant, name that subject or choose a finite clause. Some modern grammars classify many traditional subordinating conjunctions as prepositions taking clause complements. This terminology difference does not remove the temporal, causal or conditional relation that learners must interpret and preserve in writing.\n\nmain clause + so that + subject + can/could + verb: A common finite purpose pattern; context determines whether it describes an intention.\n\nso + adjective/adverb + that + result clause: A result construction associates degree with a consequent situation.\n\nWe paused while the technician tested the microphone.\nThe while-clause locates the pause during a simultaneous activity.\n\nThe adviser repeated the address so that the visitors could write it down.\nThe finite purpose clause has its own subject the visitors, distinct from the adviser.\n\nAlthough the evidence was limited, the report reached a firm conclusion.\nThe concession presents a potential obstacle to the main action without asserting that the conclusion was justified.\n\nSince and as have more than one meaning; context, not spelling alone, identifies a time, cause or manner relation.\n\nA purpose clause with an understood subject must connect to the intended participant: to inspect the roof, the engineer climbed the ladder.\n\nDifferent grammatical frameworks classify clause-introducing words differently; the pedagogical function labels remain useful if the framework is stated.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=15&section=gv5-d15-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"},{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"Prepositions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositions_2"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d16-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Coordination and subordination: what is connected","text":"Coordination and subordination: what is connected\n\nCoordination places units alongside one another at the same structural level; subordination makes one clause dependent within a larger structure.\n\nStart by finding the units being joined. In tea and coffee, and joins two nouns. In unusually calm and very patient, it joins two adjective phrases. In The alarm sounded and the staff left, it joins clauses. A clause normally contains a predicate; the second clause may repeat a subject or share material with the first. Subordination is different: because the alarm sounded gives a reason inside a larger sentence. Neither arrangement is inherently more sophisticated; choose the structure that represents the intended relation.\n\nCoordinators include and, or and but; traditional school lists also include for, nor, so and yet. Their distributions are not identical. And can combine many kinds of unit, while causal for is mainly a clause connector in relatively formal writing. A subordinator such as although or if introduces a dependent clause. A sentence may contain both: Although the alarm sounded, the staff waited and the manager checked the corridor. First identify the outer concession, then the coordination inside the main part.\n\nShared material avoids unnecessary repetition: The librarian checked and stamped the forms shares a subject and an object across coordinated predicates. Repetition is sometimes useful for emphasis or disambiguation. Coordination does not demand that every item have the same spelling or length. Even different phrase categories can perform compatible functions: The room was quiet and without windows coordinates an adjective phrase and a prepositional phrase as predicative descriptions. The editing question is whether each item fits the surrounding construction and the intended meaning.\n\nGrammatical frameworks differ over the membership of the conjunction category and the analysis of clause-linking so or temporal after. The course uses familiar school labels while keeping category separate from function. Brackets reveal hierarchy: [Mina checked the register] and [Omar locked the door] has two coordinated clauses; Mina [checked the register and locked the door] has coordinated predicates. These descriptions explain structure without requiring a claim that every coordinated unit is an independent sentence. A repeated subject, a finite verb and the wider context help decide the analysis.\n\nX and Y: Both units contribute at the same structural level.\n\nalthough + clause, main clause: The main assertion holds despite a contrasting circumstance.\n\nThe workshop supplies paper and ink.\nTwo noun heads form the direct-object coordination.\n\nThe workshop was inexpensive but exceptionally useful.\nBut coordinates two predicative adjective phrases.\n\nAlthough the hall was small, the speakers arrived early and the audience stayed.\nOne subordinate concession precedes two coordinated main clauses.\n\nUnlike categories can coordinate when their functions are compatible: calm and in control are both predicative descriptions. Surface uniformity is a useful style preference, not an exceptionless syntax law.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=16&section=gv5-d16-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"},{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d16-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Correlative patterns, agreement and balanced alternatives","text":"Correlative patterns, agreement and balanced alternatives\n\nCorrelative expressions distribute a relationship across two positions, such as both … and or either … or; their placement identifies what is being paired.\n\nBoth the receptionist and the technician attended includes two people. Either the receptionist or the technician will attend presents alternatives. Neither attended excludes both in that context. Not only adds emphasis to an inclusion: The visit was not only useful but also inexpensive. Place the first marker before the first intended unit, then make sure that the second unit can complete the same surrounding construction. Read the whole meaning before editing, because moving either or not only can change which alternatives are being offered.\n\nA subject coordinated with and normally takes plural agreement: Both the map and the timetable are useful. A single package or conventional combination can instead receive singular notional agreement: Bread and butter is all that is available. With either … or and neither … nor, mixed singular and plural subjects create variation and possible awkwardness. Agreement with the nearer subject is a common editorial solution: Either the assistant or the supervisors are attending. Recasting often gives a clearer result than treating proximity as a universal linguistic law.\n\nFronted not only normally triggers subject–auxiliary inversion when it introduces a clause: Not only did the team finish early, but it also documented the process. Within a predicate no such inversion is needed: The team not only finished early but also documented the process. Also is optional in some not only … but constructions. Correlative markers do not make every imperfectly symmetrical sentence ungrammatical; what matters first is whether the coordinated units fit the same syntactic position and whether the contrast is intelligible.\n\nNegation and scope require careful reading. Neither the manager nor the deputy signed says that each failed to sign; Not both signed excludes joint success but allows one signature. Either in an ordinary choice often suggests one alternative rather than both, but inclusive or is possible where context allows it. An instruction should say whether multiple selections are permitted. In formal editing, preserve these logical differences rather than replacing all negative or alternative expressions mechanically. Grammatical agreement, logical inclusion and stylistic balance are related questions that should be diagnosed separately.\n\nboth X and Y: Includes both coordinated alternatives.\n\nnot both X and Y: Denies their joint inclusion; it does not necessarily exclude each separately.\n\nBoth the labels and the seals are missing.\nPlural coordination takes are.\n\nEither the supervisor or the two assistants are available.\nThe nearer plural encourages are; recasting avoids mixed-number awkwardness.\n\nNot only has the archive expanded, but its catalogue has also become searchable.\nFronting licenses has before the subject; the second clause states an additional improvement.\n\nSome conventional and-coordinations describe one unit and take singular agreement. If mixed-number or-coordination sounds strained, use two clauses rather than force a contested agreement choice.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=16&section=gv5-d16-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"},{"publisher":"Not only … but also","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/not-only-but-also"},{"publisher":"Subject–verb agreement - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subject-verb-agreement"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d16-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Logic: addition, contrast, concession and qualification","text":"Logic: addition, contrast, concession and qualification\n\nA logical connector signals the relationship a writer intends between propositions; grammatical form alone does not prove that the relationship is factually justified.\n\nAddition presents another relevant point: The room is accessible; moreover, it has hearing assistance. Contrast presents a difference: The morning group worked online, whereas the evening group met in person. Concession goes further: Although the room was small, everyone found a seat presents the second fact as holding against an expectation raised by the first. Qualification limits a claim: The method is useful, provided that the samples are representative. Read the underlying claims first, then select a relationship that the evidence supports.\n\nBecause introduces a reason; so can introduce a result; so that commonly introduces purpose or result depending on context. We moved the display so that visitors could see it states an intended goal. We moved it, so visitors could see it more directly presents the resulting consequence. Before, after and then organise sequence without necessarily asserting causation. Or offers an alternative, while otherwise can introduce the consequence of rejecting an option: Submit the form today; otherwise, processing will be delayed. Each relationship needs enough context to distinguish it from plausible alternatives.\n\nUnless normally means if not in an exceptive condition, but replacing it mechanically can disturb scope. Do not enter unless authorised means authorisation is necessary for entry; it does not promise that every authorised person will enter. While can mark time or contrast; since can mark time or reason. A reader may need a less ambiguous connector when both readings are plausible. Moreover adds information but does not itself turn it into proof. Therefore presents an inference whose validity must be assessed independently of the grammar.\n\nDiscourse relations can be implicit: The road flooded. The delivery was late leaves a causal connection available without explicitly asserting it. Choosing because commits the writer more strongly to that explanation. Likewise a contrast is not automatically a concession; two different timetables need not violate anyone's expectation. Advanced revision should ask whose expectation a concessive clause invokes, what a qualification excludes and whether an apparent inference reverses cause and effect. These questions connect grammar to pragmatic interpretation while preserving a distinction between a well-formed sentence and a sound argument.\n\nbecause + reason clause: Presents the reason for the associated assertion.\n\nso that + finite clause: Often states an intended purpose; context can support a result reading.\n\nWe chose the ground floor because the lift was unavailable.\nThe subordinate clause supplies the stated reason.\n\nThe lift was unavailable; nevertheless, all visitors reached the exhibition.\nNevertheless marks a result contrary to a likely expectation.\n\nThe trial is promising, although its small sample limits the conclusion.\nThe concession qualifies the positive assessment rather than cancelling it.\n\nA connector can have several senses: since Monday marks a starting point, whereas since nobody objected can introduce a reason. Analyse the complement and context together.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=16&section=gv5-d16-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d16-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Connector classes and punctuation in use","text":"Connector classes and punctuation in use\n\nA conjunction links syntactic units; a linking adverb or discourse marker signals a relationship without necessarily supplying the grammatical clause boundary.\n\nCompare Although it rained, the match continued with Despite the rain, the match continued. Although introduces a finite clause; despite takes a noun phrase here. However behaves differently: It rained; however, the match continued. In this formal written pattern the semicolon separates the independent clauses, while however signals concession. A full stop is equally possible. Do not decide punctuation merely by where you pause aloud. First decide whether there are two independent clauses, a main clause plus dependent material, or a phrase within one clause.\n\nThe neutral pattern for two independent clauses joined by a coordinator is often a comma before and, but or so. Very short clauses may omit it, and complex clauses may justify stronger separation under an editorial style. An introductory subordinate clause usually ends with a comma. A following reason clause often needs no comma, but punctuation may distinguish supplementary reasoning or remove ambiguity. However can move inside a clause: The match, however, continued. Because cannot move in the same way while retaining the same clause structure.\n\nA word's traditional class can differ between constructions. Before lunch is prepositional; before we ate is traditionally subordinating. Contemporary grammars may analyse before as a preposition in both, with different complement types. This is a difference in framework, not an error in the sentence. Notwithstanding also has a less common formal conjunction use with a finite clause, alongside its prepositional use. Do not reject a construction simply because a beginner table lists only its most frequent pattern. Check the actual lexical entry and the intended register.\n\nDiscourse markers such as well, anyway and in fact organise conversation or an argument as well as local logical relations. Their presence cannot repair a run-on: The queue grew however the gate stayed shut still needs punctuation in edited prose. Conversely a sentence beginning with And or But is not automatically ungrammatical; its appropriateness depends on continuity and editorial style. A reliable diagnosis identifies the missing boundary, the connector's role and any genuine semantic inconsistency. It does not count the number of linking words and infer a fault without examining their scope.\n\nindependent clause; however, independent clause: Two assertions plus an explicit contrast.\n\nbecause of + noun phrase: A reason expressed through a prepositional phrase.\n\nThe bus was late because of a diversion.\nBecause of takes the noun phrase a diversion.\n\nThe bus was late because the road was closed.\nBecause introduces a finite reason clause.\n\nThe report was short. It was, however, fully documented.\nA sentence boundary separates the assertions; paired commas mark medial however.\n\nSome punctuation choices depend on style and clause length. Sentence-initial And/But and formal conjunctive notwithstanding are legitimate; do not label them universally incorrect.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=16&section=gv5-d16-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"},{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d17-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Core modals, marginal forms and substitutes","text":"Core modals, marginal forms and substitutes\n\nCore modal auxiliaries are a small class including can, could, may, might, will, would, shall, should and must; related expressions share some, but not all, of their grammatical behaviour.\n\nA core modal comes before the base form of the next verb: can swim, might leave, must be. It has no third-person singular -s: She can swim, not she cans swim. Form a negative directly with not and a question by moving the modal before the subject: She cannot attend; Can she attend? Ordinary do-support is unnecessary. The next verb can itself be an auxiliary: may have arrived, should be working. These simple structural facts apply even when the whole verb phrase expresses a complicated time relationship.\n\nCore modals lack ordinary infinitival and participial forms. To express ability after want, use want to be able to, not want to can. For a past obligation, had to often supplies a form that must lacks. Ought to contains to, while need and dare have both modal-like and lexical patterns. Compare Need we leave? with Do we need to leave? The former is relatively formal and more characteristic of British usage. Names such as semi-modal and marginal modal vary between grammars; learn the actual pattern rather than assuming all related expressions behave identically.\n\nHave to, be able to, be supposed to and be allowed to use ordinary auxiliary and tense machinery: Does she have to leave? She has been able to work. They can follow a core modal: She may have to leave. Two core modals do not normally appear together in the edited international standard taught here; use might be able to rather than might can. Some regional English varieties permit multiple modals. Describing that variation does not make the form appropriate for every written context, nor does the course default make the regional construction meaningless.\n\nModal form and temporal meaning are not a simple present/past table. Could can describe earlier ability, present tentative possibility or a polite request; would can describe reported future reference, willingness or a hypothetical result. A core modal may stand alone when its complement is recoverable: Can she attend? She can. This is ellipsis, not an incomplete sentence. When analysing a modal chain, identify the finite modal first, then each following auxiliary and its required verb form. This prevents incorrect forms such as should has gone or must to leave without making an inaccurate claim that every word following a modal is a lexical infinitive.\n\nmodal + base form: The base form can be a lexical verb or an auxiliary.\n\nmodal + subject + base form?: Canonical interrogative order without do-support.\n\nNida can swim.\nCan has no -s with the singular subject.\n\nDoes Nida have to swim today?\nHave to uses do-support here; it is not a core modal.\n\nNida might have been swimming when the call arrived.\nMight precedes perfect have, participial been and progressive swimming.\n\nRelated expressions need individual patterns: ought to includes to, but core should takes a base form. Recoverable complements may be omitted: Yes, she can.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=17&section=gv5-d17-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"},{"publisher":"Modality: forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-forms"},{"publisher":"Need","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/need"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d17-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Ability, permission, necessity and inference","text":"Ability, permission, necessity and inference\n\nModal meaning includes judgments about what is possible or likely and attitudes towards what people are able, permitted, required or willing to do.\n\nUse can for present ability in a suitable context: Farah can read the script. Can or may can grant permission, with may often more formal. Must can impose a strong obligation; must not can prohibit. Do not confuse prohibition with absence of obligation: You must not enter excludes entry; You do not have to enter permits a choice. Should commonly gives advice or expresses an expected outcome. The same words can serve different purposes, so a label should explain the sentence in context rather than become a permanent dictionary tag.\n\nCould often describes general past ability: At ten, she could swim well. For a specific successful event, managed to or was able to is often clearer: Despite the current, she managed to reach the bank. However could occurs naturally in many negative, perception and mental-activity contexts: I could hear the bell. Will can express prediction, willingness, insistence or a promise. Would commonly softens a request: Would you close the door? The interrogative asks about willingness on its surface but usually functions as a request, not a test of physical ability.\n\nDeductive must presents a conclusion drawn from evidence: The lights are on; someone must be inside. Deductive cannot rejects a possibility: She cannot be inside; the room was sealed before she arrived. This is different from cannot as prohibition or inability. May not is particularly scope-sensitive: The office may not open means opening is uncertain, whereas You may not enter in an official notice normally denies permission. Should can express expectation as well as advice: The parcel should arrive today. A speaker's confidence is affected by evidence and the type of statement, so a universal numerical ranking is misleading.\n\nNecessity and possibility are interpreted relative to assumptions, rules, abilities or evidence. In deontic necessity, the relevant background includes regulations; in epistemic necessity, it includes what the speaker believes the evidence establishes. Neither use automatically guarantees compliance or factual truth. The student must attend does not assert attendance, and The student must be attending is a strong inference that may prove mistaken. Advanced interpretation therefore separates the modal's type from the truth of its complement. A precise explanation says what the speaker commits to and what remains open, rather than replacing a modal with an unconditional assertion.\n\nmust not + verb: Commonly prohibits the action.\n\ndo not have to + verb: Expresses lack of necessity; it does not by itself prohibit.\n\nYou may use the side entrance.\nIn this permission context, may authorises entry.\n\nThe courier should arrive before noon.\nShould states an expectation rather than an obligation imposed on the speaker.\n\nThe laboratory must be empty: its lights are off and the staff have left.\nMust presents an evidence-based inference, not an order to empty the room.\n\nCould is not excluded from every specific past event: I could hear the warning is natural. Distinguish the meaning and lexical context before replacing it.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=17&section=gv5-d17-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"},{"publisher":"Modality: forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-forms"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d17-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Modal perfects: evidence, regret and unnecessary actions","text":"Modal perfects: evidence, regret and unnecessary actions\n\nA modal-perfect construction combines a modal with have and a past participle, often locating a situation before the current or another reference point while retaining the modal meaning.\n\nBegin with the form: modal + have + past participle. Must have left draws an inference about a departure; might have left leaves it possible; should have left often evaluates an earlier obligation or sensible action. Have stays in its base form even after a singular subject: she might have gone. The lexical participle must be correct: could have written, not could have wrote. These forms are not all reports of completed facts. Modal meaning determines whether an event is inferred, merely possible, desired or counterfactual.\n\nShe must have missed the train presents a strong inference. She might have missed it does not assert that she did. She should have checked the timetable often criticises an omission, but should have can also express expectation: The train should have arrived by now, although I have not checked. Could have describes an unrealised opportunity in one context and a possible explanation in another. I could have called, but chose not to is different from That noise could have been the printer. Always identify the time and the intended modal reading before paraphrasing.\n\nNeed not have + participle normally says an action happened but was unnecessary: I need not have printed the agenda; everyone had a digital copy. Did not need to states lack of necessity; it does not logically settle whether the action happened. We did not need to print it, but we did is coherent. In everyday conversation, did not need to often suggests that the action was omitted, but that inference can be cancelled. A reliable assessment must supply the event facts instead of treating a common implication as a universal rule.\n\nThe perfect can locate an event before a future reference point: By Friday, the laboratory must have completed the audit can be a requirement with a deadline, rather than a past deduction. Negation also changes modal scope: cannot have left denies the possibility of an earlier departure; might not have left presents the possibility of non-departure. Should not have sent the file usually evaluates sending negatively and commonly implies it happened, but expectation contexts can alter the interpretation. Avoid deriving truth or falsity solely from the modal-perfect shape; test the interpretation against the surrounding discourse.\n\nmust have + participle: Commonly an inference about an earlier situation; context can supply a deadline requirement.\n\nneed not have + participle: An action is treated as having occurred unnecessarily.\n\nThe gate is locked; the caretaker must have left.\nAn inference concerns an earlier departure.\n\nWe need not have paid for delivery; collection was free.\nThe payment is presented as having happened unnecessarily.\n\nBy the next inspection, the contractor must have repaired the roof.\nThe perfect locates required completion before a future deadline.\n\nDid not need to often suggests non-performance, but the suggestion is defeasible. Should have may express expectation rather than criticism; do not mark those readings as errors.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=17&section=gv5-d17-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"},{"publisher":"Need","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/need"},{"publisher":"Modality: forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-forms"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d17-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Epistemic and deontic meaning, hedging and commitment","text":"Epistemic and deontic meaning, hedging and commitment\n\nEpistemic modality concerns the status of a proposition as possible, probable or necessary given evidence; deontic modality concerns permission, requirements and related norms.\n\nCompare The visitor must be in the lobby with The visitor must sign the register. The first is usually an inference; the second is usually a requirement. These labels help explain why the same auxiliary has different paraphrases. Academic and professional writing often needs a calibrated degree of commitment. The results suggest that the treatment may help makes a limited claim. The treatment helps states the conclusion more directly. A hedge is useful when it reflects uncertainty or a restricted evidential basis, not when it merely makes a sentence longer.\n\nHedging can use modals, adverbs and lexical verbs: may, might, possibly, probably, appear and suggest. These expressions interact with context rather than assign fixed percentages. Probably is stronger than possibly in ordinary neutral use, but a whole sentence's force also depends on its evidence and qualifications. The proposal may reduce delays in small offices limits both certainty and domain. Removing in small offices broadens the claim even if may stays. In revision, track the population, time, conditions and strength of the original assertion as well as its verb form.\n\nOver-hedging can obscure a well-supported statement: It may perhaps possibly be the case that the meter is faulty stacks similar reservations without specifying the uncertainty. Prefer The meter may be faulty; a second test is needed. Conversely a legal requirement should not be weakened accidentally: Staff must report hazards is not equivalent to Staff might report hazards. Politeness may soften how a request is expressed without changing the speaker's practical expectation. Would you please send the invoice? still requests action, although its form is an interrogative about willingness.\n\nScope distinguishes a possibility of a negative proposition from a denial of possibility: It may not be safe leaves open that it is unsafe; It cannot be safe presents safety as excluded by the evidence. An epistemic statement can still be mistaken, and a deontic rule can still be broken. Transparent analysis should therefore state the modal base in plain language: given the available evidence, under the rules, or with the person's present abilities. These are explanatory paraphrases, not rigid word-by-word translations. The goal is to preserve a writer's justified commitment and a reader's ability to see what supports it.\n\nmay + base-form verb phrase: The modal can express possibility and takes semantic scope over the proposition.\n\nmust + base-form verb phrase: The modal can express a requirement or inference, according to context.\n\nThe device may fail at very low temperatures.\nA possibility is limited to a stated condition.\n\nOperators must disconnect the power before cleaning.\nThe modal expresses a procedural obligation.\n\nThe small trial suggests a benefit, but larger studies are needed before a general recommendation.\nThe claim's scope and evidence are explicitly qualified rather than ornamented.\n\nFormal hedging need not use a modal: The evidence is limited is a direct, precise statement of a limitation. A hedge must not silently weaken a quoted obligation.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=17&section=gv5-d17-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"},{"publisher":"Hedges ( just )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/hedges-just"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d18-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Conditional meaning: patterns, reality and time","text":"Conditional meaning: patterns, reality and time\n\nA conditional construction presents one situation as a condition under which another claim, action or conclusion applies. Its verb forms express both time relationships and the speaker’s way of presenting the possibility.\n\nStart with the intended relationship. If you heat this wax, it melts describes a recurring result under the relevant conditions; this is often called the zero conditional. If the repair is finished tomorrow, we will reopen presents an open future condition, commonly called a first conditional. Present is normally used for future time in that conditional clause. If the repair were cheaper, we would proceed presents a more remote alternative. Past-form were here indicates distance from the actual situation, not a repair that happened yesterday.\n\nThe second-conditional teaching pattern commonly combines a past form in the if-clause with would, could or might in the result. The third commonly combines past perfect with would/could/might have plus a participle: If we had kept a copy, we could have restored the file. It imagines a different earlier situation. Mixed patterns connect different times: If we had kept a copy, we would have the file now. The condition is earlier; the result concerns the present. Do not add perfect have mechanically to both clauses if the intended result is current.\n\nThese numbered categories are useful memory aids, not a complete grammar. Real past habits can use past forms in both clauses: If the roads flooded, we stayed home. Open conditions can contain a progressive, perfect or modal: If you have finished, send the form; If you can attend, call us. A condition may concern the appropriateness of a speech act: If you are hungry, there is soup in the kitchen offers useful information; hunger does not cause the soup to exist. Without your notes, I would have missed the detail expresses an implied condition without if.\n\nAn open condition leaves its fulfilment unsettled in the discourse. A remote condition presents distance, often hypothetical or counterfactual, but remoteness is not a precise probability percentage. Context establishes whether an imagined alternative contradicts known facts. Will and would can occur legitimately in an if-clause when expressing willingness, insistence, politeness or a later consequence: If you will wait here, someone will help you. This differs from mechanically inserting future will in a neutral time condition. State the intended reading before judging a form; an assessment must supply enough context to distinguish a routine future condition from a modal exception.\n\nif + present, will/can/may + base: A common open future pattern, with the result selected for meaning.\n\nif + past, would/could/might + base: A common remote present/future pattern.\n\nif + past perfect, would/could/might have + participle: A common imagined alternative past.\n\nif + past perfect, would + base + now: An earlier condition with a present hypothetical result.\n\nIf the sensor detects smoke, the alarm sounds.\nThe present forms describe a designed recurring relationship, not a universal scientific law.\n\nIf we had booked a room, we would not be searching for one now.\nThe past-perfect condition connects to a present result.\n\nIf you will sign the register, I can issue a visitor’s pass.\nWill can solicit willingness; it is not an automatic error inside if.\n\nA main clause can be imperative: If you notice damage, report it. A condition can be implied rather than introduced by if: With a stronger signal, we could communicate.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=18&section=gv5-d18-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Conditionals: if - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conditionals-if"},{"publisher":"Conditionals: other expressions ( unless, should, as long as )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conditionals-other-expressions-unless-should-as-long-as"},{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d18-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Wishes, regrets, preferences and apparent situations","text":"Wishes, regrets, preferences and apparent situations\n\nWish and related constructions can express a desired difference from reality, a regret about an earlier event, a preference or an impression; each construction has its own complement pattern.\n\nA present regret commonly uses wish plus a past form: I wish I knew the address means that I do not know it now. A past regret normally uses past perfect: I wish I had checked the address means that I did not check it, and now regret that omission. If only can express the same kind of wish with greater emphasis: If only we had checked. These forms locate an imagined difference from reality; past form after wish does not automatically locate the desired situation in past time. For an open desired future outcome, hope is normally appropriate: I hope the parcel arrives tomorrow.\n\nWish has other patterns. I wish to speak to the manager is a formal expression of wanting; I wish you success expresses a good wish with two objects. Wish plus would often concerns a desired change in behaviour or circumstances: I wish the neighbours would lower the volume. It is not an unrestricted replacement for any state after wish. Would rather takes a base form with the same understood subject: I would rather wait. With a different subject, a past clause is common for a present or future preference: I would rather you waited outside. A past-perfect clause can express an earlier preference: I would rather you had told me yesterday.\n\nIt is time to leave is an ordinary infinitival expression. It is time we left also refers to the present or near future, despite the past form; it can imply that the action is overdue. As if and as though introduce an apparent or imagined comparison. He looks as if he is tired leaves tiredness plausible; He behaves as if he owned the office presents a distancing comparison. The construction does not always assert that its situation is false. Perfect reference is possible: She looks as if she has been running. Reduced forms also occur: She raised a hand as if to object.\n\nIrrealis were is available with be in remote comparisons and wishes, especially in formal prose: I wish I were there; she spoke as though she were the owner. Was is also established in many less formal standard contexts with first- or third-person singular subjects, so it should not be labelled universally ungrammatical. The deliberate choice of were can make the remote interpretation explicit. Subject identity matters in would-rather constructions; changing I would rather leave to I would rather you left changes whose action is preferred. Interpretation should therefore track the holder of the preference, the intended actor and the time of the preferred event separately.\n\nwish + past form: Typically a desired difference in a present situation.\n\nwish + past perfect: Typically regret about an earlier situation.\n\nwould rather + base / would rather + subject + past clause: Same-subject and different-subject preference patterns.\n\nI wish I knew how to repair this lock.\nKnew expresses a current desired difference from not knowing.\n\nI would rather you had spoken to me before sending it.\nThe speaker prefers a different earlier action by another person.\n\nThe cyclist looks as though she has been riding in the rain.\nThe comparison is consistent with an actual recent activity; it need not be false.\n\nWish to means formally want to, not regret: We wish to renew the lease. As if can take a non-finite clause or a prepositional phrase: as if to protest; as though in pain.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=18&section=gv5-d18-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Wish","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/wish"},{"publisher":"Would rather, would sooner","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/would-rather-would-sooner"},{"publisher":"It’s time","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/it-s-time"},{"publisher":"As if and as though","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/as-if-and-as-though"},{"publisher":"Conditionals: if - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conditionals-if"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d18-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Formal conditional inversion and conditional links","text":"Formal conditional inversion and conditional links\n\nConditional inversion places should, had or irrealis were before the subject in specified formal conditional patterns, normally replacing an if-clause without requiring a question meaning.\n\nThe common starting point is an if-clause: If you should need assistance, contact reception. In formal wording, omit if and place should before the subject: Should you need assistance, contact reception. This is a conditional statement with an imperative result, not an enquiry about whether assistance is needed. The form often presents a possible contingency politely or impersonally. It can be useful in notices and correspondence, but ordinary if is equally grammatical and often simpler. Inversion is therefore a resource for a particular register, not a compulsory sign of advanced writing.\n\nFor an alternative past, Had I known the date, I would have attended corresponds to If I had known the date. Had is the auxiliary of the past perfect, and known remains a participle. For a remote present state, Were I free, I would join you corresponds to If I were free. Were followed by a subject and to-infinitive can present a remote future contingency: Were the supplier to withdraw, we would seek another. These patterns are limited: an ordinary lexical past verb cannot usually be moved in the same way. Wrote she earlier is not the normal conditional equivalent of If she wrote earlier.\n\nNegative conditional inversion usually places not after the subject: Had we not checked the figures, the error would have survived. Contractions such as Hadn’t we checked can have other, interrogative uses and should not be substituted mechanically in formal conditional prose. Keep the result clause's modal and time reference unchanged when converting: Had she called, we might have helped retains possibility rather than certainty. A transformation from might have helped to would have helped strengthens the claim. The grammatical operation changes word order and removes if; it does not license extra factual commitments.\n\nOther conditional links have their own meanings. Unless commonly means except if: We will proceed unless the regulator objects sets an exception to proceeding. Only if states a necessary condition: We will proceed only if approval is granted does not itself promise that approval guarantees proceeding. Provided that, on condition that and as long as establish conditions or limits with register differences. In case often motivates a precaution rather than stating the condition under which an action will occur: Take a copy in case the network fails instructs taking it beforehand. Replacing all these links with if can alter the logical relationship. Analyse the condition's role before attempting an equivalent inversion or paraphrase.\n\nshould + subject + base form: Formal possible contingency, replacing if + subject + should.\n\nhad + subject + past participle: Formal earlier hypothetical condition.\n\nwere + subject + complement / to-infinitive: Formal remote state or future contingency.\n\nShould the lights fail, use the marked exit.\nShould introduces a contingency; use supplies an imperative result.\n\nHad the clerk not kept a copy, we might have lost the record.\nNegation follows the subject, and might preserves a possible result.\n\nWere the lease to expire next month, the company would need alternative premises.\nWere to presents a remote future supposition.\n\nInversion applies to the licensed auxiliaries, not to every conditional verb. Retaining if as well as the inverted pattern is not the intended conversion: use If I had known or Had I known.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=18&section=gv5-d18-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Conditionals: other expressions ( unless, should, as long as )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conditionals-other-expressions-unless-should-as-long-as"},{"publisher":"Conditionals: if - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conditionals-if"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d18-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Mood, mandative meaning and cumulative analysis","text":"Mood, mandative meaning and cumulative analysis\n\nGrammatical mood concerns conventional verb forms and clause patterns associated with statements, directives or non-actual situations; it is distinct from a speaker’s emotion and from the communicative function of a sentence.\n\nTraditional teaching distinguishes indicative, imperative and subjunctive constructions. Most ordinary factual statements use indicative forms: The meeting starts at nine. An imperative typically uses a plain verb form with an understood addressee: Start at nine; do not enter. A sentence's grammatical form does not uniquely determine its social purpose. Can you wait? has interrogative form but usually functions as a request. Conversely an imperative can express an invitation or good wish rather than a harsh order: Have a seat; enjoy your visit. Analyse grammatical construction and practical purpose as separate questions.\n\nA mandative clause presents something as proposed, demanded or required. Formal examples include The policy requires that every visitor sign in and The adviser recommended that the hearing be postponed. Sign and be are plain forms, often called the mandative subjunctive. They do not acquire third-person -s or an ordinary tense ending in this pattern. British English also widely uses should: The adviser recommended that the hearing should be postponed. The plain form is especially characteristic of American formal usage but also standard in British English. These are established alternatives; changing should be to be is not a necessary correction.\n\nThe preceding verb does not automatically create mandative meaning. She insisted that she was innocent asserts a proposition; she insisted that the inspector be present demands a situation. Suggest can similarly indicate evidence, as in The marks suggest that the box was moved, rather than recommend an action. Formulaic subjunctives survive in expressions such as Long live the king and Be that as it may. Irrealis were is sometimes included under a broad traditional subjunctive label, while modern grammars may analyse it separately. The terminology varies; the form, meaning and distribution should remain clear.\n\nFor cumulative analysis, ask five connected questions: what are the clause boundaries; what functions do the clauses perform; which time is intended; how strong is the modal claim; and does the wording fit the register? A conditional can contain an embedded content clause, a relative and a mandative requirement: If the policy that we adopted requires that each visitor sign in, the receptionist should explain the rule. Parsing those units prevents an agreement rule from being applied across an unrelated clause boundary. Use the phase assessment to revisit Days 13–18, and treat uncertain written responses as material for explicit self-review rather than an automatically awarded mastery score.\n\nrequire/recommend + that + subject + plain form: A formal mandative subjunctive pattern.\n\nrequire/recommend + that + subject + should + base: A standard mandative alternative, particularly familiar in British usage.\n\nThe contract requires that the tenant pay on the first day.\nPay is plain form in a mandative clause, despite singular tenant.\n\nThe consultant recommended that the data should be checked independently.\nShould be is a legitimate British mandative construction.\n\nThe witness insisted that she had seen the vehicle.\nInsisted introduces an asserted report here, so an indicative perfect clause is appropriate.\n\nA verb such as insist or suggest has more than one meaning and complement pattern. A that-clause after it is not automatically mandative. Modern descriptions may separate irrealis were from the plain-form subjunctive.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=18&section=gv5-d18-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Verb patterns: verb + that -clause","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verb-patterns-verb-that-clause"},{"publisher":"Conditionals: if - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conditionals-if"},{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"},{"publisher":"insist verb","url":"https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/definition/english/insist"},{"publisher":"subjunctive adjective","url":"https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/definition/english/subjunctive_1"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d19-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Building the passive verb phrase","text":"Building the passive verb phrase\n\nA canonical be-passive uses a form of be plus a lexical past participle and normally presents as subject an element corresponding to an object in a related active clause.\n\nCompare The technician repairs the scanner with The scanner is repaired by the technician. The scanner is the object in the active and the subject in the passive. Be supplies the finite present form, and repaired is a participle. The subject of a passive is therefore not necessarily the performer of an action. Form a negative or a question through the first auxiliary: The scanner is not repaired here; Is the scanner repaired here? A by-phrase can identify the agent, but it is often omitted when unknown, obvious or unimportant.\n\nPreserve tense and aspect through the auxiliary chain. Active is repairing corresponds to passive is being repaired; was repairing corresponds to was being repaired. Has repaired corresponds to has been repaired; had repaired to had been repaired. A modal comes before base be: can be repaired. A modal perfect uses have been: must have been repaired. Future perfect reference can use will have been repaired. To-infinitives can likewise be passive: to be repaired; a perfect passive infinitive is to have been repaired. The lexical participle remains repaired through these changes; the preceding auxiliaries carry the grammatical distinctions.\n\nThe sequence has been being repaired is grammatically available for a perfect-progressive passive, but it is uncommon and often cumbersome. In ordinary writing, an active clause such as technicians have been repairing it, or a suitably contextual recast, is often easier to process. Avoid calling a difficult but possible combination impossible. Conversely, do not lose duration by replacing has been being repaired for a month with was repaired a month ago: the latter presents a different time relationship. Choose a recast that preserves what is known about continuation or completion.\n\nNot every sequence of be plus a participial-looking word has a uniquely verbal passive analysis. The door was closed at six can report the closing event or describe the resulting state, depending on context. A clear agent phrase or an event-focused progressive can support a verbal reading, while a contrast of states can support an adjectival reading. Individual diagnostics have limits because words can belong to more than one construction. In analysis, describe the reading actually licensed by the discourse rather than assigning a label from the surface ending alone. In transformation, check the original verb's complements before selecting a new subject.\n\nfinite be + past participle: A basic passive; be carries tense and agreement.\n\nhave + been + past participle: A perfect passive.\n\nmodal + be / modal + have been + past participle: Simple or perfect passive under a modal.\n\nto be / to have been + past participle: Passive infinitival patterns.\n\nThe labels are checked before dispatch.\nPresent passive describes the checking procedure; labels is the grammatical subject.\n\nThe labels were being checked when the power failed.\nPast progressive passive presents checking as in progress at the interruption.\n\nThe labels must have been checked before dispatch.\nThe modal-perfect passive can express a deduction in an evidential context; context determines the modal reading.\n\nPerfect-progressive passives exist but are uncommon: the bridge has been being inspected. Prefer a clear recast without pretending that the form is universally ungrammatical. Be plus participle can also describe a state.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=19&section=gv5-d19-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Passive: forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/passive-forms"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"},{"publisher":"Perfect infinitive with to ( to have worked )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/perfect-infinitive-with-to-to-have-worked"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d19-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Agents, reporting, get-passives and two-object verbs","text":"Agents, reporting, get-passives and two-object verbs\n\nPassive constructions can background an agent, report a claim or promote different objects, while get-passives often present a dynamic event in a more conversational register.\n\nAn agentless passive is useful when the affected item is the continuing topic: The scanner arrived yesterday. It was installed this morning. Omitting an unknown agent avoids inventing one. It can also obscure responsibility, so the omission should serve the reader's purpose. A known, important agent may belong in a by-phrase: The decision was overturned by the appeal panel. Active and passive versions organise information differently; neither voice is a universal rule for professional writing. Some passive clauses describe states rather than obvious deliberate actions, so agent is not always the best semantic label for every by-phrase.\n\nReporting passives include It is believed that the bridge is safe and The bridge is believed to be safe. The first uses impersonal it and a content clause; the second places bridge first with an infinitival complement. For an event earlier than the reporting belief, use a perfect infinitive where appropriate: The bridge is believed to have opened in 1980. The construction attributes a belief; it does not guarantee the proposition is true. Keep the source if it matters: The bridge is believed by local historians to have opened in 1980 is more informative than an entirely unattributed report.\n\nWith suitable two-object verbs, either recipient or transferred item can become passive subject: The charity gave the school a printer; The school was given a printer; A printer was given to the school. The alternatives are not unlimited for every verb. Explain takes the pattern explain something to someone in the intended sense; The rule was explained to her follows that pattern, whereas She was explained the rule is not the ordinary standard counterpart. Learn the lexical pattern, not just an abstract two-object formula. Different dialects and particular verbs also differ in their preferred passive alternatives.\n\nA get-passive uses get plus a participle: The parcel got damaged. It is especially common in conversation and typically foregrounds an event or the affected participant. It is not confined to bad outcomes: She got promoted reports a desirable event in many contexts. Get does not by itself prove that the subject caused or deserved the outcome. Adding a reflexive, as in He got himself locked out, can suggest responsibility, but context still matters. A state such as The answer is known does not automatically have a natural equivalent with get. Keep meaning and register in view when choosing between be, get and an active clause.\n\nit + passive reporting verb + that-clause: Reports an attributed proposition with impersonal it.\n\nsubject + passive reporting verb + to-infinitive: Puts a participant in the reported proposition first.\n\nrecipient + be + participle + transferred item: Possible with suitable ditransitive verbs such as give.\n\nget + past participle: A commonly conversational event-focused passive.\n\nThe museum was given a complete catalogue.\nMuseum is the recipient promoted from a give construction.\n\nThe manuscript is thought to have survived the fire.\nThe perfect infinitive places survival before the present attribution of belief.\n\nThe trainee got promoted after the trial period.\nGet-passive describes an event; a negative outcome is not required.\n\nGet-passives can report positive events and need not imply fault. Not all verbs allow both double-object/passive alternatives: lexical complementation must be checked.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=19&section=gv5-d19-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Passive: forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/passive-forms"},{"publisher":"Get passive","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/get-passive"},{"publisher":"Seem","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/seem"},{"publisher":"Perfect infinitive with to ( to have worked )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/perfect-infinitive-with-to-to-have-worked"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d19-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Causatives and their complement patterns","text":"Causatives and their complement patterns\n\nCausative constructions express arranging, causing or enabling another action, but have, get, make, let and help differ in grammar and in the kind of involvement they commonly convey.\n\nHave something done commonly describes arranging for someone else to act: We had the scanner repaired. The object scanner is followed by the participle repaired; the sentence does not say that we personally repaired it. Get something done can describe arranging or achieving a similar result: We got the scanner repaired before Monday. Here get is not simply an interchangeable auxiliary in every context. A causative sentence has its own subject and participant structure, so converting I repaired the scanner into I had the scanner repaired normally changes who performed or arranged the work.\n\nHave can also introduce a person and a bare infinitive: I had the technician check the wiring. Get commonly uses a person plus to-infinitive: I got the technician to check the wiring. In a suitable context it can suggest persuasion or successfully arranging cooperation. Make plus a person and a bare infinitive often expresses causing or compelling an action: The delay made us reconsider. Let normally expresses permission: The supervisor let us leave. Help accepts a bare or to-infinitive: She helped me check it; she helped me to check it. These patterns should be learned alongside their typical meanings.\n\nPassive make restores to: We were made to wait. In the permission meaning of let, be allowed to is the ordinary passive alternative: We were allowed to leave, rather than mechanically producing were let to leave. Some other uses of let, such as letting property or the fixed expression be let go, behave differently. Do not generalise a restriction on one meaning to every occurrence of a word. Help him to leave and help him leave are both standard; choosing one does not justify marking the other wrong. Register and local preference can affect frequency without changing grammaticality.\n\nHave plus object plus participle is not always an arranged service. We had our equipment stolen ordinarily reports an adverse experience, not an instruction to steal it. Context distinguishes that reading from a causative arrangement. Similarly, We had visitors waiting outside uses an -ing complement and can describe an experience or an ongoing state brought about by circumstances. The order of have and an object matters: We had repaired the scanner is past perfect, whereas We had the scanner repaired is the object-plus-participle construction. Analyse the complete verb phrase before inferring agency, time or responsibility. A meaningful transformation must preserve these distinctions rather than merely reproduce a familiar sequence.\n\nhave/get + affected object + participle: Often an arranged service or achieved result; context can supply an adverse-experience reading.\n\nhave + person + bare infinitive / get + person + to-infinitive: Related causative patterns with distinct lexical selection.\n\nmake/let + person + bare infinitive; help + person + (to-)infinitive: Complement patterns depend on the selected verb and meaning.\n\nWe had the locks changed after the move.\nThe subject arranged the service; the sentence does not identify the worker.\n\nThe noise made the lecturer pause.\nMake introduces the person affected and the bare infinitive pause.\n\nThe assistant helped the visitor to complete the form.\nTo complete is standard after help; complete without to would also be standard.\n\nHave an object stolen can report an unwanted experience rather than deliberate causation. Help admits infinitives with or without to. Fixed be let go is not evidence for unrestricted passive permission constructions.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=19&section=gv5-d19-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Have something done","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/have-something-done"},{"publisher":"Make","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/make"},{"publisher":"Let, let&rsquo;s","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/let-let-s"},{"publisher":"Help somebody (to) do","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/help-somebody-to-do"},{"publisher":"Passive: other forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/passive-other-forms"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d19-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Passivisation limits, ergative alternation and meaning","text":"Passivisation limits, ergative alternation and meaning\n\nPassivisation depends on a licensed verb-and-complement pattern; an ergative alternation instead uses the same lexical verb transitively for causing a change and intransitively for the change itself.\n\nSome active clauses have no object available for the ordinary passive operation: The parcel arrived; the children slept. The parcel was arrived is not the standard passive equivalent of arrived. A subject complement after a linking verb is not a direct object: She became a doctor cannot be mechanically turned into A doctor was become by her. These restrictions follow from the construction, not from whether the event seems important or whether the subject is human. First identify objects and complements, then ask whether the lexical verb supports the intended passive.\n\nEnglish also permits some prepositional passives: Someone looked into the complaint can correspond to The complaint was looked into. The preposition remains with the verb, even though the complaint is now subject. The bed has been slept in is another established example. Such constructions show why only verbs with a direct object can ever be passive is too broad. They do not mean that every prepositional phrase can be promoted naturally; lexical restrictions, idiomatic units and the relationship of the affected participant to the event matter. A learner's dictionary and established usage help resolve difficult individual cases.\n\nMany change-of-state verbs alternate: Heat cracked the glass; The glass cracked. In the first, heat is a cause and glass an object; in the second, glass is subject in an active intransitive clause. The glass was cracked by the heat is passive and expresses the cause differently. The active intransitive does not identify an external cause and must not be described as passive merely because its subject undergoes a change. Open, break, melt and increase permit comparable patterns in suitable senses, but not all transitive verbs alternate: A thief stole the bag does not license The bag stole with the same meaning.\n\nMeaning-preserving transformations require a final interpretation check. The scope of negation, quantifiers and modals can change when information is rearranged: Not every file was checked denies complete checking. Every file was not checked can be scope-ambiguous; use No file was checked for an explicit zero-checking claim. Keep a specific agent when that agent is part of the original informational task, preserve tense and aspect, and avoid changing an unsuccessful attempt into a completed event. Some stative transitive verbs have limited or unusual passives in ordinary senses; an awkward example is not automatically a universal ban on the verb. Distinguish syntactic possibility, normal collocation and discourse usefulness, and explain precisely which restriction an exercise intends to test.\n\ncause + transitive change verb + affected object: A causative active use, such as heat melted the wax.\n\naffected subject + intransitive change verb: An active intransitive alternation, such as the wax melted.\n\npromoted subject + be + past participle + retained preposition (+ by-agent): A licensed prepositional passive retains the preposition selected by the verb, as in The matter was looked into.\n\nThe temperature rose overnight.\nRise is intransitive here; no ordinary object can become a passive subject.\n\nThe complaint was looked into by an independent reviewer.\nA prepositional passive retains into and identifies the reviewer.\n\nThe window opened when the latch loosened.\nOpened is active intransitive, even though window undergoes the change.\n\nSome clauses without a direct object permit prepositional passives. An affected subject does not by itself make a clause passive. Stative verbs require sense-specific and usage-sensitive analysis.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=19&section=gv5-d19-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Passive: forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/passive-forms"},{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d20-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Non-finite forms and grammatical functions","text":"Non-finite forms and grammatical functions\n\nA non-finite verb form lacks the ordinary independent tense/person agreement contrasts of a finite verb. Non-finite clauses may nevertheless express complex time, aspect, voice and participant relationships through their form and larger context.\n\nBegin with four useful shapes: to write, write after an appropriate selecting expression, writing and written. In The clerk hopes to write, hopes is the finite verb and to write is an infinitival complement. In The clerk may write, may is finite and write is the following plain form. In The clerk is writing, is is finite and writing is a participial form. Written combines with have in a perfect or be in a passive: has written; was written. The spelling alone is not a complete analysis, because many past-tense and participial forms are identical, as in checked.\n\nAn -ing clause can fill several functions: Reading the instructions carefully takes time has a subject clause; She enjoys reading the instructions has a complement; Reading the instructions carefully, she noticed an exception has an adjunct. Traditional teaching often calls the first two gerunds and the last a participle. Some modern grammars use the combined label gerund-participial for the verbal form. Neither terminology makes every -ing word a verb. In the careful reading of the instructions, reading is a noun with a determiner and adjective; in reading the instructions carefully, the verb takes a direct object and adverb.\n\nBuild longer forms by applying ordinary auxiliary selection. To have written is a perfect infinitive; to be written is passive; to have been written combines perfect and passive. Having written is a perfect -ing form, and having been written is perfect passive. These forms have no independently inflected tense, but the perfect commonly locates a situation before a contextual reference point. The committee claims to have checked the files attributes the earlier checking as a claim rather than independently guaranteeing its truth. A negative can precede the non-finite phrase: not to sign; not having signed.\n\nA split infinitive places an intervening expression between infinitival to and the plain verb: to carefully compare the estimates. This is a grammatical option in contemporary standard English, not an automatic error. Adverb placement can affect attachment and emphasis. In The team decided to immediately suspend the trial, immediately clearly modifies suspend; The team immediately decided to suspend the trial can instead locate the decision. Do not move an adverb merely to obey an absolute prohibition if that move changes the intended meaning. Some editors prefer an unsplit form where it is equally clear and natural. Follow a specified house style where necessary, while distinguishing its preference from a universal rule of grammar.\n\nPresent participle and past participle are traditional form names, not automatic time labels. The files being checked tomorrow refers to future checking even though being is an -ing form; the files checked yesterday has past time because the context supplies it. A non-finite clause may contain auxiliaries, an object and other dependants; it is not merely a loose phrase with no internal syntax. Conversely, a plain verb form does not prove non-finiteness in every context: an imperative such as Write clearly is a main-clause construction. Determine the clause type and selecting environment before assigning a form or function label.\n\nto + have + been + lexical past participle: A perfect passive infinitive; the larger context supplies the reference point.\n\n-ing verb + object + adverb: A verbal -ing clause can retain ordinary verb complementation and modification.\n\nThe researcher plans to interview the witnesses.\nPlans is finite; to interview the witnesses is an infinitival complement.\n\nCarefully checking the figures revealed a discrepancy.\nThe whole -ing clause functions as subject; checking takes figures as object and carefully as modifier.\n\nThe letters are believed to have been destroyed.\nThe perfect passive infinitive presents destruction as earlier than the attributed belief; the belief is reported, not proved.\n\nTraditional gerund/participle labels and modern gerund-participial terminology analyse the same many verbal forms differently. Identify the observable pattern before arguing about the label. Irregular forms need lexical checking: written is a participle; wrote is a past-tense form.\n\nA split infinitive is not inherently ungrammatical. Register, rhythm, scope and a specified editorial style may affect which adverb position is preferred.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=20&section=gv5-d20-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"},{"publisher":"Infinitives with and without to - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/infinitives-with-and-without-to"},{"publisher":"Perfect infinitive with to ( to have worked )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/perfect-infinitive-with-to-to-have-worked"},{"publisher":"Is it OK to split an infinitive? — MLA Style Center","url":"https://style.mla.org/split-infinitives/"},{"publisher":"Split infinitive — Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/split-infinitive"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d20-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Verb complementation and differences in meaning","text":"Verb complementation and differences in meaning\n\nVerb complementation concerns the patterns licensed by a particular verb and its sense. An -ing complement and an infinitival complement may be restricted alternatives or may express different meanings.\n\nLearn a verb together with a complete pattern: avoid handling fragile objects; hope to finish; ask someone to wait. Avoid normally takes an -ing activity complement; hope normally takes a to-infinitive in this use. An object may belong to the pattern: The supervisor persuaded the clerk to wait. Leaving out or inserting a preposition by analogy can change or break the selected construction. To is not always infinitival: in look forward to meeting, to is a preposition followed by an -ing clause, whereas in hope to meet it marks an infinitive.\n\nSeveral verbs allow both forms with an important ordinary contrast. Remember to lock the cabinet concerns remembering a task before performing it; remember locking the cabinet concerns a memory of the locking. Stop to read the notice means pause another activity in order to read; stop reading means cease the reading activity. Try to restart the computer describes an effort; try restarting the computer proposes restarting as a method to see whether it helps. Neither try-pattern alone guarantees success. Go on checking continues checking; go on to check introduces a subsequent activity in an appropriate sequence.\n\nPerception verbs can license an object plus a bare infinitive or an -ing form: I saw the boat cross the channel; I saw the boat crossing the channel. The first often presents the event as a whole; the second presents a stage in progress. This contrast should not be exaggerated into a rule that the second event definitely failed to finish. Active causative make and let also normally take a bare infinitive: made us wait; let us leave. Passive made normally introduces to: we were made to wait. Help permits both help us carry and help us to carry. These are selected constructions, not exceptions to be erased by a single formula.\n\nOther pairs differ mainly in viewpoint or context: like swimming and like to swim can both be standard, and preferences vary with meaning and variety. Some patterns allow an explicit non-finite subject, while others require interpretation through the surrounding clause. A dictionary entry helps establish the complement frame; it does not remove the need to read the intended sense. When transforming a sentence, preserve participant roles, aspect and whether an event actually happened. Do not turn remembered to send into remembered sending merely because both phrases contain the same lexical verb: the memory relationship and the assumed event sequence have changed.\n\navoid + -ing; hope + to-infinitive: Selected complement patterns for these ordinary senses.\n\nstop + -ing / stop + to-infinitive: Ceasing the activity contrasts with pausing for a purpose.\n\nWe avoided disturbing the residents.\nThe -ing clause is the selected activity complement of avoided.\n\nMina stopped to compare the two maps.\nThe infinitive expresses the purpose of pausing another activity.\n\nThe mechanic saw the belt slipping but did not see it break.\nThe -ing form presents an unfolding stage; the bare infinitive presents the breaking event as a whole in the negative perception report.\n\nDo not treat every pair as sharply different in the same way. Like reading and like to read overlap, while stop reading and stop to read usually have a substantial structural and meaning contrast. Help permits an infinitive with or without to.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=20&section=gv5-d20-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Verb patterns: verb + infinitive or verb + -ing?","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verb-patterns-verb-infinitive-or-verb-ing"},{"publisher":"Infinitives with and without to - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/infinitives-with-and-without-to"},{"publisher":"Help somebody (to) do","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/help-somebody-to-do"},{"publisher":"Make","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/make"},{"publisher":"Let, let&rsquo;s","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/let-let-s"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d20-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Purpose, participial clauses and meaning-preserving reduction","text":"Purpose, participial clauses and meaning-preserving reduction\n\nA non-finite adjunct adds information such as purpose, time, reason or circumstance. Participial and infinitival clauses can make writing economical, provided their interpreted subject and their relationship to the main clause remain clear.\n\nAn infinitive can express purpose: The librarian moved the lamp to illuminate the desk. The purpose is not simply a second completed event; it explains the intended reason for moving the lamp. In order to can make that relation explicit. An -ing adjunct often shares the main subject: Checking the inventory, the librarian found a missing volume. The librarian is understood as the checker. A past-participial adjunct often gives a passive interpretation: Warned about the leak, the librarian moved the books. The librarian receives the warning rather than issuing it.\n\nPerfect participial clauses can make earlier completion explicit: Having checked the inventory, the librarian locked the store. A passive perfect is also possible: Having been warned about the leak, she moved the books. The grammatical form helps organise time and voice, but the exact logical link often comes from context. Warned about the leak may express background, reason or a concessive relation in a suitable sentence; do not translate every participial clause automatically as because. A reduced relative also needs its own interpretation: the letters written yesterday has a passive relation between letters and write, while the researcher writing today presents the researcher as the writer.\n\nA dangling adjunct arises when the understood participant is missing or misleadingly attached under the intended reading: Having inspected the roof, the leak was obvious does not clearly express who inspected it. If Samir inspected the roof, write After Samir inspected the roof, the leak was obvious to him. Replacing the main clause with Samir found the leak obvious is another valid repair. A passive main clause is not automatically incompatible with an opening participle: Warned about the leak, the tenants were evacuated is coherent because the tenants received the warning. State the intended actor before diagnosing an error.\n\nNot every opening non-finite expression shares the main subject. Absolute constructions have their own subject, as in The checks completed, the team left. Comment expressions such as strictly speaking relate to the speaker’s assessment rather than an ordinary main-clause agent. Purpose clauses can also have implicit agents recoverable in context, especially with passive main clauses; clarity, not an unconditional ban, guides editing. Reduction may remove tense, modality or emphasis: the forms that must be checked cannot simply become the forms checked without losing necessity. A sound transformation therefore compares what each version actually commits the writer to, including the role of every participant and every qualification.\n\nhaving + past participle, main clause: A perfect -ing adjunct can make prior completion explicit.\n\nnoun + past-participial modifier: Often a reduced passive relative, subject to lexical meaning and context.\n\nThe clerk opened the window to cool the room.\nThe infinitive states an intended purpose; it does not guarantee that the room cooled.\n\nHaving compared both estimates, Samir requested a third.\nSamir is understood as the person who compared the estimates.\n\nThe folders damaged in the flood were photographed before disposal.\nDamaged in the flood modifies folders with a passive interpretation; it does not make folders the agents of damage.\n\nAn absolute such as Weather permitting, we will inspect the site has an explicit subject inside the opening clause. A comment expression such as frankly speaking is not evaluated by blindly assigning its speaking to the next noun.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=20&section=gv5-d20-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Participle clauses","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/c1/participle-clauses"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"},{"publisher":"Perfect infinitive with to ( to have worked )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/perfect-infinitive-with-to-to-have-worked"},{"publisher":"Infinitives with and without to - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/infinitives-with-and-without-to"},{"publisher":"Nominative absolute, Merriam-Webster","url":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/nominative%20absolute"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d20-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Control, raising and the interpretation of non-finite subjects","text":"Control, raising and the interpretation of non-finite subjects\n\nInterpreting a non-finite clause often requires identifying its expressed or understood subject. Control and raising are analytical labels for important differences between constructions; their formal treatment varies between grammatical theories.\n\nAsk who is understood to perform the action. In Amina tried to unlock the cabinet, Amina is the person trying and the intended unlocker. In Amina persuaded Omar to unlock the cabinet, Omar is the intended unlocker, while Amina is the persuader. These are ordinary subject-control and object-control examples in traditional terminology. The first noun in the sentence does not control every following infinitive, and the nearest noun is not a universal answer either. Learn the selecting verb’s pattern and read the whole context before interpreting the missing participant.\n\nSome infinitives express their own subject: It would be useful for Omar to attend identifies Omar as the attendee. Others allow a general or contextual subject: To leave now would be unwise may concern us, another salient group, or people in general, depending on context. With a control verb, participant roles can remain stable under a main-clause passive: Amina persuaded Omar to attend; Omar was persuaded by Amina to attend. Omar is still understood as the attendee. The surface object/subject position changes, so a role-based explanation is more reliable than an instruction to select whichever noun appears immediately before to.\n\nRaising constructions behave differently. In The parcel seems to have arrived, the parcel is the understood subject of arrive, but seem does not describe an action performed by the parcel. Compare It seems that the parcel has arrived. Dummy there supplies a useful diagnostic: There seems to be a problem is grammatical even though there names no person who seems. A normal agentive control construction such as try does not simply accept dummy there in that way. The contrast concerns the intended dummy interpretation, not every possible use of the word there, which can also be an ordinary place expression.\n\nIn one established generative account, control involves an unpronounced subject conventionally written PRO, whose interpretation is supplied by a controller or context. Raising instead relates the visible subject to a position associated with the lower predicate; seem does not assign it the additional participant role that try assigns its subject. These are theoretical analyses, not observable extra words to insert into finished prose, and other frameworks describe the dependency differently. Diagnostics should be used together with meaning and lexical selection, not as one automatic test for every adjective or verb. A further pattern illustrates the limits of a simple subject rule: in The report is easy to read, report is understood as the object of read, whereas in Amina is eager to read, Amina is the understood reader. Such differences make careful syntactic analysis useful, but a beginner can begin by asking who does what and which complement pattern is licensed.\n\nsubject + try + to-infinitive: A standard subject-control example: the subject is also the intended performer in the complement.\n\nThere + seem + to be + noun phrase: An established raising pattern with dummy there.\n\nNora tried to repair the hinge.\nNora is both the person trying and the intended repairer.\n\nNora persuaded Imran to repair the hinge.\nImran is understood as the repairer; Nora is the persuader.\n\nThere seem to be several errors in the total.\nDummy there has no semantic agent role; the plural associate supports seem in this careful formal version.\n\nThe subject of an infinitive can be explicit after for, contextually understood, controlled by a main-clause participant, or involved in another dependency. Do not teach that every infinitive has the subject of its nearest finite clause.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=20&section=gv5-d20-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Control (L322), Simon Fraser University","url":"https://www.sfu.ca/person/dearmond/322/322.control.htm"},{"publisher":"Seem","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/seem"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d21-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Quoting, reporting and choosing a perspective","text":"Quoting, reporting and choosing a perspective\n\nDirect speech presents words as a quotation; indirect speech reports their content from the current speaker’s perspective. A report can preserve a message without reproducing its wording.\n\nBegin with who said what to whom. In a direct quotation, the pronouns and expressions such as here and tomorrow belong to the original speaking situation: Mina said, “I will return tomorrow.” In an indirect report, the reporter constructs a new clause: Mina said that she would return the following day. That introduces the reported statement and can often be omitted after common verbs. Quotation marks are needed for a claim to reproduce words exactly; they are not needed merely because a sentence mentions someone’s ideas.\n\nChange reference by identifying actual people, places and times, rather than by applying a substitution list blindly. If I report my own promise, I remains I. If the report occurs in the same room on the same day, here and today may remain appropriate. A reporting verb can be present when a statement is current: The notice says that applications close on Friday. Past reporting locates the act of speaking before now. The reported clause then locates its own event relative to that act and the reporter’s present.\n\nThought reporting follows comparable patterns: Rafi thought that the entrance was locked. It need not describe spoken words. Reporters must distinguish a source’s claim from their own endorsement. The manager claimed that the stock was safe does not establish that it was safe. Words such as admitted, alleged and confirmed introduce evidential and evaluative meanings beyond said. A faithful transformation therefore preserves uncertainty and attribution, not just tense forms. Do not turn “I may resign” into a statement that the person definitely resigned or promised to resign.\n\nDirect and indirect are constructions, not degrees of factual accuracy. An exact quotation can be misleading if its context is removed; a carefully attributed paraphrase can be accurate. Traditional transformation exercises often supply a single decontextualised reporting time. Real discourse permits several perspectives, so a defensible answer states its assumed reporting situation. Free indirect discourse blends a narrator’s voice with a character’s viewpoint without a reporting clause; it is useful to recognise in literary prose, but ordinary formal reports should make attribution clear whenever readers might otherwise confuse the source with the writer.\n\nX said (that) + finite clause: Reports a proposition; reference is interpreted from the reporting context.\n\nNida said, “I need this key.”\nI and this are anchored to Nida’s original situation.\n\nNida said that she needed that key.\nThe report changes person and points back to the identified key.\n\nI said that I would remain here until Friday.\nSelf-report preserves I; here is valid if the reporter is still in the relevant place.\n\nIf the reporter and original speaker are the same person, a first-person pronoun need not change: I said I was ready.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=21&section=gv5-d21-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d21-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Backshift and temporal relationships","text":"Backshift and temporal relationships\n\nBackshift is the use of a more remote tense form in a reported clause, commonly after past reporting. It does not necessarily mean that the reported situation has ceased to be true.\n\nA common reporting sequence moves present to past: “I am tired” becomes She said she was tired. A present perfect can become a past perfect, and will can become would. These changes allow the report to be viewed from the earlier speaking time. The past form in She said she lived nearby can describe residence simultaneous with her speaking, not residence before it. Keep separate the time of the report, the time of the original statement and the time of the event described.\n\nBackshift is often optional when information remains true or relevant: He said that his sister works in a laboratory is normal if her work continues. He said that his sister worked in a laboratory can be equally appropriate and does not itself prove that she has left. A clear past sequence can also be reported without changing every past simple to past perfect. In a conventional transformation exercise, use the specified reporting viewpoint; in actual writing, choose the form that accurately communicates the intended relationships.\n\nBackshift is more strongly motivated when a current-tense report would suggest a present truth that the writer does not intend. On Monday she said that the bridge was closed; it reopened on Tuesday. Describing that Monday message as she said the bridge is closed in a Wednesday report would be misleading without a special quotation-like perspective. Past perfect normally remains past perfect. Could, should, would, might and ought to often remain unchanged, while must may remain or become had to depending on whether it expresses continuing necessity, an earlier obligation or a deduction.\n\nThe term sequence of tenses describes relationships, not a mechanical rule requiring all verbs in a sentence to share tense. A past reporting verb can combine with a present generalisation, a past event and a future-in-the-past prediction in one grammatical report. Modal past forms may express politeness or hypothesis rather than earlier time. In expert analysis, distinguish absolute time reference to the reporter’s now from relative time reference to an earlier viewpoint. If two answers differ only in a legitimate viewpoint, explain the difference rather than treating one as a grammatical failure.\n\nsaid + past clause: Often simultaneous with the original speaking time.\n\nsaid + had + past participle: Often places an event before an earlier speaking viewpoint.\n\nIn April, she said that she was applying for funding.\nThe application was current at the April speaking time.\n\nHe said that water expands when it freezes.\nThe present reports an enduring generalisation.\n\nThe pilot said that the aircraft had landed before dawn.\nThe landing precedes the past report.\n\nThe past perfect is not shifted into an invented earlier tense: She said she had already paid remains possible.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=21&section=gv5-d21-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d21-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Reported questions and embedded interrogatives","text":"Reported questions and embedded interrogatives\n\nAn embedded interrogative is a question-like clause functioning inside a larger construction. Its word order normally differs from that of an independent question.\n\nCompare Where is the archive? with She asked where the archive was. The reporting clause supplies the act of asking; the embedded clause uses the order where + subject + verb. For yes–no questions, use whether or if: He asked whether the office was open. These words introduce alternatives rather than an asserted fact. Do not add that before whether or a question word in this pattern. Punctuation belongs to the whole sentence: She asked where it was. ends with a full stop.\n\nThe whole sentence can itself be a direct question: Do you know where the archive is? takes a question mark because Do you know ...? is interrogative. Inside it, the archive is retains embedded order. Subject questions need special care: Who signed the register? becomes She asked who signed the register. Here who is the subject, so there is no separate subject to move in front of signed. Do-support disappears in ordinary embedded questions: Why did they leave? becomes He asked why they had left or why they left.\n\nIf and whether overlap but are not interchangeable everywhere. Use whether before a to-infinitive (whether to apply), after a preposition (about whether it is safe), and immediately before or not (whether or not we agree). If is common for indirect yes–no questions, though it can be confused with a condition in some contexts. Ask may take a recipient (She asked me where I lived), but wonder normally does not: She wondered where I lived. A request shaped as a question may need reporting by communicative purpose rather than literal interrogative form.\n\nEmbedded questions need not report an earlier spoken question. The issue is whether the evidence is sufficient and I know how the device works contain interrogative content without a speech event. An exclamative such as how quickly they responded has a different meaning from the question how quickly did they respond? Some varieties and informal echoes permit patterns resembling direct-question order inside a report; this course’s formal edited default uses ordinary embedded order. Quoted questions remain direct speech and retain their own order: She asked, “Where is the archive?” Distinguish that quotation from an unquoted embedded clause.\n\nasked + wh-word + subject + verb: Reports a wh-question without question inversion.\n\nasked + whether/if + clause: Reports a yes–no question.\n\nThe clerk asked whether I had an appointment.\nWhether introduces the yes–no alternatives.\n\nDo you remember where we stored the backups?\nThe outer question requires ?, while the embedded clause uses we stored.\n\nShe asked who had authorised the payment.\nWho is the embedded subject; the sequence is already correct.\n\nA subject wh-question may look unchanged when embedded: Who arrived? → She asked who arrived.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=21&section=gv5-d21-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d21-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Commands, requests and reporting-verb patterns","text":"Commands, requests and reporting-verb patterns\n\nA reporting verb selects a grammatical complement and contributes its own meaning. Reporting an instruction, promise, suggestion or exclamation requires preserving that communicative meaning.\n\nCommands and many requests can be reported with a recipient and a to-infinitive: “Close the hatch” becomes She told us to close the hatch. Put not before to for a prohibition: She warned us not to touch the cable. Asked us to can report a polite request, while ordered us to makes it stronger. These choices matter: an invitation is not automatically an order. A reported exclamation may use a content clause or an evaluative verb: “What a useful guide!” can become She exclaimed that the guide was very useful.\n\nLearn a verb’s complete pattern. Say that ... and say to someone that ... differ from tell someone that ... . Advise someone to apply, promise to return and apologise for arriving late have different complement structures. Suggest allows an -ing clause or a finite content clause: They suggested waiting; they suggested that we wait or should wait. It does not normally take a recipient plus to-infinitive with that meaning: suggested us to wait is not the standard pattern. Deny taking the money and deny that one took it likewise offer different grammatical forms.\n\nReporting verbs can change the message if selected carelessly. Promise implies commitment, admit implies acceptance of an unwelcome fact, and persuade describes successfully influencing someone rather than merely advising them. He advised me to appeal does not entail I appealed. Negation also has a scope: He did not promise to return denies a promise; He promised not to return reports a promise of non-return. A formal transformation must preserve agency, addressee, obligation, uncertainty and time relations, even if a more elaborate sentence sounds impressive.\n\nTraditional reported-speech exercises sometimes treat every question as a request for information and every imperative as an order. Actual utterances can have indirect functions: “Could you lower the screen?” is usually a request, while “Have a seat” can be an invitation. Choose the reporting construction from the supplied situation. A reporting passive such as Participants were advised to retain copies can appropriately background the adviser; it does not make an instruction more authoritative by itself. When a verb’s possible complements vary with meaning or register, consult a learner’s dictionary and compare complete original sentences before ruling out an unfamiliar form.\n\ntell/ask/advise + person + (not) to-infinitive: Reports directives while retaining an addressee.\n\nsuggest + -ing / that-clause: Reports a proposal without the nonstandard suggest-person-to pattern.\n\nThe technician warned visitors not to unplug the server.\nNot scopes over unplug; visitors are the understood actors.\n\nThe committee suggested that the trial should continue.\nMandative should is a standard British pattern.\n\nHe promised not to disclose the names.\nThe commitment concerns non-disclosure, not absence of a promise.\n\nHelp can allow a to-infinitive or bare infinitive, but that flexibility must not be transferred to every reporting verb.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=21&section=gv5-d21-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"Formal and informal language","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/formal-and-informal-language"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d22-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Placing modifiers and recovering their subjects","text":"Placing modifiers and recovering their subjects\n\nA modifier adds descriptive or limiting information to another expression. A misplaced modifier suggests an unintended attachment; a dangling modifier lacks a suitable understood participant in its grammatical context.\n\nStart by asking what each description is meant to describe. In a blue folder on the desk, blue describes the folder and on the desk locates it. In an opening participial clause such as Carrying a blue folder, Leena entered, Leena is normally understood as the person carrying the folder. Writing Carrying a blue folder, the door opened instead makes the door the expected carrier. The problem is not that the sentence begins with an -ing form; it is that the grammatical connection does not fit the intended event.\n\nPlace a modifier close enough to its target that readers can recover the intended relationship. A notice reading We sell bags for children made of recycled fabric invites the absurd reading that children are made of fabric. We sell recycled-fabric bags for children removes that attachment risk. An introductory past-participial clause can correctly attach to a passive subject: Damaged in transit, the equipment was returned. Equipment is both what was damaged and what was returned; a passive main clause is not automatically a dangling construction.\n\nA squinting modifier lies between expressions it could modify in either direction: Students who revise regularly improve. Regularly may describe revising or improvement. Rewrite around the intended message rather than move the word mechanically: Students who revise on a regular schedule improve, or Students who revise show regular improvement. Purpose infinitives need semantic care too. To reach the balcony, take the stairs has an understood imperative you; the absence of a printed subject does not make it faulty. To reach the balcony, the staircase is narrow gives no suitable controller of reach.\n\nThe subject-sharing guideline governs ordinary subjectless adjunct clauses, not every initial expression. An absolute construction supplies its own subject: The inspection complete, the team left. Conventional comments such as generally speaking or to be honest concern the speaker’s stance and need not describe the next noun as an action. Some apparent dangling expressions are established discourse constructions; others create a real unintended assertion. Expert revision identifies the construction before applying a rule. If a person’s identity is unknown, use a finite clause or an impersonal alternative instead of inventing a named agent to repair the sentence.\n\nSubjectless participial adjunct, + main subject + predicate: The understood participant normally matches the main subject.\n\nNoun phrase + participle, + clause: An absolute can supply a different explicit subject.\n\nWalking beside the canal, Farah noticed the damaged railing.\nFarah is the walker and the observer.\n\nStored below zero, the samples were stable for a week.\nThe passive main subject also receives the storage action.\n\nThe access road having flooded, the engineers postponed the visit.\nThe absolute has its own subject, the access road.\n\nConventional speaker comments such as Frankly speaking, the proposal is expensive do not require the proposal to speak.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=22&section=gv5-d22-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Participle clauses","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/grammar/c1-grammar/participle-clauses"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d22-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Attachment, scope and reference","text":"Attachment, scope and reference\n\nAmbiguity occurs when a form supports more than one interpretation. Attachment concerns which expression a modifier belongs to; scope concerns how much of a message an operator such as only or not affects.\n\nA grammatical sentence can be ambiguous without containing an error. I photographed the guide with the telescope may mean that I used the telescope or that the guide had it. The prepositional phrase can attach to the photographing event or to the noun guide. To revise, state the intended interpretation: Using the telescope, I photographed the guide; or I photographed the guide who was carrying the telescope. Neither rewrite is the universally correct answer because the original supplies no decision between the two meanings.\n\nScope differences are often created by focusing words. Only Salma reviewed the draft contrasts Salma with other possible reviewers. Salma reviewed only the draft contrasts that document with other possible objects. Salma only reviewed the draft can emphasise that she reviewed it rather than approved it, although spoken stress and context may support other readings. Keep the intended contrast explicit in formal prose. Negation interacts with quantifiers too: Not all the samples failed means at least some did not fail; none of the samples failed says that every sample avoided failure.\n\nPronoun reference requires a recoverable intended person or thing, but not every pronoun needs a single preceding noun. This can refer to an entire event: The lift stopped between floors. This delayed the delivery. That is a legitimate propositional reference. By contrast, When Hina met Sara, she was worried does not establish which woman was worried. Repeat a name or restructure the sentence if the distinction matters. Avoid solving ambiguity by guessing gender, role or a stereotype; preserve uncertainty when the source does not identify the intended referent.\n\nGrammatical ambiguity can coexist with pragmatic clarity. Context may make one reading overwhelmingly likely even though the structure permits another. Expert analysis separates a possible parse from a plausible intended meaning and identifies the evidence for each. Bound-variable readings also go beyond simple name replacement: Every researcher filed their own notes can vary the referent of their with each researcher. Do not replace such pronouns with one fixed name without changing meaning. Scope can remain underspecified intentionally, as in diplomatic language, but assessments must either supply enough context for one answer or explicitly reward the analysis of multiple readings.\n\nonly + focused constituent: Sets the constituent against contextually relevant alternatives.\n\nnot all + plural noun phrase: Denies universality; it does not assert none.\n\nOnly the backup server failed.\nThe focus excludes the other relevant servers from the set that failed.\n\nThe manager did not approve every claim.\nThis need not mean that the manager approved no claims.\n\nEach student labelled their own specimen.\nTheir varies with each student; singular they need not identify one fixed person.\n\nBare this is acceptable when its propositional referent is clear; adding a summary noun is a clarity tool, not a universal grammatical requirement.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=22&section=gv5-d22-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Adverbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adverbs"},{"publisher":"It, this and that in paragraphs","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/it-this-and-that-in-paragraphs"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d22-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Parallelism, correlatives and comparisons","text":"Parallelism, correlatives and comparisons\n\nParallelism presents related elements in comparable grammatical or conceptual forms. It supports clear comparison and coordination, while grammatical coordination ultimately depends on the functions and constructions involved.\n\nLists are easier to process when their parts follow a recognisable pattern: The role involves scheduling visits, checking records and answering enquiries. Each -ing clause fills the same complement position after involves. A mixture such as involves scheduling visits, record checks and to answer enquiries becomes awkward because the last infinitive does not fit the pattern. Try each element after the shared opening. If an item cannot combine with that opening, repair the structure rather than merely changing its ending to resemble the neighbouring words.\n\nCorrelative pairs make structural expectations visible. The course is useful both for teachers and for editors places parallel prepositional phrases after both and and. The course both supports teachers and provides exercises coordinates verb phrases. Not only and but also can coordinate words, phrases or clauses. Fronted not only may require inversion in the clause it introduces, but it does not require every following item to be a full clause. Not only was the route shorter but also safer is a possible shared-copula construction; adding it was is another grammatical version.\n\nParallelism is not a rule that every coordinated unit must have an identical category label. A complement may coordinate expressions with a shared function: She is a capable editor and easy to work with can be understood as two predicative descriptions. Some mixed constructions are fully acceptable; others are confusing or disallowed by a particular head. In comparison, identify what is compared and what has been omitted. The new office is larger than the old one compares offices. The cost of the new office is higher than that of the old one compares costs, with that substituting for cost.\n\nReduced comparison clauses rely on recoverable material: Mei reads more articles than I do clearly compares readers; Mei reads more articles than me may also have that informal standard reading. Mei likes Jo more than I can mean more than I like Jo, whereas Mei likes Jo more than me can mean more than she likes me. Avoid using a case preference to erase a genuine reading. Formal revisions can expand ellipsis to show the intended relationship. At expert level, examine syntactic compatibility, omitted material and logical comparability separately; rhythmic symmetry alone does not prove grammatical or semantic equivalence.\n\nboth X and Y / either X or Y: The paired constituents should fit the same construction and intended relationship.\n\ncomparative + than-clause: Recover omitted material from context, or make it explicit.\n\nShe plans to inspect the roof and to test the alarm.\nThe two to-infinitives share a complementation role.\n\nNot only was the hall warmer but also quieter.\nThe copula is shared with coordinated predicative adjectives.\n\nThis week’s output exceeded last week’s output.\nThe compared quantities are of the same relevant type.\n\nDifferent forms can share a predicative function; evaluate the actual construction before declaring mixed categories ungrammatical.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=22&section=gv5-d22-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Parallelism","url":"https://writingcenter.unc.edu/tips-and-tools/parallelism/"},{"publisher":"Not only … but also","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/not-only-but-also"},{"publisher":"Ellipsis - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/ellipsis"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d22-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Predication and meaning-preserving revision","text":"Predication and meaning-preserving revision\n\nPredication attributes a property, identity or event to a subject. Faulty predication occurs when the expressed grammatical relationship fails to convey the intended logical relationship.\n\nA sentence can be structurally well formed yet say something the writer did not intend. The aim of the workshop is improving attendance can be grammatical as an identification of an aim, whereas The cost of the workshop is because transport is expensive mixes a thing being measured with an unexplained causal clause. A clearer version is The workshop is expensive because transport costs are high. First decide what the subject refers to, then decide what the predicate says about it. Do not use logical oddness as an excuse to ban every uncommon subject–complement combination.\n\nFaulty comparison often arises when an abbreviated form conceals the wrong relationship. For the intended claim about income, Her salary is higher than her assistant invites comparison with a person rather than that person’s salary. Her salary is higher than her assistant’s makes the relation clear. Some compressed expressions are familiar idioms and need no repair: He earns more than his assistant can be a complete comparative clause with earns understood. The appropriate revision depends on what is syntactically omitted and what readers can reliably reconstruct.\n\nPreserving meaning requires checking more than the topic. Did the original express every item or some items, possibility or certainty, a decision or a completed act, cause or sequence? The supervisor decided to close the office does not entail that the office closed. Because the equipment failed, the demonstration stopped asserts causation; The equipment failed and the demonstration stopped may leave the relation open. A grammatical revision that strengthens or changes these relationships is not a faithful correction, even when it sounds clearer. If the intended meaning is unavailable, offer alternatives and label their assumptions.\n\nRevision proceeds through diagnosis, interpretation, reconstruction and verification. Identify the smallest relevant structural unit, paraphrase its plausible readings, choose the intended one from evidence, then rebuild. Finally compare both versions for participants, time, polarity, quantification, modality, focus and register. Minimal editing is useful because it reduces unintended changes, but a small change is not automatically the best repair: it may preserve ambiguity or create a new presupposition. Expert editing sometimes needs a full finite clause rather than a compressed modifier. The objective is a defensible expression of the writer’s meaning, not obedience to a mechanical number-of-changes rule.\n\nsubject + linking verb + predicative complement: Attributes or identifies; check that the semantic relation is intended.\n\nreconstruct → compare meaning dimensions: Verification includes scope, time, modality and participants.\n\nThe reason for the delay was a missing permit.\nThe complement identifies a cause rather than an unrelated location.\n\nThe workshop was expensive because transport costs had risen.\nThe because-clause gives the cause of expense.\n\nThe board decided to suspend the scheme, but suspension had not yet begun.\nA decision is distinguished from its implementation.\n\nThe reason ... is because occurs in established English; prefer an economical alternative where useful, but do not label every occurrence inherently ungrammatical.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=22&section=gv5-d22-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Parallelism","url":"https://writingcenter.unc.edu/tips-and-tools/parallelism/"},{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d23-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Negative, concessive and conditional inversion","text":"Negative, concessive and conditional inversion\n\nInversion changes ordinary subject–verb order. Subject–auxiliary inversion places an auxiliary before its subject; other marked constructions front a complement or place a lexical verb before a following noun-phrase subject.\n\nOrdinary statements usually place the subject before the finite verb: We had never seen the original map. With a fronted negative or restrictive adjunct, formal English uses inversion: Never had we seen the original map. Move the existing finite auxiliary; if a simple present or past lexical verb has no auxiliary, add do, does or did and use the base verb: Rarely does the museum open at night. The sentence remains a statement. The inverted order does not by itself turn it into a question.\n\nScope determines where inversion occurs. Only after the review did the panel approve the grant inverts the main clause, not the after-clause. Not until the rain stopped did we leave likewise leaves the subordinate order unchanged. A negative or only-containing subject is different: Not a single applicant complained and Only Nida knew the code have ordinary subject–verb order. Hardly ... when and no sooner ... than commonly express rapid succession, often with the past perfect, but the construction does not require past perfect in every context.\n\nConditional inversion offers formal alternatives to some if-clauses: Had I known ... corresponds to If I had known ...; Should you need assistance ... corresponds to If you should need assistance ...; Were the machine to fail ... corresponds to If the machine were to fail ... . Do not simply remove if from any condition or combine both markers. Concessive patterns such as Tired though she was, she continued front the predicative expression; they do not normally invert she was. Child as he was, he understood uses an articleless fronted noun in this marked construction.\n\nLocative full inversion is a different pattern from subject–auxiliary inversion after a negative. With a full noun phrase, Here comes the courier or At the far end stood a guard can present a newly mentioned entity. With an ordinary unstressed personal pronoun, the usual order is Here she comes, not Here comes she. The construction depends on the verb, the locative and information structure; not every verb allows the same pattern. In particular, a fronted place expression does not mechanically require inversion. Compare At the far end, she waited, which preserves subject–verb order.\n\nTraditional teaching sometimes calls all marked order inversion, but distinguish subject–auxiliary inversion, full verb–subject inversion and concessive complement fronting. Each has different constraints. Conditional should can suggest a tentative possibility; replacing an ordinary if-condition may therefore alter register and nuance even when the broad condition survives. Use these constructions to emphasise a restriction or fit a formal genre, not to decorate every sentence. A direct neutral statement is often clearer in instructions and reports. When transforming, retain negation, temporal relationships and the scope of the limiting expression rather than merely moving the first two words.\n\nnegative/restrictive adjunct + auxiliary + subject + remainder: Marks emphasis within a statement.\n\nHad + subject + past participle, ...: Formal conditional alternative to an if-past-perfect clause.\n\npredicative expression + though/as + subject + verb, ...: Marked concessive fronting without ordinary question inversion.\n\nOnly after the labels were checked did the team seal the crates.\nInversion belongs to the main clause; labels were checked retains normal order.\n\nShould the printer stop, use the spare terminal.\nShould introduces a tentative future condition.\n\nExhausted though the volunteers were, they completed the inventory.\nThe adjective is fronted; the volunteers were is not inverted.\n\nA negative subject does not trigger the adjunct-inversion rule: Nobody complained is correct without did.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=23&section=gv5-d23-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Inversion - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/inversion"},{"publisher":"Inversion after negative adverbials","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/grammar/c1-grammar/inversion-after-negative-adverbials"},{"publisher":"Conditionals: other expressions ( unless, should, as long as )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conditionals-other-expressions-unless-should-as-long-as"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d23-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Fronting, clefts and organising focus","text":"Fronting, clefts and organising focus\n\nInformation structure concerns how a sentence packages its message for a particular context. Fronting and cleft constructions can establish a topic, highlight a contrast or place an important constituent in focus.\n\nCompare I can repair this lock with This lock, I can repair. The fronted object remains the thing repaired; its position makes it a useful point of contrast with other locks. Fronting does not automatically require question order. In conversation, a speaker may instead use a detached topic and a resumptive pronoun: That old lock, I can repair it. This left-dislocation is established informal discourse, though formal edited writing often prefers an integrated clause. Different structures should not be treated as one interchangeable formula.\n\nAn it-cleft divides a message between a focusing clause and a cleft clause: It was Nida who found the error. The construction highlights Nida against relevant alternatives while treating the finding of an error as background. The initial it + be remains singular even with a plural focus: It was the auditors who found the error. In a pseudo-cleft, a wh-clause sets up an open value: What we need is a reliable backup. The following phrase supplies the focused value. These structures are useful when answering or correcting a specific contextual question.\n\nFocus is not identical to new information. Known information can be contrastively focused: “Was it the director?” “No, it was the clerk.” Likewise, topic is not simply the first noun in every sentence. A topic identifies what a discourse contribution is about, while a grammatical subject fulfils a syntactic function. In thematic descriptions, theme often refers to a clause’s point of departure and rheme to the remainder, but linguistic frameworks use these terms differently. Explain the actual distribution of familiar information and emphasis rather than assume one universal terminology.\n\nClefts can carry presuppositions and contrast. It was the red cable that failed normally treats a failure as given and identifies its source; The red cable failed can introduce the entire event. Transforming freely between them may change what readers are invited to take for granted. Pseudo-cleft agreement can vary when a plural complement follows, as in What we need is/are stronger checks; avoid forcing such a contested case into a context-free single-key test. Use unambiguous singular models first, and at the expert layer explain that agreement and interpretation interact. Marked structures work best where their focus matches a real communicative need.\n\nfocused constituent, + subject + verb ...: Integrated fronting gives prominence without necessarily requiring inversion.\n\nIt + be + focus + who/that-clause: Identifies or contrasts a focused constituent.\n\nWhat-clause + be + focused value: A pseudo-cleft supplies a value for the wh-clause.\n\nThe first proposal I support; the second I oppose.\nThe first proposal and the second are fronted objects of support and oppose. Each clause keeps I as its subject; the fronting makes their contrast explicit.\n\nIt was the night shift that detected the leak.\nThe cleft identifies the detector while retaining the leak event as background.\n\nWhat the archive needs is a second scanner.\nThe wh-clause sets up a need and the final noun phrase supplies its value.\n\nA fronted object does not ordinarily cause do-inversion: This report I understand is grammatical; This report do I understand is not the normal modern pattern.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=23&section=gv5-d23-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Cleft sentences ( It was in June we got married .) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/cleft-sentences-it-was-in-june-we-got-married"},{"publisher":"Fronting","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/fronting"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d23-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Extraposition, existential presentation and end-weight","text":"Extraposition, existential presentation and end-weight\n\nExtraposition places a clausal constituent later while a dummy it occupies a grammatical position. Existential there introduces the presence of an entity or situation. Both can shape information flow without referring like ordinary pronouns.\n\nA long subject clause can be difficult to process at the start: That the shipment arrived before the deadline surprised us. An extraposed version is It surprised us that the shipment arrived before the deadline. The initial it does not refer to the shipment; it supports a construction with the content clause later. Similarly, It is useful to retain receipts is often more natural than To retain receipts is useful. Both are grammatical. The later clause is not an object merely because it appears after other material; analysis must consider the whole construction.\n\nExistential there presents existence or availability: There are two spare chairs in the corridor. It differs from locative there meaning in that place: Put the chairs there. In formal edited writing, use are with plural chairs and is with a singular presentational noun phrase. Contracted there’s with a following plural occurs in conversation, but a formal assessment can explicitly ask for the edited agreement convention. Some analyses call existential there the grammatical dummy subject and the following noun phrase the displaced or notional subject; school grammars often call only the latter the real subject.\n\nEnd-weight is the tendency to place structurally long material later; end-focus is a common tendency for important focal material to occur near the end. They interact but are not identical. We sent the committee a report containing the full sequence of maintenance logs puts the heavy report phrase last. Passive voice can maintain a familiar topic while allowing a new agent late in the clause: The mural was restored by a team from the local college. Neither subject length nor passive voice is a grammatical defect. Choose the order according to what the reader already knows and needs to learn.\n\nThese tendencies are defaults, not bans on long initial clauses or final given material. Legal drafting, literary rhetoric and contrast may justify marked weight. Extraposition is also construction-specific: I find it surprising that nobody called requires anticipatory it in that pattern, while I know that nobody called does not take an inserted dummy object. Existential presentation commonly favours indefinite or newly introduced entities, but definite phrases occur in list-like or reminder contexts: There is still the matter of the deposit. A reliable analysis considers discourse purpose, syntactic licensing and register together before classifying an unusual sentence as erroneous.\n\nIt + predicate + that/to-clause: A dummy it supports a later clausal constituent.\n\nThere + be + presentational noun phrase: Introduces existence, presence or availability.\n\nIt is essential that the seals remain intact.\nIt is anticipatory rather than a reference to a previously named object.\n\nThere is one unresolved claim.\nThe clause introduces a remaining issue.\n\nI find it reassuring that the alarm has been tested.\nThe find + it + adjective + content-clause pattern licenses anticipatory object it.\n\nExistential there can introduce a definite item in a reminder or list context: There is the signed copy you requested.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=23&section=gv5-d23-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"It","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/it"},{"publisher":"There is, there’s and there are - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/there-is-there-s-and-there-are"},{"publisher":"Fronting","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/fronting"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d23-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Emphatic auxiliaries, substitution and ellipsis","text":"Emphatic auxiliaries, substitution and ellipsis\n\nEmphatic do adds positive emphasis to a lexical verb. Substitution replaces recoverable material with an expression such as do so or one; ellipsis leaves recoverable material unexpressed.\n\nA neutral statement can be strengthened with do, does or did: I do understand your concern. The auxiliary carries tense and agreement, while understand is a bare form. Do not write does understands or did understood. If an auxiliary already exists, stress that auxiliary instead: I have checked it; She can help. Emphatic do can also make an invitation warmer or an instruction more insistent: Do come inside. Emphasis changes the speaker’s stance, not necessarily the event described.\n\nSubstitution and ellipsis avoid repetition when the missing content is clear. The archivist promised to inspect the box and did so before lunch uses did so for inspecting the box. In I could open it, but I would rather not, the omitted complement of not is recoverable. One and ones substitute for count noun phrases: The blue folders are full; use the green ones. Do not substitute one for an uncountable mass reading: If this equipment is faulty, get better equipment or a better item, not automatically a better one.\n\nEllipsis is licensed by a construction and context, not by a general permission to delete any repeated-looking word. In The first draft was accepted; the second, rejected, gapping removes was and retains a contrasting remnant. The remaining material still supports the intended clause relationship. Informal conversational ellipsis such as Seen the new notice? is established but may not fit a formal report. Leaving a relative pronoun unexpressed is possible in some defining object relatives, not in subject relatives merely to shorten them. Make missing material explicit when readers could recover more than one proposition.\n\nAuxiliary choice preserves the structure of the understood material. “Has she filed it?” “She has” retains the perfect auxiliary; “Did she file it?” “She did” uses do. Substitution may preserve meaning only approximately if tense, agency or object reference shifts. Interpret do so and do it from context rather than regard them as universally interchangeable with every predicate, especially stative ones. In discourse, efficient ellipsis supports cohesion, while excessive compression can hide comparisons or agency. Expert control therefore includes the ability to expand a reduced construction and verify exactly which words, participants and time relations a reader must reconstruct.\n\ndo/does/did + base verb: Adds positive emphasis while the auxiliary carries tense/agreement.\n\nauxiliary + recoverable omitted predicate: Retains relevant tense/aspect/modal structure in ellipsis.\n\nThe replacement did arrive, despite the earlier warning.\nDid emphasises the positive event; arrive remains bare.\n\nThe analyst checked the totals, and the editor did so too.\nDid so substitutes for the recoverable checking action.\n\nThe first box was sealed; the second, left open.\nThe second clause omits was; the contrast and passive interpretation remain recoverable.\n\nWith existing perfect have, do not add emphatic do: I have checked it can be emphatic through stress on have.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=23&section=gv5-d23-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Ellipsis - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/ellipsis"},{"publisher":"Do - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/do"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d24-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Clause boundaries, commas and semicolons","text":"Clause boundaries, commas and semicolons\n\nPunctuation marks grammatical boundaries and guides interpretation. Some choices repair an incomplete or incorrectly joined structure; others offer legitimate stylistic alternatives. A pause in speech is evidence about rhythm, not a sufficient rule for inserting a comma.\n\nA full stop normally closes a complete written statement. A comma can separate list items, enclose supplementary material and set off many introductory elements. It does not normally join two independent clauses by itself in formal edited prose: The printer failed, we used another office is a comma splice. Write two sentences, use a semicolon or add a suitable coordinator. Choose the relationship you mean rather than treating all repairs as interchangeable: because introduces a cause, whereas and merely adds information.\n\nA semicolon can link closely related independent clauses: The entrance was locked; the caretaker had gone home. A conjunctive adverb such as however does not become a coordinating conjunction. The entrance was locked; however, the side gate remained open is one defensible pattern. A semicolon can also separate complex list items that already contain commas. When an introductory subordinate clause precedes the main clause, a comma is common, particularly if it prevents a misreading. Short introductory adjuncts sometimes appear without one, depending on house style and clarity.\n\nCommas around a supplementary relative clause are normally paired when the main clause continues: The director, who had just arrived, opened the meeting. A defining relative identifying the intended member ordinarily has no such commas. Punctuation can therefore change reference, not merely rhythm. Do not separate a subject from its verb simply because the subject is long; internal commas may still enclose a parenthetical within that subject. The board, after a long discussion, approved the proposal has two commas performing that enclosing job. A comma before a coordinator may be omitted in short clear clauses or added to prevent confusion.\n\nA punctuation judgement must specify genre and intended analysis. Published literary prose sometimes uses deliberate fragments or comma splices for an identifiable effect; this does not make them a safe default for a formal answer. Conversely, a stylistic comma is not automatically an error. In cumulative editing, first identify every clause and the relation between clauses, then examine marks. A semicolon cannot turn a subordinate fragment into an independent clause. Because the lift was broken; we took the stairs remains faulty for the intended reason relationship. Write Because the lift was broken, we took the stairs instead.\n\nindependent clause ; independent clause: Links two complete, closely related statements.\n\nintroductory subordinate clause , main clause: A common clear boundary; short clauses allow some convention-dependent flexibility.\n\nThe results arrived. We checked the totals.\nTwo full stops separate complete statements.\n\nThe results arrived, but the annex was missing.\nBut coordinates two clauses and expresses contrast.\n\nThe teams came from Multan, Punjab; Sukkur, Sindh; and Quetta, Balochistan.\nSemicolons clarify complex list items containing internal commas.\n\nA short opening phrase may appear without a comma: In June we moved. Add one when it aids interpretation; do not declare one version universally ungrammatical.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=24&section=gv5-d24-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: punctuation","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/punctuation"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d24-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Colons, apostrophes and compact written forms","text":"Colons, apostrophes and compact written forms\n\nA colon introduces an explanation, elaboration, list or quotation within an appropriate written structure. An apostrophe marks certain possessive and omitted-letter forms; it is not a general mark of plurality.\n\nA colon often follows a complete introductory clause: We need three items: a torch, a map and a whistle. Avoid separating an ordinary verb from its required object just to announce a list: The items include: a torch is unnecessary punctuation in continuous prose. Write The items include a torch, a map and a whistle. After the colon, material can be a phrase rather than a full clause. The punctuation signals how that material elaborates the introduction, so a colon differs from a semicolon joining independent clauses.\n\nFor a singular possessive, a common convention adds apostrophe-s: the writer’s desk. Regular plural possessives ending in s place the apostrophe after that ending: the writers’ desks. Irregular plurals take apostrophe-s: the children’s drawings. Its is possessive without an apostrophe, while it’s contracts it is or it has. Ordinary plurals and dates take no apostrophe: five MPs, the 1990s. Some style guides use apostrophes for single-letter plurals such as dot your i’s; that special convention does not justify writing three file’s.\n\nJoint and separate relationships need meaning rather than an automatic last-name rule. Ana and Omar’s shared flat presents one jointly held flat; Ana’s and Omar’s separate flats explicitly mark two relationships. Awkward expressions can be recast as the flat shared by Ana and Omar. Singular names ending in s admit house-style variation: James’s notebook and James’ notebook both occur in established editorial systems. Possessive relationships are broader than ownership, as in yesterday’s meeting. Contractions such as don’t and we’ve are grammatical; a formal assignment may prefer uncontracted forms without turning contractions into errors.\n\nNumerals, initials and abbreviations also involve convention. A publication may choose 8 October 2026 or October 8, 2026; apply one consistent system and avoid ambiguous numerical dates where readers use different orders. Dr and Dr. belong to different punctuation conventions, not different standards of intelligence. Introduce an unfamiliar initialism in full before relying on it. The plural normally adds s, while a possessive adds the relevant apostrophe: two NGOs and an NGO’s report. Decimal separators, units and percentage spacing can depend on disciplinary style; preserve a source’s numerical meaning when changing its presentation.\n\ncomplete introduction : elaboration: The second unit expands or specifies the first.\n\nregular plural ending in -s + apostrophe: Marks a possessive relationship involving a regular plural noun.\n\nOne difficulty remained: the missing signature.\nThe noun phrase after the colon explains the difficulty.\n\nThe teachers’ comments were attached to the children’s work.\nTeachers is regular plural; children is an irregular plural.\n\nIt’s evident that the NGO has changed its timetable.\nIt’s expands to it is, while its is possessive.\n\nApostrophes in single-letter plurals are allowed by some styles for clarity. Treat that as a limited editorial convention, not a rule for every plural.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=24&section=gv5-d24-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"},{"publisher":"Possession ( John’s car, a friend of mine ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/possession-john-s-car-a-friend-of-mine"},{"publisher":"MLA Style Center: Apostrophes, One Mark, Three Ways","url":"https://style.mla.org/apostrophes-three-ways/"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: abbreviations, contractions and acronyms","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/abbreviations"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d24-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Quotation, interruption and sentence-final marks","text":"Quotation, interruption and sentence-final marks\n\nQuotation marks identify quoted wording or another explicitly defined editorial use. Parentheses, brackets, dashes, hyphens and ellipses do different jobs; none is a general substitute for any inconvenient punctuation.\n\nA direct question normally ends with a question mark: Has the office reopened? An indirect report normally ends with the mark appropriate to the whole sentence: She asked whether the office had reopened. Do you know whether it has reopened? is itself a question and takes a question mark. An exclamation mark signals an exclamatory expression or emphatic utterance. Multiple exclamation marks occur in informal messages, but restrained formal writing normally avoids them. That preference concerns register; it is not evidence that informal messages must obey a ban on repeated marks.\n\nSingle or double outer quotation marks can both be used under established styles. When a quotation contains another quotation, switch the inner pair to distinguish levels. Punctuation placement depends partly on whether a mark belongs to the quoted words and partly on the chosen convention. British logical placement and common American practice differ for commas and full stops. Question marks should reflect the intended question: Did she say “Leave now”? asks about the saying; She asked, “May I leave?” quotes a question. Do not add a second full stop mechanically after the closing quotation.\n\nRound parentheses enclose an aside; square brackets in a quotation commonly identify editorial additions or clarifications. The brackets must not make an uncertain interpretation look like the speaker’s original wording. Dashes can mark interruption, amplification or a strongly separated parenthesis. A hyphen joins elements within some compounds: a three-year project. It is shorter and has a different function from a dash. The need for hyphens varies by position and convention: a well-known author but the author is well known. An -ly adverb normally does not need a hyphen in a clearly written modifier such as a carefully planned route.\n\nAn ellipsis can mark an omission from quoted text or a trailing-off effect in an appropriate genre. Omitting words must not change the represented claim. Removing not from a quotation is a substantive distortion even if an ellipsis is printed. A complete omitted sentence and an omitted phrase may be punctuated differently by different style guides; consult the specified guide for the number and spacing of dots. Parenthetical material inside a sentence normally leaves the main sentence’s ending outside the closing parenthesis; a complete independent parenthetical sentence may retain its own final mark inside. Analyse the enclosing structure before choosing the position.\n\nouter quotation + contrasting inner quotation: Keeps nested quoted material distinguishable.\n\nnumber + unit singular + noun, with a hyphenated modifier: A common pattern: a ten-minute break; compare the break lasted ten minutes.\n\nShe asked, “Is the archive open?”\nThe question mark belongs to the quoted question.\n\nThe archive closes at six (except on Fridays).\nThe parenthesis sits inside the sentence; its full stop follows the closing parenthesis.\n\nThe report described a carefully planned, three-stage operation.\nThe -ly modifier has no hyphen; three-stage is a compound premodifier.\n\nSome familiar compounds keep a dictionary hyphen even in positions where other compounds open up. Check a reliable dictionary and the required house style rather than inventing a universal positional rule.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=24&section=gv5-d24-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: punctuation","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/punctuation"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d24-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Capitalisation, spelling and editorial consistency","text":"Capitalisation, spelling and editorial consistency\n\nOrthography is the system of written forms, including spelling, capitalisation and word division. Some orthographic choices distinguish words; others vary legitimately between national and editorial conventions.\n\nCapitalise the first word of a normal written sentence, the pronoun I and proper names: Zainab, Pakistan, Urdu and October. A common noun does not become a proper noun merely because it is important: the committee announced the decision is a normal general description. A particular organisation’s official name can require capitals: the National Library. Titles before personal names often receive capitals, while general references often remain lower case. Check whether the wording is an official name, a descriptive phrase or a house-style abbreviation before changing its capitals.\n\nBritish and American spelling conventions differ in words such as colour/color, centre/center and travelled/traveled. Both standard systems are legitimate; this course uses British defaults and identifies American alternatives. British publishing itself permits more than one convention for some words, including forms in -ise and -ize. Do not label every -ize spelling American. Programme is a common British form for a plan or broadcast, while program is standard in computing. A consistent editing policy should still allow quoted material, names and technical forms to retain their authentic spelling.\n\nCapitalisation of headings and titles varies between sentence case and title case. A sentence-case heading need not capitalise every major word. Institutions and legal publications may prescribe forms such as Section 12, while another style uses section 12 in running prose. Names of languages keep capitals; ordinary names of academic fields often do not unless they form part of an official course title. Compound words may be open, closed or hyphenated, and usage changes over time: a dictionary entry and a publication’s stated style are better evidence than a remembered universal rule.\n\nSeparate error correction from editorial normalisation. Writing enviroment for environment is a spelling error; writing organization in a British document may match an established British house style. A question that asks for one correct spelling must avoid offering two standard variants unless it states the convention being tested. Numerical forms also need a stated purpose: scientific prose, dates, tables and literary prose often use different presentation rules. In your final editing pass, check the chosen system, official names, consistency and meaning. Do not remove capitals, hyphens or abbreviations blindly from specialist material merely because the general default differs.\n\nproper name → capitalisation appropriate to the name: Distinguishes an identified name from a general description.\n\nhouse style + dictionary evidence: Resolves legitimate variants without inventing grammatical prohibitions.\n\nThe department offers courses in chemistry and English.\nEnglish names a language; chemistry is a general field name here.\n\nThe colour of the laboratory’s sign has changed.\nColour follows the course’s British editorial default.\n\nThe software program supports the festival programme.\nThe two forms can coexist under a normal British computing/general distinction.\n\nOfficial names, quotations and stipulated specialist notation can retain a different convention within an otherwise consistent document.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=24&section=gv5-d24-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: words","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/words"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: abbreviations, contractions and acronyms","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/abbreviations"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d25-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Confusable words: meaning before substitution","text":"Confusable words: meaning before substitution\n\nConfusable words are expressions whose spelling, sound or associated meaning makes them easy to interchange. A reliable choice depends on sense, grammatical pattern and context; a simple noun-versus-verb shortcut is sometimes useful but not exhaustive.\n\nLearn a contrast inside a meaningful sentence. Advice is normally the noun for a recommendation; advise is the related verb: Your advice helped; please advise me. Lose means fail to retain or win, whereas loose commonly means not firmly attached: Do not lose the loose screw. Principal can mean main or the head of an institution; principle names a rule or belief. A spelling resemblance does not make two words interchangeable. Identify the intended message first, then choose the form and the complement pattern that expresses it.\n\nAffect is commonly a verb meaning influence, while effect commonly names a result. Those common patterns do not exhaust either word. Effect can be a verb meaning bring about: The agreement effected a change. Affect also has a specialised noun sense in psychology. Judicial concerns courts or judges; judicious means showing sound judgement. A judge’s decision can therefore be judicial even if it is unwise, and a judicious decision need not come from a court. Questions must provide enough context to distinguish the intended senses rather than pretend one adjective is always the better choice.\n\nOther useful contrasts include complement, which completes or fits something, and compliment, which expresses praise; imply, which presents an indirect suggestion, and infer, which draws a conclusion from evidence. Economic concerns an economy; economical describes sparing use of resources. Farther and further overlap in distance uses, while further also clearly means additional. Continual often highlights repetition and continuous an unbroken process, but contexts and established senses overlap. Do not turn a useful tendency into an absolute rule when a reliable dictionary records a broader range.\n\nLexical diagnosis differs from proving a syntactic error. An unusual but meaningful word may be grammatical; a near-synonym can make a false or inappropriate claim without breaking syntax. Evaluate three questions separately: Is the form possible? Does it express the intended sense? Does it suit the register? In an objective item, the supplied definition or situation should eliminate alternative senses. In original writing, explain a proposed replacement. Replacing judicial with judicious merely because the latter sounds approving can change a legal classification into an evaluation; that is not a meaning-preserving correction.\n\nadvise + person + to-infinitive: A common advice-giving verb pattern.\n\neffect + noun phrase: The verb can mean bring about, as in effect a change.\n\nThe adviser offered practical advice.\nThe person noun and uncountable advice belong to different patterns.\n\nThe reforms affected prices, but their effects differed across districts.\nAffected means influenced; effects names results.\n\nThe mediator effected an agreement after a judicious intervention.\nEffected means brought about; judicious evaluates judgement rather than identifying a court function.\n\nEffect is not restricted to noun use: To effect a repair means to bring it about. Affect has a specialised noun use; use a dictionary to identify its disciplinary sense.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=25&section=gv5-d25-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"ADVISE | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/advise"},{"publisher":"AFFECT | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/affect"},{"publisher":"EFFECT | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/effect"},{"publisher":"judicial","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/judicial"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d25-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Lexical patterns and preposition choices","text":"Lexical patterns and preposition choices\n\nLexical grammar concerns the grammatical patterns selected by particular words and senses. A preposition may be required in one pattern while a different construction uses no preposition or a different complement.\n\nCompare discuss the proposal with a discussion about the proposal. The verb discuss ordinarily takes its topic as a direct object; the related noun discussion has other complement patterns. Learn words with example frames instead of memorising a single preposition beside an isolated dictionary form. Depend on the result is one common pattern, but depend can also occur without an expressed complement when the context supplies it. An incomplete classroom rule such as every verb must take its memorised preposition can therefore create mistakes.\n\nInsist illustrates why a lexical pattern is not an absolute following-word rule. A speaker can insist on a receipt, insist on checking the figures, insist that the bill is wrong, or insist that the figures be checked. The choice affects form and sometimes meaning: insisting that a claim is true asserts it firmly, whereas insisting that an action be done demands that action. Capable of handling the task contrasts with able to handle the task. Similar meanings do not license the same complementation after every adjective.\n\nPrepositions can also change a relationship rather than merely distinguish right from wrong. An increase in prices identifies what increases; an increase of ten per cent measures its amount. Inform someone of a decision and inform someone about a new service are both established patterns. Between and among depend on the relationship being expressed; between can identify distinct relationships involving more than two participants, so the number of names alone does not settle every case. Check a dictionary’s sense and examples, especially when a purported correction declares an unfamiliar combination impossible.\n\nMany restrictions belong to a particular sense, construction or register. Compare consist of with be composed of; active comprise and passive be comprised of are established, although some editorial traditions prefer alternatives. A teacher can recommend comprises for economy without marking every comprised of as a grammatical error. Identify whether the problem is a missing complement, an incompatible form or a stylistic preference. When several constructions express the same message, preserve the intended participants, direction and scope. Replacing a preposition mechanically may change the relationship even if the result remains grammatical.\n\ninsist on + noun/-ing: A demand or firm requirement focused through on.\n\ninsist (that) + finite clause: A firmly asserted proposition or a demanded action, depending on clause form and context.\n\nWe discussed the revised timetable.\nDiscuss takes its topic as a direct object here.\n\nThe guide informed us about the new entrance and of the revised fee.\nBoth information patterns can be valid; neither preposition is universally forbidden.\n\nShe insisted that the committee should reconsider the evidence.\nA standard British mandative pattern uses should in a that-clause.\n\nA common pairing does not exclude other clause patterns: insist on a receipt and insist that a receipt be issued are both standard.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=25&section=gv5-d25-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"INSIST | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/insist"},{"publisher":"INFORM | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/inform"},{"publisher":"Between or among?","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/between-or-among"},{"publisher":"BETWEEN | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/between"},{"publisher":"Consist, comprise or compose?","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/consist-comprise-or-compose"},{"publisher":"Prepositions - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositions"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d25-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Countability, agreement and national alternatives","text":"Countability, agreement and national alternatives\n\nCountability and agreement are grammatical choices associated with a noun’s sense and the construction in which it occurs. National conventions can favour different choices without making all alternatives errors.\n\nIn ordinary general English, information, advice, furniture and equipment are uncountable. Write some information or two pieces of information, not two informations for that intended sense. The restriction belongs to English usage, not to whether the real-world objects can be counted. A piece, item, study or other appropriate unit can express an individual instance. Other nouns change their countability with meaning: paper can be a material, while a paper can be an article or examination. Learn the intended sense and a natural counting expression together.\n\nResearch is normally uncountable for an activity or body of investigation: extensive research. Plural researches is also established, especially when separate investigations are in view, but it is less frequent and is not an ordinary substitute for studies in every context. A standard learner explanation should acknowledge such documented uses without encouraging an unnatural plural in every research context. Agreement also depends on analysis: the news is singular in standard use; the scissors are plural; a pair of scissors is singular because pair heads that noun phrase. A measurement can be treated as one amount: Ten kilometres is a long walk.\n\nCollective nouns such as committee can take singular unit agreement or plural member agreement in British English: The committee has issued its decision; The committee are divided in their opinions. American edited usage more often favours singular agreement for the unit. Consistency of reference is useful, but an overt switch to members can justify different number. Data admits plural and mass-noun agreement under established usage and disciplinary conventions. The data are incomplete and The data is incomplete therefore need a stated editorial context before a test can require one form exclusively.\n\nA national comparison must specify what varies. American English often permits a past simple with just, already or yet in contexts where a British default favours present perfect; it also has gotten in many get constructions. These are established alternatives, not automatic tense mistakes. Conversely, choosing a national label cannot justify a malformed auxiliary sequence such as has went. Fewer is a common edited choice for individually counted plural items; less is standard with measurements and amounts, as in less than ten miles. The rule concerns how the quantity is construed, not just the visible plural ending.\n\nuncountable noun → quantity expression + of + noun: Expresses separate units without inventing an ordinary plural of the mass noun.\n\ncollective unit/member interpretation → suitable agreement: Regional convention and intended reference affect the available choices.\n\nThe office bought three items of equipment.\nItems supplies the countable units; equipment remains uncountable.\n\nThe committee are exchanging their individual views.\nBritish member-oriented agreement is appropriate in this context.\n\nLess than eight kilometres remains to be surveyed.\nLess than treats the distance as an amount; a plural unit word does not by itself require fewer.\n\nPlural researches is documented in particular contexts, but research is normally uncountable in ordinary academic statements. Do not treat a historical or specialist sense as the everyday default.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=25&section=gv5-d25-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Nouns: countable and uncountable - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-countable-and-uncountable"},{"publisher":"Nouns: singular and plural - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-singular-and-plural"},{"publisher":"Subject–verb agreement - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subject-verb-agreement"},{"publisher":"data","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/data"},{"publisher":"RESEARCH | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/research"},{"publisher":"research noun - Definition, pictures, pronunciation and usage notes | Oxford Advanced Learner's Dictionary at OxfordLearnersDictionaries.com","url":"https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/definition/english/research_1"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d25-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Register, regional English and evidence-based editing","text":"Register, regional English and evidence-based editing\n\nRegister is language choice associated with purpose, audience and situation. A form can be grammatical and standard in a regional variety while a different expression is clearer for an unfamiliar international audience.\n\nBegin an edit by identifying the intended reader. A message to a friend, a laboratory report and an administrative request have different expectations. Formality does not require rare words, constant passive voice or avoidance of every contraction in every genre. A direct active sentence can be entirely formal: The committee rejected the bid. Neutral clear language is often the most effective default. If an assignment asks for a particular register, revise toward that register while distinguishing the preference from a claim of universal grammatical incorrectness.\n\nSome learners find English articles, countability, auxiliary patterns or prepositions difficult because their other languages organise these relationships differently. Urdu-speaking learners may recognise particular translation habits, but no individual’s error profile should be guessed from first-language identity. For example, practise He has a question rather than He has question, and Did she attend? rather than Did she attended? Explain the English pattern itself. Do not present every regional expression as evidence of deficient thought or assume that all learners share one background.\n\nPrepone is recorded in Indian English with the meaning move to an earlier time. For readers unfamiliar with it, bring forward the meeting can communicate more widely. That editorial decision does not prove prepone nonexistent. A request for someone’s good name may be a regional politeness formula; good name also has the established reputation sense in protect the organisation’s good name. Context therefore matters before a word pair is labelled wrong. Likewise, American insure can mean ensure; a British default may reserve insure mainly for insurance without declaring that broader American use invalid.\n\nUse a transparent editorial vocabulary. Ungrammatical for the intended standard pattern identifies a structural problem; unusual identifies frequency or idiomaticity; informal identifies register; regional identifies distribution; misleading identifies an interpretation problem. These labels can overlap, but none should stand in for evidence. If a sentence is already grammatical, say so and explain any optional revision. A source register should identify the authority and the sense actually checked. Newly authored practice should never claim to be an official past question or an examiner’s preference without authentic supporting records.\n\npurpose + audience + meaning → register choice: Selects wording for the communicative situation without inventing a universal grammar ban.\n\ndiagnosis label + evidence + optional alternative: Makes editing reasoning explicit and reviewable.\n\nPlease bring the appointment forward to Thursday.\nAn internationally clear paraphrase of prepone in its recorded regional sense.\n\nThe American guide advises readers to insure that the valve is closed.\nInsure can have a make-certain sense in established American use; ensure matches the course’s British default.\n\nThe department’s good name depends on honest reporting.\nGood name has a standard reputation meaning and is not inherently faulty.\n\nRegional labels describe use; they do not make every such form unsuitable in its own audience. A wider-audience alternative can be recommended explicitly as an editorial choice.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=25&section=gv5-d25-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Formal and informal language","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/formal-and-informal-language"},{"publisher":"prepone","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/prepone"},{"publisher":"insure","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/insure"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"},{"publisher":"Nouns: countable and uncountable - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-countable-and-uncountable"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d26-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Connecting sentences: cohesion, reference and recoverable omission","text":"Connecting sentences: cohesion, reference and recoverable omission\n\nCohesion consists of visible grammatical and lexical links across a text. Coherence is the interpretable connection among its ideas; a text can contain many linking words and still lack a sensible argument.\n\nRead two sentences together before judging their connection. A pronoun can carry forward an established participant: A courier arrived. She carried a sealed envelope. Repeating courier is not an error, but she is economical when its reference is clear. This and that can also refer to a whole previous event: The envelope was empty. This caused concern. A summary noun, such as this omission, can identify the intended aspect more precisely. It is useful when several earlier facts could compete, not compulsory after every this.\n\nBuild a reference chain by tracking a participant through noun phrases, pronouns and descriptions. A visitor, the visitor and she can form a chain. Do not vary technical labels just to avoid repeating a word: a permit, a licence and an authorisation may refer to different legal objects. Substitution replaces repeated material with a smaller expression: The red folder is damaged; use the blue one. Ellipsis leaves recoverable material unspoken: Mara can attend, but I cannot. The omitted content is attend, understood from the first clause.\n\nOmission has grammatical and interpretative limits. In The first room was warm; the second, cold, the missing was is recoverable in a coordinated comparison. In a detached formal sentence such as The second cold, it may instead look like a fragment. So can substitute for a proposition after suitable verbs: I think so. Do so commonly substitutes for an action: The clerk promised to check and did so. It is not interchangeable with it or one in every context. Choose the substitute by the size and kind of unit being replaced.\n\nReference can point backwards, forwards or outside the text. The first is often called anaphora, the second cataphora and the third deixis. A pronoun need not replace a single noun: propositional this can refer to a preceding clause or stretch of discourse. However, an editor should test whether readers recover the intended content rather than merely any plausible content. Cohesion also includes lexical repetition and relations such as part–whole. These support interpretation but do not establish truth or logical consequence. A therefore between unrelated claims creates a misleading inference rather than coherence.\n\nestablished noun phrase → pronoun: Maintains a participant when reference is identifiable.\n\nauxiliary + omitted verb phrase: Avoids repeating material recoverable from context.\n\nA lamp failed. It was replaced immediately.\nIt continues the lamp reference.\n\nThe first proposal was costly; the second, affordable.\nWas is recoverable in the second coordinated unit.\n\nThe supplier promised to replace the faulty part and did so.\nDid so substitutes for replacing the part, not for the supplier.\n\nInformal situational ellipsis such as Ready? is normal conversation, though a formal standalone report usually supplies the full clause.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=26&section=gv5-d26-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"It, this and that in paragraphs","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/it-this-and-that-in-paragraphs"},{"publisher":"Ellipsis - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/ellipsis"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d26-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Time, topics and the movement of information","text":"Time, topics and the movement of information\n\nTextual progression is the way a passage moves from established information to a developing point. Temporal consistency preserves intended time relationships, rather than requiring every verb to have one tense.\n\nA reader needs to know how the next sentence relates to the current one. A useful default is to begin from something already identifiable and then develop it: The school opened a workshop. The workshop offers evening classes. You can also repeat a subject deliberately to maintain a topic. Beginning with new information is legitimate when introducing a contrast or changing focus. The practical question is whether the sequence guides the intended reader, not whether every sentence follows one fixed template or starts with a different word.\n\nTransitions express relations such as addition, contrast, concession, consequence and example. They do not create those relations. The route was shorter; nevertheless, it took longer expresses an unexpected contrast. The route was shorter; therefore, it took longer would require extra circumstances to explain the result. Topic sentences often state a paragraph’s controlling idea, but can occur after an opening example or be implicit. A paragraph may contain several related claims serving one purpose. Organise supporting details so that the relationship among them remains visible.\n\nTense choices can change purposefully within a paragraph. The clinic opened in 2018 and now treats patients daily contrasts a completed opening with an ongoing practice. A research summary may use the past for methods and the present for a still-relevant interpretation. A past perfect can place an event before the narrative reference point, after which ordinary past forms may continue the sequence. Voice also changes legitimately to maintain the topic: We received a damaged instrument. It was repaired the next day. The passive keeps the instrument in view.\n\nTheme and rheme are terms used in some linguistic frameworks for the point of departure of a clause and its development. Given and new describe information status, while topic and focus describe what is discussed and what is foregrounded. These distinctions overlap but are not identical. A passive, cleft or fronted phrase can organise information without altering the underlying event. Changes can nevertheless affect implication, contrast or presupposition. When revising an extended passage, compare its entire information route; a locally concise active sentence may break the established chain that a passive sentence maintained more effectively.\n\npast event + now + present situation: Separates a completed event from a current state.\n\nclaim + nevertheless + contrasting outcome: Signals an outcome contrary to an expectation.\n\nThe roof leaked last winter. It is secure now.\nA justified past-to-present shift distinguishes then from now.\n\nAn old map was discovered. The map revealed a forgotten path.\nNew map information becomes the next sentence’s topic.\n\nThe laboratory rejected the first sample. A second sample was then requested.\nThe voice changes, but the sequence remains coherent.\n\nA repeated opening can create clear thematic continuity; variation is a rhetorical choice, not a requirement to avoid the same subject three times.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=26&section=gv5-d26-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"It, this and that in paragraphs","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/it-this-and-that-in-paragraphs"},{"publisher":"Past perfect simple ( I had worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/past-perfect-simple-i-had-worked"},{"publisher":"Present perfect simple ( I have worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-i-have-worked"},{"publisher":"Passive voice - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/passive-voice"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d26-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Academic compression, hedging and economical variety","text":"Academic compression, hedging and economical variety\n\nGrammatical compression expresses information with less explicit clausal structure; hedging adjusts commitment, scope or politeness. Neither compression nor formal vocabulary automatically improves a claim.\n\nA verb can describe an event directly: The council approved the plan. A noun can package it for later discussion: This approval was unexpected. That change is nominalisation. It is useful when an earlier action becomes a new topic, or when a technical field needs an established concept. It can also conceal agents: approval of the plan leaves the approver unnamed. Ask which form gives readers the information they need. There is no universal rule that nouns are weak and verbs are strong.\n\nCompress only material whose contribution you understand. Because the samples were contaminated, the team repeated the test can become Because of sample contamination, the team repeated the test. The noun phrase is shorter, but it no longer states the contamination as a finite clause. A reduced relative can save words: the files that were damaged becomes the damaged files, though position and scope may change. Dense noun strings can obscure relations: student travel safety review may need a phrase explaining whose travel and what is being reviewed.\n\nA cautious academic claim can mark uncertainty (may explain), evidential limits (the interviews suggest), frequency (often) or scope (in the sampled schools). These are different contributions. Removing one can exaggerate the evidence. A narrow claim is not necessarily well-supported; it still needs evidence. Likewise, a figure supplies precision but is not automatically a hedge. Several qualifications can be necessary if they perform distinct jobs: The small sample suggests that the change may help some learners. Avoid stacking synonyms that add no further distinction.\n\nSyntactic variety serves clarity when each structure fits its information task. Active clauses can identify actors; passives can maintain a topic or appropriately background an unknown actor; nominalisations can organise an argument; explicit clauses can unpack compressed relations. Register belongs to a genre and audience rather than a hierarchy of longer words. A first-person statement of method can be appropriate in academic prose if the discipline permits it. An editor should distinguish grammatical defects, unsupported assertions and optional stylistic changes, then preserve modality, attribution and logical relationships while improving the text.\n\nclause → nominalisation + complement: Packages an event for further discussion.\n\nevidence suggests that ... may ...: Separates evidential support from modal possibility.\n\nThe panel rejected the bid. This rejection delayed the project.\nThe noun packages the earlier action as a topic.\n\nThe interviews suggest that the policy may help new entrants.\nSuggest and may mark distinct aspects of commitment.\n\nBecause the labels were missing, the samples could not be identified.\nAn explicit causal clause can be clearer than a dense noun string.\n\nPurposeful repetition and a well-chosen passive may improve a text even when a shorter active alternative exists.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=26&section=gv5-d26-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Writing Concisely","url":"https://writingcenter.unc.edu/tips-and-tools/conciseness-handout/"},{"publisher":"Hedges ( just )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/hedges-just"},{"publisher":"Formal and informal language","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/formal-and-informal-language"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d27-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Summarising, précis and preserving essential qualifications","text":"Summarising, précis and preserving essential qualifications\n\nA summary selects a text’s central message; a précis is a disciplined concise restatement under specified length and presentation constraints. Neither is a licence to invent facts or strengthen a claim.\n\nBegin by identifying the source’s topic, main claim, supporting reasons and important limits. A summary is shorter because some detail is omitted, so the claim that every word or every fact must survive is unrealistic. What must survive is the source’s essential meaning at the requested level of detail. If the source says a measure may help some families, a summary saying the measure helps all families is inaccurate. A word target belongs to the assignment. One third is a common exercise convention, not a grammatical law for every summary.\n\nSeparate a proposition from its examples. If a passage lists several dishes only to illustrate a varied menu, dishes may replace the list. If the passage argues that those particular foods caused an allergy, the exact list may matter. Classification words must not add evaluation: wheat and rice can become crops, but major crops asserts importance not supplied by the list alone. Preserve a condition when it limits the claim: free entry for registered students is not equivalent to free entry. Decide what to omit by its role in the argument, not by length alone.\n\nUse grammatical compression carefully. The applicants who were selected becomes the selected applicants when the selection meaning and intended scope remain clear. Because the tank leaked can become because of a tank leak. The rewrite may lose tense or explicit participants unless the surrounding text supplies them. A decision to act does not entail action: decided to close the school cannot become closed the school merely to save words. Likewise, not all changes are reversals: failed to confirm is weaker than denied. Compare the original and revision proposition by proposition.\n\nFor an original reduction exercise, work from this fictional passage: A village library extended its Saturday opening for a six-week trial. Volunteers staffed the extra hour, so the council did not increase paid staffing. Visitor numbers rose during the trial, mainly among secondary-school pupils. However, the librarian warned that the period overlapped with examinations and was too short to show a lasting change. She recommended a longer trial before any permanent extension. A faithful brief summary reports the increased visits, volunteer arrangement and evidential caution. It must not claim the extension proved a permanent demand or that volunteers could necessarily sustain the arrangement indefinitely.\n\nsource claim + essential restriction → shorter equivalent: Retains who, what, scope and commitment while reducing wording.\n\nSelected applicants must attend an interview.\nThe reduced relative identifies the selected group.\n\nThe library’s trial coincided with examinations.\nThis qualification limits interpretation of increased visits.\n\nVolunteer staffing accompanied a temporary extension, but a longer trial was recommended.\nThe statement preserves the temporary arrangement and recommendation without claiming permanence.\n\nA short summary can omit illustrative detail while retaining essential meaning; a task demanding exact equivalence permits fewer omissions.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=27&section=gv5-d27-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Writing Concisely","url":"https://writingcenter.unc.edu/tips-and-tools/conciseness-handout/"},{"publisher":"Relative clauses - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/relative-clauses"},{"publisher":"Hedges ( just )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/hedges-just"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d27-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Paraphrase, synthesis and faithful reported claims","text":"Paraphrase, synthesis and faithful reported claims\n\nParaphrase re-expresses a source’s message; synthesis relates messages from more than one source. Both require grammatical accuracy and faithful treatment of time, modality, attribution and logical relations.\n\nA paraphrase can alter sentence structure, word choice and information order while preserving the relevant proposition. The team postponed the launch because the battery failed can become Battery failure caused the team to postpone the launch. Both identify the same actor, event and cause. Replacing words with dictionary synonyms without checking their grammatical patterns can produce errors. Depend on does not become depend from because another language uses a different relation word. Keep the source available while checking meaning, then use independent wording rather than copying its sentence and replacing a few words.\n\nTrack tense and aspect. The plant has closed presents closure with current relevance; the plant closed in March locates a completed event at a specified past time. Either can fit a paraphrase when the necessary time information is retained, but adding March without a source invents a fact. Track modality too: must may express an obligation or a deduction, while may can express permission or possibility. The school may close is not the same as the school must close. Scope matters: not every application succeeded means some failed, not that none succeeded.\n\nA synthesis must distinguish agreement, difference and silence. Source A says the scheme reduced travel time in two towns; Source B says ticket costs rose. A combined sentence can report both, but cannot claim costs rose because travel time fell unless a source establishes that relationship. Attribution can be compressed without vanishing: According to the survey ... or The authors argue ... identifies whose claim is reported. A reporting verb such as prove, admit or concede may add evidential or evaluative meaning absent from the source. Retain neutral said, report or find when stronger force is unjustified.\n\nMeaning preservation includes entailments and implications. A transformation that makes a restrictive relative supplementary may imply that every member of a group has the property. Removing only or changing the position of a negative can alter scope while leaving the sentence fluent. A paraphrase does not need to reproduce all rhetorical emphasis, but a task may require preserving contrast, caution or a writer’s stance. In expert revision, list the propositions before comparing sentences: actor, action, affected participant, time, quantifier, modality, source and connection. Where two readings remain possible, explain the ambiguity rather than certify a single equivalent mechanically.\n\nSource A reports X, whereas Source B reports Y: Makes differing attributed claims explicit.\n\nnot every + noun: Denies universality rather than asserting universal failure.\n\nThe report suggests that costs may fall.\nAttribution and possibility both remain visible.\n\nNot every licence was renewed.\nAt least one was not renewed; the sentence does not say none were.\n\nThe first survey found shorter journeys; the second recorded higher fares.\nThe synthesis relates results without inventing a causal link.\n\nA past source can be reported in a present reporting frame when its argument is treated as current; preserve the events’ actual time relations.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=27&section=gv5-d27-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Hedges ( just )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/hedges-just"},{"publisher":"Relative clauses - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/relative-clauses"},{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d27-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Translation and formal written answers","text":"Translation and formal written answers\n\nGrammatical translation conveys the intended message through the target language’s own constructions. Formal expression selects structures appropriate to a purpose and audience while retaining meaning.\n\nTranslation is not a word-by-word substitution exercise. Languages differ in word order, agreement, articles, pronouns, tense-aspect patterns and conventional combinations. Begin with a plain account of the intended meaning, including who acts and when. Then construct an English sentence from that meaning. A language without English-style articles does not supply an automatic equivalent for a and the; decide whether the English referent is countable and identifiable. Learners can use any first language for this analysis. Urdu-based examples are useful options, not assumptions about every learner’s background.\n\nFor a controlled translation exercise, the source message can be specified in plain meaning notes instead of requiring knowledge of another language. Message: speaker lives in Multan; residence began in 2019 and continues. Suitable English includes I have lived in Multan since 2019 and I have been living in Multan since 2019. The progressive highlights duration or ongoing activity; the simple perfect is also standard. A literal I am living ... since 2019 is not the ordinary edited pattern for that intended continuing interval. Check meaning and idiom rather than matching one model string.\n\nFormal correspondence needs clear participants, actions, dates and requested responses. Please send the revised form by Friday is often clearer than a chain of nominalisations. A form such as We request that the form be returned by Friday is also grammatical and may suit a formal institution. The audience determines register; first person, contractions and ordinary short verbs are not universal grammar errors. Regional expressions can be established within their communities while unfamiliar elsewhere. For international readers, choose transparent wording and explain why, instead of treating the regional speaker as grammatically incapable.\n\nAcademic answers must distinguish a source’s assertion from the writer’s interpretation. In The author argues that restrictions may reduce demand, the writer reports a cautious claim; changing may to will increases commitment. In translation and paraphrase, preserve negation, quantity, politeness, evidential status and temporal relations even if the new sentence uses a different voice. Back-translation can expose a mismatch, but it is not proof of exact equivalence because several expressions may encode a message. A good rubric recognises alternatives and evaluates specified features. It must not pretend that comparison with a model objectively grades an independently written passage.\n\nmeaning notes → idiomatic target construction: Uses English grammar to express the intended message.\n\nrequest + action + deadline: Makes formal correspondence actionable.\n\nPlease return the signed declaration by Thursday.\nThe action, document and deadline are explicit.\n\nI have lived in Multan since 2019.\nThe present perfect expresses continuing residence from a past starting point.\n\nThe authors argue that the policy may reduce waste.\nThe report preserves source and possibility rather than presenting a proven result.\n\nA regional expression can be standard in its variety; choosing a wider-audience alternative is an editorial decision, not proof of universal error.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=27&section=gv5-d27-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Formal and informal language","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/formal-and-informal-language"},{"publisher":"Present perfect simple ( I have worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-i-have-worked"},{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d28-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Diagnose the relationship before changing the words","text":"Diagnose the relationship before changing the words\n\nGrammatical error analysis identifies a problematic relationship, explains the applicable construction and checks whether a proposed repair is supported by the intended meaning. An unusual form is not itself proof of an error.\n\nRead the complete context before selecting a correction. Identify the main clauses, their subjects and finite verb phrases, then examine the relationships inside them. In The quality of the reports have improved, quality is the singular head of the subject and has is the conventional agreement repair. In The reports have improved in quality, the plural verb is already correct. The same vocabulary therefore supports different grammatical analyses. State the relationship that fails rather than pointing to the nearest unfamiliar word.\n\nSeparate a confirmed error from missing information. In Amina told Salma that her proposal was accepted, her can refer to either woman unless context settles the reference. The sentence is not structurally ungrammatical, but a report may need explicit naming. In The committee have submitted their views, British member-oriented agreement is possible, so a correction to has/its must be justified by intended unit reference or a specified editorial system. A diagnostic note should say whether the issue is agreement, reference, scope, complementation, punctuation, meaning or register.\n\nSome sentences contain more than one fault. The findings was presented very clear contains plural-subject agreement and an adjective–adverb problem for the intended description of presentation. Repairing only was leaves the second issue. Conversely, changing every marked expression can introduce errors or change tone unnecessarily. Use a staged record: original wording, intended meaning, diagnosed relation, minimum adequate repair, alternative repair and limiting exception. Minimum means sufficient to solve the stated problem, not always the fewest written characters or an obligation to retain a misleading structure.\n\nEvidence-sensitive analysis remains open to alternative frameworks. Traditional accounts may call an -ing construction a gerund phrase where a modern account analyses a non-finite clause. If both identify the same form, internal subject relationship and external function, terminology alone need not invalidate an answer. When authorities differ, ask whether they describe different registers, national conventions, senses or theoretical categories. Do not invent a source citation to make a judgement look final. Where context supports more than one correction, list the alternatives and state which meaning each preserves.\n\ncontext → structure → disputed relationship → repair → verification: Makes the reasoning behind an edit explicit.\n\none sentence → possibly several independent diagnoses: A task may contain multiple genuine faults; identify each rather than stopping automatically after one.\n\nThe list of entries is incomplete.\nSingular list controls is despite plural entries inside the of-phrase.\n\nThe officer found the account convincing.\nConvincing functions as an object complement describing the account.\n\nThe committee have discussed their individual objections.\nMember-oriented British agreement is defensible; unfamiliar plural agreement is not enough to prove error.\n\nA grammatical sentence can still be ambiguous, misleading or unsuitable for an audience. Diagnose that actual problem instead of inventing a syntactic fault.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=28&section=gv5-d28-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Subject–verb agreement - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subject-verb-agreement"},{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Nouns: singular and plural - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-singular-and-plural"},{"publisher":"Formal and informal language","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/formal-and-informal-language"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d28-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstruction with a meaning ledger","text":"Reconstruction with a meaning ledger\n\nA meaning ledger records the information a transformation must preserve: participants, event, time, polarity, quantity, modality and logical relationship. Reconstruction changes form while checking these features explicitly.\n\nStart with who did what, when and with what degree of commitment. Hana may submit the report tomorrow does not mean Hana will submit it, and Hana did not submit it does not mean that nobody did. When combining sentences, choose a connector justified by the source: The lights failed. The session ended early permits a simple and combination without proving that one caused the other. A because combination needs a supplied causal relationship. Sentence splitting must preserve a concession, condition or limitation rather than leaving two loosely associated claims.\n\nTransformations offer several structural resources: active and passive voice, finite and non-finite clauses, relative clauses, nominalisation, coordination and marked order. Each has restrictions. A passive normally needs a compatible verb construction, and it must retain an identified agent when that identity matters. The assessor rejected the appeal can become The appeal was rejected by the assessor. A shortened The appeal was rejected omits the named actor, so it may be unsuitable in an exact-content transformation. Fronting or clefting may preserve the central proposition while adding focus or a presuppositional effect.\n\nWord-order reconstruction requires hierarchy, not just arranging familiar neighbours. Identify which words form a noun phrase, which auxiliaries precede the lexical verb and which clause a modifier belongs to. Only the clerk checked the forms and The clerk checked only the forms place the focus restriction on different constituents. Keep a negative with the intended scope. Not all candidates passed allows some passes; no candidates passed excludes every pass. If a transformation silently exchanges these, grammatical fluency cannot repair the changed meaning.\n\nAfter writing a candidate reconstruction, compare its entailments with the source. Decided to leave does not entail left; advised her to leave does not entail that she obeyed; failed to leave does entail that the intended leaving did not occur. Avoid replacing an uncertain source with an authoritative-sounding nominalisation such as the proven failure. Some tasks demand only preservation of a central proposition, while others require close semantic equivalence. State which standard applies, especially when an inversion, cleft, nominalisation or passive changes emphasis, reference accessibility or assumed background.\n\nsource proposition + ledger → reconstructed structure + ledger check: Tests meaning as well as formal correctness.\n\nnot all X → partial negation; no X → total exclusion: The two quantificational claims are not equivalent.\n\nThe appeal was rejected by the assessor.\nThe passive preserves the named participants of The assessor rejected the appeal.\n\nAlthough costs rose, access improved.\nCombining the two claims with although presents an explicitly supplied concession.\n\nWhat the team needed was a longer trial.\nA pseudo-cleft foregrounds the requested remedy; it is not always pragmatically identical to neutral order.\n\nA grammatically successful transformation can legitimately change focus when the task asks for emphasis. A close-equivalence task must acknowledge that added effect rather than claim that nothing changes.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=28&section=gv5-d28-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Passive voice - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/passive-voice"},{"publisher":"Relative clauses - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/relative-clauses"},{"publisher":"Cleft sentences ( It was in June we got married .) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/cleft-sentences-it-was-in-june-we-got-married"},{"publisher":"Ellipsis - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/ellipsis"},{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d28-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Suspicious but grammatical: context and register","text":"Suspicious but grammatical: context and register\n\nA suspicious construction is one a learner expects to be wrong because it is unfamiliar, formal, informal or variable. A defensible judgement examines its licensing context and intended reading before applying a preferred rewrite.\n\nA sentence can already be correct even when a memorised classroom rule predicts trouble. This is the person I spoke to uses standard preposition stranding. It was me uses an ordinary standard object-form complement after be, while it was I belongs to a more formal style. Neither familiarity nor personal preference decides every grammatical question. When a task offers no change, consider it seriously. Identify a rule actually broken in the stated context; do not assume the exercise setter must have hidden a fault.\n\nMarked word order can be fully grammatical. Rarely have we seen such detail uses inversion after a fronted negative-frequency adjunct. Only Aisha knew the code has no inversion because only modifies the subject, whereas Only after the briefing did Aisha know the code fronts a restrictive adjunct. Reported questions use their own structure: By whom the document was signed remains unknown contains an embedded interrogative functioning as subject; it is not a direct question requiring Was the document signed ...? The whole clause’s function determines the pattern.\n\nRegional, register and contextual alternatives need explicit labels. The committee are divided is acceptable British member agreement. I have just finished and the American I just finished can both be standard in suitable contexts. Mandative should in They insisted that he should attend is standard British usage. Informal or ordinary object who does not become automatically wrong because the more formal whom exists. An editor can still choose a more formal variant for a specified purpose, but that decision should be described as an editorial preference rather than a correction of universal grammar.\n\nSome apparent anomalies depend on a different lexical sense. She is being difficult gives a temporary behavioural reading, so a blanket prohibition on progressive be or all stative expressions fails. The researcher effected a change uses a legitimate verb meaning brought about. Historical prescriptive slogans can be useful reminders in limited contexts, but a reliable analysis identifies those limits. In a formal examination practice item, avoid giving two standard variants and asking for the only correct one unless the convention or meaning is supplied. In open analysis, explain why no repair is necessary and offer an optional recast separately.\n\nmarked construction + licensing context: An unusual order may be required or licensed by a recognised construction.\n\nstandard variant + stated register/region: A different preferred form does not automatically make the source incorrect.\n\nRarely did the visitors complain.\nNegative-adjunct fronting licenses auxiliary inversion.\n\nBy whom the permit was issued remains uncertain.\nThe embedded interrogative is the subject of remains; its internal order is defensible.\n\nThe committee are considering their separate objections.\nPlural member reference is licensed in British English.\n\nA stated specialist or house-style rule can require one standard variant for that assignment. Explain its scope rather than extending it to all English.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=28&section=gv5-d28-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Inversion - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/inversion"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"},{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"Nouns: singular and plural - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-singular-and-plural"},{"publisher":"EFFECT | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/effect"},{"publisher":"Formal and informal language","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/formal-and-informal-language"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d28-m04","category":"grammar","title":"Paragraph editing and explicit editorial reasoning","text":"Paragraph editing and explicit editorial reasoning\n\nParagraph editing examines local sentence grammar together with reference, temporal relations and logical connections across the text. A successful edit preserves the author’s supportable message while making the expression accurate and clear.\n\nRead a paragraph once for its intended message before repairing details. Identify the participants and events, then check whether pronouns, tense choices and connectors allow a reader to track them. The goal is not to make every verb share one tense. A current report can explain an earlier trial and a future recommendation. Nor must every paragraph use one voice. An active sentence may introduce an actor while a passive sentence continues discussion of the affected object. Explain a change in terms of the text’s information, not a general ban.\n\nUse a worked original paragraph. Draft: The library tested a new booking system last month. Its users was asked to reserve seats online, and several advice were posted near the desk. The report suggests that the system may reduce waiting, although only twelve visitors answered the survey. The team will collect more evidence before it makes a permanent decision. A restrained edit changes users was to users were and several advice were to several pieces of advice were. It keeps may, although and only twelve because those words limit the claim.\n\nClassify each change. Users/were is agreement; pieces of advice repairs ordinary countability and preserves plurality through pieces; may is modal uncertainty; only twelve identifies a small response group without proving that no other visitors existed. Do not change suggests/may into proves/will merely to sound confident. The last sentence’s future form is compatible with the past trial and present report. Before it makes uses a present form in an ordinary future time clause. These relationships show why a grammatical paragraph can contain several time references.\n\nAn editorial report should quote the problematic wording, explain the principle, give a corrected form and identify alternatives when meaningful. Other valid repairs of several advice include some advice was or several recommendations were; the choice changes how explicitly separate items are counted. A good rubric recognises those alternatives and requires preservation of the source’s caution. If a source passage is unclear, state the ambiguity and ask for its intended reference in a real editing workflow. In a self-study task, present conditional repairs rather than silently deciding who a pronoun means or inventing a missing cause.\n\nparagraph meaning → local repairs → cross-sentence recheck: Protects the source’s central message during editing.\n\nquoted fault + principle + model + alternative: Makes a correction defensible and useful for revision.\n\nThe users were asked to reserve seats online.\nPlural users controls were; the passive is appropriate because the users remain the topic.\n\nSeveral pieces of advice were posted near the desk.\nPieces supplies countable units and plural agreement; advice remains uncountable.\n\nThe report suggests that the system may reduce waiting.\nPresent reporting and modal caution can coexist with an earlier past-time trial.\n\nAn editor can restructure substantially when the source permits several meanings, but must state the assumed interpretation. Minimum correction does not require preserving harmful ambiguity.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=28&section=gv5-d28-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Subject–verb agreement - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subject-verb-agreement"},{"publisher":"Nouns: countable and uncountable - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-countable-and-uncountable"},{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"},{"publisher":"Hedges ( just )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/hedges-just"},{"publisher":"It, this and that in paragraphs","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/it-this-and-that-in-paragraphs"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d29-m01","category":"grammar","title":"Recognise the structure, then justify the interpretation","text":"Recognise the structure, then justify the interpretation\n\nAn integrated grammar assessment samples several related abilities: recognising a form, explaining its meaning, applying it in context and revising or producing language. A selected answer supplies evidence about the item attempted, not every possible use of its grammatical topic.\n\nBegin with the whole message before inspecting its parts. In The files that the auditor requested have arrived, the subject is a noun phrase headed by files; the relative clause adds information inside that phrase. Have agrees with files, while requested belongs to the embedded clause. Naming only the nearest noun would miss the structure. For each objective item, identify what its wording asks: a word class, a grammatical function, a suitable form or a justified interpretation. These are different questions, even when they use the same example sentence.\n\nA contextual gap asks which form fits the situation stated, not which form you have memorised after a particular signal word. Since can introduce a starting point or a reason; by can locate a deadline without mechanically requiring a future perfect. Read the supplied interpretation and surrounding clauses. Where an item asks for an error, keep a genuine no-error possibility available. A familiar-looking construction may be standard in a specified variety or register. Conversely, understanding the intended message does not make every word form or clause boundary acceptable in edited prose.\n\nA difficult analysis may require more than one level. In Whether the committee recommended closure remains unclear, the initial interrogative clause functions as subject of remains. The order inside it is not direct-question inversion. The content of that clause can still be analysed into its own subject, verb and object relationship. Separate this hierarchical analysis from stylistic preference. A shorter paraphrase might be easier for a particular audience, but that does not establish a grammatical defect in the original. An answer explanation should identify the relevant relationship and show why competing interpretations fail in the stated context.\n\nTreat an objective key as a claim that must survive scrutiny. If two options appear defensible, state their readings and compare them with the question’s explicit conditions; do not invent an examiner’s preference. Some grammatical differences are genuine alternatives, while others become equivalent only after a meaning change. This assessment uses a British editorial default and identifies established alternatives where they matter. Its difficulty labels describe the intended depth of reasoning, not calibrated psychometric levels. When you self-review an unfamiliar analysis, distinguish a mistaken label from a faulty interpretation and from an unsupported rule; the revision action should address the actual gap.\n\nphrase category → grammatical function → interpretation: Three related questions that should not be collapsed into one label.\n\ncontext + form + contrast: Use all three when justifying a gap answer.\n\nThe minutes were circulated.\nMinutes is a plural noun here and the whole noun phrase is the subject of a passive clause.\n\nThe minutes that Salma revised were circulated.\nThe subject contains a relative clause; revised does not control the main-clause agreement.\n\nWhether the revised minutes were circulated remains uncertain.\nThe initial interrogative clause functions as subject of remains; its own subject is the revised minutes.\n\nAn indirect question need not contain a visually different order from its corresponding subject question: who arrived remains who arrived. Analyse function rather than demanding a mechanical word swap.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=29&section=gv5-d29-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"},{"publisher":"Subject–verb agreement - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subject-verb-agreement"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d29-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Transform, edit and write without distorting the message","text":"Transform, edit and write without distorting the message\n\nA meaning-preserving transformation changes a requested structure while retaining the relevant participants, time, modality, quantity and logical relationships. Editing also requires deciding which changes are necessary and which are optional improvements for the specified audience.\n\nWrite a small meaning ledger before a substantial revision. Record who did what, when it happened, how certain the statement is and which conditions or limits apply. The council may extend some routes if funding continues is not equivalent to The council will extend all routes. The second version changes possibility, quantity and condition at once. When transforming an active clause into a passive, preserve the tense and aspect rather than copying only a participle: has approved becomes has been approved, with agreement adjusted to the new subject. Include the agent where the task requires that information.\n\nAn open correction can have several defensible solutions. The report arrived late, the review continued can become two sentences, a semicolon-linked sentence or an appropriately coordinated sentence. The choice should preserve the intended relationship. Adding therefore would assert a result relationship that the original did not supply. A model solution is one worked demonstration, while a rubric lists the features that a different answer must satisfy. Use the rubric to compare your response, and explain legitimate alternatives rather than attempting to match every word of the model.\n\nParagraph editing adds reference and information flow to sentence-level accuracy. Resolve an ambiguous pronoun by identifying its intended referent before replacing it; do not decide who acted merely because one reading is easier to rewrite. Check that reduced clauses retain their intended subjects, comparisons compare comparable things, and connectors describe the relationship supported by the passage. Quotation and capitalisation conventions can reflect house style, so a consistent alternative may be acceptable. In formal original writing, make grammar serve the explanation: a clear short clause may communicate better than an unnecessary cleft, inversion or nominalisation.\n\nTransformation is not a promise of complete synonymy. Active and passive versions can organise information differently, and a cleft can introduce focus or a presupposed background. A grammatical transformation may therefore preserve core event content while altering discourse effect. State that limitation when relevant. The assessment asks for particular invariants so that a response can be judged fairly. If the proposed change cannot preserve them, explain the restriction rather than forcing a superficially similar sentence. Independent production must also be reviewed for appropriate commitments and coherent argument; an accurate collection of isolated forms is not automatically a successful paragraph.\n\noriginal meaning ledger → requested form → invariant check: A practical method for preserving important content.\n\nfinite clause → reduced construction: Check time, subject interpretation and information loss before accepting compression.\n\nThe panel has accepted the revised timetable.\nIts present-perfect passive is The revised timetable has been accepted by the panel.\n\nSome evening sessions may be cancelled if demand remains low.\nA summary must retain limited quantity, possibility and the condition.\n\nAfter Naila inspected the valve, the leak was repaired.\nThe finite after-clause explicitly identifies the inspector; the passive main clause need not share that subject.\n\nA passive main clause is compatible with an introductory finite clause having its own subject, as in After the engineer arrived, the test was repeated. Passivity alone does not create a dangling modifier.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=29&section=gv5-d29-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Passive voice - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/passive-voice"},{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"Cleft sentences ( It was in June we got married .) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/cleft-sentences-it-was-in-june-we-got-married"},{"publisher":"Conditionals: if - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conditionals-if"},{"publisher":"Hedges ( just )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/hedges-just"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d29-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Read the evidence report and choose a precise revision action","text":"Read the evidence report and choose a precise revision action\n\nA topic-level performance report organises evidence by the underlying grammatical concepts and abilities sampled. Completion, first-response accuracy, repeated-item retrieval and writing self-review are separate records and support different conclusions.\n\nAfter attempting the examination, list the concepts behind errors before deciding what to reread. A score of twenty correct answers does not reveal whether the remaining difficulties concern reference, tense or punctuation. Link each error to its explanation and lesson, then state the distinction in your own words. Record a written task as attempted when you have produced a response; record its self-review separately after comparing it with the rubric. A model reveal is useful feedback, but it cannot establish that your original response was independently accurate.\n\nKeep first responses separate from later retries. An answer selected after reading the explanation is evidence of a learning activity, not another unfamiliar item. A later spaced success may show retention of that particular distinction, especially when you can explain why it works. However, repeated recognition does not establish control in original writing. For an agreement weakness, combine a fresh subject analysis, a correction task and an original sentence. For a cohesion weakness, review an extended passage rather than relying only on a single-gap item about a connector.\n\nCompare ability profiles as well as topic labels. A learner may identify a passive correctly but change its tense while transforming a sentence, or choose the correct article in a gap while omitting it in a paragraph. These are different application demands. Look at the size and variety of the evidence before making a broad claim: one difficult error is not proof of failure across an entire domain, and one easy success is not mastery. Difficulty labels are instructional judgements. The course does not claim that the labels or raw counts convert directly into official examination marks, university grades or CEFR levels.\n\nA defensible report includes limits. The bank samples selected forms and interpretations; it cannot exhaust every lexical restriction, dialect or writing situation. Self-reviewed open responses depend on the learner’s judgement, while objective keys assume the context stated in each item. Use the report to set an observable next action: revise the countability contrast, explain two new examples and attempt a different short response before a scheduled retrieval. Retain the original error record so that improvement is not confused with rewriting the baseline. Continuing evaluation should gradually include unfamiliar language and independent written expression, not merely higher scores on remembered questions.\n\nerror → concept → different application → later retrieval: Links feedback to a specific revision action.\n\nfirst responses + repeated retrieval + written production: Separate evidence streams that should not be collapsed into one artificial mastery mark.\n\nFirst attempt: four of six agreement items correct.\nThis describes the sampled items; it does not certify agreement mastery.\n\nA repeated item was answered correctly after its explanation.\nThat response is useful practice, but is not a new independent question.\n\nA learner identifies a dangling opening and then writes three clear finite-clause alternatives.\nAnalysis and production supply complementary evidence about a practical correction skill.\n\nA single unusual but legitimate construction should not become an error simply because it conflicts with an oversimplified remembered rule. Recheck context, region and register before revising the report.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=29&section=gv5-d29-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"},{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Hedges ( just )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/hedges-just"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d30-m01","category":"grammar","title":"The capstone and a fair comparison with your starting point","text":"The capstone and a fair comparison with your starting point\n\nA capstone combines several abilities in an integrated task. A diagnostic comparison examines performance in related skill domains at two points; it does not automatically establish a calibrated gain in overall proficiency.\n\nKeep your initial diagnostic and the present responses as separate samples. Both include form and sentence analysis; nouns, determiners and reference; verbs, time, voice and modality; modification and relationships; conventions and usage; and discourse, editing and writing. The objective items use different sentences so that remembering an earlier answer is not the intended route to success. Some forms may nevertheless be familiar from study, which is a legitimate consequence of learning. Work through the context supplied and produce the written responses before consulting their models.\n\nA fair personal comparison begins with what was actually attempted. Three correct answers from four attempted items are not interchangeable with three correct answers from twelve attempted items. An unanswered item supplies no evidence of a correct response. Compare the grammatical distinctions behind mistakes and inspect your original writing, rather than treating a percentage as a complete description. The two assessments sample related domains, but their difficulty has not been statistically equated. Different item wording and production demands can influence results, so the report explicitly limits what its counts imply.\n\nIntegrated tasks can reveal gaps hidden by isolated recognition. A learner may select a correct modal in a gap yet remove uncertainty while writing a summary, or identify an object complement yet introduce a dangling opening during compression. Check form, function and intended interpretation together. A meaning-preserving correction should retain actors, time, quantity and logical relations; a formal alternative may change emphasis without being a required repair. Your written response can differ from the model if it meets the stated criteria. Explain that difference where a regional, register or terminology choice is relevant.\n\nMastery in the title names the aim of continuing development, not a certificate awarded by finishing thirty lessons. Evidence remains conditional on the tasks sampled, the amount of previous exposure and the reliability of self-review. Objective counts concern keyed items; an independent paragraph requires separate attention to grammatical accuracy, meaning, organisation and audience. If feedback was consulted before a response, record that limitation rather than presenting the response as unaided. A stronger capstone performance can justify a more challenging next task, but it does not prove complete control of every construction or predict an official examination result.\n\ncomparable domain + different item → cautious comparison: Related content supports reflection, while unequal difficulty limits numerical conclusions.\n\noriginal response → rubric comparison → reasoned self-review: Keeps production separate from model exposure.\n\nDiagnostic: two of four article items correct; capstone: three of four correct.\nThis is a difference on the sampled items, not a statistically calibrated article-proficiency gain.\n\nA learner selects might correctly but writes the result will occur in a summary of a possible outcome.\nRecognition and meaning-preserving production provide different evidence.\n\nThe final draft uses a passive to retain the instrument as topic.\nA justified discourse choice need not be replaced merely to increase the number of active verbs.\n\nA lower count in one domain can result from a more demanding sample. Inspect the actual errors and responses before interpreting it as a general loss of knowledge.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=30&section=gv5-d30-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"},{"publisher":"Passive voice - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/passive-voice"},{"publisher":"Hedges ( just )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/hedges-just"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d30-m02","category":"grammar","title":"Turn the topic profile into a specific continuing revision plan","text":"Turn the topic profile into a specific continuing revision plan\n\nA useful revision plan links a demonstrated difficulty to an explanation, a fresh application and a later retrieval opportunity. It responds to the evidence available rather than assigning an unsupported global proficiency label.\n\nStart with a precise description of the difficulty. Articles is a broad area; confusing indefinite introduction with identifiable reference is a usable revision target. Find the relevant lesson and reference entry, then explain the distinction beside two examples. Next attempt a different task that requires applying it. If the weakness concerns original writing, write a new short passage and inspect every relevant choice. Reading a lesson may improve understanding, but record it as study rather than as proof that the next unfamiliar use will be accurate.\n\nPrioritise recurring unresolved errors without ignoring new topics. An agreement problem caused by intervening phrases calls for subject analysis; a problem involving collectives also requires interpretation and variety. Do not apply one repair mechanically to both. Use the scheduled retrieval dates to revisit an item after an interval, and keep the original response in the record. A same-day retry after feedback is useful practice, but it should not become an additional spaced success. When a remembered item becomes easy, add a new context or a written explanation instead of relying exclusively on repetition.\n\nUse separate evidence streams when deciding what to do next. First-response recognition can indicate a distinction needing explanation; repeated retrieval can show retention of that sampled item; correction and transformation reveal how well you preserve relationships; original writing shows whether you can coordinate several choices at once. Self-review labels are your own judgements against a rubric. If a response remains uncertain, record the exact question, consult a reliable source and identify whether the uncertainty concerns grammar, region, register, style or intended meaning. An unfamiliar standard form should not be classified as an error merely to make the report appear decisive.\n\nA personalised plan can be modest and explicit. Select two priority concepts, schedule one unfamiliar response task for each, and set a separate extended-writing session. After reviewing that work, revise the plan using the new evidence. The number of days is a planning choice, not a promise about how quickly every learner acquires grammatical control. Preserve earlier samples so that improvement can be examined rather than reconstructed from memory. A strong plan also includes maintenance of areas that currently look secure and occasional work on unsampled constructions; absence of a recorded error is not evidence that a topic has been exhaustively tested.\n\nobserved difficulty → precise distinction → fresh task: Makes the revision action assessable.\n\nretained original evidence + new response: Allows improvement without rewriting the baseline.\n\nTarget: I add a pronoun inside an object relative.\nReview the gap in the report that I requested, then construct three new object relatives.\n\nTarget: I change may to will during compression.\nCompare the force of the two modals and write a summary retaining possibility.\n\nA scheduled item is answered correctly after two separated retrieval opportunities.\nThis is evidence about retention of that item, alongside rather than in place of independent writing.\n\nA learner with no recorded objective errors still needs unfamiliar production tasks. A clean notebook can reflect a small sample or little practice rather than complete proficiency.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=30&section=gv5-d30-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"A/an and the - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/a-an-and-the"},{"publisher":"Subject–verb agreement - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subject-verb-agreement"},{"publisher":"Nouns: singular and plural - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-singular-and-plural"},{"publisher":"Hedges ( just )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/hedges-just"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-d30-m03","category":"grammar","title":"Use the permanent reference and continue beyond the thirty-day path","text":"Use the permanent reference and continue beyond the thirty-day path\n\nA permanent grammar reference is a topic-organised resource for consultation and deeper study. The structured course is one route through it; completing the route does not end access or remove the need for continued reading, production and revision.\n\nUse the reference index to answer a real grammatical question. If a sentence contains an unfamiliar noun phrase, begin with the noun-phrase entry and its terminology, then follow prerequisite explanations as needed. The glossary supplies short definitions and examples; the full entry explains patterns, restrictions and contrasts. A quick rule can orient you, but a context-dependent question may require the deeper layer. Bookmark an entry when it helps with a recurring problem and write a note stating the distinction in your own words, rather than copying an isolated prohibition.\n\nChoose a continuing practice route that matches the question. The sentence analysis lab helps identify structures and functions. The transformation lab asks whether a requested change preserves time, participants and meaning. The correction and editing lab includes suspicious but grammatical examples, so leaving a form unchanged can be a reasoned answer. The usage guide compares established regional and register alternatives. Formal-writing practice connects grammar with qualification, cohesion and economy. These resources overlap deliberately through shared concepts; they are not different courses that a learner must complete separately.\n\nKeep reading and original production connected. Select a sentence from a text you are entitled to use, analyse its structure and imitate the pattern with new content. Do not assume that every published sentence illustrates the same register or editorial convention. When a source and a remembered rule conflict, check the grammatical context and the authority’s scope. A dictionary can resolve a lexical pattern; a broader grammar may be needed for syntactic analysis; a style guide can identify a house convention. Distinguish these purposes rather than turning a publisher’s preference into an absolute rule of English.\n\nContinuing development should include deliberate contact with unfamiliar contexts and independent written expression. A more technical analysis is useful when it explains a real contrast, but added terminology is not itself better writing. Conversely, a beginner-friendly explanation should remain compatible with its advanced qualification. Return to foundation layers when a prerequisite is unclear and open expert extensions when the deeper distinction matters. Keep saved drafts and earlier assessments as evidence of the work completed. The continuing goal is accurate, context-sensitive control: selecting a grammatical construction for the meaning and audience required, then being able to defend or revise that choice with appropriate evidence.\n\nquestion → topic entry → example/exception → application: Uses reference material to resolve an actual learning need.\n\nanalyse a pattern → create new content → check meaning: Connects reference study with original production.\n\nA writer is unsure whether team can take are.\nConsult collective agreement and regional interpretation rather than applying a blanket singular rule.\n\nA writer wants to reduce a report by forty words.\nCheck compression, reference and modality; preserve qualifications before deleting wording.\n\nA learner analyses an unfamiliar embedded question and writes a different one.\nThe new production tests whether the structural relationship can be transferred beyond a memorised example.\n\nA short note or conversational answer may legitimately omit material that a formal standalone paragraph needs. Consultation must take genre and context into account.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=30&section=gv5-d30-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Nouns: singular and plural - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-singular-and-plural"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"},{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"Ellipsis - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/ellipsis"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-ref-negation-scope","category":"grammar","title":"Negation, polarity and quantifier scope","text":"Negation, polarity and quantifier scope\n\nNegation rejects a proposition or a relevant part of its meaning. Scope is the portion of a structure or interpretation affected by an operator such as not, every or only. Surface word order is evidence for scope, but context and prosody can also matter.\n\nEnglish commonly forms a negative finite clause with not after an auxiliary: The clerk has not replied. Where an ordinary lexical verb has no auxiliary, do-support is normally used: The clerk did not reply. Be can itself carry negation: The office is not open. A negative subject can make the clause negative without an additional negative verb: Nobody replied. Do not add didn’t simply because the intended message is negative. The difference between no and not is structural as well as semantic: no can determine a noun phrase, as in no reply, whereas not commonly negates a clause or phrase. These examples describe ordinary standard constructions, not every historically or regionally established pattern.\n\nScope changes meaning. Not all applications were successful means that at least one was unsuccessful; it does not by itself assert that any succeeded. No applications were successful states zero success. In an everyday conversation, a speaker saying not all may encourage an inference that some did succeed, but that extra inference should not be built into an exact answer key without context. All the applications were not successful can be unclear between intended readings for some audiences; revise explicitly when the distinction matters. Quantified noun phrases can also interact: Every reviewer read a report can allow a different report for each reviewer or one report shared by all. A careful paraphrase states the intended relationship.\n\nAny, ever and related expressions occur naturally in many negative, interrogative and conditional environments: I did not find any errors; Did you find any?; If you find any, call me. They are not exclusively negative words. Free-choice any in Any qualified applicant may apply has a different distribution and interpretation. Negative concord, as in dialectal I didn’t see nobody with one intended negation, is a systematic feature of some varieties; it is not ordinarily the edited standard British or American pattern taught as the course default. For that default, use I didn’t see anybody or I saw nobody. Distinguish this concord from two independently meaningful negatives, such as It is not impossible.\n\nLogical paraphrase is useful but not a replacement for actual usage. Not impossible leaves possibility open; it does not guarantee likelihood or success. Negative questions often convey expectations, and an answer should clarify the proposition: Yes, she called or No, she did not call. Focus and contrast can narrow negation: I did not criticise the design because it was expensive may deny the criticism or deny that expense was the reason, depending on context. An assessment must stipulate the intended reading or ask learners to explain the ambiguity. Formal writing benefits from explicit scope when costs, obligations or permissions depend on it; unfamiliarity alone does not make a negative construction ungrammatical.\n\nnot all + plural noun phrase: Denies universal inclusion; it does not entail a positive number within the group.\n\nno + noun phrase / auxiliary + not + remaining verb phrase: Negative noun-phrase determination and clause negation have different structures.\n\nNot all the windows were locked.\nAt least one window was not locked; the sentence alone does not say how many were locked.\n\nNo windows were locked.\nThis states zero locked windows in the relevant set.\n\nEach inspector received a different folder.\nDifferent makes an intended distribution explicit, rather than leaving it to the scope of an indefinite folder.\n\nAny also has free-choice uses outside negative environments, so never define it as a word used only in negatives or questions.\n\nMultiple negative forms may express dialectal negative concord, litotes or genuinely separate negations. Identify the construction and editorial target before revising.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?view=reference&concept=gv5-ref-negation-scope","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Not - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/not"},{"publisher":"Double negatives and usage - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/double-negatives"},{"publisher":"Negation: two negatives - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/negation-two-negatives"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-ref-presupposition","category":"grammar","title":"Presupposition, entailment and contextual interpretation","text":"Presupposition, entailment and contextual interpretation\n\nA presupposition is information an utterance normally treats as already taken for granted, often associated with a particular expression. An entailment follows from what is asserted under the relevant reading. Pragmatic inferences also depend on context, expectations and the purpose of the utterance.\n\nCompare Leena opened the gate with Leena has stopped opening the gate at dawn. The first asserts an opening event. The second normally takes a previous practice for granted while asserting its cessation. A reader who never knew of that practice may accommodate it: provisionally add it to the shared information. Accommodation is an interpretation process, not independent evidence that the practice existed. In factual academic or professional writing, do not smuggle an unverified premise into a sentence merely by presenting it as familiar. Ask what the source explicitly states, what it presupposes and what the writer is only inferring. The grammatical form can influence these distinctions without guaranteeing real-world truth.\n\nExpressions often discussed as presupposition triggers include stop, again, definite descriptions and factive predicates such as regret. The coordinator regrets that the hearing was postponed normally treats the postponement as a fact while reporting an attitude toward it. Negating the attitude usually leaves that background assumption: The coordinator does not regret that the hearing was postponed. This persistence under negation is a useful diagnostic, not an exceptionless definition. Questions and conditionals provide further tests, and interpretations can depend on local context. Again commonly signals recurrence, but its exact attachment and focus can matter. Do not turn a short list of triggers into a mechanical method that ignores the whole sentence.\n\nEntailment and conversational inference require different reasoning. Some applicants arrived late is compatible with all applicants arriving late under its basic existential reading, although ordinary conversation often suggests not all. By contrast, The applicants all arrived late entails arrival for each member of the relevant group under that reading. A stronger statement cannot automatically be substituted for a weaker source statement in a précis. Likewise, The service was not unhelpful can convey restrained praise without being equivalent in tone or commitment to The service was excellent. Grammar provides possible structures; register and conversational expectations help explain why a writer chooses one rather than another. Preserve both propositional qualifications and the force of cautious claims when paraphrasing.\n\nPresupposition research contains substantive theoretical disagreements about conventional meaning, speaker assumptions, projection and cancellation. This course introduces practical diagnostics rather than claiming one short account resolves those debates. Presuppositions sometimes fail to project through a larger structure, and a continuation can reject or suspend an apparent background assumption. It is therefore safer to say normally presupposes under this reading than always proves. In editing, a loaded question such as Who approved the alteration? may invite the assumption that somebody did, while Nobody approved it rejects that assumption. The response is not grammatically defective merely because it declines the proposed premise. Expert analysis specifies the reading, context and evidence, and distinguishes grammatical acceptability from the truth or fairness of the assumption.\n\nX regrets that P: Normally presents P as a background fact while asserting X’s regret.\n\nX has stopped doing Y: Normally backgrounds earlier Y-activity while asserting that the activity has ceased.\n\nThe caretaker has stopped opening the side gate.\nEarlier opening is normally backgrounded; cessation is asserted.\n\nThe caretaker has not stopped opening the side gate.\nThe negative commonly preserves the assumption of earlier opening while rejecting cessation.\n\nIf there ever was a side gate, the caretaker no longer uses it.\nThe explicit conditional context can suspend existence assumptions that a simpler definite description might encourage.\n\nProjection and cancellation vary by trigger, embedding and context; a single negation test is not a complete proof.\n\nBackgrounding a claim does not verify it. Source-based writing must check the factual premise as well as the grammar.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?view=reference&concept=gv5-ref-presupposition","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Presupposition (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/presupposition/"},{"publisher":"Double negatives and usage - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/double-negatives"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-ref-morph-processes","category":"grammar","title":"Word formation beyond prefixes and suffixes","text":"Word formation beyond prefixes and suffixes\n\nWord formation creates or establishes lexemes: vocabulary items with related grammatical forms. Affixation, compounding, conversion, clipping, blending and back-formation describe different processes; a word can participate in more than one process across its history.\n\nBegin by separating a change in grammatical form from the creation of a vocabulary item. In The editors work and The editor works, works belongs to the verb work and marks a grammatical contrast. In They edit and The editor arrived, editor is a separate noun related to edit. Inflection supplies forms within a lexeme; derivation contributes a related lexeme. These useful distinctions do not mean every suffix changes word class: an abstract noun can be derived from another noun. Nor does every newly established word contain a visible prefix or suffix. Always examine its category and meaning in a complete expression, then ask which operation the analysis is intended to describe.\n\nAffixation adds a bound element to a base, as in readable or unreadable. Compounding combines lexical bases, as in school library or proofreader; spaces and hyphens do not by themselves decide whether an expression is a compound. Conversion establishes a different category without an overt derivational affix: a bottle contrasts with They bottle the juice. Clipping shortens an existing word: photo is a shortened form of photograph, and exam of examination. A blend combines material from source words, as in brunch from breakfast and lunch. A blend differs from an ordinary compound because its source words need not remain intact; some borderline formations resist one simple label.\n\nBack-formation produces a new expression by analysing an existing word as if it contained removable material. The historical relationship matters: edit developed from editor, rather than editor historically being made by adding a suffix to an already established English edit. In present-day grammatical analysis, however, speakers can understand editor as edit plus an agent-forming ending. Historical origin and a contemporary structural relationship are therefore different questions. Do not infer a word’s actual history merely because one expression is shorter. Initialisms such as BBC and word-pronounced acronyms such as NATO also establish shortened expressions, but use initial letters rather than simply removing syllables from one word. Pronunciation helps distinguish these categories.\n\nWord-formation labels describe patterns, not automatic permission to publish any possible coinage as established vocabulary. A creative conversion or blend may be interpretable in advertising yet unsuitable without explanation in an academic report. Lexicalisation means an expression becomes established with particular meanings or restrictions; those meanings need not be completely predictable from its parts. Compound stress and semantic relations are useful evidence, but neither produces an infallible test across all English compounds. In specialist discussion, conversion is sometimes analysed using a zero derivational element, while other analyses treat category change without such an element. This theoretical difference does not require learners to invent an inaudible suffix in an ordinary sentence analysis.\n\nbase + affix → related lexeme: The relation is derivational when it establishes a lexical item rather than simply a grammatical form.\n\nnoun used as lexical verb: bottle → bottle: Conversion changes category in the stated use without an overt affix.\n\nThe technician photographed the damaged panel.\nPhotographed is a past form of the verb photograph; the ending supplies inflection.\n\nThe technician emailed a photo of the panel.\nPhoto is a clipping, while emailed uses the verb email with past inflection. More than one process can be relevant to a sentence.\n\nThe journal’s editor asked us to edit the abstract.\nHistorically edit is a back-formation from editor; synchronically the two belong to a recognisable noun–verb family.\n\nNot every abbreviation is spoken in its shortened written form. Dr is normally read as doctor; exam is ordinarily spoken as exam.\n\nWord-class conversion and syntactic function must remain distinct: stone in stone wall can be a noun modifier without becoming an adjective.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?view=reference&concept=gv5-ref-morph-processes","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Word formation - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-formation"},{"publisher":"Abbreviations, initials and acronyms - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/abbreviations-initials-and-acronyms"},{"publisher":"BLEND | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/blend"},{"publisher":"BACK-FORMATION | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/back-formation"},{"publisher":"14.4 Morphological change – Essentials of Linguistics, 2nd edition","url":"https://ecampusontario.pressbooks.pub/essentialsoflinguistics2/chapter/14-4-morphological-change/"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar-v5:gv5-ref-morph-productivity","category":"grammar","title":"Roots, affix order and grammatical productivity","text":"Roots, affix order and grammatical productivity\n\nProductivity is the extent to which a grammatical or word-building pattern can be extended to additional suitable forms. It is constrained by category, meaning, sound, history and established vocabulary; it is not the claim that every imaginable combination is equally acceptable.\n\nA root is the central lexical element in an analysis. A base is any form to which an operation applies, while a stem is a form used as the base for inflection. In teachers, teach is a root, teacher is the stem receiving plural -s, and teacher is also a base for that operation. Terminology can vary between descriptions, so state which operation you mean. A bound morpheme cannot normally stand alone in its relevant use; a free morpheme can. Visible spelling pieces do not automatically correspond to meaningful morphemes: the letters er in a word are not necessarily the agent suffix found in teacher. Repeated form and a consistent grammatical relationship provide stronger evidence than appearance alone.\n\nAffix order can show hierarchical structure. In unhelpfulness, -ful first relates to help, un- negates helpful, and -ness produces the abstract noun. This analysis concerns relationships, not just a list of neighbouring letters. The plural of a derived count noun usually applies outside the derivation: teacher → teachers. Inflection may also involve an irregular change rather than a simple added segment: mouse → mice. An allomorph is an alternative form of a morpheme; English plural endings have different pronunciations in cats, dogs and buses. The spelling may look similar while the sound differs, and one grammatical category can have more than one formal realisation.\n\nA productive pattern remains open to suitable new formations, but productivity is gradual. The suffix -ness combines readily with many adjectives, whereas some other suffixes are restricted to established lexical families. Comprehensibility does not guarantee conventional status: a reader may understand a novel coinage while a learner’s dictionary recommends an established alternative for ordinary formal writing. Existing words can compete with potential regular formations; ordinary standard past went is not replaced by goed merely because regular verbs take -ed. Such cases show why a structural pattern is a starting point for analysis rather than a complete lexical authority. Check the intended meaning and established usage before declaring a new-looking form correct or incorrect.\n\nMorphological and syntactic structure can interact without being identical. A compound may have an internal head that determines its category or main semantic type, but exceptional or historically opaque compounds need separate lexical attention. A present-day speaker’s analysis may differ from an etymologist’s account, and plausible segmentation is not proof of historical origin. Word-building also changes over time: material in a blend or a borrowed item can become available for further coinages. An advanced analysis should therefore distinguish productivity, frequency, transparency and lexicalisation. A frequent word need not exemplify a productive rule; an unfamiliar but transparently constructed word need not be meaningless. This reference uses pedagogically useful segmentation and marks historical claims only when they have source support.\n\n[[un + helpful] + ness]: The outer suffix relates to the whole adjective unhelpful, not independently to help.\n\nderived stem + plural inflection: Teachers is analysed as teacher plus plural marking, rather than teach plus an arbitrary ers suffix.\n\nSeveral helpful volunteers arrived.\nHelpful is an adjective related to help by derivation; volunteers carries ordinary plural inflection.\n\nThe volunteers’ helpfulness reassured the organiser.\nHelpfulness is an abstract noun derived from an adjective; its noun status differs from the adjective’s modifier function.\n\nThe editor questioned the new term’s usefulness, not its intelligibility.\nA coinage can be understandable while its suitability for a particular audience remains a separate editorial question.\n\nPlural allomorphs are not three different meanings of plurality. Pronunciation conditions the regular variants.\n\nSome apparent affixes are opaque or historically reanalysed; a synchronic analysis should not pretend every spelling can be cleanly segmented.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?view=reference&concept=gv5-ref-morph-productivity","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"5.2 Roots, bases, and affixes – Essentials of Linguistics, 2nd edition","url":"https://ecampusontario.pressbooks.pub/essentialsoflinguistics2/chapter/5-2-types-of-morphology-affixes-and-beyond/"},{"publisher":"5.3 Morphology beyond affixes – Essentials of Linguistics, 2nd edition","url":"https://ecampusontario.pressbooks.pub/essentialsoflinguistics2/chapter/5-4-morphology-beyond-affixes/"},{"publisher":"5.6 Derivational morphology – Essentials of Linguistics, 2nd edition","url":"https://ecampusontario.pressbooks.pub/essentialsoflinguistics2/chapter/5-5-derivational-morphology/"},{"publisher":"14.4 Morphological change – Essentials of Linguistics, 2nd edition","url":"https://ecampusontario.pressbooks.pub/essentialsoflinguistics2/chapter/14-4-morphological-change/"}],"feature":"tutor"},{"id":"grammar:1:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Grammar, meaning and units of structure","text":"Grammar, meaning and units of structure\n\nGrammar describes the patterns by which words and larger units combine and change form. A grammatical analysis asks how an expression is built; interpretation asks what it means in a particular context. Neither question can be answered reliably by word order alone.\n\nBegin with a familiar message: The nurse opened the window. Its words work together to identify someone, an event and something affected. Reversing nurse and window changes who does what, even though most words remain unchanged. Grammar therefore carries meaning. Vocabulary also matters: The window congratulated the nurse follows a recognisable structural pattern but normally needs an imaginative context. Structural well-formedness and ordinary real-world plausibility are different judgements. When evaluating an unfamiliar sentence, identify which judgement you are making before calling it wrong.\n\nA phrase is a unit organised around a head, its central word; it can contain one word or several. In the unusually quiet room, room heads a noun phrase, quiet heads an adjective phrase and unusually is an adverb. A clause organises a situation around a verb, often with a subject and complements. The nurse opened the window is a finite clause. To open the window is a non-finite clause: its verb has no independent tense. This course introduces these terms now and analyses their internal structure in Days 13–15.\n\nA full written sentence normally contains a main clause and appropriate boundary punctuation. A subordinate clause can be part of that sentence: The nurse opened the window because the room was warm. Because introduces a reason but does not make the first event happen in the grammar itself; the writer asserts that causal relationship. Short answers, labels and conversational fragments can be meaningful without a full main clause. No entry on a sign and After lunch in reply to When? are useful expressions; formal paragraph writing normally requires fuller structures.\n\nGrammatical correctness, contextual acceptability and editorial style must be distinguished. A sentence may be grammatical but too informal for a report, or perfectly clear in conversation but unclear without its shared setting. Standard British and American forms can differ without one being a mistake. A descriptive account records established patterns; a prescriptive convention recommends choices for a stated purpose. In this course, British English supplies the editorial default, while alternatives are identified explicitly. An asterisk in a grammar book often marks an unacceptable intended reading; it does not prove every imaginable use impossible.\n\nmain clause + because + subordinate clause: The second clause supplies the writer’s reason for the first situation.\n\nThe courier arrived.\nA main clause forms a complete neutral written statement.\n\nThe courier arrived after the office closed.\nA subordinate time clause locates the arrival relative to the closure.\n\nTo reach the archive before noon, Sara took an earlier bus.\nThe opening non-finite clause states a purpose; Sara is understood as the person reaching the archive.\n\nClose the window is a grammatical imperative with an ordinarily unexpressed subject. Do not add you merely to satisfy a simplified statement that every sentence must display a subject.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=1&section=gv5-d01-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Subjects - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subjects"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:1:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Inside words: morphemes, roots, stems and word formation","text":"Inside words: morphemes, roots, stems and word formation\n\nA morpheme is a smallest unit carrying lexical meaning or grammatical information. A root supplies the central lexical material; a stem is the form to which an inflection is attached. Inflection supplies grammatical forms, while derivation creates a new lexical item.\n\nCompare help, helpful and helpfulness. Help carries the central meaning; -ful helps form an adjective, and -ness helps form a noun. These added parts cannot normally stand alone as words, so they are bound morphemes. Help can stand alone, making it a free morpheme. Meaningful parts are not the same as syllables: helpfulness has three readily identifiable morphemes, but a long word need not contain a meaningful part for each syllable. Avoid splitting letters into pieces unless each piece has a defensible role in contemporary English.\n\nInflection relates forms of one lexical item: file/files, walk/walked/walking and small/smaller. Derivation changes the vocabulary item: teach/teacher or happy/unhappy/happiness. Derivation can change word class, as happy to happiness does, but need not: happy and unhappy are both adjectives. In teachers, teach is the root, teacher is the stem receiving the plural ending and -s marks plural number. A base is the material to which an affix attaches at a particular step; it can already contain other affixes. These labels describe different analytical roles rather than competing spellings.\n\nA prefix precedes its base, as un- in unfair; a suffix follows it, as -ment in agreement. Compounding combines lexical bases: raincoat or classroom. Conversion uses an established form in a new class without an overt affix: a text and to text. Spelling does not supply a perfect guide to structure: compounds may be closed, hyphenated or written separately. Learn a new word together with its meaning, word class and usual pattern. A productive pattern permits new formations readily, but a familiar suffix cannot be added freely to every possible base.\n\nRoots and stems can be irregular or show changes of spelling and sound. The past went is an inflectional form of go even though it is not built by visibly adding -ed. Morphological analysis is therefore more than chopping off endings. Contemporary structure also differs from etymology, the historical origin of a word: learners need not reconstruct ancient roots before understanding a current English form. This introductory model intentionally avoids deciding every disputed boundary, such as the status of English possessive -’s; that construction receives a phrase-level account on Day 2.\n\nroot + derivational suffix + inflectional suffix: teach + -er + -s forms a plural noun meaning people who teach.\n\nThe gardeners repaired the gate.\ngarden + -er forms a person noun; plural -s gives gardeners.\n\nThe unfair decision was withdrawn.\nun- derives unfair from fair without changing adjective class.\n\nPlease email the revised schedule.\nEmail is used as a verb here; its form does not require a new derivational ending.\n\nSome grammatical contrasts have no visible added suffix: sheep may be singular or plural. Interpret the word with its determiner, verb and context.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=1&section=gv5-d01-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"MORPHEME | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/morpheme"},{"publisher":"INFLECTION | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/inflection"},{"publisher":"STEM | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/stem"},{"publisher":"Word formation - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-formation"},{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:1:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Word classes and the difference between category and function","text":"Word classes and the difference between category and function\n\nA word class is a grammatical category identified through a combination of form, distribution and possible combinations. A function is a role within a larger structure. Noun is a category; subject and modifier are functions. A word’s meaning alone does not establish its class.\n\nNouns commonly head expressions naming people, things or ideas: teacher, bridge, patience. Verbs organise actions, events or states: repair, happen, know. Adjectives commonly describe or classify nouns: a narrow bridge. Adverbs modify activities, qualities, other adverbs or wider statements: works carefully, unusually narrow, fortunately. These are major open classes: new members enter them readily. Many words have more than one class, so classify their use in a particular sentence. The spelling clean occurs as an adjective in a clean room and as a verb in clean the room.\n\nOther classes include determiners, pronouns, prepositions, conjunctions and interjections. Determiners help specify a noun’s reference or quantity: the report, this report, some reports. Pronouns can head expressions such as she, someone or mine. Prepositions head relational expressions: under the table, after lunch. Conjunctions link units: paper and ink; because it rained. Interjections express reactions: Oh! These are relatively closed classes, although change is possible. Closed does not mean semantically empty: compare before lunch with after lunch, where one small word changes the time relationship.\n\nUse several clues together. Plural marking often identifies nouns; comparison often identifies gradable adjectives; auxiliary patterns identify verbs. Each clue has exceptions. News is singular, wooden normally lacks comparative wooden-er, and fast is an adverb in move fast despite lacking -ly. A noun can modify another noun: in a stone wall, stone remains a noun functioning as a premodifier. An adjective can follow a linking verb: the wall is old. Category and position therefore interact, but neither simply equals the other. Detailed patterns are developed in the dedicated noun, verb and adjective lessons.\n\nGrammars differ in some category boundaries. Traditional accounts often call my a possessive adjective; this course usually calls it a possessive determiner because it occupies a determiner position. Some modern accounts treat pronouns as a subclass of nouns or distinguish determinative as a word class from determiner as a function. Recognise the labels without assuming the analysis has changed the sentence’s meaning. The immediate goal is a defensible explanation supported by behaviour: name the category, then state its function in the expression. Do not turn every describing word into an adjective or every -ly word into an adverb.\n\ndeterminer + adjective + noun: The adjective modifies the noun; the determiner specifies reference.\n\nnoun + noun: The first noun can function as a modifier without changing class.\n\nThose careful researchers checked the figures.\nThose is a determiner, careful an adjective and researchers the noun head.\n\nThe college will finance the project.\nFinance is a lexical verb after modal will; the project is its object.\n\nThe finance office remained open unusually late.\nFinance is a noun modifier, open is an adjective complement, and unusually modifies adverb late.\n\nOne spelling can belong to different classes: They water the plants uses a verb, while The water is cold uses a noun. Explain the local pattern rather than assign one permanent label to the spelling.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=1&section=gv5-d01-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Determiners ( the, my, some, this ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/determiners-the-my-some-this"},{"publisher":"Nouns: compound nouns - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-compound-nouns"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:1:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Subjects, predicates and the first sentence patterns","text":"Subjects, predicates and the first sentence patterns\n\nThe subject is a grammatical element identified by structural behaviour, not simply the person doing an action. The predicate is what is predicated of that subject. Objects, predicative complements and adjuncts perform different roles within clause structure.\n\nStart with The librarian opened the parcel. The librarian is the subject; opened the parcel is the predicate. Opened is the verb and the parcel is the direct object. In a neutral declarative clause, English usually places these in subject–verb–object order, abbreviated SVO. Identify the whole subject phrase before naming its head: in The new librarian opened the parcel, the new librarian is the subject and librarian is its head. Keeping those two answers separate prevents a common analysis mistake. The predicate can be short or extended with additional information.\n\nNot every clause has an object. The visitors waited follows subject–verb, or SV. The room became quiet contains a linking verb and a subject complement: quiet describes the room rather than a separate affected object. A complement can be a noun phrase: Leena is a doctor. In The coach gave the players advice, the players is an indirect object and advice is the direct object. In They considered the advice useful, useful is an object complement, describing the advice. Learn these contrasts as meaningful patterns rather than force all sentences into SVO.\n\nAdjuncts add circumstances or comments: The visitors waited outside yesterday. Outside gives place and yesterday gives time. A complement is selected by a head’s pattern; an adjunct supplies a less tightly selected addition. With put, a location normally completes the pattern: Put the parcel on the desk. Deletion is a helpful clue, not an infallible test, since some selected complements can be left understood. A passive subject can be affected rather than an agent: The parcel was opened by the librarian. Subject is therefore a syntactic function, while agent and patient describe semantic roles.\n\nThe course uses verb narrowly for the verbal element and predicate broadly for everything predicated of the subject. In broader syntactic terminology, objects are also complements because the verb selects them; when distinguishing clause patterns, subject complement and object complement mean predicative descriptions. State which sense of complement is intended. Dummy it in It is raining and dummy there in There are two seats occupy subject positions without naming an ordinary participant. Questions and some emphatic clauses change ordinary word order, so the first noun before a verb is not a universal subject test. These constructions receive detailed treatment later.\n\nS + V + O: A transitive clause: The mechanic repaired the bicycle.\n\nS + linking V + subject complement: A predicative clause: The bicycle looks new.\n\nThe musicians rehearsed.\nSV: the musicians is subject; rehearsed is the verb and complete predicate.\n\nThe volunteers painted the classroom yesterday.\nSVO plus time adjunct: yesterday is outside the object noun phrase.\n\nThe board considered the proposed timetable impractical.\nThe proposed timetable is direct object; impractical is its predicative complement.\n\nImperatives normally omit an expressed subject: Read the notice. Questions can put an auxiliary before the subject: Did the visitors wait? Analyse the whole pattern.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=1&section=gv5-d01-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Subjects - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subjects"},{"publisher":"Objects - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/objects"},{"publisher":"Complements - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/complements"},{"publisher":"Adjuncts - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adjuncts"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:1:4","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Subjects, predicates and the first sentence patterns","text":"Subjects, predicates and the first sentence patterns\n\nThe subject is a grammatical element identified by structural behaviour, not simply the person doing an action. The predicate is what is predicated of that subject. Objects, predicative complements and adjuncts perform different roles within clause structure.\n\nStart with The librarian opened the parcel. The librarian is the subject; opened the parcel is the predicate. Opened is the verb and the parcel is the direct object. In a neutral declarative clause, English usually places these in subject–verb–object order, abbreviated SVO. Identify the whole subject phrase before naming its head: in The new librarian opened the parcel, the new librarian is the subject and librarian is its head. Keeping those two answers separate prevents a common analysis mistake. The predicate can be short or extended with additional information.\n\nNot every clause has an object. The visitors waited follows subject–verb, or SV. The room became quiet contains a linking verb and a subject complement: quiet describes the room rather than a separate affected object. A complement can be a noun phrase: Leena is a doctor. In The coach gave the players advice, the players is an indirect object and advice is the direct object. In They considered the advice useful, useful is an object complement, describing the advice. Learn these contrasts as meaningful patterns rather than force all sentences into SVO.\n\nAdjuncts add circumstances or comments: The visitors waited outside yesterday. Outside gives place and yesterday gives time. A complement is selected by a head’s pattern; an adjunct supplies a less tightly selected addition. With put, a location normally completes the pattern: Put the parcel on the desk. Deletion is a helpful clue, not an infallible test, since some selected complements can be left understood. A passive subject can be affected rather than an agent: The parcel was opened by the librarian. Subject is therefore a syntactic function, while agent and patient describe semantic roles.\n\nThe course uses verb narrowly for the verbal element and predicate broadly for everything predicated of the subject. In broader syntactic terminology, objects are also complements because the verb selects them; when distinguishing clause patterns, subject complement and object complement mean predicative descriptions. State which sense of complement is intended. Dummy it in It is raining and dummy there in There are two seats occupy subject positions without naming an ordinary participant. Questions and some emphatic clauses change ordinary word order, so the first noun before a verb is not a universal subject test. These constructions receive detailed treatment later.\n\nS + V + O: A transitive clause: The mechanic repaired the bicycle.\n\nS + linking V + subject complement: A predicative clause: The bicycle looks new.\n\nThe musicians rehearsed.\nSV: the musicians is subject; rehearsed is the verb and complete predicate.\n\nThe volunteers painted the classroom yesterday.\nSVO plus time adjunct: yesterday is outside the object noun phrase.\n\nThe board considered the proposed timetable impractical.\nThe proposed timetable is direct object; impractical is its predicative complement.\n\nImperatives normally omit an expressed subject: Read the notice. Questions can put an auxiliary before the subject: Did the visitors wait? Analyse the whole pattern.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=1&section=gv5-d01-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Subjects - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subjects"},{"publisher":"Objects - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/objects"},{"publisher":"Complements - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/complements"},{"publisher":"Adjuncts - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adjuncts"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:2:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Lexical verbs, auxiliaries and the principal forms","text":"Lexical verbs, auxiliaries and the principal forms\n\nVerbs are a word class whose members organise clauses and select characteristic patterns. Lexical verbs contribute central meanings such as wait, repair and know. Auxiliaries help express grammatical meanings and constructions. Finite and non-finite describe a verb form’s grammatical status within a clause, not whether it denotes an action.\n\nLearn a lexical verb through its principal forms. Write has the plain form write, third-person singular present writes, past wrote, past participle written and -ing form writing. A regular verb normally uses the same -ed form for its past and past participle: repair, repaired, repaired. An irregular verb has an established alternative pattern, as write/wrote/written or cut/cut/cut. Identical spelling can therefore serve different grammatical roles. The clause She cut the paper contains a past form, while has cut contains a past participle selected by has. A learner’s dictionary supplies the relevant forms and pronunciation.\n\nBe, have and do are primary verbs that can function as auxiliaries or lexical verbs. Compare She has a bicycle with She has repaired a bicycle; only the second has is a perfect auxiliary. Be forms include be, am, is, are, was, were, been and being. Have has the forms have, has, had and having; do has do, does, did, done and doing. Had serves as both past and past-participle form. Core modal auxiliaries include can, could, may, might, shall, should, will, would and must. They normally select a plain verb form: can swim, not can swims. Modality receives fuller semantic treatment on Day 17.\n\nIn a finite verb phrase, the first verb is finite and the following forms are selected by preceding auxiliaries: has written, is writing, may have written. Non-finite forms include to-infinitives, plain infinitives, -ing forms and past participles. The form after to in to write does not carry its own person agreement. A past participle is not a past-tense verb merely because its name contains past; The work will be finished refers forward in time. Finite does not mean that a form always visibly changes for every subject: modal can has the same spelling with I and she.\n\nInflectional form, clause status and time reference are different dimensions. A plain form occurs in several environments, including after a modal and in an imperative such as Close the door. Modern descriptions differ in how they classify certain untensed or subjunctive constructions, so a visible -s or -ed ending cannot serve as the sole test for finiteness. Regular spelling also has conditions: stopped doubles a consonant, while opened does not; consult stress and spelling conventions rather than double every final consonant. British learnt/learned and American learned illustrate legitimate form variation, not an automatic difference in grammatical correctness.\n\nlexical verb: plain / -s / past / past participle / -ing: write / writes / wrote / written / writing are the five principal form categories.\n\nmodal auxiliary + plain verb: Can write uses a plain form after the modal.\n\nThe researcher writes carefully.\nWrites is the third-person singular present form of the lexical verb.\n\nThe researcher has written three reports.\nHas is finite; written is the past participle selected by perfect have.\n\nThe researcher may have been working late.\nMay is finite; have is plain after the modal, been follows perfect have, and working follows progressive be.\n\nA form can be irregular without changing its spelling across categories: cut serves as plain, past and past participle in different structures. Context and auxiliary selection distinguish the functions.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=5&section=gv5-d05-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"},{"publisher":"Do - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/do"},{"publisher":"Contractions - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/contractions"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:2:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Verb-phrase order, do-support, questions and negation","text":"Verb-phrase order, do-support, questions and negation\n\nA verb phrase can contain a lexical verb with one or more auxiliaries. English auxiliaries have an ordered relationship and select the form of the next verb. Do-support supplies auxiliary do where a neutral question or negative needs an auxiliary and no suitable one is already present.\n\nBegin with an existing auxiliary. She is working becomes Is she working? and She is not working. The finite auxiliary moves before the subject in this yes–no question, and not follows that auxiliary in the negative. With a modal, the same basic pattern gives Can she swim? and She cannot swim. The lexical verb does not move to the front by itself in an ordinary present-day neutral question. Be can also invert directly when it links a subject with a description: Is she ready? Do not automatically add do simply because there is only one written verb.\n\nFor an ordinary lexical verb without another auxiliary, use do, does or did: Do they work here? Does she work here? Did she work here? The lexical verb returns to its plain form because the auxiliary carries the relevant finite contrast. Negatives follow the same arrangement: They do not work here; she did not work here. Do can occur both as auxiliary and lexical verb: Do they do laboratory work? A neutral affirmative subject question ordinarily needs no added do: Who wrote the report? Object questions do use it when needed: Who did they invite? Emphatic contexts can produce different patterns.\n\nThe common auxiliary order is modal, perfect have, progressive be, passive be and lexical verb. Each item selects what immediately follows: have selects a past participle; progressive be selects an -ing form; passive be selects a past participle. Thus may have been waiting is interpretable through nested selections rather than a memorised label alone. Longer combinations can be grammatical but cumbersome, so choose a simpler wording where it preserves meaning. Questions invert the first finite auxiliary only: Has she been waiting? Negation normally follows that same first auxiliary.\n\nContractions join forms in writing and speech: is not/isn’t, do not/don’t, I am/I’m and they have/they’ve. Their suitability depends on register, audience and emphasis; formal writing does not have one universal grammatical ban on them. The spelling ’s can mean is or has, and ’d can mean had or would; the surrounding pattern resolves ambiguity where possible. Auxiliary and lexical have show regional and constructional variation, including Have you got a pen? and Do you have a pen? Neutral be normally avoids do-support in finite questions, but imperatives such as Do be careful and Don’t be late show that do and be are not universally incompatible.\n\nfinite auxiliary + subject + remaining verb phrase: Has she been waiting? inverts only the first finite auxiliary.\n\ndo/does/did + subject + plain lexical verb: Did the learners finish? uses did, leaving finish plain.\n\nDoes your brother repair bicycles?\nDoes carries the present third-person contrast; repair remains plain.\n\nThe visitors have not been waiting long.\nNot follows finite have; been and waiting retain the selected forms.\n\nDo be patient while the file loads.\nEmphatic imperative do can occur with be; this is a special construction, not an ordinary neutral yes–no question.\n\nDo is not added mechanically to every question. A neutral subject question such as Who arrived? and a be-question such as Are they ready? already have their appropriate structures.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=5&section=gv5-d05-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Do - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/do"},{"publisher":"Contractions - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/contractions"},{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"},{"publisher":"Inversion - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/inversion"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:2:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Valency, objects, predicative complements and sentence patterns","text":"Valency, objects, predicative complements and sentence patterns\n\nVerb valency concerns the participants and complement patterns associated with a verb use. Transitive uses have an object; intransitive uses do not. Linking and complex-transitive patterns contain predicative complements that describe a subject or object rather than introduce another independent participant.\n\nCompare The child laughed with The child opened the box. Laughed is intransitive here; opened is transitive and has the box as direct object. This is a grammatical pattern distinction, not simply a distinction between physical actions and states. She owns a bicycle contains a direct object even though owning is not a momentary action. Conversely, The glass broke has a subject undergoing a change without a direct object. Identify grammatical function from the structure and the particular verb use. Some verbs allow more than one pattern: She is reading and She is reading a novel are both possible.\n\nA ditransitive pattern contains two objects: The tutor gave the learner a booklet. The learner is the indirect object and a booklet the direct object. In The tutor gave a booklet to the learner, this course calls to the learner a prepositional complement rather than a second noun-phrase object. Some traditional accounts extend indirect object more broadly, so compare the stated analysis before treating a terminology difference as a grammatical fault. A linking pattern relates a subject to a description or identity: The learner seemed tired; Leena became a doctor. Tired and a doctor are predicative complements.\n\nComplex-transitive patterns add a complement describing the object: The panel considered the plan practical; They appointed Leena coordinator. The second complement is not an indirect object receiving a separate thing. Other verbs require a location complement: Put the folder on the shelf. On the shelf completes the placement pattern; merely saying Put the folder normally leaves the instruction incomplete. Complements can therefore be noun phrases, adjective phrases, prepositional phrases or clauses, depending on the verb. Optional adjuncts add circumstances: She read the report yesterday. Distinguishing optionality from the verb’s meaning requirements needs more than a single deletion test.\n\nThe verb’s lexical meaning and construction jointly determine which pattern is available. Explain normally takes a content object with a to-phrase for the listener: explain the rule to me, not explain me the rule in the broad edited standard used here. Tell allows tell me the rule, illustrating a genuine lexical difference rather than a universal conversion rule between two-object and prepositional patterns. Valency counts may include the subject as an argument in linguistic descriptions; a classroom list of objects often counts something different. Day 13 develops hierarchical parsing and diagnostic tests. Avoid defining every object as a person physically receiving an action or every complement as an adjective after be.\n\nS + V; S + V + O; S + V + subject complement: The guests laughed; the guests opened the gate; the guests seemed tired.\n\nS + V + indirect object + direct object; S + V + object + object complement: She gave him a key; they painted the gate blue.\n\nThe lecturer sent the students a reading list.\nThe students and a reading list are the two noun-phrase objects in this use.\n\nThe committee found the explanation unconvincing.\nExplanation is object; unconvincing is a complement describing it, not an adverb modifying found.\n\nPlease put the parcel beside the desk.\nThe placement verb selects a locative complement; beside the desk states the required destination.\n\nThe same lexical verb can have transitive and intransitive uses. She is eating does not become ungrammatical merely because eat can also take an object in She is eating an apple.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=5&section=gv5-d05-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"VALENCY | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/valency"},{"publisher":"Objects - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/objects"},{"publisher":"Complements - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/complements"},{"publisher":"Subjects - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subjects"},{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:2:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Alternations, dynamic meanings and states","text":"Alternations, dynamic meanings and states\n\nDynamic meanings present events, activities or changes; stative meanings present situations such as knowledge or possession without an internal activity sequence. An ergative alternation relates a transitive use with an intransitive use in which the affected object becomes the subject, as The wind opened the door and The door opened. Its intransitive partner is active, rather than a passive construction.\n\nA dynamic use can involve a deliberate action, such as writing a note, or an uncontrolled event, such as a branch falling. A stative use describes a situation such as knowing the answer, owning a bicycle or resembling a parent. This distinction concerns how a meaning is presented, not whether the real world changes over a lifetime. A person can own a bicycle only temporarily, but ownership can still be described as a state. Begin by interpreting the particular sentence rather than assigning an unchanging dynamic or stative label to the dictionary spelling.\n\nSeveral verbs have different senses with different patterns. I think the plan is useful states an opinion; I am thinking about the plan presents mental activity. She has a bicycle expresses possession; she is having lunch describes an activity. The soup tastes salty describes its perceived quality; the cook is tasting the soup describes an action. Progressive forms are more typical of dynamic uses, but the relationship is not an absolute prohibition. A speaker can present temporary behaviour with be: He is being unusually quiet. Future lessons develop the semantic choices among simple, progressive and perfect constructions.\n\nIn an ergative alternation, the affected participant appears as object in one clause and subject in another: The heat melted the wax; the wax melted. The second clause is active intransitive, not passive: it has no passive be plus participle construction. The subject need not be a conscious doer, so defining every subject as the actor produces a false analysis. Not all transitive verbs permit this alternation: The courier delivered the parcel does not yield The parcel delivered with the ordinary completed-delivery meaning. Whether an alternation is available is a lexical and constructional fact to learn.\n\nProgressive stative uses can be marked by register, temporary construal or evolving convention. I’m loving the new course is common informal evaluative English; that does not license an ordinary factual I’m knowing the answer in every context. Conversely, a temporary context can make a progressive natural with a verb otherwise often labelled stative, as The repairs are costing more than expected. Ergative is also used in wider linguistic typology for case and alignment systems. The English teaching label here refers only to the illustrated causative/inchoative alternation; it does not claim that English has a general ergative case system. Day 19 revisits alternation alongside voice.\n\ncausing subject + transitive verb + affected object ↔ affected subject + intransitive verb: The wind opened the gate ↔ The gate opened.\n\nsame spelling + different lexical sense → different aspect possibilities: Think as opinion contrasts with think as ongoing consideration.\n\nThe falling branch broke the window. The window broke.\nThe affected window is object in the transitive use and subject in the intransitive use.\n\nShe has two bicycles, but she is having lunch now.\nPossession and eating lunch are different senses of have.\n\nThe repairs are costing the club more than expected.\nThe progressive presents an ongoing developing financial consequence; a blanket stative ban would be misleading.\n\nA lexical spelling does not fix one aspect class across all senses. The cook is tasting the sauce is an ordinary dynamic use even though The sauce tastes sweet describes a state.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=5&section=gv5-d05-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"ERGATIVE | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/ergative"},{"publisher":"Stative verbs | LearnEnglish","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/b1-b2/stative-verbs"},{"publisher":"VALENCY | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/valency"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:2:4","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Alternations, dynamic meanings and states","text":"Alternations, dynamic meanings and states\n\nDynamic meanings present events, activities or changes; stative meanings present situations such as knowledge or possession without an internal activity sequence. An ergative alternation relates a transitive use with an intransitive use in which the affected object becomes the subject, as The wind opened the door and The door opened. Its intransitive partner is active, rather than a passive construction.\n\nA dynamic use can involve a deliberate action, such as writing a note, or an uncontrolled event, such as a branch falling. A stative use describes a situation such as knowing the answer, owning a bicycle or resembling a parent. This distinction concerns how a meaning is presented, not whether the real world changes over a lifetime. A person can own a bicycle only temporarily, but ownership can still be described as a state. Begin by interpreting the particular sentence rather than assigning an unchanging dynamic or stative label to the dictionary spelling.\n\nSeveral verbs have different senses with different patterns. I think the plan is useful states an opinion; I am thinking about the plan presents mental activity. She has a bicycle expresses possession; she is having lunch describes an activity. The soup tastes salty describes its perceived quality; the cook is tasting the soup describes an action. Progressive forms are more typical of dynamic uses, but the relationship is not an absolute prohibition. A speaker can present temporary behaviour with be: He is being unusually quiet. Future lessons develop the semantic choices among simple, progressive and perfect constructions.\n\nIn an ergative alternation, the affected participant appears as object in one clause and subject in another: The heat melted the wax; the wax melted. The second clause is active intransitive, not passive: it has no passive be plus participle construction. The subject need not be a conscious doer, so defining every subject as the actor produces a false analysis. Not all transitive verbs permit this alternation: The courier delivered the parcel does not yield The parcel delivered with the ordinary completed-delivery meaning. Whether an alternation is available is a lexical and constructional fact to learn.\n\nProgressive stative uses can be marked by register, temporary construal or evolving convention. I’m loving the new course is common informal evaluative English; that does not license an ordinary factual I’m knowing the answer in every context. Conversely, a temporary context can make a progressive natural with a verb otherwise often labelled stative, as The repairs are costing more than expected. Ergative is also used in wider linguistic typology for case and alignment systems. The English teaching label here refers only to the illustrated causative/inchoative alternation; it does not claim that English has a general ergative case system. Day 19 revisits alternation alongside voice.\n\ncausing subject + transitive verb + affected object ↔ affected subject + intransitive verb: The wind opened the gate ↔ The gate opened.\n\nsame spelling + different lexical sense → different aspect possibilities: Think as opinion contrasts with think as ongoing consideration.\n\nThe falling branch broke the window. The window broke.\nThe affected window is object in the transitive use and subject in the intransitive use.\n\nShe has two bicycles, but she is having lunch now.\nPossession and eating lunch are different senses of have.\n\nThe repairs are costing the club more than expected.\nThe progressive presents an ongoing developing financial consequence; a blanket stative ban would be misleading.\n\nA lexical spelling does not fix one aspect class across all senses. The cook is tasting the sauce is an ordinary dynamic use even though The sauce tastes sweet describes a state.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=5&section=gv5-d05-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"ERGATIVE | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/ergative"},{"publisher":"Stative verbs | LearnEnglish","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/b1-b2/stative-verbs"},{"publisher":"VALENCY | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/valency"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:3:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Main, subordinate, finite and non-finite clauses","text":"Main, subordinate, finite and non-finite clauses\n\nA clause is a grammatical unit organised around a verb. Main clauses supply a principal message; subordinate clauses function within a larger structure. Finite and non-finite distinguish verb forms and clause construction, not whether an event is real.\n\nThe train stopped is a main declarative clause. In the train stopped because a signal failed, because a signal failed is a subordinate reason clause associated with the main event. The first clause can form an independent neutral statement; the because-clause normally relies on a larger construction in formal connected prose. A clause is not simply any group of words that happens to contain a noun and a verb spelling. Identify the grammatical organisation of the group and its relation to the larger sentence. A phrase can contain a clause, as in the promise that she would return.\n\nA finite clause contains a finite verb or auxiliary, as in she returns, she returned and she may return. The modal may is finite even though it does not display a person contrast such as returns versus return. A non-finite clause is headed by an infinitival or participial form without independent tense inflection: to return soon, returning home, or signed yesterday in the noun phrase the documents signed yesterday. A non-finite clause can still express time relative to context. In she hoped to return next week, next week gives time reference, while to return remains non-finite.\n\nNon-finite clauses often leave their subject understood: she hopes to return normally makes her the intended returner. Some have an expressed subject: for the visitors to arrive early would be convenient. The clause for the visitors to arrive early serves as the subject of would be convenient, despite lacking independent tense. Finite clauses may also function as subjects, objects or other complements. Whether the grant will continue remains unclear has a finite interrogative clause as its subject. The whole sentence is declarative; the embedded subject clause does not use direct-question inversion.\n\nThe main/subordinate distinction is not identical to the finite/non-finite distinction. Many subordinate clauses are finite, while other subordinate clauses are non-finite. Imperatives deserve separate attention because their ordinary main verb is a plain form and their subject is usually unexpressed: take your seat. Different grammatical frameworks discuss their finiteness differently; this course identifies the imperative construction rather than inferring that take your seat is an ungrammatical fragment. Supplementary non-finite clauses such as weather permitting have their own internal structure. The practical question is how the clause is licensed and interpreted in the complete construction, not whether it mechanically contains a written subject plus an overt tense ending.\n\nmain clause + because + finite subordinate clause: The subordinate clause supplies a reason for the principal message.\n\nfor + expressed subject + to-infinitive clause: A non-finite clause can contain its own expressed subject.\n\nThe meeting ended because the chair was ill.\nEnded and was are finite; the because-clause is subordinate.\n\nTo postpone the decision would be unwise.\nThe to-infinitival clause functions as subject of the main predicate.\n\nWhether the figures have changed remains uncertain.\nA finite embedded question functions as the subject; the complete sentence is declarative.\n\nA grammatical imperative normally has an unexpressed subject: please wait is not a fragment simply because you is absent.\n\nA non-finite clause may have an explicit subject, as in for the guests to leave, and may contain temporal expressions without acquiring independent tense.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=14&section=gv5-d14-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:3:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Clause forms and communicative purposes","text":"Clause forms and communicative purposes\n\nClause form describes grammatical construction, such as declarative or interrogative. Communicative function describes what a speaker uses it to do, such as state, ask, request or exclaim; the two do not correspond one to one.\n\nA declarative clause commonly places a subject before its verb: the workshop begins at ten. An interrogative clause commonly asks a question: does the workshop begin at ten? An imperative commonly directs action: begin the workshop at ten. An exclamative commonly expresses a reaction to a striking degree or quality: how quickly the workshop ended! These are useful central patterns, not interchangeable labels for punctuation marks. A full stop does not turn a question-shaped clause into a declarative, and an exclamation mark alone does not make a structure an exclamative clause.\n\nA question tag is a short question attached to another clause: The drawer is locked, isn’t it? For an ordinary confirmation tag, identify the statement’s auxiliary or modal and the subject it refers to. Use a subject pronoun in the tag and normally reverse polarity: a positive statement takes a negative tag; a negative statement takes a positive tag. With no auxiliary in a positive simple present or past statement, use do, does or did: Sara checks the receipts, doesn’t she? This is a useful starting pattern, not a rule for every conversational tag.\n\nInterrogatives include yes-no and wh-patterns. Does Amina teach here? is a yes-no interrogative. Where does Amina teach? asks for a place. Who teaches here? asks about the subject and does not need do-support or an extra subject after the verb. An embedded question normally has different order: I wonder where Amina teaches. The complete clause I wonder ... is declarative, although one part is an interrogative complement. Pay attention to the boundary at which each form label applies rather than treating a sentence containing any wh-word as one direct question.\n\nIn The gate has not opened, has it?, the tag repeats auxiliary has and refers to the gate as it. Negative words also matter: Nobody has signed yet, have they? treats nobody as negative and uses a positive tag. Nobody takes singular has in the statement, while the referring singular they takes have in the tag. Never similarly supports a positive confirmation tag: You never park here, do you? Two conventional special cases are I’m next, aren’t I? and Let’s check again, shall we? The uncontracted am I not? is possible in a formal style. After an imperative, will you? is common, but can you?, could you? and won’t you? can express different interpersonal attitudes. Do not apply statement polarity mechanically to commands.\n\nForm and purpose can diverge. Could you close the window? is interrogative in form but often functions as a polite request. You will submit the form today can function as an instruction, prediction or reminder depending on situation and intonation. Imperatives can offer advice, invitations or permissions as well as commands: have another biscuit. A declarative with rising intonation may seek confirmation in conversation, but the absence of inversion remains part of its grammatical form. Describe the form and the likely use separately instead of replacing grammatical analysis with a guess about the speaker's attitude.\n\nOrdinary opposite-polarity tags often invite confirmation. Falling intonation commonly signals greater confidence; rising intonation commonly leaves more room for a genuine check. Neither tone mechanically determines a speaker’s intention. A positive statement can also have a positive tag: You locked it, did you? may register newly received information, surprise or a challenge. Context and delivery decide among these readings. Thus didn’t you? and did you? can both be grammatical after You locked it, but they are not automatically interchangeable. Informal right? and yeah? can remain unchanged across different subjects; they belong to conversational usage and do not follow the auxiliary agreement pattern.\n\nThe central what- and how-exclamative patterns have their own order: what an unusual design this is; how carefully she explained it. A question can also express surprise: wasn't that remarkable? It remains interrogative in construction even when it performs an exclamatory function. In formal writing, punctuation should reflect the complete utterance and its intended force. An indirect question embedded in a statement usually ends with a full stop, while can you tell me where the office is? ends with a question mark because the complete construction is a direct interrogative. These distinctions become essential in reporting speech and in distinguishing an embedded question from a faulty inversion.\n\nAn echo question can repeat a heard statement or leave the questioned expression in its original position: You put the parcel where? This can request repetition or express surprise; it is not a failed attempt at neutral Where did you put the parcel? Negative yes-no questions also carry expectations: Isn’t the library open? often checks a belief that it is open; Won’t you sit down? can invite. The full not normally follows the subject in Is the library not open?, which sounds more formal than Isn’t the library open? When answering a negative question, add an explicit clause to avoid misunderstanding: Yes, it is open; No, it isn’t open. Form, interaction, register and interpretation must all be considered before correcting conversational word order.\n\nauxiliary + subject + lexical verb: A central yes-no interrogative pattern: does she agree?\n\nwhat + noun phrase + subject + verb / how + adjective or adverb + subject + verb: Central exclamative patterns; their word order differs from a neutral direct question.\n\nstatement + auxiliary/modal + subject pronoun?: An ordinary confirmation tag normally reverses statement polarity and preserves subject reference.\n\ndeclarative order + wh-expression in the questioned position: One conversational echo-question pattern: You sent it to whom? It is not the default neutral information question.\n\nThe door is open.\nA declarative commonly used as a statement, but it could also hint that someone should close the door.\n\nWho authorised the payment?\nA subject wh-question uses authorised directly without adding did.\n\nCould you tell me where the archive is?\nThe complete sentence is interrogative, while the embedded question where the archive is has no inversion.\n\nThe spare key is in the drawer, isn’t it?\nPositive is takes negative isn’t; it refers to the key.\n\nNobody has signed yet, have they?\nNegative nobody supports a positive tag; singular they takes have, although nobody takes has.\n\nLet’s compare the two estimates, shall we?\nThe conventional shall we tag seeks agreement to an inclusive suggestion.\n\nYou stored the files where?\nA conversational echo question keeps where in place to check or react to the location just mentioned.\n\nA subject wh-question such as who left? does not use the same auxiliary-subject inversion as who did she meet?\n\nCommunicative force depends on context: a polite interrogative can request action without being an imperative in grammatical form.\n\nOpposite polarity is the ordinary confirmation pattern, not a ban on positive-positive tags. Imperative tags have their own conventional choices.\n\nNobody can take singular has in the main clause and referring singular they with have in a tag; agreement is local to each clause.\n\nEcho questions and invariant conversational tags should not be judged solely by the pattern for neutral inverted yes-no questions.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=14&section=gv5-d14-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clause types","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clause-types"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"Question tags — Cambridge English Grammar Today","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/question-tags"},{"publisher":"Question tags — British Council LearnEnglish","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/b1-b2/question-tags"},{"publisher":"Questions: echo and checking questions — Cambridge English Grammar Today","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/questions-echo-and-checking-questions"},{"publisher":"Questions: yes-no questions — Cambridge English Grammar Today","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/questions-yes-no-questions-are-you-feeling-cold"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:3:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Simple, coordinated and subordinate architecture","text":"Simple, coordinated and subordinate architecture\n\nSentence architecture describes how clauses combine. In the finite-clause classroom classification used here, a simple sentence has one main finite clause, a compound sentence coordinates main clauses, and a complex sentence includes finite subordination.\n\nA simple sentence can still be long: the patient researcher in the upstairs office checked all the entries carefully. Its length does not create another main clause. A compound sentence joins main clauses, as in the researcher checked the entries, and her colleague verified the totals. A complex sentence includes a subordinate finite clause: the researcher checked the entries because several totals looked unusual. A compound-complex sentence has at least two coordinated main finite clauses and at least one subordinate finite clause. These labels offer a useful first map, but they do not replace a detailed analysis of every embedded unit.\n\nState the counting convention when non-finite clauses are present. For the classroom labels in this lesson, the researcher hopes to finish today has one main finite clause and an infinitival complement. We describe that full structure rather than pretending that to finish today contains no clause. Some grammatical accounts use complex more broadly to include non-finite subordination. A different count based on an explicit convention need not be an error. What matters for accurate editing is that you recognise the complement relationship and the understood subject, even if a school exercise uses a narrower label.\n\nCoordination can occur below clause level. Amina opened and checked the package coordinates predicates sharing a subject and object; it does not automatically display two separately expressed main clauses. In Amina opened the package and Bilal checked the contents, the two subjects help reveal full clause coordination. Subordinate clauses can fill selected complement positions: she believes that the claim is mistaken. Others serve as adjuncts expressing reason, condition or time: she left when the discussion ended. A clause modifying a noun, such as the claim that the witness repeated, has a different internal relation from the content clause in the claim that the witness was mistaken.\n\nSubordination establishes grammatical dependency without determining every logical inference. Although the road was wet, the driver continued presents a concession, but grammar alone does not tell us whether continuing was wise. Coordination with and can suggest sequence, addition or other relations from context. Avoid assuming that any two grammatical clauses can be joined without changing the message. When transforming a complex sentence into two independent sentences, preserve the reason, condition or qualification through explicit wording if it is important. Similarly, an embedded complement can contain further subordination: she said that she would leave if the meeting ran late. A layered parse shows the conditional clause inside the reported content rather than attaching it automatically to said.\n\nmain finite clause + coordinator + main finite clause: Full main-clause coordination under the classroom convention.\n\nmain clause containing [content clause containing [adjunct clause]]: Subordination can be nested within another subordinate clause.\n\nThe technician opened and cleaned the scanner.\nThe sentence coordinates predicates with shared arguments; and does not by itself prove two full main clauses.\n\nThe technician opened the scanner, and the assistant photographed its interior.\nTwo separately expressed main clauses form compound architecture.\n\nThe technician said that the scanner would work if the sensor was replaced.\nThe reported content includes a conditional clause; brackets can make that embedding explicit.\n\nSome descriptions count non-finite subordination when calling a sentence complex; use the specified convention and still identify the non-finite clause.\n\nA sentence with several coordinated verbs is not automatically a compound sentence with several fully independent clauses.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=14&section=gv5-d14-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Clause types","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clause-types"},{"publisher":"Complements - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/complements"},{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:3:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Clause boundaries and accessible visual parsing","text":"Clause boundaries and accessible visual parsing\n\nA sentence-boundary error occurs when written clauses are left without the relationship or punctuation their intended construction needs. A bracketed parse can reveal missing main clauses, fused clauses and comma splices.\n\nFor ordinary formal paragraphs, a subordinate reason clause alone usually leaves the sentence unfinished: because the road was blocked. Adding a main clause completes the intended relationship: because the road was blocked, the delivery arrived late. A fragment may instead lack a main predicate, as in the report beside the printer. That noun phrase is perfectly useful as a label or answer fragment, but it is not a full neutral declarative sentence about what the report did or was. Context decides whether fragmentary wording is a defect or a deliberate feature of a heading, conversation or literary style.\n\nTwo independent clauses placed together without a suitable boundary form a fused sentence: the lights failed the audience waited. Joining them only with a comma creates a comma splice: the lights failed, the audience waited. Some teaching uses run-on as a broad label for both; this course names the specific error whenever possible. Repairs include a full stop, a semicolon, a coordinator with appropriate punctuation, or subordination that preserves the intended relation. A colon can also join a complete first clause to a second clause that explains or specifies it. Do not use a connector merely because it produces grammatical punctuation; its meaning must fit.\n\nA linking adverb does not behave like a coordinating conjunction. The lights failed; however, the audience waited is a grammatical contrast between independent clauses. However does not itself license joining those clauses with a comma alone. Commas around supplementary clauses and introductory material follow additional conventions developed fully in Day 24. A comma before and between short closely connected main clauses can be a style choice; the essential point here is not to separate a subject from its predicate or create a boundary that contradicts the intended structure.\n\nUse textual brackets as a visual parsing method that is also readable by assistive technology. In [MAIN the courier waited [SUBORDINATE because the office was closed]], the inner clause supplies a reason within the main construction. More detailed brackets can identify [SUBJECT NP the courier] and [VERB GROUP waited]. A parse is a representation of an analysis, not a substitute for explaining it. If a sentence seems awkward, locate the finite clauses, identify any non-finite complements, label the connectors and then inspect the boundaries. This sequence avoids a common editing error: fixing a perceived comma problem while leaving the actual missing main clause or mistaken logical relationship untouched.\n\n[MAIN ... [SUBORDINATE because ...]]: A readable nesting shows that the reason clause is dependent in the whole construction.\n\nindependent clause ; however, independent clause: A semicolon or full stop supplies the boundary; however signals a logical contrast.\n\nThe path was flooded. We took the bridge.\nTwo independent sentences have an explicit full-stop boundary.\n\nThe path was flooded, so we took the bridge.\nSo coordinates two clauses while expressing the resulting action.\n\n[MAIN We took the bridge [SUBORDINATE because the path was flooded]].\nThe bracketed parse shows the reason clause as subordinate to the main message.\n\nFragments are normal in headings and many conversational answers; the requirement for full sentences concerns the specified writing context.\n\nRun-on has broad and narrow teaching uses. Naming fused sentence or comma splice avoids a terminology-based false disagreement.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=14&section=gv5-d14-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:3:4","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Clause boundaries and accessible visual parsing","text":"Clause boundaries and accessible visual parsing\n\nA sentence-boundary error occurs when written clauses are left without the relationship or punctuation their intended construction needs. A bracketed parse can reveal missing main clauses, fused clauses and comma splices.\n\nFor ordinary formal paragraphs, a subordinate reason clause alone usually leaves the sentence unfinished: because the road was blocked. Adding a main clause completes the intended relationship: because the road was blocked, the delivery arrived late. A fragment may instead lack a main predicate, as in the report beside the printer. That noun phrase is perfectly useful as a label or answer fragment, but it is not a full neutral declarative sentence about what the report did or was. Context decides whether fragmentary wording is a defect or a deliberate feature of a heading, conversation or literary style.\n\nTwo independent clauses placed together without a suitable boundary form a fused sentence: the lights failed the audience waited. Joining them only with a comma creates a comma splice: the lights failed, the audience waited. Some teaching uses run-on as a broad label for both; this course names the specific error whenever possible. Repairs include a full stop, a semicolon, a coordinator with appropriate punctuation, or subordination that preserves the intended relation. A colon can also join a complete first clause to a second clause that explains or specifies it. Do not use a connector merely because it produces grammatical punctuation; its meaning must fit.\n\nA linking adverb does not behave like a coordinating conjunction. The lights failed; however, the audience waited is a grammatical contrast between independent clauses. However does not itself license joining those clauses with a comma alone. Commas around supplementary clauses and introductory material follow additional conventions developed fully in Day 24. A comma before and between short closely connected main clauses can be a style choice; the essential point here is not to separate a subject from its predicate or create a boundary that contradicts the intended structure.\n\nUse textual brackets as a visual parsing method that is also readable by assistive technology. In [MAIN the courier waited [SUBORDINATE because the office was closed]], the inner clause supplies a reason within the main construction. More detailed brackets can identify [SUBJECT NP the courier] and [VERB GROUP waited]. A parse is a representation of an analysis, not a substitute for explaining it. If a sentence seems awkward, locate the finite clauses, identify any non-finite complements, label the connectors and then inspect the boundaries. This sequence avoids a common editing error: fixing a perceived comma problem while leaving the actual missing main clause or mistaken logical relationship untouched.\n\n[MAIN ... [SUBORDINATE because ...]]: A readable nesting shows that the reason clause is dependent in the whole construction.\n\nindependent clause ; however, independent clause: A semicolon or full stop supplies the boundary; however signals a logical contrast.\n\nThe path was flooded. We took the bridge.\nTwo independent sentences have an explicit full-stop boundary.\n\nThe path was flooded, so we took the bridge.\nSo coordinates two clauses while expressing the resulting action.\n\n[MAIN We took the bridge [SUBORDINATE because the path was flooded]].\nThe bracketed parse shows the reason clause as subordinate to the main message.\n\nFragments are normal in headings and many conversational answers; the requirement for full sentences concerns the specified writing context.\n\nRun-on has broad and narrow teaching uses. Naming fused sentence or comma splice avoids a terminology-based false disagreement.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=14&section=gv5-d14-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:4:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Main, subordinate, finite and non-finite clauses","text":"Main, subordinate, finite and non-finite clauses\n\nA clause is a grammatical unit organised around a verb. Main clauses supply a principal message; subordinate clauses function within a larger structure. Finite and non-finite distinguish verb forms and clause construction, not whether an event is real.\n\nThe train stopped is a main declarative clause. In the train stopped because a signal failed, because a signal failed is a subordinate reason clause associated with the main event. The first clause can form an independent neutral statement; the because-clause normally relies on a larger construction in formal connected prose. A clause is not simply any group of words that happens to contain a noun and a verb spelling. Identify the grammatical organisation of the group and its relation to the larger sentence. A phrase can contain a clause, as in the promise that she would return.\n\nA finite clause contains a finite verb or auxiliary, as in she returns, she returned and she may return. The modal may is finite even though it does not display a person contrast such as returns versus return. A non-finite clause is headed by an infinitival or participial form without independent tense inflection: to return soon, returning home, or signed yesterday in the noun phrase the documents signed yesterday. A non-finite clause can still express time relative to context. In she hoped to return next week, next week gives time reference, while to return remains non-finite.\n\nNon-finite clauses often leave their subject understood: she hopes to return normally makes her the intended returner. Some have an expressed subject: for the visitors to arrive early would be convenient. The clause for the visitors to arrive early serves as the subject of would be convenient, despite lacking independent tense. Finite clauses may also function as subjects, objects or other complements. Whether the grant will continue remains unclear has a finite interrogative clause as its subject. The whole sentence is declarative; the embedded subject clause does not use direct-question inversion.\n\nThe main/subordinate distinction is not identical to the finite/non-finite distinction. Many subordinate clauses are finite, while other subordinate clauses are non-finite. Imperatives deserve separate attention because their ordinary main verb is a plain form and their subject is usually unexpressed: take your seat. Different grammatical frameworks discuss their finiteness differently; this course identifies the imperative construction rather than inferring that take your seat is an ungrammatical fragment. Supplementary non-finite clauses such as weather permitting have their own internal structure. The practical question is how the clause is licensed and interpreted in the complete construction, not whether it mechanically contains a written subject plus an overt tense ending.\n\nmain clause + because + finite subordinate clause: The subordinate clause supplies a reason for the principal message.\n\nfor + expressed subject + to-infinitive clause: A non-finite clause can contain its own expressed subject.\n\nThe meeting ended because the chair was ill.\nEnded and was are finite; the because-clause is subordinate.\n\nTo postpone the decision would be unwise.\nThe to-infinitival clause functions as subject of the main predicate.\n\nWhether the figures have changed remains uncertain.\nA finite embedded question functions as the subject; the complete sentence is declarative.\n\nA grammatical imperative normally has an unexpressed subject: please wait is not a fragment simply because you is absent.\n\nA non-finite clause may have an explicit subject, as in for the guests to leave, and may contain temporal expressions without acquiring independent tense.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=14&section=gv5-d14-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:4:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Clause forms and communicative purposes","text":"Clause forms and communicative purposes\n\nClause form describes grammatical construction, such as declarative or interrogative. Communicative function describes what a speaker uses it to do, such as state, ask, request or exclaim; the two do not correspond one to one.\n\nA declarative clause commonly places a subject before its verb: the workshop begins at ten. An interrogative clause commonly asks a question: does the workshop begin at ten? An imperative commonly directs action: begin the workshop at ten. An exclamative commonly expresses a reaction to a striking degree or quality: how quickly the workshop ended! These are useful central patterns, not interchangeable labels for punctuation marks. A full stop does not turn a question-shaped clause into a declarative, and an exclamation mark alone does not make a structure an exclamative clause.\n\nA question tag is a short question attached to another clause: The drawer is locked, isn’t it? For an ordinary confirmation tag, identify the statement’s auxiliary or modal and the subject it refers to. Use a subject pronoun in the tag and normally reverse polarity: a positive statement takes a negative tag; a negative statement takes a positive tag. With no auxiliary in a positive simple present or past statement, use do, does or did: Sara checks the receipts, doesn’t she? This is a useful starting pattern, not a rule for every conversational tag.\n\nInterrogatives include yes-no and wh-patterns. Does Amina teach here? is a yes-no interrogative. Where does Amina teach? asks for a place. Who teaches here? asks about the subject and does not need do-support or an extra subject after the verb. An embedded question normally has different order: I wonder where Amina teaches. The complete clause I wonder ... is declarative, although one part is an interrogative complement. Pay attention to the boundary at which each form label applies rather than treating a sentence containing any wh-word as one direct question.\n\nIn The gate has not opened, has it?, the tag repeats auxiliary has and refers to the gate as it. Negative words also matter: Nobody has signed yet, have they? treats nobody as negative and uses a positive tag. Nobody takes singular has in the statement, while the referring singular they takes have in the tag. Never similarly supports a positive confirmation tag: You never park here, do you? Two conventional special cases are I’m next, aren’t I? and Let’s check again, shall we? The uncontracted am I not? is possible in a formal style. After an imperative, will you? is common, but can you?, could you? and won’t you? can express different interpersonal attitudes. Do not apply statement polarity mechanically to commands.\n\nForm and purpose can diverge. Could you close the window? is interrogative in form but often functions as a polite request. You will submit the form today can function as an instruction, prediction or reminder depending on situation and intonation. Imperatives can offer advice, invitations or permissions as well as commands: have another biscuit. A declarative with rising intonation may seek confirmation in conversation, but the absence of inversion remains part of its grammatical form. Describe the form and the likely use separately instead of replacing grammatical analysis with a guess about the speaker's attitude.\n\nOrdinary opposite-polarity tags often invite confirmation. Falling intonation commonly signals greater confidence; rising intonation commonly leaves more room for a genuine check. Neither tone mechanically determines a speaker’s intention. A positive statement can also have a positive tag: You locked it, did you? may register newly received information, surprise or a challenge. Context and delivery decide among these readings. Thus didn’t you? and did you? can both be grammatical after You locked it, but they are not automatically interchangeable. Informal right? and yeah? can remain unchanged across different subjects; they belong to conversational usage and do not follow the auxiliary agreement pattern.\n\nThe central what- and how-exclamative patterns have their own order: what an unusual design this is; how carefully she explained it. A question can also express surprise: wasn't that remarkable? It remains interrogative in construction even when it performs an exclamatory function. In formal writing, punctuation should reflect the complete utterance and its intended force. An indirect question embedded in a statement usually ends with a full stop, while can you tell me where the office is? ends with a question mark because the complete construction is a direct interrogative. These distinctions become essential in reporting speech and in distinguishing an embedded question from a faulty inversion.\n\nAn echo question can repeat a heard statement or leave the questioned expression in its original position: You put the parcel where? This can request repetition or express surprise; it is not a failed attempt at neutral Where did you put the parcel? Negative yes-no questions also carry expectations: Isn’t the library open? often checks a belief that it is open; Won’t you sit down? can invite. The full not normally follows the subject in Is the library not open?, which sounds more formal than Isn’t the library open? When answering a negative question, add an explicit clause to avoid misunderstanding: Yes, it is open; No, it isn’t open. Form, interaction, register and interpretation must all be considered before correcting conversational word order.\n\nauxiliary + subject + lexical verb: A central yes-no interrogative pattern: does she agree?\n\nwhat + noun phrase + subject + verb / how + adjective or adverb + subject + verb: Central exclamative patterns; their word order differs from a neutral direct question.\n\nstatement + auxiliary/modal + subject pronoun?: An ordinary confirmation tag normally reverses statement polarity and preserves subject reference.\n\ndeclarative order + wh-expression in the questioned position: One conversational echo-question pattern: You sent it to whom? It is not the default neutral information question.\n\nThe door is open.\nA declarative commonly used as a statement, but it could also hint that someone should close the door.\n\nWho authorised the payment?\nA subject wh-question uses authorised directly without adding did.\n\nCould you tell me where the archive is?\nThe complete sentence is interrogative, while the embedded question where the archive is has no inversion.\n\nThe spare key is in the drawer, isn’t it?\nPositive is takes negative isn’t; it refers to the key.\n\nNobody has signed yet, have they?\nNegative nobody supports a positive tag; singular they takes have, although nobody takes has.\n\nLet’s compare the two estimates, shall we?\nThe conventional shall we tag seeks agreement to an inclusive suggestion.\n\nYou stored the files where?\nA conversational echo question keeps where in place to check or react to the location just mentioned.\n\nA subject wh-question such as who left? does not use the same auxiliary-subject inversion as who did she meet?\n\nCommunicative force depends on context: a polite interrogative can request action without being an imperative in grammatical form.\n\nOpposite polarity is the ordinary confirmation pattern, not a ban on positive-positive tags. Imperative tags have their own conventional choices.\n\nNobody can take singular has in the main clause and referring singular they with have in a tag; agreement is local to each clause.\n\nEcho questions and invariant conversational tags should not be judged solely by the pattern for neutral inverted yes-no questions.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=14&section=gv5-d14-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clause types","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clause-types"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"Question tags — Cambridge English Grammar Today","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/question-tags"},{"publisher":"Question tags — British Council LearnEnglish","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/b1-b2/question-tags"},{"publisher":"Questions: echo and checking questions — Cambridge English Grammar Today","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/questions-echo-and-checking-questions"},{"publisher":"Questions: yes-no questions — Cambridge English Grammar Today","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/questions-yes-no-questions-are-you-feeling-cold"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:4:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Simple, coordinated and subordinate architecture","text":"Simple, coordinated and subordinate architecture\n\nSentence architecture describes how clauses combine. In the finite-clause classroom classification used here, a simple sentence has one main finite clause, a compound sentence coordinates main clauses, and a complex sentence includes finite subordination.\n\nA simple sentence can still be long: the patient researcher in the upstairs office checked all the entries carefully. Its length does not create another main clause. A compound sentence joins main clauses, as in the researcher checked the entries, and her colleague verified the totals. A complex sentence includes a subordinate finite clause: the researcher checked the entries because several totals looked unusual. A compound-complex sentence has at least two coordinated main finite clauses and at least one subordinate finite clause. These labels offer a useful first map, but they do not replace a detailed analysis of every embedded unit.\n\nState the counting convention when non-finite clauses are present. For the classroom labels in this lesson, the researcher hopes to finish today has one main finite clause and an infinitival complement. We describe that full structure rather than pretending that to finish today contains no clause. Some grammatical accounts use complex more broadly to include non-finite subordination. A different count based on an explicit convention need not be an error. What matters for accurate editing is that you recognise the complement relationship and the understood subject, even if a school exercise uses a narrower label.\n\nCoordination can occur below clause level. Amina opened and checked the package coordinates predicates sharing a subject and object; it does not automatically display two separately expressed main clauses. In Amina opened the package and Bilal checked the contents, the two subjects help reveal full clause coordination. Subordinate clauses can fill selected complement positions: she believes that the claim is mistaken. Others serve as adjuncts expressing reason, condition or time: she left when the discussion ended. A clause modifying a noun, such as the claim that the witness repeated, has a different internal relation from the content clause in the claim that the witness was mistaken.\n\nSubordination establishes grammatical dependency without determining every logical inference. Although the road was wet, the driver continued presents a concession, but grammar alone does not tell us whether continuing was wise. Coordination with and can suggest sequence, addition or other relations from context. Avoid assuming that any two grammatical clauses can be joined without changing the message. When transforming a complex sentence into two independent sentences, preserve the reason, condition or qualification through explicit wording if it is important. Similarly, an embedded complement can contain further subordination: she said that she would leave if the meeting ran late. A layered parse shows the conditional clause inside the reported content rather than attaching it automatically to said.\n\nmain finite clause + coordinator + main finite clause: Full main-clause coordination under the classroom convention.\n\nmain clause containing [content clause containing [adjunct clause]]: Subordination can be nested within another subordinate clause.\n\nThe technician opened and cleaned the scanner.\nThe sentence coordinates predicates with shared arguments; and does not by itself prove two full main clauses.\n\nThe technician opened the scanner, and the assistant photographed its interior.\nTwo separately expressed main clauses form compound architecture.\n\nThe technician said that the scanner would work if the sensor was replaced.\nThe reported content includes a conditional clause; brackets can make that embedding explicit.\n\nSome descriptions count non-finite subordination when calling a sentence complex; use the specified convention and still identify the non-finite clause.\n\nA sentence with several coordinated verbs is not automatically a compound sentence with several fully independent clauses.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=14&section=gv5-d14-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Clause types","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clause-types"},{"publisher":"Complements - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/complements"},{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:4:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Clause boundaries and accessible visual parsing","text":"Clause boundaries and accessible visual parsing\n\nA sentence-boundary error occurs when written clauses are left without the relationship or punctuation their intended construction needs. A bracketed parse can reveal missing main clauses, fused clauses and comma splices.\n\nFor ordinary formal paragraphs, a subordinate reason clause alone usually leaves the sentence unfinished: because the road was blocked. Adding a main clause completes the intended relationship: because the road was blocked, the delivery arrived late. A fragment may instead lack a main predicate, as in the report beside the printer. That noun phrase is perfectly useful as a label or answer fragment, but it is not a full neutral declarative sentence about what the report did or was. Context decides whether fragmentary wording is a defect or a deliberate feature of a heading, conversation or literary style.\n\nTwo independent clauses placed together without a suitable boundary form a fused sentence: the lights failed the audience waited. Joining them only with a comma creates a comma splice: the lights failed, the audience waited. Some teaching uses run-on as a broad label for both; this course names the specific error whenever possible. Repairs include a full stop, a semicolon, a coordinator with appropriate punctuation, or subordination that preserves the intended relation. A colon can also join a complete first clause to a second clause that explains or specifies it. Do not use a connector merely because it produces grammatical punctuation; its meaning must fit.\n\nA linking adverb does not behave like a coordinating conjunction. The lights failed; however, the audience waited is a grammatical contrast between independent clauses. However does not itself license joining those clauses with a comma alone. Commas around supplementary clauses and introductory material follow additional conventions developed fully in Day 24. A comma before and between short closely connected main clauses can be a style choice; the essential point here is not to separate a subject from its predicate or create a boundary that contradicts the intended structure.\n\nUse textual brackets as a visual parsing method that is also readable by assistive technology. In [MAIN the courier waited [SUBORDINATE because the office was closed]], the inner clause supplies a reason within the main construction. More detailed brackets can identify [SUBJECT NP the courier] and [VERB GROUP waited]. A parse is a representation of an analysis, not a substitute for explaining it. If a sentence seems awkward, locate the finite clauses, identify any non-finite complements, label the connectors and then inspect the boundaries. This sequence avoids a common editing error: fixing a perceived comma problem while leaving the actual missing main clause or mistaken logical relationship untouched.\n\n[MAIN ... [SUBORDINATE because ...]]: A readable nesting shows that the reason clause is dependent in the whole construction.\n\nindependent clause ; however, independent clause: A semicolon or full stop supplies the boundary; however signals a logical contrast.\n\nThe path was flooded. We took the bridge.\nTwo independent sentences have an explicit full-stop boundary.\n\nThe path was flooded, so we took the bridge.\nSo coordinates two clauses while expressing the resulting action.\n\n[MAIN We took the bridge [SUBORDINATE because the path was flooded]].\nThe bracketed parse shows the reason clause as subordinate to the main message.\n\nFragments are normal in headings and many conversational answers; the requirement for full sentences concerns the specified writing context.\n\nRun-on has broad and narrow teaching uses. Naming fused sentence or comma splice avoids a terminology-based false disagreement.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=14&section=gv5-d14-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Clauses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses_2"},{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:5:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Person, number and the structure of the subject","text":"Person, number and the structure of the subject\n\nSubject–verb agreement is the correspondence between a subject’s grammatical person and number and the finite verb forms that show those distinctions. Agreement depends on structure and interpretation, not simply the nearest visible noun.\n\nCompare The guard checks the door and The guards check the door. In present simple, an ordinary lexical verb normally adds -s with a third-person singular subject. I and you use the base form even when referring to one person: I check; you check. Be has am, is and are in the present and was and were in the past. Have has has with a third-person singular subject. Most other past-tense verbs do not change for number: the guard checked and the guards checked. Agreement therefore does not mean adding an ending to every verb in every sentence.\n\nFind the complete subject and its head, the central noun in an ordinary noun phrase. In The condition of the external walls is worrying, condition is the head; walls occurs inside a modifying of-phrase. A relative clause or parenthetical phrase also does not normally replace that head as agreement controller: The folder that contains the photographs is missing. When auxiliaries occur, the finite auxiliary carries the relevant agreement: The folders have been moved. Do not alter moved to make it plural. Subject identification is a structural analysis; merely crossing out every prepositional phrase can remove information needed to interpret the sentence.\n\nCoordination with and normally creates a plural subject: The clerk and the porter are waiting. An accompanying phrase such as together with, as well as or along with is not ordinary subject coordination: The clerk, together with two porters, is waiting. If two descriptions identify a single individual, the subject may be interpreted as singular: The founder and treasurer is one person in this organisation. If the founder and the treasurer are different people, use plural agreement. Articles and context help identify the intended reference; do not decide identity from a memorised surface formula alone.\n\nThe familiar head-noun rule is a reliable starting point for ordinary noun phrases, not an exhaustive theory of English agreement. Quantificational constructions, collectives and notional readings require further analysis in later modules. Distinguish genuine coordination from a phrase merely mentioning another person. Also distinguish the subject from an object or predicative complement: The main difficulty is the missing records does not make records the subject of is in this ordinary clause. Where the number or identity of coordinated referents is unclear, rewriting to make that identity explicit is often better than defending an ambiguous choice by rule recital.\n\nthird-person singular subject + present verb in -s: A common present-simple agreement pattern; be and some verbs have special forms.\n\nhead noun + modifying phrase + finite verb: In an ordinary noun phrase, the modifier does not replace the head as agreement controller.\n\nThe colour of the curtains has faded.\nSingular colour controls has; curtains is inside the of-phrase.\n\nThe inspectors have been reviewing the photographs.\nPlural inspectors controls finite have; the other forms do not acquire plural endings.\n\nThe director, as well as two advisers, is attending.\nAs well as introduces an accompanying phrase rather than a second coordinated subject.\n\nMost lexical past-tense forms do not distinguish singular and plural: The driver arrived and The drivers arrived. Do not manufacture a plural past ending.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=10&section=gv5-d10-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Subject–verb agreement - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subject-verb-agreement"},{"publisher":"Subject-Verb Agreement","url":"https://webapps.towson.edu/ows/sub-verb.htm"},{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:5:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Collectives, indefinite pronouns and competing agreement cues","text":"Collectives, indefinite pronouns and competing agreement cues\n\nEnglish agreement can reflect grammatical form, the meaning of a group, or proximity in particular coordinated patterns. Collective nouns and quantifying pronouns require qualified guidance about reference, register and established regional variation.\n\nA collective noun names a group: team, government, committee, family. In British English, singular agreement commonly presents the group as a unit, while plural agreement can present its members: The committee has issued its report; The committee have changed their minds. American English more often uses singular agreement with these ordinary group nouns, especially when acting as one unit. This is a broad convention rather than a claim that every collective has identical behaviour in all varieties. Police ordinarily takes plural agreement: The police are investigating. Do not transfer a rule for committee blindly to police.\n\nIndefinite pronouns and determiners form different patterns. Everyone, somebody, nobody and each ordinarily take singular finite agreement: Everyone is ready; Each of the rooms has a window. Both, few, many and several are plural in their ordinary countable uses. Singular they can refer back to a grammatically singular person expression without changing its verb: Everyone has their own ticket. All, some and most depend on what is quantified: Some of the water is contaminated; Some of the wells are contaminated. None can also support singular or plural interpretation, particularly when referring to a countable set.\n\nNeither of and either of commonly take singular agreement in careful formal prose: Neither of the plans is suitable. Plural agreement occurs in established conversational usage, especially when a plural noun phrase is close to the verb; a register-sensitive exercise should identify its target convention. With subjects joined by or or nor, a common editing convention makes the verb agree with the nearer coordinated part: Neither the director nor the assistants are available; Neither the assistants nor the director is available. Recast awkward combinations, particularly those mixing person, rather than assuming proximity alone makes every possible sequence elegant or uncontroversial.\n\nNotional agreement follows an interpretation of number rather than surface form alone. Proximity agreement follows a nearby eligible expression in constructions where that convention is established. These should not be confused with accidental agreement attraction, as in The cost of the repairs are high in ordinary formal prose. Collective pronouns should make the intended unit-versus-members reference clear across the passage, though a writer may deliberately shift perspective when context supports it. None of is especially unsuitable for an absolute always-singular rule: authoritative usage accounts describe both singular and plural verbs. State the desired register and interpretation when a task depends on the choice.\n\ncollective noun + singular/plural verb: Possible unit/member contrast, particularly in British English; conventions vary by noun and variety.\n\neach/everyone + singular verb; both/several + plural verb: Ordinary agreement of these specific quantifying expressions.\n\nEveryone has their own locker.\nEveryone takes singular has; singular their provides inclusive personal reference.\n\nThe committee have not agreed among themselves.\nBritish plural agreement foregrounds the members’ differing views.\n\nNeither the secretary nor the assistants are free.\nPlural are follows the common nearer-subject convention, with assistants nearest.\n\nNone of the candidates is available and None of the candidates are available both occur in standard usage; guidance differs in its preferred register. A trustworthy answer key must not reject one without an explicitly justified criterion.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=10&section=gv5-d10-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Group nouns | LearnEnglish","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/english-grammar-reference/group-nouns"},{"publisher":"Nouns: singular and plural - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-singular-and-plural"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"},{"publisher":"Neither, neither … nor and not … either - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/neither-neither-nor-and-not-either"},{"publisher":"No, none and none of - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/none"},{"publisher":"none pronoun - Definition, pictures, pronunciation and usage notes | Oxford Advanced Learner's Dictionary at OxfordLearnersDictionaries.com","url":"https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/definition/english/none_1"},{"publisher":"Subject-Verb Agreement","url":"https://webapps.towson.edu/ows/sub-verb.htm"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:5:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Relative clauses, inversion and existential constructions","text":"Relative clauses, inversion and existential constructions\n\nEach clause has its own agreement relationships. Relative-clause verbs follow the intended antecedent, and inversion can place a subject after an auxiliary or verb. Existential there introduces an additional construction whose formal agreement commonly follows its postverbal noun phrase.\n\nIn The drivers who work nights receive an allowance, the relative pronoun who refers to plural drivers, so its clause uses work. The main clause also has plural drivers as its subject. In The driver who works nights receives an allowance, both relevant subjects are singular. Analyse the clauses separately; the verb of one clause does not mechanically control the other. A longer subject can contain a plural antecedent inside a singular outer phrase: One of the drivers who work nights receives an allowance. Outer one controls receives, while drivers supplies the usual antecedent for who work nights.\n\nExpressions such as one of those people who usually select a relative clause describing the plural group: She is one of those people who notice everything. A relative attached specifically to the individual one has a different interpretation and may take a singular verb. The only one of the drivers who has a permit normally singles out the one permitted individual. Do not decide all relative attachments from the presence of the word only alone. Where the intended scope is important, make it explicit: She is the only driver who has a permit; among the permitted drivers, she is the only one available.\n\nIn questions, the subject often follows a finite auxiliary: Are the documents ready? Does the document contain a signature? In locative inversion, the subject can follow the lexical verb: On the shelf were three sealed boxes. Find boxes before selecting were. Existential There is a problem and There are several problems conventionally match the form of be to the postverbal noun phrase in careful formal writing. Contracted There’s several problems is widespread in conversation; revise it to There are several problems for the stated formal convention without presenting all conversational occurrences as evidence of ignorance.\n\nGrammatical frameworks differ in analysing existential there: many modern accounts treat it as a dummy subject, while traditional teaching calls the following noun phrase the real subject. Both descriptions can support the same practical formal-agreement guidance if terminology is made explicit. Not every word after a verb is a subject: compare On the desk lies the report with The clerk filed the report. Negative inversion also leaves agreement with the actual subject: Never have the records been so clear. Parsing requires clause structure and reference, not an instruction to choose whichever noun is closest to the finite verb.\n\nantecedent + who/which/that + agreeing finite verb: Relative-clause agreement reflects the intended antecedent.\n\nThere is + singular noun phrase; There are + plural noun phrase: Common careful formal pattern in existential be-constructions.\n\nOne of the books that explain the method is missing.\nBooks controls explain in the relative; one controls main-clause is.\n\nNear the entrance stand two noticeboards.\nPlural noticeboards follows stand and is its subject.\n\nThere are three reasons why the proposal needs revision.\nThe plural postverbal noun phrase selects are in this formal existential construction.\n\nInformal contracted there’s commonly introduces plural associates. For formal prose, use the agreed full form; distinguish register-sensitive editing from a universal ban on attested conversational grammar.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=10&section=gv5-d10-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"There is, there’s and there are - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/there-is-there-s-and-there-are"},{"publisher":"Subject-Verb Agreement","url":"https://webapps.towson.edu/ows/sub-verb.htm"},{"publisher":"Inversion - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/inversion"},{"publisher":"Relative clauses - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/relative-clauses"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:5:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Quantities, measures, titles and plural-looking forms","text":"Quantities, measures, titles and plural-looking forms\n\nAgreement with a quantity depends on what is measured or counted and how it is construed. The spelling of a noun, a percentage or a numerical expression does not by itself establish the number required in every use.\n\nSome nouns look plural but take singular agreement in their ordinary meanings: The news is encouraging; Mathematics is compulsory. Other nouns normally take plural agreement: The trousers are wet; The scissors are blunt. A pair of scissors is different because pair is singular: This pair of scissors is blunt. Series has the same spelling in singular and plural: This series is useful; These two series are useful. Use determiners, meaning and a learner’s dictionary where the form alone is misleading. Do not add an invented singular police or a plural news merely to make the ending match a simplified pattern.\n\nFractions and percentages commonly follow the number interpretation of the of-complement: Half of the water is gone; Half of the bottles are empty; Twenty per cent of the applicants were absent. A percentage without an explicit complement still requires context: Twenty per cent is a substantial share treats the proportion as one amount. If the complement is a collective, its own possible unit/member readings can matter. A number of applications have arrived is a conventional plural quantifying construction; The number of applications has risen treats number as the quantity being discussed. These are different structures, not contradictory examples of the same rule.\n\nA measure phrase can denote one amount: Ten kilometres is too far to walk today; Three years is a long wait. Plural agreement remains natural when separate units or their passage are in view: Three years have passed. A title naming one work commonly receives singular agreement despite plural words inside it: Great Expectations is a novel. This does not make every plural proper name singular in every variety; team and organisation names may follow collective conventions. Mathematical statements also have conventional forms, such as Two plus two is four, but do not infer a universal rule for every coordinated numerical expression.\n\nMeaning can change the number behaviour of the same spelling. Statistics is difficult can name a discipline; The statistics are misleading refers to numerical figures. Politics can name an academic topic or a person’s views, with differing agreement: Her politics are well known. Such contrasts illustrate why grammatical number, semantic quantity and lexical convention must be distinguished. A rigid instruction to make the verb agree with the nearest noun fails with The number of files is increasing; an unrestricted head-noun slogan also needs qualification for quantifying A number of files are missing. Analyse the construction, then state which meaning and editorial convention support the chosen verb.\n\nfraction/percentage + of + mass/plural count noun: Agreement commonly follows the relevant mass or count interpretation.\n\na number of + plural noun + plural verb; the number of + plural noun + singular verb: Conventional contrast between quantifying several items and discussing their number.\n\nHalf of the evidence is missing.\nEvidence is a mass noun here, so the fraction has singular agreement.\n\nHalf of the witnesses are waiting outside.\nThe fraction ranges over plural people, giving plural agreement.\n\nThe number of reports has doubled, although a number of reports are still incomplete.\nThe first subject concerns a figure; the second quantifies several reports.\n\nA period can be construed as one measure or as separate years whose passage is asserted: Three years is a long time and Three years have passed are both ordinary. Do not impose singular agreement on every expression containing a measurement.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=10&section=gv5-d10-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Subject-Verb Agreement","url":"https://webapps.towson.edu/ows/sub-verb.htm"},{"publisher":"Nouns: singular and plural - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-singular-and-plural"},{"publisher":"series noun - Definition, pictures, pronunciation and usage notes | Oxford Advanced Learner's Dictionary at OxfordLearnersDictionaries.com","url":"https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/definition/english/series"},{"publisher":"Half - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/half"},{"publisher":"PERCENT | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/percent"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:5:4","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Quantities, measures, titles and plural-looking forms","text":"Quantities, measures, titles and plural-looking forms\n\nAgreement with a quantity depends on what is measured or counted and how it is construed. The spelling of a noun, a percentage or a numerical expression does not by itself establish the number required in every use.\n\nSome nouns look plural but take singular agreement in their ordinary meanings: The news is encouraging; Mathematics is compulsory. Other nouns normally take plural agreement: The trousers are wet; The scissors are blunt. A pair of scissors is different because pair is singular: This pair of scissors is blunt. Series has the same spelling in singular and plural: This series is useful; These two series are useful. Use determiners, meaning and a learner’s dictionary where the form alone is misleading. Do not add an invented singular police or a plural news merely to make the ending match a simplified pattern.\n\nFractions and percentages commonly follow the number interpretation of the of-complement: Half of the water is gone; Half of the bottles are empty; Twenty per cent of the applicants were absent. A percentage without an explicit complement still requires context: Twenty per cent is a substantial share treats the proportion as one amount. If the complement is a collective, its own possible unit/member readings can matter. A number of applications have arrived is a conventional plural quantifying construction; The number of applications has risen treats number as the quantity being discussed. These are different structures, not contradictory examples of the same rule.\n\nA measure phrase can denote one amount: Ten kilometres is too far to walk today; Three years is a long wait. Plural agreement remains natural when separate units or their passage are in view: Three years have passed. A title naming one work commonly receives singular agreement despite plural words inside it: Great Expectations is a novel. This does not make every plural proper name singular in every variety; team and organisation names may follow collective conventions. Mathematical statements also have conventional forms, such as Two plus two is four, but do not infer a universal rule for every coordinated numerical expression.\n\nMeaning can change the number behaviour of the same spelling. Statistics is difficult can name a discipline; The statistics are misleading refers to numerical figures. Politics can name an academic topic or a person’s views, with differing agreement: Her politics are well known. Such contrasts illustrate why grammatical number, semantic quantity and lexical convention must be distinguished. A rigid instruction to make the verb agree with the nearest noun fails with The number of files is increasing; an unrestricted head-noun slogan also needs qualification for quantifying A number of files are missing. Analyse the construction, then state which meaning and editorial convention support the chosen verb.\n\nfraction/percentage + of + mass/plural count noun: Agreement commonly follows the relevant mass or count interpretation.\n\na number of + plural noun + plural verb; the number of + plural noun + singular verb: Conventional contrast between quantifying several items and discussing their number.\n\nHalf of the evidence is missing.\nEvidence is a mass noun here, so the fraction has singular agreement.\n\nHalf of the witnesses are waiting outside.\nThe fraction ranges over plural people, giving plural agreement.\n\nThe number of reports has doubled, although a number of reports are still incomplete.\nThe first subject concerns a figure; the second quantifies several reports.\n\nA period can be construed as one measure or as separate years whose passage is asserted: Three years is a long time and Three years have passed are both ordinary. Do not impose singular agreement on every expression containing a measurement.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=10&section=gv5-d10-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Subject-Verb Agreement","url":"https://webapps.towson.edu/ows/sub-verb.htm"},{"publisher":"Nouns: singular and plural - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-singular-and-plural"},{"publisher":"series noun - Definition, pictures, pronunciation and usage notes | Oxford Advanced Learner's Dictionary at OxfordLearnersDictionaries.com","url":"https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/definition/english/series"},{"publisher":"Half - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/half"},{"publisher":"PERCENT | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/percent"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:6:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Clause boundaries, commas and semicolons","text":"Clause boundaries, commas and semicolons\n\nPunctuation marks grammatical boundaries and guides interpretation. Some choices repair an incomplete or incorrectly joined structure; others offer legitimate stylistic alternatives. A pause in speech is evidence about rhythm, not a sufficient rule for inserting a comma.\n\nA full stop normally closes a complete written statement. A comma can separate list items, enclose supplementary material and set off many introductory elements. It does not normally join two independent clauses by itself in formal edited prose: The printer failed, we used another office is a comma splice. Write two sentences, use a semicolon or add a suitable coordinator. Choose the relationship you mean rather than treating all repairs as interchangeable: because introduces a cause, whereas and merely adds information.\n\nA semicolon can link closely related independent clauses: The entrance was locked; the caretaker had gone home. A conjunctive adverb such as however does not become a coordinating conjunction. The entrance was locked; however, the side gate remained open is one defensible pattern. A semicolon can also separate complex list items that already contain commas. When an introductory subordinate clause precedes the main clause, a comma is common, particularly if it prevents a misreading. Short introductory adjuncts sometimes appear without one, depending on house style and clarity.\n\nCommas around a supplementary relative clause are normally paired when the main clause continues: The director, who had just arrived, opened the meeting. A defining relative identifying the intended member ordinarily has no such commas. Punctuation can therefore change reference, not merely rhythm. Do not separate a subject from its verb simply because the subject is long; internal commas may still enclose a parenthetical within that subject. The board, after a long discussion, approved the proposal has two commas performing that enclosing job. A comma before a coordinator may be omitted in short clear clauses or added to prevent confusion.\n\nA punctuation judgement must specify genre and intended analysis. Published literary prose sometimes uses deliberate fragments or comma splices for an identifiable effect; this does not make them a safe default for a formal answer. Conversely, a stylistic comma is not automatically an error. In cumulative editing, first identify every clause and the relation between clauses, then examine marks. A semicolon cannot turn a subordinate fragment into an independent clause. Because the lift was broken; we took the stairs remains faulty for the intended reason relationship. Write Because the lift was broken, we took the stairs instead.\n\nindependent clause ; independent clause: Links two complete, closely related statements.\n\nintroductory subordinate clause , main clause: A common clear boundary; short clauses allow some convention-dependent flexibility.\n\nThe results arrived. We checked the totals.\nTwo full stops separate complete statements.\n\nThe results arrived, but the annex was missing.\nBut coordinates two clauses and expresses contrast.\n\nThe teams came from Multan, Punjab; Sukkur, Sindh; and Quetta, Balochistan.\nSemicolons clarify complex list items containing internal commas.\n\nA short opening phrase may appear without a comma: In June we moved. Add one when it aids interpretation; do not declare one version universally ungrammatical.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=24&section=gv5-d24-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: punctuation","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/punctuation"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:6:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Colons, apostrophes and compact written forms","text":"Colons, apostrophes and compact written forms\n\nA colon introduces an explanation, elaboration, list or quotation within an appropriate written structure. An apostrophe marks certain possessive and omitted-letter forms; it is not a general mark of plurality.\n\nA colon often follows a complete introductory clause: We need three items: a torch, a map and a whistle. Avoid separating an ordinary verb from its required object just to announce a list: The items include: a torch is unnecessary punctuation in continuous prose. Write The items include a torch, a map and a whistle. After the colon, material can be a phrase rather than a full clause. The punctuation signals how that material elaborates the introduction, so a colon differs from a semicolon joining independent clauses.\n\nFor a singular possessive, a common convention adds apostrophe-s: the writer’s desk. Regular plural possessives ending in s place the apostrophe after that ending: the writers’ desks. Irregular plurals take apostrophe-s: the children’s drawings. Its is possessive without an apostrophe, while it’s contracts it is or it has. Ordinary plurals and dates take no apostrophe: five MPs, the 1990s. Some style guides use apostrophes for single-letter plurals such as dot your i’s; that special convention does not justify writing three file’s.\n\nJoint and separate relationships need meaning rather than an automatic last-name rule. Ana and Omar’s shared flat presents one jointly held flat; Ana’s and Omar’s separate flats explicitly mark two relationships. Awkward expressions can be recast as the flat shared by Ana and Omar. Singular names ending in s admit house-style variation: James’s notebook and James’ notebook both occur in established editorial systems. Possessive relationships are broader than ownership, as in yesterday’s meeting. Contractions such as don’t and we’ve are grammatical; a formal assignment may prefer uncontracted forms without turning contractions into errors.\n\nNumerals, initials and abbreviations also involve convention. A publication may choose 8 October 2026 or October 8, 2026; apply one consistent system and avoid ambiguous numerical dates where readers use different orders. Dr and Dr. belong to different punctuation conventions, not different standards of intelligence. Introduce an unfamiliar initialism in full before relying on it. The plural normally adds s, while a possessive adds the relevant apostrophe: two NGOs and an NGO’s report. Decimal separators, units and percentage spacing can depend on disciplinary style; preserve a source’s numerical meaning when changing its presentation.\n\ncomplete introduction : elaboration: The second unit expands or specifies the first.\n\nregular plural ending in -s + apostrophe: Marks a possessive relationship involving a regular plural noun.\n\nOne difficulty remained: the missing signature.\nThe noun phrase after the colon explains the difficulty.\n\nThe teachers’ comments were attached to the children’s work.\nTeachers is regular plural; children is an irregular plural.\n\nIt’s evident that the NGO has changed its timetable.\nIt’s expands to it is, while its is possessive.\n\nApostrophes in single-letter plurals are allowed by some styles for clarity. Treat that as a limited editorial convention, not a rule for every plural.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=24&section=gv5-d24-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"},{"publisher":"Possession ( John’s car, a friend of mine ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/possession-john-s-car-a-friend-of-mine"},{"publisher":"MLA Style Center: Apostrophes, One Mark, Three Ways","url":"https://style.mla.org/apostrophes-three-ways/"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: abbreviations, contractions and acronyms","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/abbreviations"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:6:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Quotation, interruption and sentence-final marks","text":"Quotation, interruption and sentence-final marks\n\nQuotation marks identify quoted wording or another explicitly defined editorial use. Parentheses, brackets, dashes, hyphens and ellipses do different jobs; none is a general substitute for any inconvenient punctuation.\n\nA direct question normally ends with a question mark: Has the office reopened? An indirect report normally ends with the mark appropriate to the whole sentence: She asked whether the office had reopened. Do you know whether it has reopened? is itself a question and takes a question mark. An exclamation mark signals an exclamatory expression or emphatic utterance. Multiple exclamation marks occur in informal messages, but restrained formal writing normally avoids them. That preference concerns register; it is not evidence that informal messages must obey a ban on repeated marks.\n\nSingle or double outer quotation marks can both be used under established styles. When a quotation contains another quotation, switch the inner pair to distinguish levels. Punctuation placement depends partly on whether a mark belongs to the quoted words and partly on the chosen convention. British logical placement and common American practice differ for commas and full stops. Question marks should reflect the intended question: Did she say “Leave now”? asks about the saying; She asked, “May I leave?” quotes a question. Do not add a second full stop mechanically after the closing quotation.\n\nRound parentheses enclose an aside; square brackets in a quotation commonly identify editorial additions or clarifications. The brackets must not make an uncertain interpretation look like the speaker’s original wording. Dashes can mark interruption, amplification or a strongly separated parenthesis. A hyphen joins elements within some compounds: a three-year project. It is shorter and has a different function from a dash. The need for hyphens varies by position and convention: a well-known author but the author is well known. An -ly adverb normally does not need a hyphen in a clearly written modifier such as a carefully planned route.\n\nAn ellipsis can mark an omission from quoted text or a trailing-off effect in an appropriate genre. Omitting words must not change the represented claim. Removing not from a quotation is a substantive distortion even if an ellipsis is printed. A complete omitted sentence and an omitted phrase may be punctuated differently by different style guides; consult the specified guide for the number and spacing of dots. Parenthetical material inside a sentence normally leaves the main sentence’s ending outside the closing parenthesis; a complete independent parenthetical sentence may retain its own final mark inside. Analyse the enclosing structure before choosing the position.\n\nouter quotation + contrasting inner quotation: Keeps nested quoted material distinguishable.\n\nnumber + unit singular + noun, with a hyphenated modifier: A common pattern: a ten-minute break; compare the break lasted ten minutes.\n\nShe asked, “Is the archive open?”\nThe question mark belongs to the quoted question.\n\nThe archive closes at six (except on Fridays).\nThe parenthesis sits inside the sentence; its full stop follows the closing parenthesis.\n\nThe report described a carefully planned, three-stage operation.\nThe -ly modifier has no hyphen; three-stage is a compound premodifier.\n\nSome familiar compounds keep a dictionary hyphen even in positions where other compounds open up. Check a reliable dictionary and the required house style rather than inventing a universal positional rule.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=24&section=gv5-d24-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: punctuation","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/punctuation"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:6:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Capitalisation, spelling and editorial consistency","text":"Capitalisation, spelling and editorial consistency\n\nOrthography is the system of written forms, including spelling, capitalisation and word division. Some orthographic choices distinguish words; others vary legitimately between national and editorial conventions.\n\nCapitalise the first word of a normal written sentence, the pronoun I and proper names: Zainab, Pakistan, Urdu and October. A common noun does not become a proper noun merely because it is important: the committee announced the decision is a normal general description. A particular organisation’s official name can require capitals: the National Library. Titles before personal names often receive capitals, while general references often remain lower case. Check whether the wording is an official name, a descriptive phrase or a house-style abbreviation before changing its capitals.\n\nBritish and American spelling conventions differ in words such as colour/color, centre/center and travelled/traveled. Both standard systems are legitimate; this course uses British defaults and identifies American alternatives. British publishing itself permits more than one convention for some words, including forms in -ise and -ize. Do not label every -ize spelling American. Programme is a common British form for a plan or broadcast, while program is standard in computing. A consistent editing policy should still allow quoted material, names and technical forms to retain their authentic spelling.\n\nCapitalisation of headings and titles varies between sentence case and title case. A sentence-case heading need not capitalise every major word. Institutions and legal publications may prescribe forms such as Section 12, while another style uses section 12 in running prose. Names of languages keep capitals; ordinary names of academic fields often do not unless they form part of an official course title. Compound words may be open, closed or hyphenated, and usage changes over time: a dictionary entry and a publication’s stated style are better evidence than a remembered universal rule.\n\nSeparate error correction from editorial normalisation. Writing enviroment for environment is a spelling error; writing organization in a British document may match an established British house style. A question that asks for one correct spelling must avoid offering two standard variants unless it states the convention being tested. Numerical forms also need a stated purpose: scientific prose, dates, tables and literary prose often use different presentation rules. In your final editing pass, check the chosen system, official names, consistency and meaning. Do not remove capitals, hyphens or abbreviations blindly from specialist material merely because the general default differs.\n\nproper name → capitalisation appropriate to the name: Distinguishes an identified name from a general description.\n\nhouse style + dictionary evidence: Resolves legitimate variants without inventing grammatical prohibitions.\n\nThe department offers courses in chemistry and English.\nEnglish names a language; chemistry is a general field name here.\n\nThe colour of the laboratory’s sign has changed.\nColour follows the course’s British editorial default.\n\nThe software program supports the festival programme.\nThe two forms can coexist under a normal British computing/general distinction.\n\nOfficial names, quotations and stipulated specialist notation can retain a different convention within an otherwise consistent document.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=24&section=gv5-d24-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: words","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/words"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: abbreviations, contractions and acronyms","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/abbreviations"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:7:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Past simple: completed events, states and narrative steps","text":"Past simple: completed events, states and narrative steps\n\nThe past simple uses a finite past form and commonly locates events or states within a past frame. It can describe single events, sequences, repeated activities and past states.\n\nTo report a completed event, use the past form: The courier knocked. Regular forms normally add -ed; irregular forms include took, wrote, brought and left. A past form need not mention its date in the same sentence if the conversation already establishes when: Who moved the box? Mina moved it. Past simple can also describe a state: The office belonged to a local charity. Do not equate past tense with physical action. In a narrative, a sequence such as She unlocked the door, entered and switched on the light normally advances the story from one event to the next.\n\nOrdinary lexical verbs use did not + base in negatives and Did + subject + base in questions: They did not return; Did they return? Did carries the tense, so did returned duplicates past marking. Be instead uses was or were and inverts directly: Was the office open? It was not open. A subject question normally lacks added did: Who opened the gate? Compare Who did you meet?, where who is the object and you is the subject. Negation, emphasis or another structure can change the auxiliary pattern, so first identify what the question is asking.\n\nPast simple can cover a long period or a repeated routine: She worked there for twenty years; We walked to school every day. The length of an event does not by itself require progressive aspect. A finished past interval normally permits simple past even if its effects continue now: The bridge closed last month, and traffic is still diverted. A present consequence does not force present perfect. Conversely, a past form may express hypothetical distance rather than past time in a later lesson’s conditional construction. Meaning comes from the whole clause and context.\n\nIn historical and biographical writing, establish dates and reference frames clearly. Past forms report events; present forms can evaluate a source that is available now: The expedition left in 1912; the diary describes its first week. Such a shift is justified, not a lapse in consistency. The historic present is another established narrative choice, but a beginner should maintain one narrative viewpoint until a reason for changing it is clear. Tense consistency means coherent relationships between viewpoints, rather than banning every different tense from one paragraph or assuming that each past verb moves the narrative clock forward.\n\nsubject + past form: Past simple affirmative.\n\ndid not + base; Did + subject + base?: Ordinary lexical negatives and non-subject questions.\n\nThe assistant locked the cabinet at six.\nLocked locates a completed event in a specified past interval.\n\nWho brought the spare battery?\nWho is the subject, so this affirmative subject question uses brought without did.\n\nThe laboratory operated for thirty years before it closed.\nA long completed period can use simple past; duration does not compel progressive.\n\nPast be has was/were rather than did be: Were the windows open? It also appears in irrealis constructions, which Day 18 develops.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=7&section=gv5-d07-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Past simple ( I worked ) - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/past-simple-i-worked"},{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:7:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Past progressive: background, overlap and an unfinished viewpoint","text":"Past progressive: background, overlap and an unfinished viewpoint\n\nThe past progressive combines was/were with an -ing form, presenting a situation as in progress around a past reference time. It does not by itself establish completion or interruption.\n\nUse was or were + -ing: I was listening; they were listening. Add not after was/were for a negative and invert that auxiliary for a question: Were they listening? This construction lets a narrator place a reader inside an activity. At ten, the workers were loading the van says loading was in progress around ten. It does not itself tell us when the task started or finished. A past time expression is therefore compatible with progressive aspect. The slogan yesterday means simple past is too broad: Yesterday at ten, we were loading the van is grammatical.\n\nWhen one event occurs during another, progressive often supplies background while simple past introduces the event: I was checking the address when the taxi arrived. Calling this an interruption is convenient, but the arrival need not stop the checking. Two progressives can present overlap: While I was checking, Salma was packing. Two simple pasts can also describe concurrent completed activities: While I checked the address, Salma packed. These are different viewpoints, not competing versions where one must be an error. A question must specify the intended scene before it can demand a particular aspect.\n\nProgressive also supports temporary repeated past activity: During the renovation, we were meeting in the kitchen every morning. Compare We met in the kitchen every morning for an ordinary retrospective habit. The progressive can suggest an unfolding pattern with boundaries in the context. With always, a speaker may express an attitude to repetition: He was always volunteering for difficult jobs. Context may make this admiring rather than critical. State verbs retain sense-dependent restrictions. I knew the answer is the ordinary form for knowledge; I was thinking about the answer presents an active process.\n\nThe so-called imperfective viewpoint shows an internal phase without asserting the whole event’s completion. She was crossing the road when I saw her does not guarantee that she reached the far side, though ordinary knowledge may suggest it. Do not confuse what the grammar entails with what a reader reasonably infers. Similarly, an achievement can receive a progressive reading when an event has an approach or repeated phases: The guests were arriving as the musicians tuned up. This does not make arrive an unrestricted substitute for every stative verb. Interpret the lexical meaning and the viewpoint together.\n\nwas/were + -ing: Past progressive affirmative.\n\nWhile + past progressive, past simple/progressive: A common way to frame an overlapping situation, not a compulsory tense recipe.\n\nAt sunrise, the team was unloading the truck.\nThe narrator views unloading as in progress at sunrise.\n\nThe radio was playing when the power failed.\nWas playing is background; failed marks a new event. The relation is supplied by context.\n\nGuests were arriving throughout the afternoon, while volunteers were directing them to the hall.\nTwo overlapping repeated patterns are presented as ongoing.\n\nA short event can occur in a progressive when viewed in development or repetition: A light was flashing. Duration is not a mechanical verb-type label.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=7&section=gv5-d07-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Past continuous ( I was working ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/past-continuous-i-was-working"},{"publisher":"Stative verbs | LearnEnglish","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/b1-b2/stative-verbs"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:7:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Past perfect and past perfect progressive: looking back from then","text":"Past perfect and past perfect progressive: looking back from then\n\nThe past perfect uses had plus a past participle to relate a prior situation to a past viewpoint. The past perfect progressive adds been + -ing to foreground a process or duration leading towards that viewpoint.\n\nImagine arriving at a workshop yesterday and finding it empty. The instructor had left tells the reader that the departure preceded your arrival. Had left is past perfect: had + the participle left. It looks back from a past viewpoint rather than directly from now. Make negatives with had not and questions with Had + subject: Had the instructor left? No separate did is needed. The reference point can appear in the same sentence, elsewhere in the story or in the shared conversation. It need not be a second event explicitly named after every perfect verb.\n\nPast perfect does more than put the earliest of two events in a special form. It can establish background information, report an earlier statement, or show that a plan changed: I had intended to stay, but the last train was leaving. Where before or after already establishes sequence, simple past may be sufficient: After she locked the room, she handed over the keys. Past perfect can still foreground completion: After she had locked the room, she handed over the keys. Do not mark every simple past before another past as wrong, or assume that past perfect cannot appear with dates.\n\nFor activity leading towards a past viewpoint, use had been + -ing: We had been waiting for an hour when the caretaker arrived. The waiting may then stop or continue. It can also explain traces at a past moment: The pavement was wet because it had been raining. Rain might already have stopped. Compare had written three letters, a completed tally, with had been writing letters, an activity. With states such as knowing someone, ordinary past perfect simple is used: We had known each other for years. Since and for express starting point and duration; they do not compel progressive aspect.\n\nAnalyse reference relationships before rewriting. In When the assistant arrived, the gate had closed, closing is earlier than arrival. In When the assistant arrived, the gate closed, the natural reading links closing with the arrival, though wider context can modify that interpretation. A transformation from one to the other may change the facts. Perfect marking also occurs in counterfactual constructions discussed later: If we had left earlier is an imagined past, not a historical claim that we actually left earlier. Keep time sequence, modality and the truth of a proposition distinct when interpreting a perfect form.\n\nhad + past participle: Prior situation viewed from a past reference point.\n\nhad been + -ing: Activity or duration leading up to a past viewpoint.\n\nBy noon, the team had checked all the meters.\nThe checks are completed by the past reference point noon.\n\nHad you been working there long when the building closed?\nHad been working asks about duration preceding the closure.\n\nThe guide had promised a longer visit, but we left early because the weather worsened.\nThe earlier promise is distinguished from the later departure; the promise does not guarantee fulfilment.\n\nPast perfect accepts definite past reference points: By 2018, the shop had closed. The restriction on present perfect with finished event times does not apply identically.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=7&section=gv5-d07-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Past perfect simple ( I had worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/past-perfect-simple-i-had-worked"},{"publisher":"Past perfect continuous ( I had been working ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/past-perfect-continuous-i-had-been-working"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:7:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Past habits, used to, habitual would and coherent reporting","text":"Past habits, used to, habitual would and coherent reporting\n\nUsed to + base describes a past habit or state contrasted with the present. Habitual would + base recalls repeated past events within an established past frame; it is distinct from other modal meanings of would.\n\nTo describe an earlier routine, you can use simple past: We walked along the canal after school. Used to can explicitly contrast that routine with the present: We used to walk there, but the path is closed now. It also describes a past state: The yard used to belong to my grandparents. The form does not change with the person of the subject. In carefully edited questions and negatives, use Did you use to live here? and We did not use to live here. Spellings with used after did occur, but the uninflected use is this course’s formal editorial default.\n\nOnce a past frame is established, would can recall typical events: During our summer visits, Grandfather would open the shop early and we would sweep the pavement. It need not imply a conditional promise. Ordinary continuing states usually take used to or simple past rather than habitual would: We used to own a small shop. Some repeated situations containing be can occur with habitual would when they mean something that repeatedly happened, such as On market days, the street would be crowded. The key is an established repeated-event reading, not a prohibition on the spelling be.\n\nDistinguish used to from be used to and get used to. I used to work nights describes an earlier habit. I am used to working nights means night work is familiar; to here is a preposition, so working follows it. I am getting used to working nights describes becoming accustomed. The similarity in sound conceals different structures. Avoid turning a whole story into would without first orienting the reader: outside a past frame, would can suggest a hypothetical outcome, a polite request or a future viewed from the past. Clear time framing prevents this ambiguity.\n\nWhen reporting history, preserve whether an event was asserted, remembered as a habit, merely planned or imagined. The phrase was going to open expresses a past intention, without proving that opening occurred. Used to normally implies a contrast with current circumstances, but it should not be used to invent a change that the evidence does not support. If a source simply records regular deliveries in 1990, simple past may be the most neutral choice. A reliable historical paragraph can combine simple past narrative, progressive background, perfect anteriority and habitual constructions when each serves a clear temporal or discourse function.\n\nused to + base; did not use to + base: Past habit/state and the formal negative default.\n\npast frame + would + base: A typical repeated past event, when context establishes the habitual reading.\n\nbe/get used to + noun or -ing: Being/becoming accustomed; not the same construction as used to + base.\n\nThe station used to have a wooden roof.\nUsed to describes a former state.\n\nWhen I was an apprentice, we would check the shutters every evening.\nThe opening frame establishes habitual past events.\n\nI used to work alone, but I am now used to sharing the workshop.\nThe first construction describes a former habit; the second describes current familiarity.\n\nHabitual would can describe repeated temporary states in context: On delivery days, the corridor would be full of boxes. It does not normally replace used to for an unchanging possession state.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=7&section=gv5-d07-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Used to - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/used-to"},{"publisher":"Would - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/would"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:7:4","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Past habits, used to, habitual would and coherent reporting","text":"Past habits, used to, habitual would and coherent reporting\n\nUsed to + base describes a past habit or state contrasted with the present. Habitual would + base recalls repeated past events within an established past frame; it is distinct from other modal meanings of would.\n\nTo describe an earlier routine, you can use simple past: We walked along the canal after school. Used to can explicitly contrast that routine with the present: We used to walk there, but the path is closed now. It also describes a past state: The yard used to belong to my grandparents. The form does not change with the person of the subject. In carefully edited questions and negatives, use Did you use to live here? and We did not use to live here. Spellings with used after did occur, but the uninflected use is this course’s formal editorial default.\n\nOnce a past frame is established, would can recall typical events: During our summer visits, Grandfather would open the shop early and we would sweep the pavement. It need not imply a conditional promise. Ordinary continuing states usually take used to or simple past rather than habitual would: We used to own a small shop. Some repeated situations containing be can occur with habitual would when they mean something that repeatedly happened, such as On market days, the street would be crowded. The key is an established repeated-event reading, not a prohibition on the spelling be.\n\nDistinguish used to from be used to and get used to. I used to work nights describes an earlier habit. I am used to working nights means night work is familiar; to here is a preposition, so working follows it. I am getting used to working nights describes becoming accustomed. The similarity in sound conceals different structures. Avoid turning a whole story into would without first orienting the reader: outside a past frame, would can suggest a hypothetical outcome, a polite request or a future viewed from the past. Clear time framing prevents this ambiguity.\n\nWhen reporting history, preserve whether an event was asserted, remembered as a habit, merely planned or imagined. The phrase was going to open expresses a past intention, without proving that opening occurred. Used to normally implies a contrast with current circumstances, but it should not be used to invent a change that the evidence does not support. If a source simply records regular deliveries in 1990, simple past may be the most neutral choice. A reliable historical paragraph can combine simple past narrative, progressive background, perfect anteriority and habitual constructions when each serves a clear temporal or discourse function.\n\nused to + base; did not use to + base: Past habit/state and the formal negative default.\n\npast frame + would + base: A typical repeated past event, when context establishes the habitual reading.\n\nbe/get used to + noun or -ing: Being/becoming accustomed; not the same construction as used to + base.\n\nThe station used to have a wooden roof.\nUsed to describes a former state.\n\nWhen I was an apprentice, we would check the shutters every evening.\nThe opening frame establishes habitual past events.\n\nI used to work alone, but I am now used to sharing the workshop.\nThe first construction describes a former habit; the second describes current familiarity.\n\nHabitual would can describe repeated temporary states in context: On delivery days, the corridor would be full of boxes. It does not normally replace used to for an unchanging possession state.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=7&section=gv5-d07-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Used to - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/used-to"},{"publisher":"Would - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/would"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:8:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Combining perfect and progressive viewpoints","text":"Combining perfect and progressive viewpoints\n\nPerfect constructions relate a situation to a later reference point; progressive constructions present a situation from within its development. They can combine, and neither construction alone tells the whole story about completion or present consequences.\n\nCompare The technician has repaired the pump with The technician has been repairing the pump. In the first, the repair is presented as an achieved event relevant now. In the second, attention falls on the activity over a recent interval. The second sentence does not by itself say whether the pump is now repaired. Form a perfect with have plus a past participle, and a progressive with be plus an -ing form. Their combination is have been repairing: been is the participle of be, and repairing carries the progressive meaning. With a past reference point, change the finite auxiliary to had.\n\nInterpret the whole expression, including the verb and its object. She has written three reports presents three completed products. She has been writing reports presents a process, perhaps repeatedly, without specifying how many were completed. A perfect simple can also describe a continuing state: We have owned this shop for years. A perfect progressive can describe an activity that has just stopped: You look exhausted; have you been carrying boxes? The visible exhaustion is evidence for recent activity; it does not prove the person is still carrying anything while the question is asked.\n\nThe same contrast operates with a past viewpoint: By noon, they had repaired the roof presents the repair as achieved before noon, whereas They had been repairing the roof before the storm arrived describes earlier activity. A simple perfect does not necessarily promise a permanent result. The roof has been repaired does not rule out a new leak tomorrow. Conversely, ongoing activity can produce results without reaching an intended endpoint. Consider a wet floor after someone has been washing it. Ask separately whether an event occurred, whether its endpoint was reached, and which consequences the context makes relevant.\n\nAdvanced analysis distinguishes grammatical aspect from the situation type expressed by the whole predicate. Write reports allows an open activity; write three reports supplies a countable endpoint. This interaction is sometimes called lexical aspect or situation aspect. Perfect progressive does not automatically mean a longer duration than perfect simple, nor is simple a guarantee of a short event. Both I have worked here for ten years and I have been working here for ten years can be appropriate; the latter puts greater attention on the activity or its continuing course. Translation and editing should preserve the intended viewpoint rather than mechanically substituting forms.\n\nhave/has/had + past participle: A perfect construction interpreted relative to now or another reference point.\n\nhave/has/had + been + -ing: Perfect and progressive meanings combine; completion must be inferred from the full context.\n\nThe archivist has catalogued eighty letters.\nThe stated total concerns completed cataloguing events.\n\nThe archivist has been cataloguing letters all morning.\nThe activity occupies a recent interval; the completed total is unspecified.\n\nThe floor was slippery because someone had been washing it.\nEarlier washing explains a later past condition; washing need not still have been happening.\n\nAn achievement verb can occur in a progressive when context supports repetition or an unfolding stage: The old engine has been stopping unexpectedly describes repeated stops, not one indefinitely extended instant.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=8&section=gv5-d08-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Present perfect simple or present perfect continuous? - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-or-present-perfect-continuous"},{"publisher":"Past perfect simple or past perfect continuous? - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/past-perfect-simple-or-past-perfect-continuous"},{"publisher":"Present perfect simple ( I have worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-i-have-worked"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:8:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: States, actions, temporary interpretations and repetition","text":"States, actions, temporary interpretations and repetition\n\nStative and dynamic describe interpretations of predicates. A stative reading presents a condition or relationship; a dynamic reading presents activity or change. Many verbs support more than one reading, and progressive compatibility depends on that reading and its context.\n\nUse the simple form for ordinary possession, knowledge or belief: I have two sisters; We know the address; I believe the explanation. These describe conditions rather than actions unfolding before us. Merely adding at the moment does not make every state naturally progressive: I know the answer at the moment is ordinary English. The term stative is a useful tendency, not a permanent prohibition attached to a spelling. Compare We have a small kitchen, describing possession, with We are having lunch, describing an event. The verb is the same word, but its meaning and pattern change.\n\nSeveral familiar contrasts illustrate the importance of interpretation. I think the figures are wrong expresses an opinion; I am thinking about the figures describes a process of consideration. The soup tastes salty reports a perception; The cook is tasting the soup describes deliberate sampling. He is polite presents a characteristic; He is being unusually polite describes behaviour in the current situation. Progressive be is normally about conduct or a temporary presentation, not ordinary identity: She is being a dentist is not the normal way to state her profession. Read the complements and the situation before selecting a form.\n\nSome predicates permit more flexibility than a short classroom list suggests. I feel tired and I am feeling tired can both describe a current condition, with the progressive making its present or temporary character more prominent. Informal I am loving this concert presents enjoyment as an immediate experience; it is marked by context and register rather than automatically ungrammatical. Repeated brief events can also receive progressive interpretation: Someone has been tapping on the window. A progressive with always can express an evaluative view of repeated behaviour, as in He is always interrupting, without making annoyance an obligatory part of the grammar.\n\nTemporary does not define progressive, and permanent does not define simple. We are currently living with relatives can be progressive without a promise that the arrangement will soon end; I live with relatives is a neutral characterisation, even when the arrangement is temporary. A speaker chooses how to package the situation. Duration is another independent property: I have known her for thirty years is lengthy but normally simple. Where a creative advertisement or a special discourse uses a normally stative verb progressively, identify the intended interpretation and register. Do not extend that exceptional use to every context, and do not reject it solely because a memorised list says never.\n\nsimple have + noun phrase: Possession or another stative relationship, according to the noun and context.\n\nbe + having/thinking/tasting + complement: A dynamic interpretation such as experiencing, considering or deliberately sampling.\n\nThe parcel weighs two kilograms.\nWeighs expresses a measured property; simple is ordinary.\n\nThe assistant is weighing the parcel.\nWeighing describes an action performed on the parcel.\n\nI have been thinking about your suggestion, although I still think it needs revision.\nThinking is a process; think expresses the opinion reached or held.\n\nSome expressions allow simple and progressive state-like readings: I feel better and I am feeling better are both established. A learner should explain the viewpoint rather than force a universal ban.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=8&section=gv5-d08-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Stative verbs | LearnEnglish","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/b1-b2/stative-verbs"},{"publisher":"Present perfect simple or present perfect continuous? - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-or-present-perfect-continuous"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:8:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Duration, starting points and temporal endpoints","text":"Duration, starting points and temporal endpoints\n\nFor normally introduces a duration, since a starting point in temporal uses, by a latest time or deadline, and until the endpoint of a continuing situation. These meanings guide interpretation but do not select one tense mechanically.\n\nA duration answers How long? Use for with a measured interval: for twenty minutes, for a decade, for several weeks. A temporal starting point answers From when? Use since with a point or a clause locating that point: since Tuesday; since the office moved. Compare We have waited for an hour and We have waited since nine. Both can describe waiting that began earlier and continues now. Do not replace for with since merely because the sentence contains a perfect construction. The complement after the preposition determines which expression accurately represents the intended interval.\n\nFor works with past, present and future reference: We waited for an hour yesterday; We have waited for an hour; We will stay for an hour. Since often combines with perfect constructions when measuring from an earlier point to a later viewpoint, but it is not exclusively a present-perfect signal. By 2022, she had worked here for five years is a past-perfect account, and She had been unwell since Monday measures to a past reference point. Since can also mean because: Since the gate was locked, we used another entrance gives a reason, not the beginning of a duration.\n\nBy sets a latest acceptable or relevant time: Send the signed form by Friday means no later than Friday. Until presents continuation to an endpoint: Keep the original until Friday means retain it through the relevant interval. Negation changes the relationship: She did not sign until Friday locates the eventual signing no earlier than Friday in the ordinary reading. Compare She had signed by Friday, which presents signing as achieved at or before that point. These are not stylistic substitutes. Preserve the intended deadline, continuation or delay when correcting a sentence, and do not change a promise into a mere prediction.\n\nThe temporal expression and the verb viewpoint contribute separate information. By six, I will have finished explicitly looks back from a future boundary; I will finish by six can communicate the same deadline without a perfect form. A finished date is compatible with past perfect when another past viewpoint is established: At the review in May, we discovered that the permit had expired in March. The finished-time restriction on ordinary present perfect does not ban past perfect with dates. Until and by can also have non-temporal uses, and temporal clauses require their own analysis. Describe the particular construction rather than converting a useful contrast into an unrestricted word-substitution rule.\n\nfor + duration; since + starting point: Measure an interval or locate its beginning.\n\nby + deadline; continuing situation + until + endpoint: Distinguish completion no later than a time from continuation up to a time.\n\nThe exhibition remained open until six.\nRemaining open continues to the stated endpoint.\n\nThe driver must return the vehicle by six.\nThe return is due no later than the deadline.\n\nBy last winter, the family had lived there for a decade.\nA past viewpoint licenses a past perfect with a duration; no present continuation is asserted.\n\nSince is not always temporal: Since you have the key, please open the cabinet means because. Its presence does not itself require a perfect construction in every clause.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=8&section=gv5-d08-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"For or since ? - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/for-or-since"},{"publisher":"By - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/by"},{"publisher":"Until - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/until"},{"publisher":"Past perfect simple ( I had worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/past-perfect-simple-i-had-worked"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:8:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Already, yet, just, lately and recently in context","text":"Already, yet, just, lately and recently in context\n\nTime adverbs help locate events and convey expectations. Their meaning, position and compatibility with a construction must be interpreted together; they are not automatic tense selectors.\n\nAlready commonly presents a situation as existing or achieved by a relevant point, often earlier than expected: The bus has already left. Yet in a question asks whether an expected event has happened by now: Has the bus left yet? In a negative, The bus has not left yet indicates that departure has not happened up to the reference point and commonly remains expected. Just can mean a very short time ago: The bus has just left. These adverbs add interpretation to the clause; the verb form still needs to fit the broader temporal setting.\n\nPosition changes emphasis and can affect naturalness. Already and recent-event just commonly follow the first auxiliary: We have already checked the locks; We had just finished. Already can also appear at the end: Have you finished already? often expresses surprise. Yet commonly appears at the end of a question or negative statement. It also has other established uses: a yet more difficult problem means a still more difficult one, while The result is promising yet uncertain uses yet as a coordinator. Identify the use before applying a rule learned for temporal questions.\n\nLately normally refers to a recent interval, especially with states, repeated events or continuing activity: I have been sleeping badly lately. Recently can do that too, but also readily locates a single completed past event: We inspected the lift recently. A precise finished time such as last Tuesday normally selects a past construction for the event: We inspected it last Tuesday. British English frequently uses present perfect for recent events with just, already and yet; American English also commonly uses simple past in such contexts: Did you send it yet? This is an established regional alternative, not evidence of carelessness.\n\nAdverbs carry scope and pragmatic expectations as well as clock time. Just may mean only, exactly, or emphatic merely, so I just need a signature need not describe a very recent need. Already does not require perfect aspect: We already know the answer is simple present. Special contexts permit negated already, for example a question challenging a presupposition, so not usually is more accurate than never. An assessment must give sufficient context when two tense-aspect forms or adverb positions remain defensible. Where the task asks for the most natural expression in a stated register, explain that criterion rather than marking every less frequent alternative as structurally impossible.\n\nhave + already/just + past participle: Common position for already and recent-event just with a perfect auxiliary.\n\nquestion/negative clause + yet: Common temporal use concerning an expected event up to the relevant point.\n\nThe catering team has already prepared the room.\nAlready indicates completion by now, perhaps earlier than expected.\n\nHas the repair team tested the alarm yet?\nYet asks whether the expected test has occurred up to now.\n\nThe manager recently visited the site, but maintenance has been irregular lately.\nRecently locates one visit; lately frames a recent interval of maintenance.\n\nThese words have other senses and constructions: yet can mean nevertheless or still in combinations, and just can mean only. Analyse the actual use instead of applying a tense rule to the spelling alone.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=8&section=gv5-d08-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Already - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/already"},{"publisher":"Yet - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/yet"},{"publisher":"Just - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/just"},{"publisher":"Late or lately ? - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/late-or-lately"},{"publisher":"RECENTLY | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/recently"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:8:4","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Already, yet, just, lately and recently in context","text":"Already, yet, just, lately and recently in context\n\nTime adverbs help locate events and convey expectations. Their meaning, position and compatibility with a construction must be interpreted together; they are not automatic tense selectors.\n\nAlready commonly presents a situation as existing or achieved by a relevant point, often earlier than expected: The bus has already left. Yet in a question asks whether an expected event has happened by now: Has the bus left yet? In a negative, The bus has not left yet indicates that departure has not happened up to the reference point and commonly remains expected. Just can mean a very short time ago: The bus has just left. These adverbs add interpretation to the clause; the verb form still needs to fit the broader temporal setting.\n\nPosition changes emphasis and can affect naturalness. Already and recent-event just commonly follow the first auxiliary: We have already checked the locks; We had just finished. Already can also appear at the end: Have you finished already? often expresses surprise. Yet commonly appears at the end of a question or negative statement. It also has other established uses: a yet more difficult problem means a still more difficult one, while The result is promising yet uncertain uses yet as a coordinator. Identify the use before applying a rule learned for temporal questions.\n\nLately normally refers to a recent interval, especially with states, repeated events or continuing activity: I have been sleeping badly lately. Recently can do that too, but also readily locates a single completed past event: We inspected the lift recently. A precise finished time such as last Tuesday normally selects a past construction for the event: We inspected it last Tuesday. British English frequently uses present perfect for recent events with just, already and yet; American English also commonly uses simple past in such contexts: Did you send it yet? This is an established regional alternative, not evidence of carelessness.\n\nAdverbs carry scope and pragmatic expectations as well as clock time. Just may mean only, exactly, or emphatic merely, so I just need a signature need not describe a very recent need. Already does not require perfect aspect: We already know the answer is simple present. Special contexts permit negated already, for example a question challenging a presupposition, so not usually is more accurate than never. An assessment must give sufficient context when two tense-aspect forms or adverb positions remain defensible. Where the task asks for the most natural expression in a stated register, explain that criterion rather than marking every less frequent alternative as structurally impossible.\n\nhave + already/just + past participle: Common position for already and recent-event just with a perfect auxiliary.\n\nquestion/negative clause + yet: Common temporal use concerning an expected event up to the relevant point.\n\nThe catering team has already prepared the room.\nAlready indicates completion by now, perhaps earlier than expected.\n\nHas the repair team tested the alarm yet?\nYet asks whether the expected test has occurred up to now.\n\nThe manager recently visited the site, but maintenance has been irregular lately.\nRecently locates one visit; lately frames a recent interval of maintenance.\n\nThese words have other senses and constructions: yet can mean nevertheless or still in combinations, and just can mean only. Analyse the actual use instead of applying a tense rule to the spelling alone.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=8&section=gv5-d08-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Already - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/already"},{"publisher":"Yet - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/yet"},{"publisher":"Just - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/just"},{"publisher":"Late or lately ? - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/late-or-lately"},{"publisher":"RECENTLY | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/recently"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:9:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Will, shall and be going to: prediction, intention and commitment","text":"Will, shall and be going to: prediction, intention and commitment\n\nEnglish uses several constructions to refer to future time. Will and shall are modal auxiliaries, while be going to combines a form of be with going to and a plain-form verb. Their meanings overlap but their use depends on the speaker’s purpose and viewpoint.\n\nUse will with the base form: The driver will return. Add not for a negative, The driver will not return, and put will before the subject for a question, Will the driver return? The contraction will not becomes won’t. A promise, prediction, offer and decision can all use will: I will return your call; The river will rise; I will carry that bag; I will take the early train. The word future identifies the intended time, not a guarantee that a prediction is factually certain. Identify what the speaker is doing as well as when the event is expected.\n\nBe going to commonly highlights an intention already held or evidence visible now: We are going to replace the roof; Those loose tiles are going to fall. Its finite be agrees with the subject: I am, she is, they are. Make negatives and questions with be: They are not going to move; Are they going to move? Going to need not describe physical travel. Compare They are going to the clinic, where to the clinic is a destination phrase, with They are going to inspect the clinic, where inspect belongs to the intended action. Keep these different structures separate.\n\nWill and going to often both fit the same future event, with a different conversational emphasis. I will call her can make a decision or commitment; I am going to call her can state a prior intention. A context may favour one without making the other structurally impossible. Shall with I and we remains possible for formal future reference, especially in British English, though will is more widespread. Shall I open the window? offers action or asks what the speaker should do; Shall we begin? proposes joint action. These questions have a modal purpose, not merely an announcement of future time.\n\nFuture expressions interact with modality: they present expectations, intentions and commitments from the speaker’s current standpoint. A spontaneous decision is a useful context for teaching will, not a test proving that will can never describe an earlier plan. Likewise, visible evidence favours going to without banning will from evidence-based predictions. Informal gonna represents a reduced pronunciation of going to in the intended-action construction; it is unsuitable as a default spelling in formal reports. A careful transformation preserves the communicative force: changing I promise I will help into I may help weakens commitment, even though both clauses are grammatically formed.\n\nwill/shall + base form: Modal future reference; interpretation may include prediction, willingness, decision or commitment.\n\nam/is/are + going to + base form: Common intention or evidence-based prediction construction.\n\nI will send the receipt tonight.\nWill can express the speaker’s commitment to a future action.\n\nThe panels are going to fall if the supports move again.\nThe prediction is presented in relation to an existing dangerous condition.\n\nShall we discuss the draft before lunch?\nThis is a proposal about joint action, an established British use of shall.\n\nThe form going to can describe destination rather than intention: We are going to Lahore contains a destination phrase. Do not analyse every occurrence as a future auxiliary construction.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=9&section=gv5-d09-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Future: will and shall - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-will-and-shall"},{"publisher":"Future: be going to ( I am going to work ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-be-going-to-i-am-going-to-work"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:9:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Present forms for arrangements, timetables and future facts","text":"Present forms for arrangements, timetables and future facts\n\nPresent-tense forms can have future reference. Present progressive commonly presents an arrangement, while present simple commonly presents a timetable, fixed schedule or future fact. These uses rely on context and do not create new verb endings.\n\nConsider The interview starts at nine tomorrow. Starts is present tense, but tomorrow places the event in the future. A timetable, programme or scheduled event commonly uses present simple: The coach departs at six; The term ends in July. Negative and question formation stays the same as for present simple: The coach does not stop here; When does the coach leave? Be also keeps its ordinary present forms: The ceremony is on Friday. A future time expression helps the reader recognise the intended reference; without one, the surrounding conversation may supply it.\n\nPresent progressive commonly presents an arrangement: I am meeting the surveyor at ten; We are moving next month. An arrangement can involve other people, a booking, or the speaker’s own settled plan; another person need not appear in every sentence. Build it with finite be and an -ing form, and form questions and negatives with be. Compare Are you meeting the surveyor? and You are not meeting the surveyor today. The construction presents the action as planned or arranged from the present viewpoint. It does not assert that the future is immune to cancellation or disruption.\n\nA single event can be described as schedule or arrangement: The delegation leaves on Thursday and The delegation is leaving on Thursday may both be natural. The first can report its programme, while the second draws attention to an arrangement in progress. An objective item should not ask for the only correct form when both readings are available. Present progressive is not the ordinary neutral choice for a bare speculative prediction such as a distant weather guess; will or going to normally serves that purpose. Context may support marked conversational uses, so explain the default rather than invent an absolute prohibition.\n\nForm and temporal interpretation are distinct levels of analysis. A present-tense schedule can concern one future occasion, not an eternal habit: The eclipse begins at 14:10 tomorrow. Present simple also occurs in subordinate time clauses such as when the train arrives, a structure studied in the final module of this day. Do not confuse that syntactic pattern with a main-clause timetable merely because both verbs look present. When editing, preserve the source of certainty: The timetable says the train leaves at six explicitly attributes the schedule; I expect the train will leave at six expresses an expectation. Both are grammatical but make different claims.\n\npresent simple + future context: Commonly presents a schedule, fixed programme or future fact.\n\nam/is/are + -ing + future context: Commonly presents a current arrangement for a later event.\n\nThe examination begins at nine on Monday.\nPresent begins reports an official schedule with future reference.\n\nWe are interviewing two applicants tomorrow.\nThe progressive presents an arrangement already established.\n\nThe timetable says the last bus departs at 22:15, but we are leaving earlier.\nA stated schedule and a personal arrangement coexist without requiring identical constructions.\n\nPresent simple can refer to a unique future fact, not only a repeated timetable: Her birthday falls on a Sunday next year. Do not require repetition where the context supplies a fixed future occurrence.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=9&section=gv5-d09-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Future: present continuous to talk about the future ( I’m working tomorrow ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-present-continuous-to-talk-about-the-future-i-m-working-tomorrow"},{"publisher":"Future: present simple to talk about the future ( I work tomorrow ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-present-simple-to-talk-about-the-future-i-work-tomorrow"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:9:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Future progressive, perfect and perfect progressive","text":"Future progressive, perfect and perfect progressive\n\nFuture-oriented auxiliary combinations can present an activity in progress at a later point, a situation viewed from that later point, or the duration of earlier activity up to it. The construction and the temporal context must both be considered.\n\nFuture progressive uses will be plus an -ing form: At ten tomorrow, I will be interviewing applicants. It places the reader within an activity expected to be in progress at that point. Negate the finite modal, I will not be interviewing, and invert it in a question, Will you be interviewing? Do not change be to is after will: a modal selects the base form. This construction can also present an expected course of events, as in I will be passing the pharmacy, so I can collect the prescription. The intended time is supplied by the context.\n\nFuture perfect uses will have plus a past participle: By ten, I will have interviewed three applicants. It looks back from a future boundary and presents what will then be completed or true. A state can also extend to that boundary: By June, she will have known him for ten years. Future perfect progressive uses will have been plus -ing: By ten, I will have been interviewing for two hours. It draws attention to activity and its duration up to the future viewpoint. It does not necessarily state that the activity stops at that boundary.\n\nCompare completed quantity with continuing course: By Friday, we will have inspected six bridges reports a completed total; By Friday, we will have been inspecting bridges for a week highlights the period of activity. Neither construction alone guarantees the whole project is finished unless the project’s limits are stated. With some predicates, both future perfect simple and progressive are defensible: In May, I will have worked here for five years and I will have been working here for five years can describe the same employment period. A question must specify the desired construction or intended focus if it is to have a unique answer.\n\nThe so-called future perfect is a combination of modal will and perfect have; its conventional label describes a common temporal interpretation. A deadline does not automatically make it obligatory: We will finish by Friday is ordinary English beside We will have finished by Friday. The latter explicitly adopts a later retrospective viewpoint. Progressive restrictions also remain sensitive to meaning: will have known is the ordinary form for a continuing knowledge state, while will have been considering describes a process. Avoid mechanically generating all possible auxiliaries for every verb. A formally elaborate sequence can be semantically awkward even when the component endings appear in the correct order.\n\nwill + be + -ing: An activity viewed as in progress at a future point.\n\nwill + have + past participle: A retrospective view from a future point.\n\nwill + have + been + -ing: Activity and its duration leading up to a future viewpoint.\n\nAt this time tomorrow, the committee will be hearing evidence.\nThe future point lies within the projected hearing activity.\n\nBy the end of tomorrow, the committee will have heard six witnesses.\nThe future perfect presents a completed count by the boundary.\n\nBy noon, the volunteers will have been working for four hours.\nThe perfect progressive foregrounds duration without necessarily ending the work at noon.\n\nSome duration predicates allow either simple or progressive perfect forms with overlapping reference: will have lived and will have been living can both measure a period up to a future point. A duration phrase does not alone select one uniquely.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=9&section=gv5-d09-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Future continuous ( I will be working ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-continuous-i-will-be-working"},{"publisher":"Future perfect simple ( I will have worked eight hours ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-perfect-simple-i-will-have-worked-eight-hours"},{"publisher":"Future perfect continuous ( I will have been working here ten years ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-perfect-continuous-i-will-have-been-working-here-ten-years"},{"publisher":"Present perfect simple or present perfect continuous? - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-or-present-perfect-continuous"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:9:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Future in the past, time clauses and consistent viewpoints","text":"Future in the past, time clauses and consistent viewpoints\n\nFuture reference is relative to a viewpoint. A past narrator can describe an event that was still future then. Time and condition clauses follow their own patterns, and reporting may shift a viewpoint without requiring every verb to become the same tense.\n\nFuture in the past describes a later situation viewed from an earlier past point: On Monday, she said she would return on Tuesday. Would is the usual backshifted counterpart of will in this reporting pattern. Past forms of other future expressions are also available: She was going to return; She was leaving the next day. The construction does not by itself establish that the planned return happened. Add the outcome if it matters: She was going to return, but the storm cancelled the ferry. Identify the earlier viewpoint before deciding what a later expression means.\n\nIn ordinary future time clauses, use a present form rather than future will: I will call when the meeting ends; We will leave after she has signed the register. Present perfect in the time clause can emphasise prior completion. The main clause can still contain will. Similar teaching patterns occur with open conditions: If the weather improves, we will leave. This guidance concerns a particular subordinate use; it is not a ban on will after the spelling when or if. Distinguish a clause locating an event from an embedded question asking what time an event will occur.\n\nCompare I will phone when the train arrives with I do not know when the train will arrive. The first when-clause supplies the time of phoning; the second is an embedded interrogative complement expressing an unknown arrival time. Will is appropriate in that embedded question. In an if-clause, will may express willingness or insistence rather than simple future location: If you will hold the ladder, I can carry the tools. This means if you are willing to hold it. A later lesson develops the full range of conditional meanings. Do not remove a modal mechanically when it carries such an independent meaning.\n\nBackshift is common after past reporting verbs, but reporting also considers whether a statement remains true or a plan remains future at report time. She said the office opens at nine can retain a currently valid schedule; She said the office opened at nine adopts a past reporting viewpoint without necessarily asserting that the schedule changed. Choose and explain the viewpoint. Temporal consistency is coherence, not uniform tense: Yesterday I learned that the exhibition opens next month contains a past discovery and a future schedule. Time and place words also depend on the reporting context. Use explicit dates when a transformation would otherwise make tomorrow, next week or the following day ambiguous.\n\npast reporting verb + would + base form: A common future-in-the-past reporting pattern.\n\nmain future clause + when/after/until + present form: Common future time-clause pattern; not the pattern of every clause introduced by the same word.\n\nThe witness said she would send the photographs the next day.\nWould presents sending as future from the earlier statement.\n\nWe will publish the report after the auditors have checked the figures.\nThe present perfect time clause presents checking as prior to publication.\n\nI cannot tell you when the report will appear.\nThe when-clause is an embedded question, so future will is appropriate.\n\nWill can occur in a conditional clause with willingness: If you will sign here, we can proceed. Explain its modal meaning instead of teaching never use will after if.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=9&section=gv5-d09-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Future in the past - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/future-in-the-past"},{"publisher":"Conditionals: if - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conditionals-if"},{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"By - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/by"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:10:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Core modals, marginal forms and substitutes","text":"Core modals, marginal forms and substitutes\n\nCore modal auxiliaries are a small class including can, could, may, might, will, would, shall, should and must; related expressions share some, but not all, of their grammatical behaviour.\n\nA core modal comes before the base form of the next verb: can swim, might leave, must be. It has no third-person singular -s: She can swim, not she cans swim. Form a negative directly with not and a question by moving the modal before the subject: She cannot attend; Can she attend? Ordinary do-support is unnecessary. The next verb can itself be an auxiliary: may have arrived, should be working. These simple structural facts apply even when the whole verb phrase expresses a complicated time relationship.\n\nCore modals lack ordinary infinitival and participial forms. To express ability after want, use want to be able to, not want to can. For a past obligation, had to often supplies a form that must lacks. Ought to contains to, while need and dare have both modal-like and lexical patterns. Compare Need we leave? with Do we need to leave? The former is relatively formal and more characteristic of British usage. Names such as semi-modal and marginal modal vary between grammars; learn the actual pattern rather than assuming all related expressions behave identically.\n\nHave to, be able to, be supposed to and be allowed to use ordinary auxiliary and tense machinery: Does she have to leave? She has been able to work. They can follow a core modal: She may have to leave. Two core modals do not normally appear together in the edited international standard taught here; use might be able to rather than might can. Some regional English varieties permit multiple modals. Describing that variation does not make the form appropriate for every written context, nor does the course default make the regional construction meaningless.\n\nModal form and temporal meaning are not a simple present/past table. Could can describe earlier ability, present tentative possibility or a polite request; would can describe reported future reference, willingness or a hypothetical result. A core modal may stand alone when its complement is recoverable: Can she attend? She can. This is ellipsis, not an incomplete sentence. When analysing a modal chain, identify the finite modal first, then each following auxiliary and its required verb form. This prevents incorrect forms such as should has gone or must to leave without making an inaccurate claim that every word following a modal is a lexical infinitive.\n\nmodal + base form: The base form can be a lexical verb or an auxiliary.\n\nmodal + subject + base form?: Canonical interrogative order without do-support.\n\nNida can swim.\nCan has no -s with the singular subject.\n\nDoes Nida have to swim today?\nHave to uses do-support here; it is not a core modal.\n\nNida might have been swimming when the call arrived.\nMight precedes perfect have, participial been and progressive swimming.\n\nRelated expressions need individual patterns: ought to includes to, but core should takes a base form. Recoverable complements may be omitted: Yes, she can.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=17&section=gv5-d17-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"},{"publisher":"Modality: forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-forms"},{"publisher":"Need","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/need"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:10:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Ability, permission, necessity and inference","text":"Ability, permission, necessity and inference\n\nModal meaning includes judgments about what is possible or likely and attitudes towards what people are able, permitted, required or willing to do.\n\nUse can for present ability in a suitable context: Farah can read the script. Can or may can grant permission, with may often more formal. Must can impose a strong obligation; must not can prohibit. Do not confuse prohibition with absence of obligation: You must not enter excludes entry; You do not have to enter permits a choice. Should commonly gives advice or expresses an expected outcome. The same words can serve different purposes, so a label should explain the sentence in context rather than become a permanent dictionary tag.\n\nCould often describes general past ability: At ten, she could swim well. For a specific successful event, managed to or was able to is often clearer: Despite the current, she managed to reach the bank. However could occurs naturally in many negative, perception and mental-activity contexts: I could hear the bell. Will can express prediction, willingness, insistence or a promise. Would commonly softens a request: Would you close the door? The interrogative asks about willingness on its surface but usually functions as a request, not a test of physical ability.\n\nDeductive must presents a conclusion drawn from evidence: The lights are on; someone must be inside. Deductive cannot rejects a possibility: She cannot be inside; the room was sealed before she arrived. This is different from cannot as prohibition or inability. May not is particularly scope-sensitive: The office may not open means opening is uncertain, whereas You may not enter in an official notice normally denies permission. Should can express expectation as well as advice: The parcel should arrive today. A speaker's confidence is affected by evidence and the type of statement, so a universal numerical ranking is misleading.\n\nNecessity and possibility are interpreted relative to assumptions, rules, abilities or evidence. In deontic necessity, the relevant background includes regulations; in epistemic necessity, it includes what the speaker believes the evidence establishes. Neither use automatically guarantees compliance or factual truth. The student must attend does not assert attendance, and The student must be attending is a strong inference that may prove mistaken. Advanced interpretation therefore separates the modal's type from the truth of its complement. A precise explanation says what the speaker commits to and what remains open, rather than replacing a modal with an unconditional assertion.\n\nmust not + verb: Commonly prohibits the action.\n\ndo not have to + verb: Expresses lack of necessity; it does not by itself prohibit.\n\nYou may use the side entrance.\nIn this permission context, may authorises entry.\n\nThe courier should arrive before noon.\nShould states an expectation rather than an obligation imposed on the speaker.\n\nThe laboratory must be empty: its lights are off and the staff have left.\nMust presents an evidence-based inference, not an order to empty the room.\n\nCould is not excluded from every specific past event: I could hear the warning is natural. Distinguish the meaning and lexical context before replacing it.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=17&section=gv5-d17-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"},{"publisher":"Modality: forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-forms"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:10:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Modal perfects: evidence, regret and unnecessary actions","text":"Modal perfects: evidence, regret and unnecessary actions\n\nA modal-perfect construction combines a modal with have and a past participle, often locating a situation before the current or another reference point while retaining the modal meaning.\n\nBegin with the form: modal + have + past participle. Must have left draws an inference about a departure; might have left leaves it possible; should have left often evaluates an earlier obligation or sensible action. Have stays in its base form even after a singular subject: she might have gone. The lexical participle must be correct: could have written, not could have wrote. These forms are not all reports of completed facts. Modal meaning determines whether an event is inferred, merely possible, desired or counterfactual.\n\nShe must have missed the train presents a strong inference. She might have missed it does not assert that she did. She should have checked the timetable often criticises an omission, but should have can also express expectation: The train should have arrived by now, although I have not checked. Could have describes an unrealised opportunity in one context and a possible explanation in another. I could have called, but chose not to is different from That noise could have been the printer. Always identify the time and the intended modal reading before paraphrasing.\n\nNeed not have + participle normally says an action happened but was unnecessary: I need not have printed the agenda; everyone had a digital copy. Did not need to states lack of necessity; it does not logically settle whether the action happened. We did not need to print it, but we did is coherent. In everyday conversation, did not need to often suggests that the action was omitted, but that inference can be cancelled. A reliable assessment must supply the event facts instead of treating a common implication as a universal rule.\n\nThe perfect can locate an event before a future reference point: By Friday, the laboratory must have completed the audit can be a requirement with a deadline, rather than a past deduction. Negation also changes modal scope: cannot have left denies the possibility of an earlier departure; might not have left presents the possibility of non-departure. Should not have sent the file usually evaluates sending negatively and commonly implies it happened, but expectation contexts can alter the interpretation. Avoid deriving truth or falsity solely from the modal-perfect shape; test the interpretation against the surrounding discourse.\n\nmust have + participle: Commonly an inference about an earlier situation; context can supply a deadline requirement.\n\nneed not have + participle: An action is treated as having occurred unnecessarily.\n\nThe gate is locked; the caretaker must have left.\nAn inference concerns an earlier departure.\n\nWe need not have paid for delivery; collection was free.\nThe payment is presented as having happened unnecessarily.\n\nBy the next inspection, the contractor must have repaired the roof.\nThe perfect locates required completion before a future deadline.\n\nDid not need to often suggests non-performance, but the suggestion is defeasible. Should have may express expectation rather than criticism; do not mark those readings as errors.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=17&section=gv5-d17-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"},{"publisher":"Need","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/need"},{"publisher":"Modality: forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-forms"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:10:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Epistemic and deontic meaning, hedging and commitment","text":"Epistemic and deontic meaning, hedging and commitment\n\nEpistemic modality concerns the status of a proposition as possible, probable or necessary given evidence; deontic modality concerns permission, requirements and related norms.\n\nCompare The visitor must be in the lobby with The visitor must sign the register. The first is usually an inference; the second is usually a requirement. These labels help explain why the same auxiliary has different paraphrases. Academic and professional writing often needs a calibrated degree of commitment. The results suggest that the treatment may help makes a limited claim. The treatment helps states the conclusion more directly. A hedge is useful when it reflects uncertainty or a restricted evidential basis, not when it merely makes a sentence longer.\n\nHedging can use modals, adverbs and lexical verbs: may, might, possibly, probably, appear and suggest. These expressions interact with context rather than assign fixed percentages. Probably is stronger than possibly in ordinary neutral use, but a whole sentence's force also depends on its evidence and qualifications. The proposal may reduce delays in small offices limits both certainty and domain. Removing in small offices broadens the claim even if may stays. In revision, track the population, time, conditions and strength of the original assertion as well as its verb form.\n\nOver-hedging can obscure a well-supported statement: It may perhaps possibly be the case that the meter is faulty stacks similar reservations without specifying the uncertainty. Prefer The meter may be faulty; a second test is needed. Conversely a legal requirement should not be weakened accidentally: Staff must report hazards is not equivalent to Staff might report hazards. Politeness may soften how a request is expressed without changing the speaker's practical expectation. Would you please send the invoice? still requests action, although its form is an interrogative about willingness.\n\nScope distinguishes a possibility of a negative proposition from a denial of possibility: It may not be safe leaves open that it is unsafe; It cannot be safe presents safety as excluded by the evidence. An epistemic statement can still be mistaken, and a deontic rule can still be broken. Transparent analysis should therefore state the modal base in plain language: given the available evidence, under the rules, or with the person's present abilities. These are explanatory paraphrases, not rigid word-by-word translations. The goal is to preserve a writer's justified commitment and a reader's ability to see what supports it.\n\nmay + base-form verb phrase: The modal can express possibility and takes semantic scope over the proposition.\n\nmust + base-form verb phrase: The modal can express a requirement or inference, according to context.\n\nThe device may fail at very low temperatures.\nA possibility is limited to a stated condition.\n\nOperators must disconnect the power before cleaning.\nThe modal expresses a procedural obligation.\n\nThe small trial suggests a benefit, but larger studies are needed before a general recommendation.\nThe claim's scope and evidence are explicitly qualified rather than ornamented.\n\nFormal hedging need not use a modal: The evidence is limited is a direct, precise statement of a limitation. A hedge must not silently weaken a quoted obligation.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=17&section=gv5-d17-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"},{"publisher":"Hedges ( just )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/hedges-just"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:10:4","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Epistemic and deontic meaning, hedging and commitment","text":"Epistemic and deontic meaning, hedging and commitment\n\nEpistemic modality concerns the status of a proposition as possible, probable or necessary given evidence; deontic modality concerns permission, requirements and related norms.\n\nCompare The visitor must be in the lobby with The visitor must sign the register. The first is usually an inference; the second is usually a requirement. These labels help explain why the same auxiliary has different paraphrases. Academic and professional writing often needs a calibrated degree of commitment. The results suggest that the treatment may help makes a limited claim. The treatment helps states the conclusion more directly. A hedge is useful when it reflects uncertainty or a restricted evidential basis, not when it merely makes a sentence longer.\n\nHedging can use modals, adverbs and lexical verbs: may, might, possibly, probably, appear and suggest. These expressions interact with context rather than assign fixed percentages. Probably is stronger than possibly in ordinary neutral use, but a whole sentence's force also depends on its evidence and qualifications. The proposal may reduce delays in small offices limits both certainty and domain. Removing in small offices broadens the claim even if may stays. In revision, track the population, time, conditions and strength of the original assertion as well as its verb form.\n\nOver-hedging can obscure a well-supported statement: It may perhaps possibly be the case that the meter is faulty stacks similar reservations without specifying the uncertainty. Prefer The meter may be faulty; a second test is needed. Conversely a legal requirement should not be weakened accidentally: Staff must report hazards is not equivalent to Staff might report hazards. Politeness may soften how a request is expressed without changing the speaker's practical expectation. Would you please send the invoice? still requests action, although its form is an interrogative about willingness.\n\nScope distinguishes a possibility of a negative proposition from a denial of possibility: It may not be safe leaves open that it is unsafe; It cannot be safe presents safety as excluded by the evidence. An epistemic statement can still be mistaken, and a deontic rule can still be broken. Transparent analysis should therefore state the modal base in plain language: given the available evidence, under the rules, or with the person's present abilities. These are explanatory paraphrases, not rigid word-by-word translations. The goal is to preserve a writer's justified commitment and a reader's ability to see what supports it.\n\nmay + base-form verb phrase: The modal can express possibility and takes semantic scope over the proposition.\n\nmust + base-form verb phrase: The modal can express a requirement or inference, according to context.\n\nThe device may fail at very low temperatures.\nA possibility is limited to a stated condition.\n\nOperators must disconnect the power before cleaning.\nThe modal expresses a procedural obligation.\n\nThe small trial suggests a benefit, but larger studies are needed before a general recommendation.\nThe claim's scope and evidence are explicitly qualified rather than ornamented.\n\nFormal hedging need not use a modal: The evidence is limited is a direct, precise statement of a limitation. A hedge must not silently weaken a quoted obligation.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=17&section=gv5-d17-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"},{"publisher":"Hedges ( just )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/hedges-just"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:11:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Building the passive verb phrase","text":"Building the passive verb phrase\n\nA canonical be-passive uses a form of be plus a lexical past participle and normally presents as subject an element corresponding to an object in a related active clause.\n\nCompare The technician repairs the scanner with The scanner is repaired by the technician. The scanner is the object in the active and the subject in the passive. Be supplies the finite present form, and repaired is a participle. The subject of a passive is therefore not necessarily the performer of an action. Form a negative or a question through the first auxiliary: The scanner is not repaired here; Is the scanner repaired here? A by-phrase can identify the agent, but it is often omitted when unknown, obvious or unimportant.\n\nPreserve tense and aspect through the auxiliary chain. Active is repairing corresponds to passive is being repaired; was repairing corresponds to was being repaired. Has repaired corresponds to has been repaired; had repaired to had been repaired. A modal comes before base be: can be repaired. A modal perfect uses have been: must have been repaired. Future perfect reference can use will have been repaired. To-infinitives can likewise be passive: to be repaired; a perfect passive infinitive is to have been repaired. The lexical participle remains repaired through these changes; the preceding auxiliaries carry the grammatical distinctions.\n\nThe sequence has been being repaired is grammatically available for a perfect-progressive passive, but it is uncommon and often cumbersome. In ordinary writing, an active clause such as technicians have been repairing it, or a suitably contextual recast, is often easier to process. Avoid calling a difficult but possible combination impossible. Conversely, do not lose duration by replacing has been being repaired for a month with was repaired a month ago: the latter presents a different time relationship. Choose a recast that preserves what is known about continuation or completion.\n\nNot every sequence of be plus a participial-looking word has a uniquely verbal passive analysis. The door was closed at six can report the closing event or describe the resulting state, depending on context. A clear agent phrase or an event-focused progressive can support a verbal reading, while a contrast of states can support an adjectival reading. Individual diagnostics have limits because words can belong to more than one construction. In analysis, describe the reading actually licensed by the discourse rather than assigning a label from the surface ending alone. In transformation, check the original verb's complements before selecting a new subject.\n\nfinite be + past participle: A basic passive; be carries tense and agreement.\n\nhave + been + past participle: A perfect passive.\n\nmodal + be / modal + have been + past participle: Simple or perfect passive under a modal.\n\nto be / to have been + past participle: Passive infinitival patterns.\n\nThe labels are checked before dispatch.\nPresent passive describes the checking procedure; labels is the grammatical subject.\n\nThe labels were being checked when the power failed.\nPast progressive passive presents checking as in progress at the interruption.\n\nThe labels must have been checked before dispatch.\nThe modal-perfect passive can express a deduction in an evidential context; context determines the modal reading.\n\nPerfect-progressive passives exist but are uncommon: the bridge has been being inspected. Prefer a clear recast without pretending that the form is universally ungrammatical. Be plus participle can also describe a state.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=19&section=gv5-d19-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Passive: forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/passive-forms"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"},{"publisher":"Perfect infinitive with to ( to have worked )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/perfect-infinitive-with-to-to-have-worked"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:11:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Agents, reporting, get-passives and two-object verbs","text":"Agents, reporting, get-passives and two-object verbs\n\nPassive constructions can background an agent, report a claim or promote different objects, while get-passives often present a dynamic event in a more conversational register.\n\nAn agentless passive is useful when the affected item is the continuing topic: The scanner arrived yesterday. It was installed this morning. Omitting an unknown agent avoids inventing one. It can also obscure responsibility, so the omission should serve the reader's purpose. A known, important agent may belong in a by-phrase: The decision was overturned by the appeal panel. Active and passive versions organise information differently; neither voice is a universal rule for professional writing. Some passive clauses describe states rather than obvious deliberate actions, so agent is not always the best semantic label for every by-phrase.\n\nReporting passives include It is believed that the bridge is safe and The bridge is believed to be safe. The first uses impersonal it and a content clause; the second places bridge first with an infinitival complement. For an event earlier than the reporting belief, use a perfect infinitive where appropriate: The bridge is believed to have opened in 1980. The construction attributes a belief; it does not guarantee the proposition is true. Keep the source if it matters: The bridge is believed by local historians to have opened in 1980 is more informative than an entirely unattributed report.\n\nWith suitable two-object verbs, either recipient or transferred item can become passive subject: The charity gave the school a printer; The school was given a printer; A printer was given to the school. The alternatives are not unlimited for every verb. Explain takes the pattern explain something to someone in the intended sense; The rule was explained to her follows that pattern, whereas She was explained the rule is not the ordinary standard counterpart. Learn the lexical pattern, not just an abstract two-object formula. Different dialects and particular verbs also differ in their preferred passive alternatives.\n\nA get-passive uses get plus a participle: The parcel got damaged. It is especially common in conversation and typically foregrounds an event or the affected participant. It is not confined to bad outcomes: She got promoted reports a desirable event in many contexts. Get does not by itself prove that the subject caused or deserved the outcome. Adding a reflexive, as in He got himself locked out, can suggest responsibility, but context still matters. A state such as The answer is known does not automatically have a natural equivalent with get. Keep meaning and register in view when choosing between be, get and an active clause.\n\nit + passive reporting verb + that-clause: Reports an attributed proposition with impersonal it.\n\nsubject + passive reporting verb + to-infinitive: Puts a participant in the reported proposition first.\n\nrecipient + be + participle + transferred item: Possible with suitable ditransitive verbs such as give.\n\nget + past participle: A commonly conversational event-focused passive.\n\nThe museum was given a complete catalogue.\nMuseum is the recipient promoted from a give construction.\n\nThe manuscript is thought to have survived the fire.\nThe perfect infinitive places survival before the present attribution of belief.\n\nThe trainee got promoted after the trial period.\nGet-passive describes an event; a negative outcome is not required.\n\nGet-passives can report positive events and need not imply fault. Not all verbs allow both double-object/passive alternatives: lexical complementation must be checked.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=19&section=gv5-d19-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Passive: forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/passive-forms"},{"publisher":"Get passive","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/get-passive"},{"publisher":"Seem","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/seem"},{"publisher":"Perfect infinitive with to ( to have worked )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/perfect-infinitive-with-to-to-have-worked"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:11:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Causatives and their complement patterns","text":"Causatives and their complement patterns\n\nCausative constructions express arranging, causing or enabling another action, but have, get, make, let and help differ in grammar and in the kind of involvement they commonly convey.\n\nHave something done commonly describes arranging for someone else to act: We had the scanner repaired. The object scanner is followed by the participle repaired; the sentence does not say that we personally repaired it. Get something done can describe arranging or achieving a similar result: We got the scanner repaired before Monday. Here get is not simply an interchangeable auxiliary in every context. A causative sentence has its own subject and participant structure, so converting I repaired the scanner into I had the scanner repaired normally changes who performed or arranged the work.\n\nHave can also introduce a person and a bare infinitive: I had the technician check the wiring. Get commonly uses a person plus to-infinitive: I got the technician to check the wiring. In a suitable context it can suggest persuasion or successfully arranging cooperation. Make plus a person and a bare infinitive often expresses causing or compelling an action: The delay made us reconsider. Let normally expresses permission: The supervisor let us leave. Help accepts a bare or to-infinitive: She helped me check it; she helped me to check it. These patterns should be learned alongside their typical meanings.\n\nPassive make restores to: We were made to wait. In the permission meaning of let, be allowed to is the ordinary passive alternative: We were allowed to leave, rather than mechanically producing were let to leave. Some other uses of let, such as letting property or the fixed expression be let go, behave differently. Do not generalise a restriction on one meaning to every occurrence of a word. Help him to leave and help him leave are both standard; choosing one does not justify marking the other wrong. Register and local preference can affect frequency without changing grammaticality.\n\nHave plus object plus participle is not always an arranged service. We had our equipment stolen ordinarily reports an adverse experience, not an instruction to steal it. Context distinguishes that reading from a causative arrangement. Similarly, We had visitors waiting outside uses an -ing complement and can describe an experience or an ongoing state brought about by circumstances. The order of have and an object matters: We had repaired the scanner is past perfect, whereas We had the scanner repaired is the object-plus-participle construction. Analyse the complete verb phrase before inferring agency, time or responsibility. A meaningful transformation must preserve these distinctions rather than merely reproduce a familiar sequence.\n\nhave/get + affected object + participle: Often an arranged service or achieved result; context can supply an adverse-experience reading.\n\nhave + person + bare infinitive / get + person + to-infinitive: Related causative patterns with distinct lexical selection.\n\nmake/let + person + bare infinitive; help + person + (to-)infinitive: Complement patterns depend on the selected verb and meaning.\n\nWe had the locks changed after the move.\nThe subject arranged the service; the sentence does not identify the worker.\n\nThe noise made the lecturer pause.\nMake introduces the person affected and the bare infinitive pause.\n\nThe assistant helped the visitor to complete the form.\nTo complete is standard after help; complete without to would also be standard.\n\nHave an object stolen can report an unwanted experience rather than deliberate causation. Help admits infinitives with or without to. Fixed be let go is not evidence for unrestricted passive permission constructions.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=19&section=gv5-d19-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Have something done","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/have-something-done"},{"publisher":"Make","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/make"},{"publisher":"Let, let&rsquo;s","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/let-let-s"},{"publisher":"Help somebody (to) do","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/help-somebody-to-do"},{"publisher":"Passive: other forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/passive-other-forms"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:11:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Passivisation limits, ergative alternation and meaning","text":"Passivisation limits, ergative alternation and meaning\n\nPassivisation depends on a licensed verb-and-complement pattern; an ergative alternation instead uses the same lexical verb transitively for causing a change and intransitively for the change itself.\n\nSome active clauses have no object available for the ordinary passive operation: The parcel arrived; the children slept. The parcel was arrived is not the standard passive equivalent of arrived. A subject complement after a linking verb is not a direct object: She became a doctor cannot be mechanically turned into A doctor was become by her. These restrictions follow from the construction, not from whether the event seems important or whether the subject is human. First identify objects and complements, then ask whether the lexical verb supports the intended passive.\n\nEnglish also permits some prepositional passives: Someone looked into the complaint can correspond to The complaint was looked into. The preposition remains with the verb, even though the complaint is now subject. The bed has been slept in is another established example. Such constructions show why only verbs with a direct object can ever be passive is too broad. They do not mean that every prepositional phrase can be promoted naturally; lexical restrictions, idiomatic units and the relationship of the affected participant to the event matter. A learner's dictionary and established usage help resolve difficult individual cases.\n\nMany change-of-state verbs alternate: Heat cracked the glass; The glass cracked. In the first, heat is a cause and glass an object; in the second, glass is subject in an active intransitive clause. The glass was cracked by the heat is passive and expresses the cause differently. The active intransitive does not identify an external cause and must not be described as passive merely because its subject undergoes a change. Open, break, melt and increase permit comparable patterns in suitable senses, but not all transitive verbs alternate: A thief stole the bag does not license The bag stole with the same meaning.\n\nMeaning-preserving transformations require a final interpretation check. The scope of negation, quantifiers and modals can change when information is rearranged: Not every file was checked denies complete checking. Every file was not checked can be scope-ambiguous; use No file was checked for an explicit zero-checking claim. Keep a specific agent when that agent is part of the original informational task, preserve tense and aspect, and avoid changing an unsuccessful attempt into a completed event. Some stative transitive verbs have limited or unusual passives in ordinary senses; an awkward example is not automatically a universal ban on the verb. Distinguish syntactic possibility, normal collocation and discourse usefulness, and explain precisely which restriction an exercise intends to test.\n\ncause + transitive change verb + affected object: A causative active use, such as heat melted the wax.\n\naffected subject + intransitive change verb: An active intransitive alternation, such as the wax melted.\n\npromoted subject + be + past participle + retained preposition (+ by-agent): A licensed prepositional passive retains the preposition selected by the verb, as in The matter was looked into.\n\nThe temperature rose overnight.\nRise is intransitive here; no ordinary object can become a passive subject.\n\nThe complaint was looked into by an independent reviewer.\nA prepositional passive retains into and identifies the reviewer.\n\nThe window opened when the latch loosened.\nOpened is active intransitive, even though window undergoes the change.\n\nSome clauses without a direct object permit prepositional passives. An affected subject does not by itself make a clause passive. Stative verbs require sense-specific and usage-sensitive analysis.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=19&section=gv5-d19-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Passive: forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/passive-forms"},{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:11:4","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Passivisation limits, ergative alternation and meaning","text":"Passivisation limits, ergative alternation and meaning\n\nPassivisation depends on a licensed verb-and-complement pattern; an ergative alternation instead uses the same lexical verb transitively for causing a change and intransitively for the change itself.\n\nSome active clauses have no object available for the ordinary passive operation: The parcel arrived; the children slept. The parcel was arrived is not the standard passive equivalent of arrived. A subject complement after a linking verb is not a direct object: She became a doctor cannot be mechanically turned into A doctor was become by her. These restrictions follow from the construction, not from whether the event seems important or whether the subject is human. First identify objects and complements, then ask whether the lexical verb supports the intended passive.\n\nEnglish also permits some prepositional passives: Someone looked into the complaint can correspond to The complaint was looked into. The preposition remains with the verb, even though the complaint is now subject. The bed has been slept in is another established example. Such constructions show why only verbs with a direct object can ever be passive is too broad. They do not mean that every prepositional phrase can be promoted naturally; lexical restrictions, idiomatic units and the relationship of the affected participant to the event matter. A learner's dictionary and established usage help resolve difficult individual cases.\n\nMany change-of-state verbs alternate: Heat cracked the glass; The glass cracked. In the first, heat is a cause and glass an object; in the second, glass is subject in an active intransitive clause. The glass was cracked by the heat is passive and expresses the cause differently. The active intransitive does not identify an external cause and must not be described as passive merely because its subject undergoes a change. Open, break, melt and increase permit comparable patterns in suitable senses, but not all transitive verbs alternate: A thief stole the bag does not license The bag stole with the same meaning.\n\nMeaning-preserving transformations require a final interpretation check. The scope of negation, quantifiers and modals can change when information is rearranged: Not every file was checked denies complete checking. Every file was not checked can be scope-ambiguous; use No file was checked for an explicit zero-checking claim. Keep a specific agent when that agent is part of the original informational task, preserve tense and aspect, and avoid changing an unsuccessful attempt into a completed event. Some stative transitive verbs have limited or unusual passives in ordinary senses; an awkward example is not automatically a universal ban on the verb. Distinguish syntactic possibility, normal collocation and discourse usefulness, and explain precisely which restriction an exercise intends to test.\n\ncause + transitive change verb + affected object: A causative active use, such as heat melted the wax.\n\naffected subject + intransitive change verb: An active intransitive alternation, such as the wax melted.\n\npromoted subject + be + past participle + retained preposition (+ by-agent): A licensed prepositional passive retains the preposition selected by the verb, as in The matter was looked into.\n\nThe temperature rose overnight.\nRise is intransitive here; no ordinary object can become a passive subject.\n\nThe complaint was looked into by an independent reviewer.\nA prepositional passive retains into and identifies the reviewer.\n\nThe window opened when the latch loosened.\nOpened is active intransitive, even though window undergoes the change.\n\nSome clauses without a direct object permit prepositional passives. An affected subject does not by itself make a clause passive. Stative verbs require sense-specific and usage-sensitive analysis.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=19&section=gv5-d19-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Passive: forms","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/passive-forms"},{"publisher":"Verbs: basic forms - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verbs-basic-forms"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:12:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Quoting, reporting and choosing a perspective","text":"Quoting, reporting and choosing a perspective\n\nDirect speech presents words as a quotation; indirect speech reports their content from the current speaker’s perspective. A report can preserve a message without reproducing its wording.\n\nBegin with who said what to whom. In a direct quotation, the pronouns and expressions such as here and tomorrow belong to the original speaking situation: Mina said, “I will return tomorrow.” In an indirect report, the reporter constructs a new clause: Mina said that she would return the following day. That introduces the reported statement and can often be omitted after common verbs. Quotation marks are needed for a claim to reproduce words exactly; they are not needed merely because a sentence mentions someone’s ideas.\n\nChange reference by identifying actual people, places and times, rather than by applying a substitution list blindly. If I report my own promise, I remains I. If the report occurs in the same room on the same day, here and today may remain appropriate. A reporting verb can be present when a statement is current: The notice says that applications close on Friday. Past reporting locates the act of speaking before now. The reported clause then locates its own event relative to that act and the reporter’s present.\n\nThought reporting follows comparable patterns: Rafi thought that the entrance was locked. It need not describe spoken words. Reporters must distinguish a source’s claim from their own endorsement. The manager claimed that the stock was safe does not establish that it was safe. Words such as admitted, alleged and confirmed introduce evidential and evaluative meanings beyond said. A faithful transformation therefore preserves uncertainty and attribution, not just tense forms. Do not turn “I may resign” into a statement that the person definitely resigned or promised to resign.\n\nDirect and indirect are constructions, not degrees of factual accuracy. An exact quotation can be misleading if its context is removed; a carefully attributed paraphrase can be accurate. Traditional transformation exercises often supply a single decontextualised reporting time. Real discourse permits several perspectives, so a defensible answer states its assumed reporting situation. Free indirect discourse blends a narrator’s voice with a character’s viewpoint without a reporting clause; it is useful to recognise in literary prose, but ordinary formal reports should make attribution clear whenever readers might otherwise confuse the source with the writer.\n\nX said (that) + finite clause: Reports a proposition; reference is interpreted from the reporting context.\n\nNida said, “I need this key.”\nI and this are anchored to Nida’s original situation.\n\nNida said that she needed that key.\nThe report changes person and points back to the identified key.\n\nI said that I would remain here until Friday.\nSelf-report preserves I; here is valid if the reporter is still in the relevant place.\n\nIf the reporter and original speaker are the same person, a first-person pronoun need not change: I said I was ready.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=21&section=gv5-d21-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:12:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Backshift and temporal relationships","text":"Backshift and temporal relationships\n\nBackshift is the use of a more remote tense form in a reported clause, commonly after past reporting. It does not necessarily mean that the reported situation has ceased to be true.\n\nA common reporting sequence moves present to past: “I am tired” becomes She said she was tired. A present perfect can become a past perfect, and will can become would. These changes allow the report to be viewed from the earlier speaking time. The past form in She said she lived nearby can describe residence simultaneous with her speaking, not residence before it. Keep separate the time of the report, the time of the original statement and the time of the event described.\n\nBackshift is often optional when information remains true or relevant: He said that his sister works in a laboratory is normal if her work continues. He said that his sister worked in a laboratory can be equally appropriate and does not itself prove that she has left. A clear past sequence can also be reported without changing every past simple to past perfect. In a conventional transformation exercise, use the specified reporting viewpoint; in actual writing, choose the form that accurately communicates the intended relationships.\n\nBackshift is more strongly motivated when a current-tense report would suggest a present truth that the writer does not intend. On Monday she said that the bridge was closed; it reopened on Tuesday. Describing that Monday message as she said the bridge is closed in a Wednesday report would be misleading without a special quotation-like perspective. Past perfect normally remains past perfect. Could, should, would, might and ought to often remain unchanged, while must may remain or become had to depending on whether it expresses continuing necessity, an earlier obligation or a deduction.\n\nThe term sequence of tenses describes relationships, not a mechanical rule requiring all verbs in a sentence to share tense. A past reporting verb can combine with a present generalisation, a past event and a future-in-the-past prediction in one grammatical report. Modal past forms may express politeness or hypothesis rather than earlier time. In expert analysis, distinguish absolute time reference to the reporter’s now from relative time reference to an earlier viewpoint. If two answers differ only in a legitimate viewpoint, explain the difference rather than treating one as a grammatical failure.\n\nsaid + past clause: Often simultaneous with the original speaking time.\n\nsaid + had + past participle: Often places an event before an earlier speaking viewpoint.\n\nIn April, she said that she was applying for funding.\nThe application was current at the April speaking time.\n\nHe said that water expands when it freezes.\nThe present reports an enduring generalisation.\n\nThe pilot said that the aircraft had landed before dawn.\nThe landing precedes the past report.\n\nThe past perfect is not shifted into an invented earlier tense: She said she had already paid remains possible.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=21&section=gv5-d21-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:12:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Reported questions and embedded interrogatives","text":"Reported questions and embedded interrogatives\n\nAn embedded interrogative is a question-like clause functioning inside a larger construction. Its word order normally differs from that of an independent question.\n\nCompare Where is the archive? with She asked where the archive was. The reporting clause supplies the act of asking; the embedded clause uses the order where + subject + verb. For yes–no questions, use whether or if: He asked whether the office was open. These words introduce alternatives rather than an asserted fact. Do not add that before whether or a question word in this pattern. Punctuation belongs to the whole sentence: She asked where it was. ends with a full stop.\n\nThe whole sentence can itself be a direct question: Do you know where the archive is? takes a question mark because Do you know ...? is interrogative. Inside it, the archive is retains embedded order. Subject questions need special care: Who signed the register? becomes She asked who signed the register. Here who is the subject, so there is no separate subject to move in front of signed. Do-support disappears in ordinary embedded questions: Why did they leave? becomes He asked why they had left or why they left.\n\nIf and whether overlap but are not interchangeable everywhere. Use whether before a to-infinitive (whether to apply), after a preposition (about whether it is safe), and immediately before or not (whether or not we agree). If is common for indirect yes–no questions, though it can be confused with a condition in some contexts. Ask may take a recipient (She asked me where I lived), but wonder normally does not: She wondered where I lived. A request shaped as a question may need reporting by communicative purpose rather than literal interrogative form.\n\nEmbedded questions need not report an earlier spoken question. The issue is whether the evidence is sufficient and I know how the device works contain interrogative content without a speech event. An exclamative such as how quickly they responded has a different meaning from the question how quickly did they respond? Some varieties and informal echoes permit patterns resembling direct-question order inside a report; this course’s formal edited default uses ordinary embedded order. Quoted questions remain direct speech and retain their own order: She asked, “Where is the archive?” Distinguish that quotation from an unquoted embedded clause.\n\nasked + wh-word + subject + verb: Reports a wh-question without question inversion.\n\nasked + whether/if + clause: Reports a yes–no question.\n\nThe clerk asked whether I had an appointment.\nWhether introduces the yes–no alternatives.\n\nDo you remember where we stored the backups?\nThe outer question requires ?, while the embedded clause uses we stored.\n\nShe asked who had authorised the payment.\nWho is the embedded subject; the sequence is already correct.\n\nA subject wh-question may look unchanged when embedded: Who arrived? → She asked who arrived.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=21&section=gv5-d21-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:12:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Commands, requests and reporting-verb patterns","text":"Commands, requests and reporting-verb patterns\n\nA reporting verb selects a grammatical complement and contributes its own meaning. Reporting an instruction, promise, suggestion or exclamation requires preserving that communicative meaning.\n\nCommands and many requests can be reported with a recipient and a to-infinitive: “Close the hatch” becomes She told us to close the hatch. Put not before to for a prohibition: She warned us not to touch the cable. Asked us to can report a polite request, while ordered us to makes it stronger. These choices matter: an invitation is not automatically an order. A reported exclamation may use a content clause or an evaluative verb: “What a useful guide!” can become She exclaimed that the guide was very useful.\n\nLearn a verb’s complete pattern. Say that ... and say to someone that ... differ from tell someone that ... . Advise someone to apply, promise to return and apologise for arriving late have different complement structures. Suggest allows an -ing clause or a finite content clause: They suggested waiting; they suggested that we wait or should wait. It does not normally take a recipient plus to-infinitive with that meaning: suggested us to wait is not the standard pattern. Deny taking the money and deny that one took it likewise offer different grammatical forms.\n\nReporting verbs can change the message if selected carelessly. Promise implies commitment, admit implies acceptance of an unwelcome fact, and persuade describes successfully influencing someone rather than merely advising them. He advised me to appeal does not entail I appealed. Negation also has a scope: He did not promise to return denies a promise; He promised not to return reports a promise of non-return. A formal transformation must preserve agency, addressee, obligation, uncertainty and time relations, even if a more elaborate sentence sounds impressive.\n\nTraditional reported-speech exercises sometimes treat every question as a request for information and every imperative as an order. Actual utterances can have indirect functions: “Could you lower the screen?” is usually a request, while “Have a seat” can be an invitation. Choose the reporting construction from the supplied situation. A reporting passive such as Participants were advised to retain copies can appropriately background the adviser; it does not make an instruction more authoritative by itself. When a verb’s possible complements vary with meaning or register, consult a learner’s dictionary and compare complete original sentences before ruling out an unfamiliar form.\n\ntell/ask/advise + person + (not) to-infinitive: Reports directives while retaining an addressee.\n\nsuggest + -ing / that-clause: Reports a proposal without the nonstandard suggest-person-to pattern.\n\nThe technician warned visitors not to unplug the server.\nNot scopes over unplug; visitors are the understood actors.\n\nThe committee suggested that the trial should continue.\nMandative should is a standard British pattern.\n\nHe promised not to disclose the names.\nThe commitment concerns non-disclosure, not absence of a promise.\n\nHelp can allow a to-infinitive or bare infinitive, but that flexibility must not be transferred to every reporting verb.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=21&section=gv5-d21-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"Formal and informal language","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/formal-and-informal-language"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:12:4","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Commands, requests and reporting-verb patterns","text":"Commands, requests and reporting-verb patterns\n\nA reporting verb selects a grammatical complement and contributes its own meaning. Reporting an instruction, promise, suggestion or exclamation requires preserving that communicative meaning.\n\nCommands and many requests can be reported with a recipient and a to-infinitive: “Close the hatch” becomes She told us to close the hatch. Put not before to for a prohibition: She warned us not to touch the cable. Asked us to can report a polite request, while ordered us to makes it stronger. These choices matter: an invitation is not automatically an order. A reported exclamation may use a content clause or an evaluative verb: “What a useful guide!” can become She exclaimed that the guide was very useful.\n\nLearn a verb’s complete pattern. Say that ... and say to someone that ... differ from tell someone that ... . Advise someone to apply, promise to return and apologise for arriving late have different complement structures. Suggest allows an -ing clause or a finite content clause: They suggested waiting; they suggested that we wait or should wait. It does not normally take a recipient plus to-infinitive with that meaning: suggested us to wait is not the standard pattern. Deny taking the money and deny that one took it likewise offer different grammatical forms.\n\nReporting verbs can change the message if selected carelessly. Promise implies commitment, admit implies acceptance of an unwelcome fact, and persuade describes successfully influencing someone rather than merely advising them. He advised me to appeal does not entail I appealed. Negation also has a scope: He did not promise to return denies a promise; He promised not to return reports a promise of non-return. A formal transformation must preserve agency, addressee, obligation, uncertainty and time relations, even if a more elaborate sentence sounds impressive.\n\nTraditional reported-speech exercises sometimes treat every question as a request for information and every imperative as an order. Actual utterances can have indirect functions: “Could you lower the screen?” is usually a request, while “Have a seat” can be an invitation. Choose the reporting construction from the supplied situation. A reporting passive such as Participants were advised to retain copies can appropriately background the adviser; it does not make an instruction more authoritative by itself. When a verb’s possible complements vary with meaning or register, consult a learner’s dictionary and compare complete original sentences before ruling out an unfamiliar form.\n\ntell/ask/advise + person + (not) to-infinitive: Reports directives while retaining an addressee.\n\nsuggest + -ing / that-clause: Reports a proposal without the nonstandard suggest-person-to pattern.\n\nThe technician warned visitors not to unplug the server.\nNot scopes over unplug; visitors are the understood actors.\n\nThe committee suggested that the trial should continue.\nMandative should is a standard British pattern.\n\nHe promised not to disclose the names.\nThe commitment concerns non-disclosure, not absence of a promise.\n\nHelp can allow a to-infinitive or bare infinitive, but that flexibility must not be transferred to every reporting verb.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=21&section=gv5-d21-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"Formal and informal language","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/formal-and-informal-language"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:13:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Articles, identification and general reference","text":"Articles, identification and general reference\n\nArticles are a, an and the. A/an introduces one member of a countable class; the normally signals that the intended referent is identifiable in the situation or discourse. Zero article means that no article occurs, not that the noun phrase has no grammatical structure.\n\nStart with a singular count use: a report, an interview. A/an depends on the next sound rather than the next letter: a university, an hour, an unusual report and a useful report. The next sound may belong to an adjective, not to the noun. An introduces one unspecified member for the listener, but the speaker may already know which one: I spoke to a specialist yesterday can describe a specific person. Do not confuse indefinite form with a speaker having no particular individual in mind.\n\nUse the when the intended referent is identifiable. A visitor rang the bell. The visitor asked for directions introduces the person and then refers back. First mention is not the only source of identification: Please close the door can work when the relevant door is obvious. A bridging link also helps: We entered a house. The kitchen was bright. The kitchen is identifiable through its relationship to the house even though kitchen has not appeared before. If several doors or kitchens are equally relevant, add enough description to establish which is meant.\n\nGeneral reference has several structures. Tigers are mammals uses a bare plural for the class generally. A tiger is a mammal describes a representative member. The tiger is endangered presents the species as a category; this singular generic pattern is conventional but cannot replace every bare-plural generalisation mechanically. Noncount nouns often have zero article for general reference: Water is essential. The water in this bottle is warm identifies a particular quantity. A noun being abstract does not itself forbid the: Justice matters, but the justice of the decision was disputed.\n\nDefiniteness, specificity and uniqueness are related ideas, not synonyms. The author of this report can identify a unique role even if the reader does not know the author’s name. I need a book about wetlands can be nonspecific, whereas I bought a book about wetlands may refer to a particular purchased book. Context decides whether a description sufficiently identifies its referent; grammar does not supply a universal first-mention checklist. A/an normally accompanies a singular count use, but determiners and constructions permit other patterns, developed below. The article belongs to the noun phrase and does not tell you the noun’s function by itself.\n\na/an + singular count noun: One member of a class, with the article chosen by the following sound.\n\nthe + identifying noun phrase: The writer presents the referent as recoverable from context, discourse or descriptive information.\n\nA pilot phoned. The pilot left a message.\nFirst indefinite mention is followed by an identifiable reference to the same person.\n\nThe roof of the library needs repairs.\nThe of-phrase helps identify the intended roof without an earlier mention of roof.\n\nCopper conducts electricity; the copper in this cable is damaged.\nThe first clause is generic; the second identifies a particular material quantity.\n\nLetters alone do not decide a/an: an MP uses the initial sound /em/, while a USB cable begins with /juː/. Accent and how an abbreviation is spoken can change the appropriate choice.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=3&section=gv5-d03-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"A/an and the - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/a-an-and-the"},{"publisher":"Nouns: countable and uncountable - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-countable-and-uncountable"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:13:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Articles with institutions, names and abstract nouns","text":"Articles with institutions, names and abstract nouns\n\nArticle patterns depend on noun meaning, conventional names and the activity being described. A geographical or institutional expression must be learned as a whole pattern, with regional alternatives identified where they affect interpretation.\n\nCompare school as an institution and a school as a building or organisation. The children are at school normally describes participation as pupils. I waited outside the school identifies a particular location. British English commonly uses in hospital for a patient, while in the hospital can identify a building; American English commonly uses in the hospital for the patient meaning too. Bed has a conventional zero-article sleep use: go to bed. These are established constructions, not evidence that every building used for its purpose loses its article. A person may go to the bank for ordinary banking.\n\nNames have conventional article patterns. Many personal names, cities, countries and individual mountains have zero article: Sana, Karachi, Pakistan and Mount Kenya. Some country names conventionally include the, including the United Kingdom and the United States. Rivers, seas, oceans and mountain ranges commonly take the: the Indus, the Arabian Sea and the Himalayas. A plural name or a descriptive political name can therefore differ from a simple country name. Learn the complete name, rather than infer the article from national importance or size. Local institutional names also have their own conventions.\n\nAbstract and material nouns can be general, specific or conventionally countable in different senses. Education is important is general; the education she received was expensive identifies particular education. She had an excellent education uses a recognised count interpretation of an educational experience. The same contrast explains life and a life, knowledge and a detailed knowledge of the region. Do not add a/an to ordinary information or advice merely because an adjective intervenes. Useful advice remains noncount. Meals, languages, sports and subjects often have zero article in general activity uses, but a restrictive description can identify an instance: the lunch we ordered arrived late.\n\nArticle omission also occurs in names, headlines, notices, coordinated expressions and role descriptions. They elected her chair is a recognised complement construction; it does not license They bought chair for an ordinary object. Proper names may acquire modified or count uses, as a young Mozart or the London of her childhood, and such expressions need contextual interpretation. British and American conventions should be described at the construction level. At university is a useful British educational pattern; at college is common in American contexts. A writer choosing a particular national convention should preserve it while recognising legitimate standard alternatives.\n\nat school / in hospital in relevant British institutional uses: The noun evokes the institutional activity or participant status.\n\nthe + specified abstract/material noun: The phrase identifies a particular instance, kind or quantity.\n\nHer son is at school, but she is at the school for a meeting.\nThe first describes pupil status; the second identifies a location.\n\nThe expedition crossed the Indus before travelling towards Islamabad.\nThe river name takes the; the city name normally does not.\n\nA detailed knowledge of local law would help; knowledge alone is not enough.\nThe first has an established qualified count expression; the second is a general noncount use.\n\nInstitutional zero article is lexical and regional: go to prison does not imply that every prison-related noun phrase must omit the. The prison beside the river needs the when it identifies a building.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=3&section=gv5-d03-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"A/an and the - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/a-an-and-the"},{"publisher":"Nouns: countable and uncountable - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/nouns-countable-and-uncountable"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:13:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Determiners, distribution and quantity","text":"Determiners, distribution and quantity\n\nA determiner helps specify reference or quantity within a noun phrase. Articles, demonstratives, possessive determiners, numbers and many quantifiers take part in this system. Their distribution depends on number, countability and the meaning of the construction.\n\nDemonstratives distinguish near or currently prominent reference from more distant reference: this form, that form, these forms, those forms. Possessive determiners include my, your, her, our and their; they normally replace an article in the central determiner position: my report, not the my report. Whose and which can introduce noun phrases in questions: Which route is open? What kind of evidence is available? In this course, these uses are called determiner uses. Some school descriptions use labels such as demonstrative adjective; identifying the noun-phrase role matters more than confusing terminology with correctness.\n\nCardinal numbers state quantities, such as three rooms; ordinal numbers order members, such as the third room. Quantifiers specify amount or number more loosely: many rooms, much space, several applications and enough evidence. Many combines with plural count nouns; much combines with noncount nouns. Much is especially ordinary in negatives and questions, and also in formal positive phrases such as much research. A lot of can combine with either pattern. Enough precedes a noun but follows an adjective: enough time and clear enough. Its meaning is sufficiency for a stated or understood purpose, not simply a large quantity.\n\nEach and every distribute attention across individual members: each proposal is different; every proposal needs a signature. Each can stand alone and can occur with of: each of the proposals. Every normally needs a singular count head; use every one of the proposals rather than every of them. Both refers to two together; either selects or permits one of two, and neither excludes the two alternatives. All is not limited to two: all the proposals. These words can interact with negation: not all proposals passed means some did not, whereas no proposals passed excludes every one. Their meanings cannot be reduced to interchangeable substitutes for many.\n\nThe of-pattern distinguishes a whole general class from a specified set: many students but many of the students. Compare all students, all the students and all of the students; all allows some patterns that each or several do not. Both my colleagues and both of my colleagues are possible. Distributive quantity and grammatical agreement interact, but agreement receives full treatment on Day 10. Either can also mean both in expressions such as on either side of the road, so interpretation must follow the construction. A determiner is not defined as a word that must always be directly adjacent to its noun; adjectives and other modifiers may intervene.\n\nquantifier + plural count/noncount noun: Choose many applicants or much evidence according to the noun’s sense.\n\nquantifier + of + specified noun phrase: Many of the applicants selects from an identified set.\n\nEach of the three files needs a label.\nEach distributes the requirement to individual files and takes an of-phrase.\n\nBoth our buses have enough space.\nBoth identifies the two buses; enough gives a sufficiency judgement about noncount space.\n\nNot all of the recommendations were adopted.\nNegation excludes complete adoption without saying that none were adopted.\n\nEither side of a road can mean each of its two sides. This established construction differs from choosing either of two proposed routes.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=3&section=gv5-d03-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Determiners ( the, my, some, this ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/determiners-the-my-some-this"},{"publisher":"Much, many, a lot of, lots of : quantifiers - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/much-many-a-lot-of-lots-of-quantifiers"},{"publisher":"A/an and the - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/a-an-and-the"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:13:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Some, any, few and little: meaning under quantity and negation","text":"Some, any, few and little: meaning under quantity and negation\n\nQuantity expressions differ in the expectations they create as well as the nouns they permit. Some and any interact with questions, negation and unrestricted choice; few and little often imply insufficiency, whereas a few and a little present a positive quantity.\n\nUse a few with plural count nouns and a little with noncount nouns: a few pages, a little time. Removing a changes the usual implication: few pages suggests not many, often fewer than hoped; little time suggests not much. A few and a little assert that some quantity is available, often enough for a modest purpose. The real situation matters: a few mistakes can be reassuring in a long report but serious in a safety procedure. Grammatical form expresses a perspective; it does not calculate whether a quantity is objectively good or bad.\n\nSome often introduces an indefinite quantity in an affirmative statement: We bought some paper. Any is common in negatives and open questions: We did not buy any paper; Do you have any paper? These are tendencies, not a rule banning some from questions. Would you like some tea? is an ordinary offer, and Have you got some spare paper? can suggest an expected positive answer. Any also has a free-choice use: Choose any seat means there is no restriction on which seat you select. That use is compatible with a singular count noun.\n\nIn careful edited comparison, fewer commonly accompanies plural count nouns and less accompanies noncount nouns: fewer delays, less disruption. Established exceptions include measurements, sums and time spans regarded as amounts: less than ten minutes and less than twenty pounds. Less is also widespread with plural count nouns, especially in informal usage; a formal editor may prefer fewer ordinary individual items. Do not mark less than five miles wrong by applying a count-noun slogan to a measurement. Meaning and editorial convention are both relevant. More and most do not have separate count and noncount forms.\n\nScope helps explain quantity expressions that seem similar. I did not read some of the reports can identify a particular subset that went unread, while I did not read any of the reports normally says none were read. Some can be stressed to create a contrast, so intonation and context contribute to interpretation. Few and little are negative in orientation and can license expressions associated with negation: Few applicants have ever complained. A few applicants have complained instead presents an existing positive set. Avoid classifying either sentence by a memorised positive-versus-negative word list without interpreting what is asserted about the set.\n\nfew/a few + plural count noun; little/a little + noncount noun: The article changes the usual negative or positive orientation.\n\nnot + any + relevant set: Ordinarily expresses absence of even one member or amount in that set.\n\nWe have a little fuel, so we can reach the next village.\nA little presents a positive amount, linked here to sufficiency.\n\nFew applicants had read all the instructions.\nFew presents a small proportion and is negative in orientation.\n\nThe repair took less than thirty minutes but required fewer tools.\nMinutes is a measured span; tools are separate counted objects.\n\nSome is normal in offers and questions with a positive expectation: Would you like some water? Do not replace it automatically with any.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=3&section=gv5-d03-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Some and any - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/some-and-any"},{"publisher":"Little, a little, few, a few - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/little-a-little-few-a-few"},{"publisher":"Less or fewer ? - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/less-or-fewer"},{"publisher":"Much, many, a lot of, lots of : quantifiers - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/much-many-a-lot-of-lots-of-quantifiers"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:14:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Personal forms, case and reflexive meaning","text":"Personal forms, case and reflexive meaning\n\nPersonal pronouns mark speaker, addressee or other referents. Their forms can distinguish grammatical case, such as I/me and she/her. Reflexive forms such as myself and themselves can refer back to a local subject or emphasise a participant; those uses must be distinguished from ordinary object forms.\n\nCompare She thanked him with He thanked her. She and he are subject forms; him and her are object forms. The forms show grammatical relationships even when the people remain the same. I, we and they contrast with me, us and them; you and it do not show this case contrast in spelling. Possessive determiners accompany a noun, as my folder and their house, while independent possessives can stand without that noun, as mine and theirs. Hers, ours, yours and theirs have no additional apostrophe. Learn each form together with a short sentence rather than memorising a list without its function.\n\nCoordination does not automatically change the case expected in careful edited prose: Sara and I attended; the tutor spoke to Sara and me. Temporarily removing Sara and can help reveal the ordinary subject or object pattern, although coordination has additional informal usage conventions. Do not insert I simply because it sounds polite. After be, object forms are normal standard usage: It is me and That is her. It is I survives as a highly formal alternative; calling every object form after be wrong confuses a restrictive traditional preference with present-day grammar.\n\nWhen subject and object refer to the same participant in a simple clause, a reflexive often expresses that relation: Leena blamed herself. Leena blamed her normally directs blame towards a different female referent. Reflexives can also emphasise: Leena herself checked the figures; Leena checked the figures herself. The emphatic form adds contrast or responsibility and is not the subject on its own. By herself can mean alone or without help. Do not assume that every first-language reflexive construction needs an English reflexive: She washed and dressed is ordinary when no special emphasis on doing it unaided is intended.\n\nCase choices interact with register and construction. Than me is standard in comparisons, while than I am uses an expanded clause; compare their actual meanings rather than enforcing a bare subject form. Informal coordinated subjects with me occur in established speech, but Sara and I is the conservative edited choice. Some independent or polite reflexives occur, such as people like yourself; they are not all governed by the simple same-clause rule for direct-object reflexives. This course therefore teaches the local subject–object contrast as a reliable basic pattern while identifying emphatic, polite and discourse-sensitive uses separately.\n\nsubject + verb + reflexive object: Leena blamed herself presents subject and object as the same person.\n\ncoordinated object after a preposition: The invitation is for Bilal and me in careful edited prose.\n\nThe curator gave us her notes; the final copy is hers.\nUs is an object form, her accompanies a noun, and hers is an independent possessive.\n\nThe scientist herself recalculated the result.\nHerself is emphatic; the scientist remains the subject phrase.\n\nFarah is taller than me, but her brother is taller than I am.\nBoth comparison patterns are standard; the latter includes an explicit clause.\n\nA polite expression such as someone like yourself can be standard without a local reflexive antecedent. This does not make Myself attended the seminar an ordinary neutral replacement for I attended the seminar.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=4&section=gv5-d04-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Pronouns: personal ( I, me, you, him, it, they, etc.) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/pronouns-personal-i-me-you-him-it-they-etc"},{"publisher":"Pronouns: reflexive ( myself, themselves, etc.) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/pronouns-reflexive-myself-themselves-etc"},{"publisher":"Possession ( John’s car, a friend of mine ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/possession-john-s-car-a-friend-of-mine"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:14:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Pronoun families and relative choices","text":"Pronoun families and relative choices\n\nPronoun families are grouped by their grammatical patterns and meanings. Interrogative pronouns ask for an identity, relative expressions connect a clause with an antecedent, demonstratives point, indefinites leave identity open, and reciprocal expressions present a mutual relationship.\n\nWho, what and which can ask for an identity: Who arrived? What happened? Which do you prefer? This, that, these and those can point without repeating a noun: These are ready. When a noun follows, the same forms have determiner uses: these reports. Indefinite pronouns such as someone, anything and nobody refer without naming an individual or thing. Everybody refers to people distributively but normally takes a singular present verb: Everybody knows. Each, either and neither can stand independently when the relevant set is understood. Their determiner uses were introduced on Day 3.\n\nRelative clauses give information connected with an antecedent: the driver who helped us, the form that I submitted and the office which opened yesterday. Who is used for people; which is used for things and can introduce a relative comment on a larger situation. Whose expresses a relationship and can refer to people or things: a house whose roof needs repair. That is possible in many defining relatives, but it does not replace who or which freely in every supplementary construction. Day 15 develops defining, supplementary and reduced relatives in detail; today concentrates on reference and pronoun form.\n\nWho commonly functions as either subject or object in contemporary English: Who arrived? Who did you invite? Whom is an object form associated especially with formal prose and directly preceding prepositions: To whom was the letter addressed? Avoid choosing whom solely because a verb or preposition occurs nearby. In Who do you think will lead the group?, who is the subject of will lead, not the object of think. Identify the pronoun’s role inside its own clause. A formal object-relative construction can use whom, while standard object who should not be rejected without a specified register requirement.\n\nEach other and one another ordinarily express mutual relations among two or more participants. The inherited convention reserving each other for two and one another for more than two is not a dependable modern grammar rule; the two are normally interchangeable. They helped themselves can describe self-directed action, while they helped each other describes mutual help. Mutual reference does not always mean every member acted towards every other member; context determines the pattern. Each other is written as two words, and its genitive is each other’s. Different grammatical frameworks analyse some traditional pronoun labels differently, so this course makes the actual construction explicit.\n\nantecedent + relative clause: The learner who asked the question links who to learner.\n\nplural participants + reciprocal expression: The colleagues supported each other presents mutual support.\n\nThose are the files that we requested.\nThose has an independent demonstrative use; that connects a relative clause to files.\n\nThe house whose windows were broken is being repaired.\nWhose relates windows to the inanimate antecedent house.\n\nWho do you believe can solve the problem?\nWho is subject of can solve despite the intervening do you believe.\n\nObject who is established standard English, especially outside highly formal preposition-first patterns. A formal exercise must state that it requires whom if both forms otherwise fit.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=4&section=gv5-d04-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Who, whom - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/who-whom"},{"publisher":"Each other, one another - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/each-other-one-another"},{"publisher":"Determiners ( the, my, some, this ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/determiners-the-my-some-this"},{"publisher":"Pronouns: indefinite (- body, - one, - thing, - where ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/pronouns-indefinite-body-one-thing-where"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:14:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Reference chains, agreement and inclusive pronouns","text":"Reference chains, agreement and inclusive pronouns\n\nReference is the connection between an expression and what it identifies in context. Anaphora looks back to an earlier expression, cataphora anticipates a later one, and deixis depends on the speaker’s situation. Clear pronoun choices maintain those connections across sentences.\n\nRead Mina found a wallet. She handed it to the receptionist. She refers back to Mina and it to the wallet; both are anaphoric links. A pronoun does not always replace an earlier noun: I and you identify participants in the current exchange, while this can point to an object in view. When she entered the hall, Mina noticed the broken window can use she cataphorically, with Mina supplying the later identification. The order of mention alone therefore does not decide whether a pronoun is grammatical. The reader must have enough information to establish the intended relationship.\n\nKeep reference clear when several potential antecedents appear. Aisha told Hina that she had won the prize leaves the winner uncertain without more context. Repeating a name can improve clarity even though the original sentence is grammatically possible. A pronoun can refer to a larger event or proposition: The bridge was closed. This delayed deliveries. Here this refers to the closure situation, not simply to bridge. For formal exposition, a fuller phrase such as this closure often makes the link clearer. Do not declare every such broad reference wrong, but ask whether readers can recover it reliably.\n\nSingular they is an established way to refer to an indefinite individual: Someone has left their umbrella; if anyone calls, ask them to wait. It also serves people who use they as their personal pronoun. A singular referent does not change its usual verb forms: they are, they have. You should not automatically replace singular they with he or insist that unknown people have a binary gender. Respect a person’s stated pronouns and retain clear reference. Where a sentence becomes cumbersome, pluralising the noun phrase or repeating a name may help, provided the meaning remains equivalent.\n\nAgreement is not a mechanical matching of neighbouring letters. Someone normally takes singular has, while subsequent they has its established are/have forms even when the referent is one person. British collective reference can follow a member-focused plural: The committee have finished their separate reports. A writer must make the intended construal coherent, rather than impose one universal rule about all group nouns. Themselves is widely used with singular they. Themself is also documented, but authorities differ in its status: Cambridge labels it not standard, while Merriam-Webster records it and discusses its increasing use. Institutional editorial preferences can therefore differ. This introductory lesson uses themselves as the broad default without calling every documented alternative nonexistent. Day 10 treats collective and notional agreement in detail.\n\nindefinite singular antecedent + singular they: Someone has left their folder is a standard gender-neutral reference chain.\n\nambiguous pronoun → explicit reference: Repeat a name or descriptive noun when more than one antecedent is plausible.\n\nEvery applicant should bring their identification.\nTheir can refer inclusively to each individual applicant.\n\nBefore he spoke, Omar checked the microphone.\nHe can anticipate the later name Omar; this is cataphoric reference.\n\nThe reservoir was contaminated. This finding prompted a new inspection.\nThis finding makes the connection to the preceding proposition explicit.\n\nPronouns can identify a situation rather than one noun: She resigned. That surprised us. This is grammatical when the event reference is clear; a fuller noun phrase can improve precision.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=4&section=gv5-d04-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Pronouns: personal ( I, me, you, him, it, they, etc.) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/pronouns-personal-i-me-you-him-it-they-etc"},{"publisher":"Sexist language - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/sexist-language"},{"publisher":"THEY | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/they"},{"publisher":"DEIXIS | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/deixis"},{"publisher":"Subject–verb agreement - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subject-verb-agreement"},{"publisher":"THEMSELF Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster","url":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/themself"},{"publisher":"THEMSELF | English meaning - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/themself"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:14:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Dummy subjects, generic reference and local interpretation","text":"Dummy subjects, generic reference and local interpretation\n\nA dummy subject fills a grammatical subject position without identifying a normal participant, as it in It is raining. Generic pronouns refer to people generally. Practical binding concerns how pronouns and reflexives are linked to other expressions within grammatical structure.\n\nIn English, a neutral finite statement normally has a subject even when no ordinary actor is involved: It is raining; It is nearly noon. It is not a person causing rain or a previously mentioned object. Existential there introduces something’s existence or presence: There is a spare chair in the corridor. This there differs from locative there in Put the chair there, where it indicates a place. Grammatical descriptions differ in how they analyse the following noun phrase, but treating there as a dummy subject is useful for understanding why the sentence does not need a named actor before is.\n\nDummy it also permits delayed content: It is useful to check the references; It is clear that the figures differ. The infinitival or that-clause supplies the important content, while it fills the initial subject slot. This arrangement is called extraposition in the relevant constructions and receives advanced treatment on Day 23. Do not assume every it is empty: I found the report. It is useful has an ordinary referring it. The form alone does not settle the interpretation; examine what it is doing in that particular sentence.\n\nGeneric you, we, one and they allow different perspectives. You can learn a great deal by listening addresses people generally in many instructional contexts. One must check one’s sources is more formal and can sound detached. We can include writer and readers in a shared generalisation; unspecified they can mean a relevant institution or authority, though naming the authority may be clearer in formal reporting. Once a perspective is selected, avoid unexplained switches such as One should check your sources. Generic reference is not an excuse to invent claims about what every reader personally experiences.\n\nFor ordinary reflexive objects, identify the relevant local subject: Nida said that Sana blamed herself normally links herself with Sana. Replacing herself with himself does not ordinarily allow it to refer across that finite clause to an earlier male name. These practical restrictions are often discussed under binding in syntax. They are not a complete theory of every reflexive use: emphatic forms, exempt or perspective-sensitive reflexives and picture-noun constructions can be more complex. A pronoun can also be grammatically possible yet misleading if its antecedent is unclear. Separate the structural restriction from the editorial task of making a reference chain easy to follow.\n\nit + weather/time expression: It is raining fills the subject position without a referring participant.\n\nthere + be + noun phrase: There is a spare chair introduces the existence or presence of the chair.\n\nIt is snowing outside.\nIt is a dummy weather subject.\n\nThere are three unclaimed parcels at reception.\nExistential there introduces parcels; careful edited agreement is plural are.\n\nAmir said that Leena blamed herself for the delay.\nThe local subject Leena supplies the ordinary antecedent of herself.\n\nReflexives used for politeness or emphasis do not all follow the ordinary local-object pattern. Do not extend a beginner’s same-clause test into a claim that all other reflexive constructions are impossible.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=4&section=gv5-d04-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Dummy subjects - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/dummy-subjects"},{"publisher":"Pronouns: personal ( I, me, you, him, it, they, etc.) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/pronouns-personal-i-me-you-him-it-they-etc"},{"publisher":"Pronouns: reflexive ( myself, themselves, etc.) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/pronouns-reflexive-myself-themselves-etc"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:15:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Placing modifiers and recovering their subjects","text":"Placing modifiers and recovering their subjects\n\nA modifier adds descriptive or limiting information to another expression. A misplaced modifier suggests an unintended attachment; a dangling modifier lacks a suitable understood participant in its grammatical context.\n\nStart by asking what each description is meant to describe. In a blue folder on the desk, blue describes the folder and on the desk locates it. In an opening participial clause such as Carrying a blue folder, Leena entered, Leena is normally understood as the person carrying the folder. Writing Carrying a blue folder, the door opened instead makes the door the expected carrier. The problem is not that the sentence begins with an -ing form; it is that the grammatical connection does not fit the intended event.\n\nPlace a modifier close enough to its target that readers can recover the intended relationship. A notice reading We sell bags for children made of recycled fabric invites the absurd reading that children are made of fabric. We sell recycled-fabric bags for children removes that attachment risk. An introductory past-participial clause can correctly attach to a passive subject: Damaged in transit, the equipment was returned. Equipment is both what was damaged and what was returned; a passive main clause is not automatically a dangling construction.\n\nA squinting modifier lies between expressions it could modify in either direction: Students who revise regularly improve. Regularly may describe revising or improvement. Rewrite around the intended message rather than move the word mechanically: Students who revise on a regular schedule improve, or Students who revise show regular improvement. Purpose infinitives need semantic care too. To reach the balcony, take the stairs has an understood imperative you; the absence of a printed subject does not make it faulty. To reach the balcony, the staircase is narrow gives no suitable controller of reach.\n\nThe subject-sharing guideline governs ordinary subjectless adjunct clauses, not every initial expression. An absolute construction supplies its own subject: The inspection complete, the team left. Conventional comments such as generally speaking or to be honest concern the speaker’s stance and need not describe the next noun as an action. Some apparent dangling expressions are established discourse constructions; others create a real unintended assertion. Expert revision identifies the construction before applying a rule. If a person’s identity is unknown, use a finite clause or an impersonal alternative instead of inventing a named agent to repair the sentence.\n\nSubjectless participial adjunct, + main subject + predicate: The understood participant normally matches the main subject.\n\nNoun phrase + participle, + clause: An absolute can supply a different explicit subject.\n\nWalking beside the canal, Farah noticed the damaged railing.\nFarah is the walker and the observer.\n\nStored below zero, the samples were stable for a week.\nThe passive main subject also receives the storage action.\n\nThe access road having flooded, the engineers postponed the visit.\nThe absolute has its own subject, the access road.\n\nConventional speaker comments such as Frankly speaking, the proposal is expensive do not require the proposal to speak.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=22&section=gv5-d22-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Participle clauses","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/grammar/c1-grammar/participle-clauses"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:15:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Attachment, scope and reference","text":"Attachment, scope and reference\n\nAmbiguity occurs when a form supports more than one interpretation. Attachment concerns which expression a modifier belongs to; scope concerns how much of a message an operator such as only or not affects.\n\nA grammatical sentence can be ambiguous without containing an error. I photographed the guide with the telescope may mean that I used the telescope or that the guide had it. The prepositional phrase can attach to the photographing event or to the noun guide. To revise, state the intended interpretation: Using the telescope, I photographed the guide; or I photographed the guide who was carrying the telescope. Neither rewrite is the universally correct answer because the original supplies no decision between the two meanings.\n\nScope differences are often created by focusing words. Only Salma reviewed the draft contrasts Salma with other possible reviewers. Salma reviewed only the draft contrasts that document with other possible objects. Salma only reviewed the draft can emphasise that she reviewed it rather than approved it, although spoken stress and context may support other readings. Keep the intended contrast explicit in formal prose. Negation interacts with quantifiers too: Not all the samples failed means at least some did not fail; none of the samples failed says that every sample avoided failure.\n\nPronoun reference requires a recoverable intended person or thing, but not every pronoun needs a single preceding noun. This can refer to an entire event: The lift stopped between floors. This delayed the delivery. That is a legitimate propositional reference. By contrast, When Hina met Sara, she was worried does not establish which woman was worried. Repeat a name or restructure the sentence if the distinction matters. Avoid solving ambiguity by guessing gender, role or a stereotype; preserve uncertainty when the source does not identify the intended referent.\n\nGrammatical ambiguity can coexist with pragmatic clarity. Context may make one reading overwhelmingly likely even though the structure permits another. Expert analysis separates a possible parse from a plausible intended meaning and identifies the evidence for each. Bound-variable readings also go beyond simple name replacement: Every researcher filed their own notes can vary the referent of their with each researcher. Do not replace such pronouns with one fixed name without changing meaning. Scope can remain underspecified intentionally, as in diplomatic language, but assessments must either supply enough context for one answer or explicitly reward the analysis of multiple readings.\n\nonly + focused constituent: Sets the constituent against contextually relevant alternatives.\n\nnot all + plural noun phrase: Denies universality; it does not assert none.\n\nOnly the backup server failed.\nThe focus excludes the other relevant servers from the set that failed.\n\nThe manager did not approve every claim.\nThis need not mean that the manager approved no claims.\n\nEach student labelled their own specimen.\nTheir varies with each student; singular they need not identify one fixed person.\n\nBare this is acceptable when its propositional referent is clear; adding a summary noun is a clarity tool, not a universal grammatical requirement.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=22&section=gv5-d22-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Adverbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adverbs"},{"publisher":"It, this and that in paragraphs","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/it-this-and-that-in-paragraphs"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:15:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Parallelism, correlatives and comparisons","text":"Parallelism, correlatives and comparisons\n\nParallelism presents related elements in comparable grammatical or conceptual forms. It supports clear comparison and coordination, while grammatical coordination ultimately depends on the functions and constructions involved.\n\nLists are easier to process when their parts follow a recognisable pattern: The role involves scheduling visits, checking records and answering enquiries. Each -ing clause fills the same complement position after involves. A mixture such as involves scheduling visits, record checks and to answer enquiries becomes awkward because the last infinitive does not fit the pattern. Try each element after the shared opening. If an item cannot combine with that opening, repair the structure rather than merely changing its ending to resemble the neighbouring words.\n\nCorrelative pairs make structural expectations visible. The course is useful both for teachers and for editors places parallel prepositional phrases after both and and. The course both supports teachers and provides exercises coordinates verb phrases. Not only and but also can coordinate words, phrases or clauses. Fronted not only may require inversion in the clause it introduces, but it does not require every following item to be a full clause. Not only was the route shorter but also safer is a possible shared-copula construction; adding it was is another grammatical version.\n\nParallelism is not a rule that every coordinated unit must have an identical category label. A complement may coordinate expressions with a shared function: She is a capable editor and easy to work with can be understood as two predicative descriptions. Some mixed constructions are fully acceptable; others are confusing or disallowed by a particular head. In comparison, identify what is compared and what has been omitted. The new office is larger than the old one compares offices. The cost of the new office is higher than that of the old one compares costs, with that substituting for cost.\n\nReduced comparison clauses rely on recoverable material: Mei reads more articles than I do clearly compares readers; Mei reads more articles than me may also have that informal standard reading. Mei likes Jo more than I can mean more than I like Jo, whereas Mei likes Jo more than me can mean more than she likes me. Avoid using a case preference to erase a genuine reading. Formal revisions can expand ellipsis to show the intended relationship. At expert level, examine syntactic compatibility, omitted material and logical comparability separately; rhythmic symmetry alone does not prove grammatical or semantic equivalence.\n\nboth X and Y / either X or Y: The paired constituents should fit the same construction and intended relationship.\n\ncomparative + than-clause: Recover omitted material from context, or make it explicit.\n\nShe plans to inspect the roof and to test the alarm.\nThe two to-infinitives share a complementation role.\n\nNot only was the hall warmer but also quieter.\nThe copula is shared with coordinated predicative adjectives.\n\nThis week’s output exceeded last week’s output.\nThe compared quantities are of the same relevant type.\n\nDifferent forms can share a predicative function; evaluate the actual construction before declaring mixed categories ungrammatical.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=22&section=gv5-d22-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Parallelism","url":"https://writingcenter.unc.edu/tips-and-tools/parallelism/"},{"publisher":"Not only … but also","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/not-only-but-also"},{"publisher":"Ellipsis - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/ellipsis"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:15:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Predication and meaning-preserving revision","text":"Predication and meaning-preserving revision\n\nPredication attributes a property, identity or event to a subject. Faulty predication occurs when the expressed grammatical relationship fails to convey the intended logical relationship.\n\nA sentence can be structurally well formed yet say something the writer did not intend. The aim of the workshop is improving attendance can be grammatical as an identification of an aim, whereas The cost of the workshop is because transport is expensive mixes a thing being measured with an unexplained causal clause. A clearer version is The workshop is expensive because transport costs are high. First decide what the subject refers to, then decide what the predicate says about it. Do not use logical oddness as an excuse to ban every uncommon subject–complement combination.\n\nFaulty comparison often arises when an abbreviated form conceals the wrong relationship. For the intended claim about income, Her salary is higher than her assistant invites comparison with a person rather than that person’s salary. Her salary is higher than her assistant’s makes the relation clear. Some compressed expressions are familiar idioms and need no repair: He earns more than his assistant can be a complete comparative clause with earns understood. The appropriate revision depends on what is syntactically omitted and what readers can reliably reconstruct.\n\nPreserving meaning requires checking more than the topic. Did the original express every item or some items, possibility or certainty, a decision or a completed act, cause or sequence? The supervisor decided to close the office does not entail that the office closed. Because the equipment failed, the demonstration stopped asserts causation; The equipment failed and the demonstration stopped may leave the relation open. A grammatical revision that strengthens or changes these relationships is not a faithful correction, even when it sounds clearer. If the intended meaning is unavailable, offer alternatives and label their assumptions.\n\nRevision proceeds through diagnosis, interpretation, reconstruction and verification. Identify the smallest relevant structural unit, paraphrase its plausible readings, choose the intended one from evidence, then rebuild. Finally compare both versions for participants, time, polarity, quantification, modality, focus and register. Minimal editing is useful because it reduces unintended changes, but a small change is not automatically the best repair: it may preserve ambiguity or create a new presupposition. Expert editing sometimes needs a full finite clause rather than a compressed modifier. The objective is a defensible expression of the writer’s meaning, not obedience to a mechanical number-of-changes rule.\n\nsubject + linking verb + predicative complement: Attributes or identifies; check that the semantic relation is intended.\n\nreconstruct → compare meaning dimensions: Verification includes scope, time, modality and participants.\n\nThe reason for the delay was a missing permit.\nThe complement identifies a cause rather than an unrelated location.\n\nThe workshop was expensive because transport costs had risen.\nThe because-clause gives the cause of expense.\n\nThe board decided to suspend the scheme, but suspension had not yet begun.\nA decision is distinguished from its implementation.\n\nThe reason ... is because occurs in established English; prefer an economical alternative where useful, but do not label every occurrence inherently ungrammatical.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=22&section=gv5-d22-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Parallelism","url":"https://writingcenter.unc.edu/tips-and-tools/parallelism/"},{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:16:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Time, space and abstract relationships","text":"Time, space and abstract relationships\n\nA preposition commonly heads a phrase expressing a relationship; spatial meanings often extend to time, cause, means and other abstract relations.\n\nBegin with visible relations: a folder on the desk, a letter in the folder, a visitor at the gate. In often presents containment, on often presents contact with a surface, and at can locate an activity or point. These are useful meaning patterns, not a size-based rule that decides every case. A person may be at the office as a workplace or in the office as an interior. Direction contrasts with location: the child is in the garden, but runs into the garden. From identifies a source or starting point, while towards gives a direction without guaranteeing arrival.\n\nTime expressions have conventional patterns: at noon, on Tuesday, in October and in the evening. On Tuesday morning includes a named day and therefore differs from in the morning. At night is a conventional expression; during the night selects an interval. Since gives a starting point, for a duration, by a deadline and until an endpoint for continuation. I will send it by Friday promises completion no later than Friday; I will stay until Friday describes a continuing stay. These choices interact with tense and aspect, so a time word cannot substitute for understanding the event.\n\nAbstract uses include cause because of the delay, purpose for the examination, means by train, instrument with a key and relationship between two proposals. The same preposition can express different meanings, and alternative constructions may make related claims. Between is not restricted to exactly two: negotiations between the five named departments presents separate participants, whereas disagreement among the staff presents a group setting. By and with differ when an agent and an instrument are expressed: the cabinet was opened by the caretaker with a spare key. Learning the entire relation is more reliable than translating one preposition in isolation.\n\nSpatial perspectives permit legitimate alternatives: a bird in a tree and an apple on a tree select different relationships, not different sized locations. Abstract prepositions can be productive rather than arbitrary: under pressure and beyond the budget extend spatial schemas. Nevertheless, idiomatic selection limits prediction, and region or institutional convention may affect at the weekend versus on the weekend. Give the intended time span, route or relation before declaring an alternative wrong. In cumulative review, separate an aspect error from a preposition error: has lived here since March requires an appropriate verb phrase as well as since, and a past residence ending before now needs a different temporal frame.\n\nby + deadline: Completion occurs no later than the stated time.\n\nuntil + endpoint: A state or activity continues up to a boundary.\n\nThe parcel travelled from Quetta to Sukkur.\nFrom identifies source and to identifies destination.\n\nThe office will remain open until six.\nUntil marks the endpoint of a continuing state.\n\nThe agreement between the four laboratories covers shared equipment.\nBetween presents distinguishable participants; four does not make it ungrammatical.\n\nAt the weekend is a common British pattern; on the weekend is established American usage.\n\nBetween can relate more than two individually identified participants.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=12&section=gv5-d12-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Prepositions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositions_2"},{"publisher":"Present perfect simple ( I have worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-i-have-worked"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:16:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Prepositional phrases, complements and stranding","text":"Prepositional phrases, complements and stranding\n\nA prepositional phrase contains a preposition as head and, commonly, a complement; the complete phrase can perform several grammatical functions.\n\nIn beside the window, beside is the preposition and the window is its noun-phrase complement. The whole phrase can describe a noun, as in the desk beside the window, or locate an event, as in she waited beside the window. Pronouns normally use their object form after a preposition: with her, between you and me. The complement need not be one isolated word. A complex noun phrase such as the narrow window at the end of the hall remains a single complement of beside even though it contains another prepositional phrase.\n\nCommon complements include noun phrases, pronouns and -ing clauses: about the delay, with us, without checking the figures. Prepositions can also take other kinds of complement in particular patterns: from there has a place expression; until after the interview contains another prepositional phrase; surprised at what she wrote has a wh-clause. The familiar instruction use -ing after a preposition is useful when choosing an ordinary non-finite verbal complement, but it should not be expanded into the false claim that every complement is a gerund or that clauses can never follow a preposition.\n\nIn a question or relative construction, a preposition may remain at the end while its complement is understood earlier: Which bench did you sit on? This is preposition stranding and is normal standard English. On which bench did you sit? moves the preposition together with the question phrase, a pattern often called pied-piping. It is more formal and can sound natural in legal or academic contexts. Selection matters: we cannot freely separate parts of every idiom, and some fronted patterns are very unnatural. Where both versions are established, choose according to register and clarity rather than a blanket ban on sentence-final prepositions.\n\nFunction and category remain distinct. In the proposal on transport, the prepositional phrase postmodifies a noun; in interested in transport, it complements an adjective; in rely on transport, it belongs to the verb's lexical pattern. Modern grammatical frameworks differ in their classification of before in before lunch and before she left: some keep a broader preposition category, while many teaching grammars call the finite-clause use a conjunction. This course names the framework where it matters and always identifies the complement and function. A change of label must not turn an otherwise acceptable construction into an alleged error.\n\npreposition + noun phrase / -ing clause / licensed wh-clause: The complement type depends on the preposition and construction.\n\nwh-phrase ... preposition / preposition + wh-phrase ...: Stranding and pied-piping are alternative question patterns where licensed.\n\nThe folder beside the printer is empty.\nBeside the printer postmodifies folder; beside is its head.\n\nWe left without checking the figures.\nWithout takes an -ing clause as complement.\n\nThe question on which the panel disagreed concerned funding.\nOn which is a fronted prepositional phrase inside the relative clause.\n\nPrepositions license several complement types; at what she wrote is a genuine wh-clause complement.\n\nFrameworks may call before she left a subordinating conjunction pattern or a preposition with a clause complement; the label must be stated consistently.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=12&section=gv5-d12-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Prepositional phrases","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositional-phrases"},{"publisher":"Prepositions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositions_2"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:16:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Lexical selection, fixed combinations and meaning","text":"Lexical selection, fixed combinations and meaning\n\nLexical selection is the requirement or preference of a particular word for a complement pattern, including a specific preposition where its meaning requires one.\n\nSome verb patterns are straightforward only after the whole expression is learned: depend on a colleague, listen to a recording, discuss a problem. Discuss takes a direct object in the ordinary meaning illustrated here; listen introduces the thing heard with to. Do not infer that one pattern applies to every synonym. Explain a problem to someone differs from tell someone about a problem. A useful dictionary entry records the word's sense, the types of complement it permits, and examples of the actual construction. Keep a pattern notebook using complete short sentences, not an undifferentiated list of supposedly interchangeable prepositions.\n\nAdjectives and nouns select patterns too: interested in a subject, responsible for a decision, an increase in costs and a solution to a problem. Some words allow alternatives with overlapping meanings or different emphases; a dictionary, not a simplistic single-partner slogan, should resolve a doubtful case. Insist on a receipt and insist that the receipt be issued are both grammatical. The first contains a prepositional complement; the second contains a content clause. Insist does not always need on. Likewise, enter the room and enter into an agreement use different senses and should not be collapsed into one prohibition.\n\nThe spelling to belongs to different constructions. In hope to leave, it marks an infinitive. In look forward to leaving, it is a preposition within a lexical pattern and takes an -ing complement. Be used to describes accustomedness and differs from used to for a past habit: she is used to cycling versus she used to cycle. No visual shortcut based solely on the word to can decide the structure. Look at the governing expression and the intended meaning. A correct form may still make the wrong claim: used to work does not by itself say that work continues now.\n\nLexical grammar overlaps vocabulary, syntax and register. Different from is an appropriate British editorial default; different than is established in American English, while different to occurs especially in British speech. Formal preference does not justify scoring a standard regional alternative as universally wrong. In review, explain the selected sense and its complement, then compare a close alternative. This is particularly useful when a first language distributes relations differently, including for many Urdu-speaking learners, but no learner should be presumed to make the same error because of background. Record personal evidence from actual writing and practice, and revisit patterns that genuinely cause difficulty.\n\ninsist on + noun phrase / -ing clause: Demand or maintain something in an on-pattern.\n\ninsist that + content clause: State insistence through a proposition or mandative clause.\n\nWe discussed the revised timetable.\nDiscuss takes the timetable directly in the intended sense.\n\nRida insisted that the minutes were accurate.\nThe content clause expresses the proposition she maintained; no on is needed.\n\nThe team is used to working remotely but used to meet in person.\nAccustomedness takes -ing; the past-habit construction takes a base infinitive.\n\nInsist also takes content clauses, so insist always takes on is false.\n\nA verb's other sense can permit another pattern: enter a hall versus enter into negotiations.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=12&section=gv5-d12-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Prepositions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositions_2"},{"publisher":"Prepositional phrases","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositional-phrases"},{"publisher":"Verb patterns: verb + infinitive or verb + -ing?","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verb-patterns-verb-infinitive-or-verb-ing"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:16:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Particles, multi-word verbs and cumulative review","text":"Particles, multi-word verbs and cumulative review\n\nMulti-word verbs combine a verb with a particle, preposition or both; their object-placement patterns reveal important differences.\n\nIn turn off the lamp, off is a particle in a phrasal verb. With this separable pattern, an ordinary noun object can precede or follow the particle: turn the lamp off and turn off the lamp. A personal-pronoun object normally comes between: turn it off. This placement is not available with look after a child: after introduces its complement, so look the child after is not the ordinary care-for construction. Learn whether a particular expression is transitive or intransitive as well as whether it separates; the bus broke down needs no object, whereas someone may break down a calculation into parts.\n\nTraditional teaching often calls all multi-word expressions phrasal verbs, but a narrower classification distinguishes three patterns. A phrasal verb has a particle, such as give up; a prepositional verb has a selected preposition, such as rely on; a phrasal-prepositional verb has both, such as put up with. In put up with the noise, the noun complement follows with. The whole expression means tolerate, but its meaning is not obtained by translating each word separately. Dictionaries vary in their labels, so check the actual object placement shown by their examples rather than treating a broad label as a complete rule.\n\nFormality belongs to individual expressions and contexts. Carry out an investigation and account for an increase are entirely at home in formal writing, although put off may be less formal than postpone. A single-word substitute must preserve meaning: give up a habit, give up a seat and give up hope do not all invite the same replacement. Longer noun objects often favour position after a particle because it is easier to process the verb sequence before a heavy phrase. Personal-pronoun placement remains the useful elementary diagnostic for many separable particle verbs, but idiomatic restrictions still require lexical checking.\n\nThe cumulative tasks revisit past narrative sequence, perfect/progressive meanings, future reference, agreement and degree from Days 7–11. Separate first-encounter recognition from an immediate retry: seeing the same item again can measure recall of that item without demonstrating transfer to a new sentence. For each error, name the underlying distinction before choosing a review lesson. A perfect-aspect error in has been waiting since noon should link to aspect and temporal reference, not merely be counted as a generic preposition mistake. Open-response explanations and short corrections supply production evidence through self-review; a displayed model is an aid to analysis, not an independent automated grade.\n\nseparable verb + noun + particle / verb + particle + noun: Both positions occur with an ordinary noun object where the lexical pattern permits.\n\nseparable verb + personal pronoun + particle: Ordinary placement: turn it off, not turn off it.\n\nPlease turn the reading lamp off.\nThe noun object precedes the separable particle.\n\nPlease turn it off before leaving.\nThe personal pronoun appears between verb and particle.\n\nWe could not put up with the noise from the generator.\nPut up with is phrasal-prepositional; the complement follows with.\n\nSome phrasal verbs are intransitive: the generator broke down.\n\nA formally acceptable multi-word verb need not be replaced by a single word merely to look academic.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=12&section=gv5-d12-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Phrasal verbs and multi-word verbs","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/phrasal-verbs-and-multi-word-verbs"},{"publisher":"Prepositions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/prepositions_2"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:17:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Non-finite forms and grammatical functions","text":"Non-finite forms and grammatical functions\n\nA non-finite verb form lacks the ordinary independent tense/person agreement contrasts of a finite verb. Non-finite clauses may nevertheless express complex time, aspect, voice and participant relationships through their form and larger context.\n\nBegin with four useful shapes: to write, write after an appropriate selecting expression, writing and written. In The clerk hopes to write, hopes is the finite verb and to write is an infinitival complement. In The clerk may write, may is finite and write is the following plain form. In The clerk is writing, is is finite and writing is a participial form. Written combines with have in a perfect or be in a passive: has written; was written. The spelling alone is not a complete analysis, because many past-tense and participial forms are identical, as in checked.\n\nAn -ing clause can fill several functions: Reading the instructions carefully takes time has a subject clause; She enjoys reading the instructions has a complement; Reading the instructions carefully, she noticed an exception has an adjunct. Traditional teaching often calls the first two gerunds and the last a participle. Some modern grammars use the combined label gerund-participial for the verbal form. Neither terminology makes every -ing word a verb. In the careful reading of the instructions, reading is a noun with a determiner and adjective; in reading the instructions carefully, the verb takes a direct object and adverb.\n\nBuild longer forms by applying ordinary auxiliary selection. To have written is a perfect infinitive; to be written is passive; to have been written combines perfect and passive. Having written is a perfect -ing form, and having been written is perfect passive. These forms have no independently inflected tense, but the perfect commonly locates a situation before a contextual reference point. The committee claims to have checked the files attributes the earlier checking as a claim rather than independently guaranteeing its truth. A negative can precede the non-finite phrase: not to sign; not having signed.\n\nA split infinitive places an intervening expression between infinitival to and the plain verb: to carefully compare the estimates. This is a grammatical option in contemporary standard English, not an automatic error. Adverb placement can affect attachment and emphasis. In The team decided to immediately suspend the trial, immediately clearly modifies suspend; The team immediately decided to suspend the trial can instead locate the decision. Do not move an adverb merely to obey an absolute prohibition if that move changes the intended meaning. Some editors prefer an unsplit form where it is equally clear and natural. Follow a specified house style where necessary, while distinguishing its preference from a universal rule of grammar.\n\nPresent participle and past participle are traditional form names, not automatic time labels. The files being checked tomorrow refers to future checking even though being is an -ing form; the files checked yesterday has past time because the context supplies it. A non-finite clause may contain auxiliaries, an object and other dependants; it is not merely a loose phrase with no internal syntax. Conversely, a plain verb form does not prove non-finiteness in every context: an imperative such as Write clearly is a main-clause construction. Determine the clause type and selecting environment before assigning a form or function label.\n\nto + have + been + lexical past participle: A perfect passive infinitive; the larger context supplies the reference point.\n\n-ing verb + object + adverb: A verbal -ing clause can retain ordinary verb complementation and modification.\n\nThe researcher plans to interview the witnesses.\nPlans is finite; to interview the witnesses is an infinitival complement.\n\nCarefully checking the figures revealed a discrepancy.\nThe whole -ing clause functions as subject; checking takes figures as object and carefully as modifier.\n\nThe letters are believed to have been destroyed.\nThe perfect passive infinitive presents destruction as earlier than the attributed belief; the belief is reported, not proved.\n\nTraditional gerund/participle labels and modern gerund-participial terminology analyse the same many verbal forms differently. Identify the observable pattern before arguing about the label. Irregular forms need lexical checking: written is a participle; wrote is a past-tense form.\n\nA split infinitive is not inherently ungrammatical. Register, rhythm, scope and a specified editorial style may affect which adverb position is preferred.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=20&section=gv5-d20-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"},{"publisher":"Infinitives with and without to - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/infinitives-with-and-without-to"},{"publisher":"Perfect infinitive with to ( to have worked )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/perfect-infinitive-with-to-to-have-worked"},{"publisher":"Is it OK to split an infinitive? — MLA Style Center","url":"https://style.mla.org/split-infinitives/"},{"publisher":"Split infinitive — Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/split-infinitive"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:17:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Verb complementation and differences in meaning","text":"Verb complementation and differences in meaning\n\nVerb complementation concerns the patterns licensed by a particular verb and its sense. An -ing complement and an infinitival complement may be restricted alternatives or may express different meanings.\n\nLearn a verb together with a complete pattern: avoid handling fragile objects; hope to finish; ask someone to wait. Avoid normally takes an -ing activity complement; hope normally takes a to-infinitive in this use. An object may belong to the pattern: The supervisor persuaded the clerk to wait. Leaving out or inserting a preposition by analogy can change or break the selected construction. To is not always infinitival: in look forward to meeting, to is a preposition followed by an -ing clause, whereas in hope to meet it marks an infinitive.\n\nSeveral verbs allow both forms with an important ordinary contrast. Remember to lock the cabinet concerns remembering a task before performing it; remember locking the cabinet concerns a memory of the locking. Stop to read the notice means pause another activity in order to read; stop reading means cease the reading activity. Try to restart the computer describes an effort; try restarting the computer proposes restarting as a method to see whether it helps. Neither try-pattern alone guarantees success. Go on checking continues checking; go on to check introduces a subsequent activity in an appropriate sequence.\n\nPerception verbs can license an object plus a bare infinitive or an -ing form: I saw the boat cross the channel; I saw the boat crossing the channel. The first often presents the event as a whole; the second presents a stage in progress. This contrast should not be exaggerated into a rule that the second event definitely failed to finish. Active causative make and let also normally take a bare infinitive: made us wait; let us leave. Passive made normally introduces to: we were made to wait. Help permits both help us carry and help us to carry. These are selected constructions, not exceptions to be erased by a single formula.\n\nOther pairs differ mainly in viewpoint or context: like swimming and like to swim can both be standard, and preferences vary with meaning and variety. Some patterns allow an explicit non-finite subject, while others require interpretation through the surrounding clause. A dictionary entry helps establish the complement frame; it does not remove the need to read the intended sense. When transforming a sentence, preserve participant roles, aspect and whether an event actually happened. Do not turn remembered to send into remembered sending merely because both phrases contain the same lexical verb: the memory relationship and the assumed event sequence have changed.\n\navoid + -ing; hope + to-infinitive: Selected complement patterns for these ordinary senses.\n\nstop + -ing / stop + to-infinitive: Ceasing the activity contrasts with pausing for a purpose.\n\nWe avoided disturbing the residents.\nThe -ing clause is the selected activity complement of avoided.\n\nMina stopped to compare the two maps.\nThe infinitive expresses the purpose of pausing another activity.\n\nThe mechanic saw the belt slipping but did not see it break.\nThe -ing form presents an unfolding stage; the bare infinitive presents the breaking event as a whole in the negative perception report.\n\nDo not treat every pair as sharply different in the same way. Like reading and like to read overlap, while stop reading and stop to read usually have a substantial structural and meaning contrast. Help permits an infinitive with or without to.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=20&section=gv5-d20-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Verb patterns: verb + infinitive or verb + -ing?","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/verb-patterns-verb-infinitive-or-verb-ing"},{"publisher":"Infinitives with and without to - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/infinitives-with-and-without-to"},{"publisher":"Help somebody (to) do","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/help-somebody-to-do"},{"publisher":"Make","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/make"},{"publisher":"Let, let&rsquo;s","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/let-let-s"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:17:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Purpose, participial clauses and meaning-preserving reduction","text":"Purpose, participial clauses and meaning-preserving reduction\n\nA non-finite adjunct adds information such as purpose, time, reason or circumstance. Participial and infinitival clauses can make writing economical, provided their interpreted subject and their relationship to the main clause remain clear.\n\nAn infinitive can express purpose: The librarian moved the lamp to illuminate the desk. The purpose is not simply a second completed event; it explains the intended reason for moving the lamp. In order to can make that relation explicit. An -ing adjunct often shares the main subject: Checking the inventory, the librarian found a missing volume. The librarian is understood as the checker. A past-participial adjunct often gives a passive interpretation: Warned about the leak, the librarian moved the books. The librarian receives the warning rather than issuing it.\n\nPerfect participial clauses can make earlier completion explicit: Having checked the inventory, the librarian locked the store. A passive perfect is also possible: Having been warned about the leak, she moved the books. The grammatical form helps organise time and voice, but the exact logical link often comes from context. Warned about the leak may express background, reason or a concessive relation in a suitable sentence; do not translate every participial clause automatically as because. A reduced relative also needs its own interpretation: the letters written yesterday has a passive relation between letters and write, while the researcher writing today presents the researcher as the writer.\n\nA dangling adjunct arises when the understood participant is missing or misleadingly attached under the intended reading: Having inspected the roof, the leak was obvious does not clearly express who inspected it. If Samir inspected the roof, write After Samir inspected the roof, the leak was obvious to him. Replacing the main clause with Samir found the leak obvious is another valid repair. A passive main clause is not automatically incompatible with an opening participle: Warned about the leak, the tenants were evacuated is coherent because the tenants received the warning. State the intended actor before diagnosing an error.\n\nNot every opening non-finite expression shares the main subject. Absolute constructions have their own subject, as in The checks completed, the team left. Comment expressions such as strictly speaking relate to the speaker’s assessment rather than an ordinary main-clause agent. Purpose clauses can also have implicit agents recoverable in context, especially with passive main clauses; clarity, not an unconditional ban, guides editing. Reduction may remove tense, modality or emphasis: the forms that must be checked cannot simply become the forms checked without losing necessity. A sound transformation therefore compares what each version actually commits the writer to, including the role of every participant and every qualification.\n\nhaving + past participle, main clause: A perfect -ing adjunct can make prior completion explicit.\n\nnoun + past-participial modifier: Often a reduced passive relative, subject to lexical meaning and context.\n\nThe clerk opened the window to cool the room.\nThe infinitive states an intended purpose; it does not guarantee that the room cooled.\n\nHaving compared both estimates, Samir requested a third.\nSamir is understood as the person who compared the estimates.\n\nThe folders damaged in the flood were photographed before disposal.\nDamaged in the flood modifies folders with a passive interpretation; it does not make folders the agents of damage.\n\nAn absolute such as Weather permitting, we will inspect the site has an explicit subject inside the opening clause. A comment expression such as frankly speaking is not evaluated by blindly assigning its speaking to the next noun.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=20&section=gv5-d20-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Participle clauses","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/free-resources/grammar/c1/participle-clauses"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"},{"publisher":"Perfect infinitive with to ( to have worked )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/perfect-infinitive-with-to-to-have-worked"},{"publisher":"Infinitives with and without to - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/infinitives-with-and-without-to"},{"publisher":"Nominative absolute, Merriam-Webster","url":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/nominative%20absolute"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:17:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Control, raising and the interpretation of non-finite subjects","text":"Control, raising and the interpretation of non-finite subjects\n\nInterpreting a non-finite clause often requires identifying its expressed or understood subject. Control and raising are analytical labels for important differences between constructions; their formal treatment varies between grammatical theories.\n\nAsk who is understood to perform the action. In Amina tried to unlock the cabinet, Amina is the person trying and the intended unlocker. In Amina persuaded Omar to unlock the cabinet, Omar is the intended unlocker, while Amina is the persuader. These are ordinary subject-control and object-control examples in traditional terminology. The first noun in the sentence does not control every following infinitive, and the nearest noun is not a universal answer either. Learn the selecting verb’s pattern and read the whole context before interpreting the missing participant.\n\nSome infinitives express their own subject: It would be useful for Omar to attend identifies Omar as the attendee. Others allow a general or contextual subject: To leave now would be unwise may concern us, another salient group, or people in general, depending on context. With a control verb, participant roles can remain stable under a main-clause passive: Amina persuaded Omar to attend; Omar was persuaded by Amina to attend. Omar is still understood as the attendee. The surface object/subject position changes, so a role-based explanation is more reliable than an instruction to select whichever noun appears immediately before to.\n\nRaising constructions behave differently. In The parcel seems to have arrived, the parcel is the understood subject of arrive, but seem does not describe an action performed by the parcel. Compare It seems that the parcel has arrived. Dummy there supplies a useful diagnostic: There seems to be a problem is grammatical even though there names no person who seems. A normal agentive control construction such as try does not simply accept dummy there in that way. The contrast concerns the intended dummy interpretation, not every possible use of the word there, which can also be an ordinary place expression.\n\nIn one established generative account, control involves an unpronounced subject conventionally written PRO, whose interpretation is supplied by a controller or context. Raising instead relates the visible subject to a position associated with the lower predicate; seem does not assign it the additional participant role that try assigns its subject. These are theoretical analyses, not observable extra words to insert into finished prose, and other frameworks describe the dependency differently. Diagnostics should be used together with meaning and lexical selection, not as one automatic test for every adjective or verb. A further pattern illustrates the limits of a simple subject rule: in The report is easy to read, report is understood as the object of read, whereas in Amina is eager to read, Amina is the understood reader. Such differences make careful syntactic analysis useful, but a beginner can begin by asking who does what and which complement pattern is licensed.\n\nsubject + try + to-infinitive: A standard subject-control example: the subject is also the intended performer in the complement.\n\nThere + seem + to be + noun phrase: An established raising pattern with dummy there.\n\nNora tried to repair the hinge.\nNora is both the person trying and the intended repairer.\n\nNora persuaded Imran to repair the hinge.\nImran is understood as the repairer; Nora is the persuader.\n\nThere seem to be several errors in the total.\nDummy there has no semantic agent role; the plural associate supports seem in this careful formal version.\n\nThe subject of an infinitive can be explicit after for, contextually understood, controlled by a main-clause participant, or involved in another dependency. Do not teach that every infinitive has the subject of its nearest finite clause.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=20&section=gv5-d20-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Control (L322), Simon Fraser University","url":"https://www.sfu.ca/person/dearmond/322/322.control.htm"},{"publisher":"Seem","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/seem"},{"publisher":"Clauses: finite and non-finite","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-finite-and-non-finite"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:18:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Adjective classes, positions and ordered description","text":"Adjective classes, positions and ordered description\n\nAn adjective is a word class with characteristic modifying and predicative uses; no single meaning or suffix identifies every member.\n\nStart with a small contrast: a quiet room and the room is quiet. In the first, quiet is an attributive modifier inside a noun phrase. In the second, it is a predicative complement after a linking verb. Both describe the room, but they occupy different structures. An adjective phrase may contain only its head adjective or additional words: very quiet, unusually quiet for this building. A noun used before another noun remains a noun in many modern analyses: stone in a stone wall names material but need not become an adjective merely because it modifies wall.\n\nAdjectives express many kinds of meaning: evaluative excellent, descriptive narrow, relational medical and participial exhausted or convincing. Participial adjectives resemble verb participles; the surrounding construction helps identify their use. The exhausted nurse needed rest describes a state, while the long shift had exhausted the nurse describes an event with a verbal object. Attributive adjective order reflects meaning: an attractive small wooden desk is neutral, with opinion before size before material. This is a preference shaped by meaning and grouping, not a mechanical requirement to fill nine slots before every noun.\n\nSome adjectives favour particular positions. Asleep normally follows a linking verb: the child is asleep, rather than an asleep child. Mere normally precedes its noun: a mere suggestion. Available can occur before or after the noun with small differences of information structure: available rooms and the rooms available tonight. Complements also affect placement: a room suitable for interviews is more natural than a suitable for interviews room. Adjectives ending in -ed often describe the affected experiencer, and -ing forms often describe a cause, but bored/boring is a lexical pattern to learn rather than a universal translation rule.\n\nPostpositive means positioned after the head noun within its phrase, not necessarily a separate clause complement. Something useful has postpositive useful; the proposal is useful has a predicative complement. Coordination and adjective grouping affect punctuation: a clear, practical explanation permits two independent evaluations, whereas a dark green folder contains a colour unit. Order can change deliberately when a writer contrasts wooden rather than metal or old rather than new. Relational adjectives may acquire evaluative readings: a very political decision concerns degree of political motivation, although the same adjective can classify a subject without degree. Analyse the actual sense and structure together.\n\ndeterminer + adjective phrase + noun: A common attributive pattern: an unusually quiet room.\n\nsubject + linking verb + adjective phrase: A predicative description: the room became quiet.\n\nThe soup is hot.\nHot is an adjective functioning as subject complement, not an object.\n\nThey reserved a small circular table.\nSize precedes shape in a neutral description.\n\nThe documents available after the hearing included a revised timetable.\nAvailable after the hearing postmodifies documents within the subject noun phrase.\n\nPosition is lexical: asleep is normally predicative, while mere is normally attributive.\n\nAn adjective's figurative or evaluative sense may be gradable even when its classifying sense is not.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=11&section=gv5-d11-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Adjectives: order","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adjectives-order"},{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Adjectives","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adjectives_2"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:18:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Degree, comparison and sense-dependent gradability","text":"Degree, comparison and sense-dependent gradability\n\nDegree constructions locate a quality on a scale or compare it with a contextual standard; the scale depends on the adjective's sense.\n\nA gradable adjective can describe different amounts of a quality: fairly warm, very warm, warmer. A comparative relates two values or groups; a superlative identifies an extreme within a comparison set. Form is partly lexical. Short adjectives commonly take -er and -est, with spelling adjustments such as large/larger and happy/happier. Many longer adjectives use more and most. Good/better/best and bad/worse/worst are irregular. Do not add more to an already comparative form for an ordinary comparison: more colder duplicates the degree marking. Check a dictionary when a two-syllable adjective allows competing forms.\n\nComparisons require a recoverable standard: this route is shorter than the coastal route. As ... as expresses equality against a standard; not as ... as denies that equality. A comparative clause may be reduced because repeated material is understood: the office is quieter than it was yesterday. When comparing quantities associated with different places, make the intended objects clear: the district's budget is smaller than the city's, rather than a budget being smaller than a city. A clause can itself supply an amount: our revenue is less than what the branch collected. Matching surface word classes is not a universal requirement.\n\nIntensifiers increase a degree, while downtoners reduce it: extremely helpful, rather difficult, slightly cheaper. Their compatibility differs: much cheaper, not normally very cheaper; absolutely impossible, rather than an ordinary scale reading of very impossible. Some adjectives have an endpoint meaning, such as complete meaning having all parts, but a related sense may allow comparison: a more complete account can mean one covering more information. Unique meaning the only example of its kind resists degree logically; unique meaning unusual can be graded in established usage. In precise formal work, choose a wording that makes the intended sense explicit.\n\nComparison involves grammar and interpretation. One of the better applications selects from a relatively strong group; one of the best applications makes a stronger ranking claim. Neither pattern requires the other. The better of two is the traditional formal default, while the best of two is common in informal standard usage. Comparative complements may be phrases or clauses, and than me is an ordinary alternative to the explicit than I am when meaning permits it. A sentence such as Kim likes Noor more than Ali is ambiguous between people liked and people doing the liking; repeating a verb or object resolves the comparison rather than merely changing a pronoun form.\n\nmore/less + adjective + than + standard: Compare a quality using a recoverable standard.\n\nas + adjective + as + standard: Express equality, with not as ... as expressing a lower degree.\n\nThis path is narrower than the track beside the canal.\nNarrower marks one comparison; than introduces its standard.\n\nHer explanation was much clearer than mine.\nMuch modifies a comparative; mine stands for my explanation.\n\nThe revised estimate is higher than we expected it to be.\nThe standard is supplied by a finite comparative clause with understood degree.\n\nFormal prose often prefers the better of two; informal standard usage also has the best of two.\n\nGradability belongs to a sense in context. A more complete account need not claim that a fully complete object becomes more than complete.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=11&section=gv5-d11-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Comparison: adjectives (bigger, biggest, more interesting)","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/comparison-adjectives-bigger-biggest-more-interesting"},{"publisher":"Comparison: clauses (bigger than we had imagined)","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/comparison-clauses-bigger-than-we-had-imagined"},{"publisher":"Unique: Usage Guide","url":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/unique"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:18:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Adverb forms, phrases, placement and local scope","text":"Adverb forms, phrases, placement and local scope\n\nAn adverb phrase is headed by an adverb; it may modify an event, an adjective, another adverb, or a larger unit, depending on its use.\n\nMany manner adverbs have an adjective plus -ly form: careful becomes carefully. The ending is a useful clue, not a definition. Fast, hard and late can be adverbs without -ly; friendly and costly are commonly adjectives. Ask what construction the word participates in. She spoke calmly describes the speaking, whereas she seemed calm characterises the subject through a linking verb. A linking verb can also take a noun phrase or other complement, so this contrast should not become a claim that every word after be must be an adjective. The whole clause determines the relation.\n\nAdverbs can express manner, place, time, frequency and degree, among other meanings. An adverb phrase may contain its own modifier: very slowly, quite unexpectedly. Adverbials are functions, not a single word class: at noon is a prepositional phrase functioning as a time adjunct, while yesterday can do similar work as an adverb. Neutral frequency adverbs often precede a lexical verb, follow main-verb be, or follow the first auxiliary: she usually walks; she is usually early; she has usually arrived by nine. These are common placements, not the only grammatical possibilities in every context.\n\nSome related forms have separate meanings. Hard means with effort or force, but hardly means almost not. Late concerns a time later than expected; lately means recently. Near concerns closeness; nearly means almost. Well commonly describes successful performance, while good is an adjective; well can also be a predicative adjective meaning healthy. Placement may change interpretation or information focus. They deliberately did not reply describes a deliberate decision not to reply; they did not deliberately reply can deny intention while leaving open whether a reply occurred. Do not diagnose scope from the nearest word alone.\n\nDistinguish an adverb's lexical category from its clause-level function and semantic scope. An adverb can modify a verb phrase while taking a broader proposition as its interpretive domain. Fronting, parenthetical position and stress create marked readings: repeatedly she had asked for a meeting foregrounds recurrence, while she had repeatedly asked is more neutral. A long object may make an intervening manner adverb acceptable even when a short-object version sounds awkward. Analyse acceptability in context rather than replacing every unconventional position automatically. When revising, state the intended frequency, manner or scope first, then choose an order that expresses it clearly.\n\nsubject + frequency adverb + lexical verb: A common neutral position: she often travels.\n\nsubject + first auxiliary + frequency adverb + remaining verb phrase: A common neutral position: she has often travelled.\n\nThe train arrived late.\nLate is an adverb here; no -ly ending is needed.\n\nThe train has rarely arrived before sunrise.\nRarely follows the first auxiliary and expresses frequency.\n\nThey did not deliberately omit the appendix.\nNegation can deny deliberate intention; it does not by itself prove whether the omission occurred.\n\nWell can be an adjective meaning healthy: I feel well today.\n\nFrequency and manner adverbs have neutral positions, but emphasis and constituent length permit alternatives.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=11&section=gv5-d11-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Word classes and phrase classes - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/word-classes-and-phrase-classes"},{"publisher":"Comparison: adjectives (bigger, biggest, more interesting)","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/comparison-adjectives-bigger-biggest-more-interesting"},{"publisher":"Also, as well or too?","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/also-as-well-or-too"},{"publisher":"Adverbs","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adverbs_2"},{"publisher":"Adverbs and adverb phrases: position","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adverbs-and-adverb-phrases-position"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:18:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Focus, sentence adverbials and responsible interpretation","text":"Focus, sentence adverbials and responsible interpretation\n\nA focusing adverb highlights alternatives; a sentence adverbial comments on a whole proposition, its likelihood, or the speaker's relation to it.\n\nOnly, even and also guide a listener toward relevant alternatives. Only Leena signed says that no other relevant person signed. Leena signed only the form contrasts that object with other things she might have signed. Even Leena signed presents her participation as surprising against an expectation. These meanings depend on a context: surprising to whom, and compared with which alternatives? When a question offers two plausible positions without supplying that context, more than one answer may be grammatical. Your task as a writer is to make the intended comparison easy to recover, not to treat every other reading as impossible.\n\nOnly often occupies normal mid-position even when its focus is later: Leena only signed two forms can mean that two was the limit. Moving it nearer the focus can improve precision in an ambiguous written context, but adjacency is not a universal syntax rule. Also can add a new action by the same person, another participant in the same action, or another related fact. A preceding sentence usually determines which reading is relevant. In deliberate editing, write the intended alternative in a margin note, then check whether a reader has enough context to find that interpretation.\n\nSentence adverbials express stance or organise discourse: probably, fortunately, frankly and therefore do different jobs. Probably qualifies certainty; fortunately evaluates an event; frankly comments on the manner of speaking; therefore presents a conclusion. Compare Fortunately, the files survived with The files were stored fortunately, where end placement makes a manner interpretation awkward. Parenthetical commas often help separate a comment from the proposition, but punctuation follows structure and intended reading. Conjunctive adverbs do not independently license joining two full clauses by a comma: the server failed; therefore, the session ended is a standard written pattern.\n\nScope can interact with negation, quantification and presupposed alternatives. Not all applicants replied denies universal response; all applicants did not reply may be interpreted in more than one way, so an explicit none or some did not is clearer. Even presupposes a scale of expectation in many uses but does not tell us a measurable probability. An evaluative adverb can comment on an entire event without describing how an agent acted: surprisingly, the silent room was useful does not mean anyone acted surprisingly. Advanced analysis should identify these distinctions without pretending that every conversational inference is encoded as a rigid grammatical rule.\n\nonly + focused subject + predicate: Exclude relevant alternative subjects.\n\nsentence adverbial, + clause: A frequent pattern for an explicit comment on the proposition.\n\nOnly Farah checked the totals.\nThe exclusion concerns other relevant checkers.\n\nFarah also checked the totals after verifying the dates.\nThe context makes also add a second checking activity.\n\nThe totals were probably wrong; nevertheless, the estimate remained useful for planning.\nProbably qualifies confidence in the first claim; nevertheless marks concession between claims.\n\nMid-position only can focus later material. Context and stress help identify its scope.\n\nA sentence adverbial may be mobile, but mobility does not make every punctuation pattern equivalent.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=11&section=gv5-d11-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Only","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/only"},{"publisher":"Also, as well or too?","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/also-as-well-or-too"},{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:19:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Coordination and subordination: what is connected","text":"Coordination and subordination: what is connected\n\nCoordination places units alongside one another at the same structural level; subordination makes one clause dependent within a larger structure.\n\nStart by finding the units being joined. In tea and coffee, and joins two nouns. In unusually calm and very patient, it joins two adjective phrases. In The alarm sounded and the staff left, it joins clauses. A clause normally contains a predicate; the second clause may repeat a subject or share material with the first. Subordination is different: because the alarm sounded gives a reason inside a larger sentence. Neither arrangement is inherently more sophisticated; choose the structure that represents the intended relation.\n\nCoordinators include and, or and but; traditional school lists also include for, nor, so and yet. Their distributions are not identical. And can combine many kinds of unit, while causal for is mainly a clause connector in relatively formal writing. A subordinator such as although or if introduces a dependent clause. A sentence may contain both: Although the alarm sounded, the staff waited and the manager checked the corridor. First identify the outer concession, then the coordination inside the main part.\n\nShared material avoids unnecessary repetition: The librarian checked and stamped the forms shares a subject and an object across coordinated predicates. Repetition is sometimes useful for emphasis or disambiguation. Coordination does not demand that every item have the same spelling or length. Even different phrase categories can perform compatible functions: The room was quiet and without windows coordinates an adjective phrase and a prepositional phrase as predicative descriptions. The editing question is whether each item fits the surrounding construction and the intended meaning.\n\nGrammatical frameworks differ over the membership of the conjunction category and the analysis of clause-linking so or temporal after. The course uses familiar school labels while keeping category separate from function. Brackets reveal hierarchy: [Mina checked the register] and [Omar locked the door] has two coordinated clauses; Mina [checked the register and locked the door] has coordinated predicates. These descriptions explain structure without requiring a claim that every coordinated unit is an independent sentence. A repeated subject, a finite verb and the wider context help decide the analysis.\n\nX and Y: Both units contribute at the same structural level.\n\nalthough + clause, main clause: The main assertion holds despite a contrasting circumstance.\n\nThe workshop supplies paper and ink.\nTwo noun heads form the direct-object coordination.\n\nThe workshop was inexpensive but exceptionally useful.\nBut coordinates two predicative adjective phrases.\n\nAlthough the hall was small, the speakers arrived early and the audience stayed.\nOne subordinate concession precedes two coordinated main clauses.\n\nUnlike categories can coordinate when their functions are compatible: calm and in control are both predicative descriptions. Surface uniformity is a useful style preference, not an exceptionless syntax law.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=16&section=gv5-d16-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"},{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:19:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Correlative patterns, agreement and balanced alternatives","text":"Correlative patterns, agreement and balanced alternatives\n\nCorrelative expressions distribute a relationship across two positions, such as both … and or either … or; their placement identifies what is being paired.\n\nBoth the receptionist and the technician attended includes two people. Either the receptionist or the technician will attend presents alternatives. Neither attended excludes both in that context. Not only adds emphasis to an inclusion: The visit was not only useful but also inexpensive. Place the first marker before the first intended unit, then make sure that the second unit can complete the same surrounding construction. Read the whole meaning before editing, because moving either or not only can change which alternatives are being offered.\n\nA subject coordinated with and normally takes plural agreement: Both the map and the timetable are useful. A single package or conventional combination can instead receive singular notional agreement: Bread and butter is all that is available. With either … or and neither … nor, mixed singular and plural subjects create variation and possible awkwardness. Agreement with the nearer subject is a common editorial solution: Either the assistant or the supervisors are attending. Recasting often gives a clearer result than treating proximity as a universal linguistic law.\n\nFronted not only normally triggers subject–auxiliary inversion when it introduces a clause: Not only did the team finish early, but it also documented the process. Within a predicate no such inversion is needed: The team not only finished early but also documented the process. Also is optional in some not only … but constructions. Correlative markers do not make every imperfectly symmetrical sentence ungrammatical; what matters first is whether the coordinated units fit the same syntactic position and whether the contrast is intelligible.\n\nNegation and scope require careful reading. Neither the manager nor the deputy signed says that each failed to sign; Not both signed excludes joint success but allows one signature. Either in an ordinary choice often suggests one alternative rather than both, but inclusive or is possible where context allows it. An instruction should say whether multiple selections are permitted. In formal editing, preserve these logical differences rather than replacing all negative or alternative expressions mechanically. Grammatical agreement, logical inclusion and stylistic balance are related questions that should be diagnosed separately.\n\nboth X and Y: Includes both coordinated alternatives.\n\nnot both X and Y: Denies their joint inclusion; it does not necessarily exclude each separately.\n\nBoth the labels and the seals are missing.\nPlural coordination takes are.\n\nEither the supervisor or the two assistants are available.\nThe nearer plural encourages are; recasting avoids mixed-number awkwardness.\n\nNot only has the archive expanded, but its catalogue has also become searchable.\nFronting licenses has before the subject; the second clause states an additional improvement.\n\nSome conventional and-coordinations describe one unit and take singular agreement. If mixed-number or-coordination sounds strained, use two clauses rather than force a contested agreement choice.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=16&section=gv5-d16-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"},{"publisher":"Not only … but also","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/not-only-but-also"},{"publisher":"Subject–verb agreement - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/subject-verb-agreement"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:19:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Logic: addition, contrast, concession and qualification","text":"Logic: addition, contrast, concession and qualification\n\nA logical connector signals the relationship a writer intends between propositions; grammatical form alone does not prove that the relationship is factually justified.\n\nAddition presents another relevant point: The room is accessible; moreover, it has hearing assistance. Contrast presents a difference: The morning group worked online, whereas the evening group met in person. Concession goes further: Although the room was small, everyone found a seat presents the second fact as holding against an expectation raised by the first. Qualification limits a claim: The method is useful, provided that the samples are representative. Read the underlying claims first, then select a relationship that the evidence supports.\n\nBecause introduces a reason; so can introduce a result; so that commonly introduces purpose or result depending on context. We moved the display so that visitors could see it states an intended goal. We moved it, so visitors could see it more directly presents the resulting consequence. Before, after and then organise sequence without necessarily asserting causation. Or offers an alternative, while otherwise can introduce the consequence of rejecting an option: Submit the form today; otherwise, processing will be delayed. Each relationship needs enough context to distinguish it from plausible alternatives.\n\nUnless normally means if not in an exceptive condition, but replacing it mechanically can disturb scope. Do not enter unless authorised means authorisation is necessary for entry; it does not promise that every authorised person will enter. While can mark time or contrast; since can mark time or reason. A reader may need a less ambiguous connector when both readings are plausible. Moreover adds information but does not itself turn it into proof. Therefore presents an inference whose validity must be assessed independently of the grammar.\n\nDiscourse relations can be implicit: The road flooded. The delivery was late leaves a causal connection available without explicitly asserting it. Choosing because commits the writer more strongly to that explanation. Likewise a contrast is not automatically a concession; two different timetables need not violate anyone's expectation. Advanced revision should ask whose expectation a concessive clause invokes, what a qualification excludes and whether an apparent inference reverses cause and effect. These questions connect grammar to pragmatic interpretation while preserving a distinction between a well-formed sentence and a sound argument.\n\nbecause + reason clause: Presents the reason for the associated assertion.\n\nso that + finite clause: Often states an intended purpose; context can support a result reading.\n\nWe chose the ground floor because the lift was unavailable.\nThe subordinate clause supplies the stated reason.\n\nThe lift was unavailable; nevertheless, all visitors reached the exhibition.\nNevertheless marks a result contrary to a likely expectation.\n\nThe trial is promising, although its small sample limits the conclusion.\nThe concession qualifies the positive assessment rather than cancelling it.\n\nA connector can have several senses: since Monday marks a starting point, whereas since nobody objected can introduce a reason. Analyse the complement and context together.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=16&section=gv5-d16-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:19:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Connector classes and punctuation in use","text":"Connector classes and punctuation in use\n\nA conjunction links syntactic units; a linking adverb or discourse marker signals a relationship without necessarily supplying the grammatical clause boundary.\n\nCompare Although it rained, the match continued with Despite the rain, the match continued. Although introduces a finite clause; despite takes a noun phrase here. However behaves differently: It rained; however, the match continued. In this formal written pattern the semicolon separates the independent clauses, while however signals concession. A full stop is equally possible. Do not decide punctuation merely by where you pause aloud. First decide whether there are two independent clauses, a main clause plus dependent material, or a phrase within one clause.\n\nThe neutral pattern for two independent clauses joined by a coordinator is often a comma before and, but or so. Very short clauses may omit it, and complex clauses may justify stronger separation under an editorial style. An introductory subordinate clause usually ends with a comma. A following reason clause often needs no comma, but punctuation may distinguish supplementary reasoning or remove ambiguity. However can move inside a clause: The match, however, continued. Because cannot move in the same way while retaining the same clause structure.\n\nA word's traditional class can differ between constructions. Before lunch is prepositional; before we ate is traditionally subordinating. Contemporary grammars may analyse before as a preposition in both, with different complement types. This is a difference in framework, not an error in the sentence. Notwithstanding also has a less common formal conjunction use with a finite clause, alongside its prepositional use. Do not reject a construction simply because a beginner table lists only its most frequent pattern. Check the actual lexical entry and the intended register.\n\nDiscourse markers such as well, anyway and in fact organise conversation or an argument as well as local logical relations. Their presence cannot repair a run-on: The queue grew however the gate stayed shut still needs punctuation in edited prose. Conversely a sentence beginning with And or But is not automatically ungrammatical; its appropriateness depends on continuity and editorial style. A reliable diagnosis identifies the missing boundary, the connector's role and any genuine semantic inconsistency. It does not count the number of linking words and infer a fault without examining their scope.\n\nindependent clause; however, independent clause: Two assertions plus an explicit contrast.\n\nbecause of + noun phrase: A reason expressed through a prepositional phrase.\n\nThe bus was late because of a diversion.\nBecause of takes the noun phrase a diversion.\n\nThe bus was late because the road was closed.\nBecause introduces a finite reason clause.\n\nThe report was short. It was, however, fully documented.\nA sentence boundary separates the assertions; paired commas mark medial however.\n\nSome punctuation choices depend on style and clause length. Sentence-initial And/But and formal conjunctive notwithstanding are legitimate; do not label them universally incorrect.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=16&section=gv5-d16-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Conjunctions","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/conjunctions"},{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:20:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Placing modifiers and recovering their subjects","text":"Placing modifiers and recovering their subjects\n\nA modifier adds descriptive or limiting information to another expression. A misplaced modifier suggests an unintended attachment; a dangling modifier lacks a suitable understood participant in its grammatical context.\n\nStart by asking what each description is meant to describe. In a blue folder on the desk, blue describes the folder and on the desk locates it. In an opening participial clause such as Carrying a blue folder, Leena entered, Leena is normally understood as the person carrying the folder. Writing Carrying a blue folder, the door opened instead makes the door the expected carrier. The problem is not that the sentence begins with an -ing form; it is that the grammatical connection does not fit the intended event.\n\nPlace a modifier close enough to its target that readers can recover the intended relationship. A notice reading We sell bags for children made of recycled fabric invites the absurd reading that children are made of fabric. We sell recycled-fabric bags for children removes that attachment risk. An introductory past-participial clause can correctly attach to a passive subject: Damaged in transit, the equipment was returned. Equipment is both what was damaged and what was returned; a passive main clause is not automatically a dangling construction.\n\nA squinting modifier lies between expressions it could modify in either direction: Students who revise regularly improve. Regularly may describe revising or improvement. Rewrite around the intended message rather than move the word mechanically: Students who revise on a regular schedule improve, or Students who revise show regular improvement. Purpose infinitives need semantic care too. To reach the balcony, take the stairs has an understood imperative you; the absence of a printed subject does not make it faulty. To reach the balcony, the staircase is narrow gives no suitable controller of reach.\n\nThe subject-sharing guideline governs ordinary subjectless adjunct clauses, not every initial expression. An absolute construction supplies its own subject: The inspection complete, the team left. Conventional comments such as generally speaking or to be honest concern the speaker’s stance and need not describe the next noun as an action. Some apparent dangling expressions are established discourse constructions; others create a real unintended assertion. Expert revision identifies the construction before applying a rule. If a person’s identity is unknown, use a finite clause or an impersonal alternative instead of inventing a named agent to repair the sentence.\n\nSubjectless participial adjunct, + main subject + predicate: The understood participant normally matches the main subject.\n\nNoun phrase + participle, + clause: An absolute can supply a different explicit subject.\n\nWalking beside the canal, Farah noticed the damaged railing.\nFarah is the walker and the observer.\n\nStored below zero, the samples were stable for a week.\nThe passive main subject also receives the storage action.\n\nThe access road having flooded, the engineers postponed the visit.\nThe absolute has its own subject, the access road.\n\nConventional speaker comments such as Frankly speaking, the proposal is expensive do not require the proposal to speak.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=22&section=gv5-d22-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Participle clauses","url":"https://learnenglish.britishcouncil.org/grammar/c1-grammar/participle-clauses"},{"publisher":"Finite and non-finite verbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/finite-and-non-finite-verbs"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:20:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Attachment, scope and reference","text":"Attachment, scope and reference\n\nAmbiguity occurs when a form supports more than one interpretation. Attachment concerns which expression a modifier belongs to; scope concerns how much of a message an operator such as only or not affects.\n\nA grammatical sentence can be ambiguous without containing an error. I photographed the guide with the telescope may mean that I used the telescope or that the guide had it. The prepositional phrase can attach to the photographing event or to the noun guide. To revise, state the intended interpretation: Using the telescope, I photographed the guide; or I photographed the guide who was carrying the telescope. Neither rewrite is the universally correct answer because the original supplies no decision between the two meanings.\n\nScope differences are often created by focusing words. Only Salma reviewed the draft contrasts Salma with other possible reviewers. Salma reviewed only the draft contrasts that document with other possible objects. Salma only reviewed the draft can emphasise that she reviewed it rather than approved it, although spoken stress and context may support other readings. Keep the intended contrast explicit in formal prose. Negation interacts with quantifiers too: Not all the samples failed means at least some did not fail; none of the samples failed says that every sample avoided failure.\n\nPronoun reference requires a recoverable intended person or thing, but not every pronoun needs a single preceding noun. This can refer to an entire event: The lift stopped between floors. This delayed the delivery. That is a legitimate propositional reference. By contrast, When Hina met Sara, she was worried does not establish which woman was worried. Repeat a name or restructure the sentence if the distinction matters. Avoid solving ambiguity by guessing gender, role or a stereotype; preserve uncertainty when the source does not identify the intended referent.\n\nGrammatical ambiguity can coexist with pragmatic clarity. Context may make one reading overwhelmingly likely even though the structure permits another. Expert analysis separates a possible parse from a plausible intended meaning and identifies the evidence for each. Bound-variable readings also go beyond simple name replacement: Every researcher filed their own notes can vary the referent of their with each researcher. Do not replace such pronouns with one fixed name without changing meaning. Scope can remain underspecified intentionally, as in diplomatic language, but assessments must either supply enough context for one answer or explicitly reward the analysis of multiple readings.\n\nonly + focused constituent: Sets the constituent against contextually relevant alternatives.\n\nnot all + plural noun phrase: Denies universality; it does not assert none.\n\nOnly the backup server failed.\nThe focus excludes the other relevant servers from the set that failed.\n\nThe manager did not approve every claim.\nThis need not mean that the manager approved no claims.\n\nEach student labelled their own specimen.\nTheir varies with each student; singular they need not identify one fixed person.\n\nBare this is acceptable when its propositional referent is clear; adding a summary noun is a clarity tool, not a universal grammatical requirement.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=22&section=gv5-d22-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Adverbs - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/adverbs"},{"publisher":"It, this and that in paragraphs","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/it-this-and-that-in-paragraphs"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:20:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Parallelism, correlatives and comparisons","text":"Parallelism, correlatives and comparisons\n\nParallelism presents related elements in comparable grammatical or conceptual forms. It supports clear comparison and coordination, while grammatical coordination ultimately depends on the functions and constructions involved.\n\nLists are easier to process when their parts follow a recognisable pattern: The role involves scheduling visits, checking records and answering enquiries. Each -ing clause fills the same complement position after involves. A mixture such as involves scheduling visits, record checks and to answer enquiries becomes awkward because the last infinitive does not fit the pattern. Try each element after the shared opening. If an item cannot combine with that opening, repair the structure rather than merely changing its ending to resemble the neighbouring words.\n\nCorrelative pairs make structural expectations visible. The course is useful both for teachers and for editors places parallel prepositional phrases after both and and. The course both supports teachers and provides exercises coordinates verb phrases. Not only and but also can coordinate words, phrases or clauses. Fronted not only may require inversion in the clause it introduces, but it does not require every following item to be a full clause. Not only was the route shorter but also safer is a possible shared-copula construction; adding it was is another grammatical version.\n\nParallelism is not a rule that every coordinated unit must have an identical category label. A complement may coordinate expressions with a shared function: She is a capable editor and easy to work with can be understood as two predicative descriptions. Some mixed constructions are fully acceptable; others are confusing or disallowed by a particular head. In comparison, identify what is compared and what has been omitted. The new office is larger than the old one compares offices. The cost of the new office is higher than that of the old one compares costs, with that substituting for cost.\n\nReduced comparison clauses rely on recoverable material: Mei reads more articles than I do clearly compares readers; Mei reads more articles than me may also have that informal standard reading. Mei likes Jo more than I can mean more than I like Jo, whereas Mei likes Jo more than me can mean more than she likes me. Avoid using a case preference to erase a genuine reading. Formal revisions can expand ellipsis to show the intended relationship. At expert level, examine syntactic compatibility, omitted material and logical comparability separately; rhythmic symmetry alone does not prove grammatical or semantic equivalence.\n\nboth X and Y / either X or Y: The paired constituents should fit the same construction and intended relationship.\n\ncomparative + than-clause: Recover omitted material from context, or make it explicit.\n\nShe plans to inspect the roof and to test the alarm.\nThe two to-infinitives share a complementation role.\n\nNot only was the hall warmer but also quieter.\nThe copula is shared with coordinated predicative adjectives.\n\nThis week’s output exceeded last week’s output.\nThe compared quantities are of the same relevant type.\n\nDifferent forms can share a predicative function; evaluate the actual construction before declaring mixed categories ungrammatical.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=22&section=gv5-d22-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Parallelism","url":"https://writingcenter.unc.edu/tips-and-tools/parallelism/"},{"publisher":"Not only … but also","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/not-only-but-also"},{"publisher":"Ellipsis - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/ellipsis"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:21:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Clause boundaries, commas and semicolons","text":"Clause boundaries, commas and semicolons\n\nPunctuation marks grammatical boundaries and guides interpretation. Some choices repair an incomplete or incorrectly joined structure; others offer legitimate stylistic alternatives. A pause in speech is evidence about rhythm, not a sufficient rule for inserting a comma.\n\nA full stop normally closes a complete written statement. A comma can separate list items, enclose supplementary material and set off many introductory elements. It does not normally join two independent clauses by itself in formal edited prose: The printer failed, we used another office is a comma splice. Write two sentences, use a semicolon or add a suitable coordinator. Choose the relationship you mean rather than treating all repairs as interchangeable: because introduces a cause, whereas and merely adds information.\n\nA semicolon can link closely related independent clauses: The entrance was locked; the caretaker had gone home. A conjunctive adverb such as however does not become a coordinating conjunction. The entrance was locked; however, the side gate remained open is one defensible pattern. A semicolon can also separate complex list items that already contain commas. When an introductory subordinate clause precedes the main clause, a comma is common, particularly if it prevents a misreading. Short introductory adjuncts sometimes appear without one, depending on house style and clarity.\n\nCommas around a supplementary relative clause are normally paired when the main clause continues: The director, who had just arrived, opened the meeting. A defining relative identifying the intended member ordinarily has no such commas. Punctuation can therefore change reference, not merely rhythm. Do not separate a subject from its verb simply because the subject is long; internal commas may still enclose a parenthetical within that subject. The board, after a long discussion, approved the proposal has two commas performing that enclosing job. A comma before a coordinator may be omitted in short clear clauses or added to prevent confusion.\n\nA punctuation judgement must specify genre and intended analysis. Published literary prose sometimes uses deliberate fragments or comma splices for an identifiable effect; this does not make them a safe default for a formal answer. Conversely, a stylistic comma is not automatically an error. In cumulative editing, first identify every clause and the relation between clauses, then examine marks. A semicolon cannot turn a subordinate fragment into an independent clause. Because the lift was broken; we took the stairs remains faulty for the intended reason relationship. Write Because the lift was broken, we took the stairs instead.\n\nindependent clause ; independent clause: Links two complete, closely related statements.\n\nintroductory subordinate clause , main clause: A common clear boundary; short clauses allow some convention-dependent flexibility.\n\nThe results arrived. We checked the totals.\nTwo full stops separate complete statements.\n\nThe results arrived, but the annex was missing.\nBut coordinates two clauses and expresses contrast.\n\nThe teams came from Multan, Punjab; Sukkur, Sindh; and Quetta, Balochistan.\nSemicolons clarify complex list items containing internal commas.\n\nA short opening phrase may appear without a comma: In June we moved. Add one when it aids interpretation; do not declare one version universally ungrammatical.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=24&section=gv5-d24-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"Clauses and sentences - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/clauses-and-sentences"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: punctuation","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/punctuation"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:21:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Colons, apostrophes and compact written forms","text":"Colons, apostrophes and compact written forms\n\nA colon introduces an explanation, elaboration, list or quotation within an appropriate written structure. An apostrophe marks certain possessive and omitted-letter forms; it is not a general mark of plurality.\n\nA colon often follows a complete introductory clause: We need three items: a torch, a map and a whistle. Avoid separating an ordinary verb from its required object just to announce a list: The items include: a torch is unnecessary punctuation in continuous prose. Write The items include a torch, a map and a whistle. After the colon, material can be a phrase rather than a full clause. The punctuation signals how that material elaborates the introduction, so a colon differs from a semicolon joining independent clauses.\n\nFor a singular possessive, a common convention adds apostrophe-s: the writer’s desk. Regular plural possessives ending in s place the apostrophe after that ending: the writers’ desks. Irregular plurals take apostrophe-s: the children’s drawings. Its is possessive without an apostrophe, while it’s contracts it is or it has. Ordinary plurals and dates take no apostrophe: five MPs, the 1990s. Some style guides use apostrophes for single-letter plurals such as dot your i’s; that special convention does not justify writing three file’s.\n\nJoint and separate relationships need meaning rather than an automatic last-name rule. Ana and Omar’s shared flat presents one jointly held flat; Ana’s and Omar’s separate flats explicitly mark two relationships. Awkward expressions can be recast as the flat shared by Ana and Omar. Singular names ending in s admit house-style variation: James’s notebook and James’ notebook both occur in established editorial systems. Possessive relationships are broader than ownership, as in yesterday’s meeting. Contractions such as don’t and we’ve are grammatical; a formal assignment may prefer uncontracted forms without turning contractions into errors.\n\nNumerals, initials and abbreviations also involve convention. A publication may choose 8 October 2026 or October 8, 2026; apply one consistent system and avoid ambiguous numerical dates where readers use different orders. Dr and Dr. belong to different punctuation conventions, not different standards of intelligence. Introduce an unfamiliar initialism in full before relying on it. The plural normally adds s, while a possessive adds the relevant apostrophe: two NGOs and an NGO’s report. Decimal separators, units and percentage spacing can depend on disciplinary style; preserve a source’s numerical meaning when changing its presentation.\n\ncomplete introduction : elaboration: The second unit expands or specifies the first.\n\nregular plural ending in -s + apostrophe: Marks a possessive relationship involving a regular plural noun.\n\nOne difficulty remained: the missing signature.\nThe noun phrase after the colon explains the difficulty.\n\nThe teachers’ comments were attached to the children’s work.\nTeachers is regular plural; children is an irregular plural.\n\nIt’s evident that the NGO has changed its timetable.\nIt’s expands to it is, while its is possessive.\n\nApostrophes in single-letter plurals are allowed by some styles for clarity. Treat that as a limited editorial convention, not a rule for every plural.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=24&section=gv5-d24-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"},{"publisher":"Possession ( John’s car, a friend of mine ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/possession-john-s-car-a-friend-of-mine"},{"publisher":"MLA Style Center: Apostrophes, One Mark, Three Ways","url":"https://style.mla.org/apostrophes-three-ways/"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: abbreviations, contractions and acronyms","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/abbreviations"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:21:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Quotation, interruption and sentence-final marks","text":"Quotation, interruption and sentence-final marks\n\nQuotation marks identify quoted wording or another explicitly defined editorial use. Parentheses, brackets, dashes, hyphens and ellipses do different jobs; none is a general substitute for any inconvenient punctuation.\n\nA direct question normally ends with a question mark: Has the office reopened? An indirect report normally ends with the mark appropriate to the whole sentence: She asked whether the office had reopened. Do you know whether it has reopened? is itself a question and takes a question mark. An exclamation mark signals an exclamatory expression or emphatic utterance. Multiple exclamation marks occur in informal messages, but restrained formal writing normally avoids them. That preference concerns register; it is not evidence that informal messages must obey a ban on repeated marks.\n\nSingle or double outer quotation marks can both be used under established styles. When a quotation contains another quotation, switch the inner pair to distinguish levels. Punctuation placement depends partly on whether a mark belongs to the quoted words and partly on the chosen convention. British logical placement and common American practice differ for commas and full stops. Question marks should reflect the intended question: Did she say “Leave now”? asks about the saying; She asked, “May I leave?” quotes a question. Do not add a second full stop mechanically after the closing quotation.\n\nRound parentheses enclose an aside; square brackets in a quotation commonly identify editorial additions or clarifications. The brackets must not make an uncertain interpretation look like the speaker’s original wording. Dashes can mark interruption, amplification or a strongly separated parenthesis. A hyphen joins elements within some compounds: a three-year project. It is shorter and has a different function from a dash. The need for hyphens varies by position and convention: a well-known author but the author is well known. An -ly adverb normally does not need a hyphen in a clearly written modifier such as a carefully planned route.\n\nAn ellipsis can mark an omission from quoted text or a trailing-off effect in an appropriate genre. Omitting words must not change the represented claim. Removing not from a quotation is a substantive distortion even if an ellipsis is printed. A complete omitted sentence and an omitted phrase may be punctuated differently by different style guides; consult the specified guide for the number and spacing of dots. Parenthetical material inside a sentence normally leaves the main sentence’s ending outside the closing parenthesis; a complete independent parenthetical sentence may retain its own final mark inside. Analyse the enclosing structure before choosing the position.\n\nouter quotation + contrasting inner quotation: Keeps nested quoted material distinguishable.\n\nnumber + unit singular + noun, with a hyphenated modifier: A common pattern: a ten-minute break; compare the break lasted ten minutes.\n\nShe asked, “Is the archive open?”\nThe question mark belongs to the quoted question.\n\nThe archive closes at six (except on Fridays).\nThe parenthesis sits inside the sentence; its full stop follows the closing parenthesis.\n\nThe report described a carefully planned, three-stage operation.\nThe -ly modifier has no hyphen; three-stage is a compound premodifier.\n\nSome familiar compounds keep a dictionary hyphen even in positions where other compounds open up. Check a reliable dictionary and the required house style rather than inventing a universal positional rule.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=24&section=gv5-d24-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: punctuation","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/punctuation"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:21:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Capitalisation, spelling and editorial consistency","text":"Capitalisation, spelling and editorial consistency\n\nOrthography is the system of written forms, including spelling, capitalisation and word division. Some orthographic choices distinguish words; others vary legitimately between national and editorial conventions.\n\nCapitalise the first word of a normal written sentence, the pronoun I and proper names: Zainab, Pakistan, Urdu and October. A common noun does not become a proper noun merely because it is important: the committee announced the decision is a normal general description. A particular organisation’s official name can require capitals: the National Library. Titles before personal names often receive capitals, while general references often remain lower case. Check whether the wording is an official name, a descriptive phrase or a house-style abbreviation before changing its capitals.\n\nBritish and American spelling conventions differ in words such as colour/color, centre/center and travelled/traveled. Both standard systems are legitimate; this course uses British defaults and identifies American alternatives. British publishing itself permits more than one convention for some words, including forms in -ise and -ize. Do not label every -ize spelling American. Programme is a common British form for a plan or broadcast, while program is standard in computing. A consistent editing policy should still allow quoted material, names and technical forms to retain their authentic spelling.\n\nCapitalisation of headings and titles varies between sentence case and title case. A sentence-case heading need not capitalise every major word. Institutions and legal publications may prescribe forms such as Section 12, while another style uses section 12 in running prose. Names of languages keep capitals; ordinary names of academic fields often do not unless they form part of an official course title. Compound words may be open, closed or hyphenated, and usage changes over time: a dictionary entry and a publication’s stated style are better evidence than a remembered universal rule.\n\nSeparate error correction from editorial normalisation. Writing enviroment for environment is a spelling error; writing organization in a British document may match an established British house style. A question that asks for one correct spelling must avoid offering two standard variants unless it states the convention being tested. Numerical forms also need a stated purpose: scientific prose, dates, tables and literary prose often use different presentation rules. In your final editing pass, check the chosen system, official names, consistency and meaning. Do not remove capitals, hyphens or abbreviations blindly from specialist material merely because the general default differs.\n\nproper name → capitalisation appropriate to the name: Distinguishes an identified name from a general description.\n\nhouse style + dictionary evidence: Resolves legitimate variants without inventing grammatical prohibitions.\n\nThe department offers courses in chemistry and English.\nEnglish names a language; chemistry is a general field name here.\n\nThe colour of the laboratory’s sign has changed.\nColour follows the course’s British editorial default.\n\nThe software program supports the festival programme.\nThe two forms can coexist under a normal British computing/general distinction.\n\nOfficial names, quotations and stipulated specialist notation can retain a different convention within an otherwise consistent document.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=24&section=gv5-d24-m04&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Punctuation","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/punctuation"},{"publisher":"British and American English - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/british-and-american-english"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: words","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/words"},{"publisher":"University of Oxford Style Guide: abbreviations, contractions and acronyms","url":"https://www.ox.ac.uk/about/the-university/brand/style-guide/abbreviations"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:22:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Summarising, précis and preserving essential qualifications","text":"Summarising, précis and preserving essential qualifications\n\nA summary selects a text’s central message; a précis is a disciplined concise restatement under specified length and presentation constraints. Neither is a licence to invent facts or strengthen a claim.\n\nBegin by identifying the source’s topic, main claim, supporting reasons and important limits. A summary is shorter because some detail is omitted, so the claim that every word or every fact must survive is unrealistic. What must survive is the source’s essential meaning at the requested level of detail. If the source says a measure may help some families, a summary saying the measure helps all families is inaccurate. A word target belongs to the assignment. One third is a common exercise convention, not a grammatical law for every summary.\n\nSeparate a proposition from its examples. If a passage lists several dishes only to illustrate a varied menu, dishes may replace the list. If the passage argues that those particular foods caused an allergy, the exact list may matter. Classification words must not add evaluation: wheat and rice can become crops, but major crops asserts importance not supplied by the list alone. Preserve a condition when it limits the claim: free entry for registered students is not equivalent to free entry. Decide what to omit by its role in the argument, not by length alone.\n\nUse grammatical compression carefully. The applicants who were selected becomes the selected applicants when the selection meaning and intended scope remain clear. Because the tank leaked can become because of a tank leak. The rewrite may lose tense or explicit participants unless the surrounding text supplies them. A decision to act does not entail action: decided to close the school cannot become closed the school merely to save words. Likewise, not all changes are reversals: failed to confirm is weaker than denied. Compare the original and revision proposition by proposition.\n\nFor an original reduction exercise, work from this fictional passage: A village library extended its Saturday opening for a six-week trial. Volunteers staffed the extra hour, so the council did not increase paid staffing. Visitor numbers rose during the trial, mainly among secondary-school pupils. However, the librarian warned that the period overlapped with examinations and was too short to show a lasting change. She recommended a longer trial before any permanent extension. A faithful brief summary reports the increased visits, volunteer arrangement and evidential caution. It must not claim the extension proved a permanent demand or that volunteers could necessarily sustain the arrangement indefinitely.\n\nsource claim + essential restriction → shorter equivalent: Retains who, what, scope and commitment while reducing wording.\n\nSelected applicants must attend an interview.\nThe reduced relative identifies the selected group.\n\nThe library’s trial coincided with examinations.\nThis qualification limits interpretation of increased visits.\n\nVolunteer staffing accompanied a temporary extension, but a longer trial was recommended.\nThe statement preserves the temporary arrangement and recommendation without claiming permanence.\n\nA short summary can omit illustrative detail while retaining essential meaning; a task demanding exact equivalence permits fewer omissions.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=27&section=gv5-d27-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Writing Concisely","url":"https://writingcenter.unc.edu/tips-and-tools/conciseness-handout/"},{"publisher":"Relative clauses - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/relative-clauses"},{"publisher":"Hedges ( just )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/hedges-just"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:22:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Paraphrase, synthesis and faithful reported claims","text":"Paraphrase, synthesis and faithful reported claims\n\nParaphrase re-expresses a source’s message; synthesis relates messages from more than one source. Both require grammatical accuracy and faithful treatment of time, modality, attribution and logical relations.\n\nA paraphrase can alter sentence structure, word choice and information order while preserving the relevant proposition. The team postponed the launch because the battery failed can become Battery failure caused the team to postpone the launch. Both identify the same actor, event and cause. Replacing words with dictionary synonyms without checking their grammatical patterns can produce errors. Depend on does not become depend from because another language uses a different relation word. Keep the source available while checking meaning, then use independent wording rather than copying its sentence and replacing a few words.\n\nTrack tense and aspect. The plant has closed presents closure with current relevance; the plant closed in March locates a completed event at a specified past time. Either can fit a paraphrase when the necessary time information is retained, but adding March without a source invents a fact. Track modality too: must may express an obligation or a deduction, while may can express permission or possibility. The school may close is not the same as the school must close. Scope matters: not every application succeeded means some failed, not that none succeeded.\n\nA synthesis must distinguish agreement, difference and silence. Source A says the scheme reduced travel time in two towns; Source B says ticket costs rose. A combined sentence can report both, but cannot claim costs rose because travel time fell unless a source establishes that relationship. Attribution can be compressed without vanishing: According to the survey ... or The authors argue ... identifies whose claim is reported. A reporting verb such as prove, admit or concede may add evidential or evaluative meaning absent from the source. Retain neutral said, report or find when stronger force is unjustified.\n\nMeaning preservation includes entailments and implications. A transformation that makes a restrictive relative supplementary may imply that every member of a group has the property. Removing only or changing the position of a negative can alter scope while leaving the sentence fluent. A paraphrase does not need to reproduce all rhetorical emphasis, but a task may require preserving contrast, caution or a writer’s stance. In expert revision, list the propositions before comparing sentences: actor, action, affected participant, time, quantifier, modality, source and connection. Where two readings remain possible, explain the ambiguity rather than certify a single equivalent mechanically.\n\nSource A reports X, whereas Source B reports Y: Makes differing attributed claims explicit.\n\nnot every + noun: Denies universality rather than asserting universal failure.\n\nThe report suggests that costs may fall.\nAttribution and possibility both remain visible.\n\nNot every licence was renewed.\nAt least one was not renewed; the sentence does not say none were.\n\nThe first survey found shorter journeys; the second recorded higher fares.\nThe synthesis relates results without inventing a causal link.\n\nA past source can be reported in a present reporting frame when its argument is treated as current; preserve the events’ actual time relations.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=27&section=gv5-d27-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Hedges ( just )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/hedges-just"},{"publisher":"Relative clauses - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/relative-clauses"},{"publisher":"Reported speech: indirect speech - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/reported-speech-indirect-speech"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:22:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Translation and formal written answers","text":"Translation and formal written answers\n\nGrammatical translation conveys the intended message through the target language’s own constructions. Formal expression selects structures appropriate to a purpose and audience while retaining meaning.\n\nTranslation is not a word-by-word substitution exercise. Languages differ in word order, agreement, articles, pronouns, tense-aspect patterns and conventional combinations. Begin with a plain account of the intended meaning, including who acts and when. Then construct an English sentence from that meaning. A language without English-style articles does not supply an automatic equivalent for a and the; decide whether the English referent is countable and identifiable. Learners can use any first language for this analysis. Urdu-based examples are useful options, not assumptions about every learner’s background.\n\nFor a controlled translation exercise, the source message can be specified in plain meaning notes instead of requiring knowledge of another language. Message: speaker lives in Multan; residence began in 2019 and continues. Suitable English includes I have lived in Multan since 2019 and I have been living in Multan since 2019. The progressive highlights duration or ongoing activity; the simple perfect is also standard. A literal I am living ... since 2019 is not the ordinary edited pattern for that intended continuing interval. Check meaning and idiom rather than matching one model string.\n\nFormal correspondence needs clear participants, actions, dates and requested responses. Please send the revised form by Friday is often clearer than a chain of nominalisations. A form such as We request that the form be returned by Friday is also grammatical and may suit a formal institution. The audience determines register; first person, contractions and ordinary short verbs are not universal grammar errors. Regional expressions can be established within their communities while unfamiliar elsewhere. For international readers, choose transparent wording and explain why, instead of treating the regional speaker as grammatically incapable.\n\nAcademic answers must distinguish a source’s assertion from the writer’s interpretation. In The author argues that restrictions may reduce demand, the writer reports a cautious claim; changing may to will increases commitment. In translation and paraphrase, preserve negation, quantity, politeness, evidential status and temporal relations even if the new sentence uses a different voice. Back-translation can expose a mismatch, but it is not proof of exact equivalence because several expressions may encode a message. A good rubric recognises alternatives and evaluates specified features. It must not pretend that comparison with a model objectively grades an independently written passage.\n\nmeaning notes → idiomatic target construction: Uses English grammar to express the intended message.\n\nrequest + action + deadline: Makes formal correspondence actionable.\n\nPlease return the signed declaration by Thursday.\nThe action, document and deadline are explicit.\n\nI have lived in Multan since 2019.\nThe present perfect expresses continuing residence from a past starting point.\n\nThe authors argue that the policy may reduce waste.\nThe report preserves source and possibility rather than presenting a proven result.\n\nA regional expression can be standard in its variety; choosing a wider-audience alternative is an editorial decision, not proof of universal error.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=27&section=gv5-d27-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Formal and informal language","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/formal-and-informal-language"},{"publisher":"Present perfect simple ( I have worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-i-have-worked"},{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:22:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Translation and formal written answers","text":"Translation and formal written answers\n\nGrammatical translation conveys the intended message through the target language’s own constructions. Formal expression selects structures appropriate to a purpose and audience while retaining meaning.\n\nTranslation is not a word-by-word substitution exercise. Languages differ in word order, agreement, articles, pronouns, tense-aspect patterns and conventional combinations. Begin with a plain account of the intended meaning, including who acts and when. Then construct an English sentence from that meaning. A language without English-style articles does not supply an automatic equivalent for a and the; decide whether the English referent is countable and identifiable. Learners can use any first language for this analysis. Urdu-based examples are useful options, not assumptions about every learner’s background.\n\nFor a controlled translation exercise, the source message can be specified in plain meaning notes instead of requiring knowledge of another language. Message: speaker lives in Multan; residence began in 2019 and continues. Suitable English includes I have lived in Multan since 2019 and I have been living in Multan since 2019. The progressive highlights duration or ongoing activity; the simple perfect is also standard. A literal I am living ... since 2019 is not the ordinary edited pattern for that intended continuing interval. Check meaning and idiom rather than matching one model string.\n\nFormal correspondence needs clear participants, actions, dates and requested responses. Please send the revised form by Friday is often clearer than a chain of nominalisations. A form such as We request that the form be returned by Friday is also grammatical and may suit a formal institution. The audience determines register; first person, contractions and ordinary short verbs are not universal grammar errors. Regional expressions can be established within their communities while unfamiliar elsewhere. For international readers, choose transparent wording and explain why, instead of treating the regional speaker as grammatically incapable.\n\nAcademic answers must distinguish a source’s assertion from the writer’s interpretation. In The author argues that restrictions may reduce demand, the writer reports a cautious claim; changing may to will increases commitment. In translation and paraphrase, preserve negation, quantity, politeness, evidential status and temporal relations even if the new sentence uses a different voice. Back-translation can expose a mismatch, but it is not proof of exact equivalence because several expressions may encode a message. A good rubric recognises alternatives and evaluates specified features. It must not pretend that comparison with a model objectively grades an independently written passage.\n\nmeaning notes → idiomatic target construction: Uses English grammar to express the intended message.\n\nrequest + action + deadline: Makes formal correspondence actionable.\n\nPlease return the signed declaration by Thursday.\nThe action, document and deadline are explicit.\n\nI have lived in Multan since 2019.\nThe present perfect expresses continuing residence from a past starting point.\n\nThe authors argue that the policy may reduce waste.\nThe report preserves source and possibility rather than presenting a proven result.\n\nA regional expression can be standard in its variety; choosing a wider-audience alternative is an editorial decision, not proof of universal error.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=27&section=gv5-d27-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Formal and informal language","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/formal-and-informal-language"},{"publisher":"Present perfect simple ( I have worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-i-have-worked"},{"publisher":"Modality: meanings and uses","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/modality-meanings-and-uses"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:23:0","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Connecting sentences: cohesion, reference and recoverable omission","text":"Connecting sentences: cohesion, reference and recoverable omission\n\nCohesion consists of visible grammatical and lexical links across a text. Coherence is the interpretable connection among its ideas; a text can contain many linking words and still lack a sensible argument.\n\nRead two sentences together before judging their connection. A pronoun can carry forward an established participant: A courier arrived. She carried a sealed envelope. Repeating courier is not an error, but she is economical when its reference is clear. This and that can also refer to a whole previous event: The envelope was empty. This caused concern. A summary noun, such as this omission, can identify the intended aspect more precisely. It is useful when several earlier facts could compete, not compulsory after every this.\n\nBuild a reference chain by tracking a participant through noun phrases, pronouns and descriptions. A visitor, the visitor and she can form a chain. Do not vary technical labels just to avoid repeating a word: a permit, a licence and an authorisation may refer to different legal objects. Substitution replaces repeated material with a smaller expression: The red folder is damaged; use the blue one. Ellipsis leaves recoverable material unspoken: Mara can attend, but I cannot. The omitted content is attend, understood from the first clause.\n\nOmission has grammatical and interpretative limits. In The first room was warm; the second, cold, the missing was is recoverable in a coordinated comparison. In a detached formal sentence such as The second cold, it may instead look like a fragment. So can substitute for a proposition after suitable verbs: I think so. Do so commonly substitutes for an action: The clerk promised to check and did so. It is not interchangeable with it or one in every context. Choose the substitute by the size and kind of unit being replaced.\n\nReference can point backwards, forwards or outside the text. The first is often called anaphora, the second cataphora and the third deixis. A pronoun need not replace a single noun: propositional this can refer to a preceding clause or stretch of discourse. However, an editor should test whether readers recover the intended content rather than merely any plausible content. Cohesion also includes lexical repetition and relations such as part–whole. These support interpretation but do not establish truth or logical consequence. A therefore between unrelated claims creates a misleading inference rather than coherence.\n\nestablished noun phrase → pronoun: Maintains a participant when reference is identifiable.\n\nauxiliary + omitted verb phrase: Avoids repeating material recoverable from context.\n\nA lamp failed. It was replaced immediately.\nIt continues the lamp reference.\n\nThe first proposal was costly; the second, affordable.\nWas is recoverable in the second coordinated unit.\n\nThe supplier promised to replace the faulty part and did so.\nDid so substitutes for replacing the part, not for the supplier.\n\nInformal situational ellipsis such as Ready? is normal conversation, though a formal standalone report usually supplies the full clause.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=26&section=gv5-d26-m01&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"It, this and that in paragraphs","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/it-this-and-that-in-paragraphs"},{"publisher":"Ellipsis - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/ellipsis"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:23:1","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Time, topics and the movement of information","text":"Time, topics and the movement of information\n\nTextual progression is the way a passage moves from established information to a developing point. Temporal consistency preserves intended time relationships, rather than requiring every verb to have one tense.\n\nA reader needs to know how the next sentence relates to the current one. A useful default is to begin from something already identifiable and then develop it: The school opened a workshop. The workshop offers evening classes. You can also repeat a subject deliberately to maintain a topic. Beginning with new information is legitimate when introducing a contrast or changing focus. The practical question is whether the sequence guides the intended reader, not whether every sentence follows one fixed template or starts with a different word.\n\nTransitions express relations such as addition, contrast, concession, consequence and example. They do not create those relations. The route was shorter; nevertheless, it took longer expresses an unexpected contrast. The route was shorter; therefore, it took longer would require extra circumstances to explain the result. Topic sentences often state a paragraph’s controlling idea, but can occur after an opening example or be implicit. A paragraph may contain several related claims serving one purpose. Organise supporting details so that the relationship among them remains visible.\n\nTense choices can change purposefully within a paragraph. The clinic opened in 2018 and now treats patients daily contrasts a completed opening with an ongoing practice. A research summary may use the past for methods and the present for a still-relevant interpretation. A past perfect can place an event before the narrative reference point, after which ordinary past forms may continue the sequence. Voice also changes legitimately to maintain the topic: We received a damaged instrument. It was repaired the next day. The passive keeps the instrument in view.\n\nTheme and rheme are terms used in some linguistic frameworks for the point of departure of a clause and its development. Given and new describe information status, while topic and focus describe what is discussed and what is foregrounded. These distinctions overlap but are not identical. A passive, cleft or fronted phrase can organise information without altering the underlying event. Changes can nevertheless affect implication, contrast or presupposition. When revising an extended passage, compare its entire information route; a locally concise active sentence may break the established chain that a passive sentence maintained more effectively.\n\npast event + now + present situation: Separates a completed event from a current state.\n\nclaim + nevertheless + contrasting outcome: Signals an outcome contrary to an expectation.\n\nThe roof leaked last winter. It is secure now.\nA justified past-to-present shift distinguishes then from now.\n\nAn old map was discovered. The map revealed a forgotten path.\nNew map information becomes the next sentence’s topic.\n\nThe laboratory rejected the first sample. A second sample was then requested.\nThe voice changes, but the sequence remains coherent.\n\nA repeated opening can create clear thematic continuity; variation is a rhetorical choice, not a requirement to avoid the same subject three times.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=26&section=gv5-d26-m02&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"It, this and that in paragraphs","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/it-this-and-that-in-paragraphs"},{"publisher":"Past perfect simple ( I had worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/past-perfect-simple-i-had-worked"},{"publisher":"Present perfect simple ( I have worked ) - Cambridge Grammar","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/present-perfect-simple-i-have-worked"},{"publisher":"Passive voice - Grammar - Cambridge Dictionary","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/passive-voice"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:23:2","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Academic compression, hedging and economical variety","text":"Academic compression, hedging and economical variety\n\nGrammatical compression expresses information with less explicit clausal structure; hedging adjusts commitment, scope or politeness. Neither compression nor formal vocabulary automatically improves a claim.\n\nA verb can describe an event directly: The council approved the plan. A noun can package it for later discussion: This approval was unexpected. That change is nominalisation. It is useful when an earlier action becomes a new topic, or when a technical field needs an established concept. It can also conceal agents: approval of the plan leaves the approver unnamed. Ask which form gives readers the information they need. There is no universal rule that nouns are weak and verbs are strong.\n\nCompress only material whose contribution you understand. Because the samples were contaminated, the team repeated the test can become Because of sample contamination, the team repeated the test. The noun phrase is shorter, but it no longer states the contamination as a finite clause. A reduced relative can save words: the files that were damaged becomes the damaged files, though position and scope may change. Dense noun strings can obscure relations: student travel safety review may need a phrase explaining whose travel and what is being reviewed.\n\nA cautious academic claim can mark uncertainty (may explain), evidential limits (the interviews suggest), frequency (often) or scope (in the sampled schools). These are different contributions. Removing one can exaggerate the evidence. A narrow claim is not necessarily well-supported; it still needs evidence. Likewise, a figure supplies precision but is not automatically a hedge. Several qualifications can be necessary if they perform distinct jobs: The small sample suggests that the change may help some learners. Avoid stacking synonyms that add no further distinction.\n\nSyntactic variety serves clarity when each structure fits its information task. Active clauses can identify actors; passives can maintain a topic or appropriately background an unknown actor; nominalisations can organise an argument; explicit clauses can unpack compressed relations. Register belongs to a genre and audience rather than a hierarchy of longer words. A first-person statement of method can be appropriate in academic prose if the discipline permits it. An editor should distinguish grammatical defects, unsupported assertions and optional stylistic changes, then preserve modality, attribution and logical relationships while improving the text.\n\nclause → nominalisation + complement: Packages an event for further discussion.\n\nevidence suggests that ... may ...: Separates evidential support from modal possibility.\n\nThe panel rejected the bid. This rejection delayed the project.\nThe noun packages the earlier action as a topic.\n\nThe interviews suggest that the policy may help new entrants.\nSuggest and may mark distinct aspects of commitment.\n\nBecause the labels were missing, the samples could not be identified.\nAn explicit causal clause can be clearer than a dense noun string.\n\nPurposeful repetition and a well-chosen passive may improve a text even when a shorter active alternative exists.","attribution":"CSS VISTA original reconstructed grammar teaching, edition grammar-v5-2026-10. Context-sensitive instruction; not an official past question.","return_path":"/grammar-course?day=26&section=gv5-d26-m03&edition=v5","date":null,"sources":[{"publisher":"Writing Concisely","url":"https://writingcenter.unc.edu/tips-and-tools/conciseness-handout/"},{"publisher":"Hedges ( just )","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/hedges-just"},{"publisher":"Formal and informal language","url":"https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/formal-and-informal-language"}],"feature":"tutor","legacy_alias":true},{"id":"grammar:23:3","category":"grammar","title":"Reconstructed related topic: Academic compression, hedging and economical variety","text":"Academic compression, hedging and economical variety\n\nGrammatical compression expresses information with less explicit clausal structure; hedging adjusts commitment, scope or politeness. Neither compression nor formal vocabulary automatically improves a claim.\n\nA verb can describe an event directly: The council approved the plan. A noun can package it for later discussion: This approval was unexpected. That change is nominalisation. It is useful when an earlier action becomes a new topic, or when a technical field needs an established concept. It can also conceal agents: approval of the plan leaves the approver unnamed. Ask which form gives readers the information they need. There is no universal rule that nouns are weak and verbs are strong.\n\nCompress only material whose contribution you understand. Because the samples were contaminated, the team repeated the test can become Because of sample contamination, the team repeated the test. The noun phrase is shorter, but it no longer states the contamination as a finite clause. A reduced relative can save words: the files that were damaged becomes the damaged files, though position and scope may change. Dense noun strings can obscure relations: student travel safety review may need a phrase explaining whose travel and what is being reviewed.\n\nA cautious academic claim can mark uncertainty (may explain), evidential limits (the interviews suggest), frequency (often) or scope (in the sampled schools). These are different contributions. Removing one can exaggerate the evidence. A narrow claim is not necessari